    "bodyMarkdown": "# Meowdoku!: Player Review Digest\n\n*Player-review digest, not a playtest. This analysis combines the US listing with 50 saved US App Store reviews dated 2026-11-09–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 1.24.0. This self-selected sample is not a representative survey; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMeowdoku! has an appealingly strict cat-placement puzzle beneath its cozy theme. The saved players often enjoy the reasoning, but their accounts complicate the developer’s promise of undisturbed concentration.\n\n## One cat, several overlapping restrictions\n\nEach colored region needs exactly one cat. Cats cannot share a row or column, and cannot touch even diagonally. This is region-placement logic, not standard number Sudoku. A square that works for one region may eliminate needed options somewhere else on the board.\n\nThe listing specifies double-tapping to place a cat and three hearts for mistakes. Daily puzzles and fastest-time leaderboards provide additional goals. That makes careful commitment important: the game’s cute presentation does not imply a penalty-free scratchpad.\n\n## Why players keep solving\n\nSpecific positive accounts describe a little challenge at a satisfying level and enjoyment of testing their own thinking. Another calls it a fun alternative to Sudoku. These comments support the attraction of deduction without needing to turn casual claims about brain benefits into scientific findings.\n\nThe control and presentation complaints are more revealing than a generic difficulty warning. One player reports accidental double-taps causing strikes. Another objects to a blinking last-move highlight that makes studying the next move difficult. Both concerns directly affect a puzzle where careful observation matters.\n\nA reviewer also describes repeated levels and unhelpful hints. That is a report about their progression experience, not a verified count of duplicate boards or proof that deduction eventually becomes impossible.\n\n## Ads: the listing and sample diverge\n\nThe developer advertises nonintrusive ads. Some reviewers likewise report no ads or only optional ones. Others describe ads between levels, long closing sequences, or interruptions severe enough to stop playing.\n\nThese conflicts prevent an ad-free recommendation. One account says ads appeared after an initially ad-free stretch, but that does not establish a universal trigger. A paid-ad-removal complaint also appears; it is not independent proof of what the offer covers.\n\nThe saved download is free, and offline play is a developer claim rather than a tested finding. Players who chiefly want the placement logic have a clear reason to consider it. Those sensitive to flashing highlights, accidental input penalties, or interruptions have specific concerns to check first.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMeowdoku’s region logic has genuine supporters, but the sample makes its calm, nonintrusive positioning conditional. Consider it for the cats-and-constraints puzzle, not a verified promise of quiet or ad-free play.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is Meowdoku ordinary Sudoku?\n\nNo. You place one cat per colored region while respecting row, column, and adjacency restrictions.\n\n### Does Meowdoku have forced ads?\n\nSome sampled reviewers describe between-level ads; others report no ads or optional ads. The disagreement remains unresolved.\n\n### Can accidental taps cost a heart?\n\nThe listing penalizes wrong placements, and one reviewer specifically reports accidental double-taps causing strikes.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/meowdoku/id6761760135?uo=4).",
