    "bodyMarkdown": "# Magic Sort!: Player Review Digest\n\n*Player-review digest, not a playtest. This analysis combines the US listing with 50 saved US App Store reviews dated 2026-11-05–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 34.25. This recent, self-selected sample is not a representative survey; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMagic Sort! has a clear strategic appeal: consolidate layered water into single-color bottles without using up the space needed for your next pour. Recent feedback likes that idea more consistently than the systems around it.\n\n## The puzzle beneath the potion theme\n\nTap a source bottle, then a destination. The listing permits a pour when the receiving bottle has room and its top color matches. Your broader objective is to leave each bottle containing only one color. Empty capacity is therefore a resource, not wasted space: a locally convenient pour can still leave the remaining colors blocked.\n\nUndo and Shuffle are advertised recovery tools. The magical framing adds ingredients and boosts, but the important buying question is whether those assists complement satisfying reasoning or become the route around frustrating levels.\n\n## What the saved reviewers actually describe\n\nThe positive side is concrete. One reviewer enjoys having to think through completion; another calls harder levels a balance of strategy and relaxation. A separate positive review explicitly likes the hard levels. Difficulty is not automatically a complaint in this sample.\n\nThe opposing experience is that some stages stop feeling explainable. Reviewers describe being stuck, running out of lives, or feeling pushed toward extra bottles and coins. Those accounts support a warning about frustration and perceived spending pressure, not a factual finding that a level is mathematically impossible or deliberately rigged.\n\nOne reviewer reports midgame crashes. That is a specific stability concern to watch for, not enough evidence to label the entire app unstable. Reports about content length also conflict: a claim of only 65 levels sits alongside accounts naming levels above 1,000. The sample does not resolve why.\n\n## Ads and spending: a genuine disagreement\n\nSeveral reviews object to long advertisements or difficult closing flows, including a five-star review whose text says ads prompted deletion. Star labels alone would miss that criticism. In direct contrast, other reviewers report no ads at all.\n\nNeither experience should be erased. These reports do not establish a single ad schedule or explain whether account, purchase, or rollout differences are involved. Spending complaints center on lives and assistance; the sample does not supply a reliable current price list.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe sorting loop has identifiable fans, including players who welcome challenge. Approach cautiously if interrupted play or life-based retries spoil logic games for you; the conflicting ad reports prevent an ad-free recommendation.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is Magic Sort! ad-free?\n\nReviewers disagree: some report no ads, while others describe intrusive advertising. No universal ad-free claim is justified.\n\n### Why do players mention extra bottles?\n\nSome describe extra bottles or coins as ways around difficult stages. Their accounts do not prove those purchases are required for every player.\n\n### Does Magic Sort! have Undo?\n\nUndo and Shuffle are advertised in the store description. Their availability and cost have not been independently tested.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/magic-sort/id6499209744?uo=4).",
