    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block Blast！: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nBlock Blast turns an empty square of board space into a strategic asset. You drag shapes onto an 8×8 grid, complete rows or columns, and try to preserve room for the next placement.\n\n## Placement rather than falling-block reflexes\n\nThe listing describes dragging blocks onto the board. Its core challenge is therefore choosing locations, not assuming the familiar falling-piece controls of every game with square tiles. Completing a row or column clears tiles and contributes to your score.\n\nThe interesting trade-off is between a tempting immediate clear and a board that remains usable afterward. A placement can earn points while leaving awkward holes; another can hold off on a clear but preserve a larger area. The developer encourages combos, adding a reason to look beyond a single line at a time.\n\nAn endless high-score mode supplies a repeatable target without requiring a story. Adventure mode offers progressive levels with jigsaw-style designs and changing themes, while daily puzzles provide another route through the same general placement vocabulary. These are distinct advertised goals, not evidence that all modes share identical rules or difficulty.\n\n## Which kind of puzzle appetite it serves\n\nBlock Blast is an obvious candidate for players who prefer spatial organization over word knowledge or number deduction. The appeal is making a congested board workable again, then trying to improve a previous result. Anyone who primarily wants handcrafted logic problems with one provable answer should recognize that the high-score framing asks a different kind of question.\n\nThe description mentions sharing results and competing with friends. It does not establish whether social features are necessary to progress. Nor does the promise of daily rewards tell us how much participation is expected or how quickly any reward track fills.\n\n## Advertising and cost remain separate questions\n\nThe saved download is free. The supplied description does not document ad frequency, paid removal, or the full purchase system. Those omissions matter because a clean-looking board is not evidence of uninterrupted play. There are no review bodies in this collection from which to derive player satisfaction, annoyance, or stability reports.\n\nThe case for trying it rests on the well-defined board and clearing loop, not on treating its aggregate popularity as a tested quality score.\n\n## Verdict\n\nChoose Block Blast for spatial placement and repeat score attempts, with daily and Adventure alternatives advertised. Pass if you specifically want a traditional falling-block game or a verified interruption-free experience.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How big is the Block Blast board?\n\nThe listing specifies an 8×8 board for its block-placement puzzle.\n\n### How do you clear blocks?\n\nComplete rows or columns of tiles, according to the listed rules.\n\n### Does Block Blast have levels?\n\nAdventure mode advertises progressive levels; the listing also describes an endless high-score system.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-blast/id1617391485?uo=4).",
