    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bus Traffic Fever!: Player Review Digest\n\n*Player-review digest, not a playtest. This analysis combines the US listing with 50 saved US App Store reviews dated 2026-11-05–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 1.8.1. This self-selected sample is not a representative survey; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nBus Traffic Fever! attracts players with a satisfying congestion puzzle, then divides them over how much room, time, and assistance the later challenges allow.\n\n## Parking and colors, with rules players want explained\n\nThe brief listing describes moving, parking, and collecting in a parking jam that combines color sorting with clearing space. It does not document every queue or vehicle rule. The review sample adds reports of slots, hidden incoming colors, and timed vehicles, but those accounts are not a substitute for a verified level-by-level guide.\n\nThe positive case is not just vague enthusiasm. One reviewer likes thinking ahead; another finds the game intellectually stimulating and visually pleasing. A further account welcomes a challenge greater than expected while distinguishing between-level ads from interruptions during play.\n\n## Where strategy starts feeling constrained\n\nThe thinking-ahead reviewer also reports an unexplained “count limit reached” message at level 37. Another reports similar failures across levels 36, 37, and 40 despite believing space remained. These are specific reported glitches, not proof that every loss is erroneous.\n\nTimers are a separate complaint. Players who liked the organizing puzzle describe losing the relaxed feeling when timed elements appear. Another objects to not seeing incoming tunnel colors far enough ahead. Together, these accounts explain why a player might enjoy the concept yet dislike the information and time constraints around it.\n\n## Ads and spending are intertwined, but not identical\n\nSome reviewers feel later stages push them toward paid help. That is evidence of perceived spending pressure, not a demonstrated requirement that nobody can progress free. One positive reviewer reports ads only when choosing a currency reward, whereas others describe unavoidable between-play advertising.\n\nA purchaser says an ad-removal offer stopped between-game ads but not ads used for advantages. The distinction matters: removing interstitial advertising need not mean every reward is granted without a video. The sample does not establish current offer terms or prices. Failed reward loading is another reported issue, separate from disliking ad length.\n\nThe free download is most relevant to readers who want to inspect the basic routing loop. It is not a reason to assume a relaxed pace, sufficient free assistance, or predictable ad behavior.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBus Traffic Fever! has real planning appeal, but this sample raises concrete concerns about timers, unexplained limits, and monetized recovery. Skip it if you want a dependable no-pressure sorting environment.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does Bus Traffic Fever! use timers?\n\nSeveral sampled players describe timed later stages or vehicles. No specific level boundary has been independently verified.\n\n### Does paying remove every ad?\n\nOne purchaser says their offer removed between-game ads but not videos for advantages. Current purchase terms need separate confirmation.\n\n### What is the count-limit complaint?\n\nSome reviewers report losses labeled count limit reached despite believing slots remained. These reports have not been reproduced.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bus-traffic-fever/id6759763476?uo=4).",
