    "bodyMarkdown": "# Township: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nTownship belongs on a puzzle shortlist only if you also want a town to manage. Crops, factories, buildings, and decorations are the main framework; match-3 is part of that broader progression.\n\n## Two activities sharing one town\n\nThe listing puts you in the mayor’s role, growing crops and adding houses, factories, and community buildings. Decoration and layout give the town a personal dimension, while events and collections provide goals beyond a single construction project.\n\nMatch-3 levels are described as a way to earn rewards and speed up progress. That makes the puzzles connected to the management game rather than a completely isolated collection. A useful distinction for prospective players is whether they want the town itself or merely tolerate it to reach the puzzles. The former audience is much closer to the design being advertised.\n\nRegattas, competitions, artifacts, and profile pictures extend the activity list. Facebook and Game Center connections are also mentioned. These systems point toward a continuing game with social and event layers, not a finite book of logic problems you complete and put away.\n\n## Offline has a stated boundary\n\nThe developer says a connection is not required for basic play and advertises offline access. It also explicitly states that competitions and additional features need an internet connection. These two statements should be read together: “offline” is not a promise that every mode works in airplane mode.\n\nThis distinction is particularly relevant if competitions are your reason to install. Conversely, someone primarily attracted to designing a farm town may care less about losing access to an event. The evidence does not establish exactly which additional functions remain available without service.\n\n## Purchases and audience\n\nTownship is listed as free to play, with real-money purchases that include randomized items. That is a more concrete spending disclosure than simply calling the game free. It does not reveal a tested no-spend progression speed, and it does not establish an ad-free experience.\n\nChoose it if the link between farming, decoration, and puzzle rewards is the attraction. Skip it if you want all your attention on a self-contained match-3 board with no town economy or event layer around it.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTownship’s differentiator is the management game surrounding the puzzles. It is a more relevant choice for would-be town builders than for anyone seeking a pure puzzle collection.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is Township only a match-3 game?\n\nNo. Its listing centers on city building and farming, with match-3 levels supplying rewards and progression benefits.\n\n### Can Township be played offline?\n\nThe developer says basic play is available offline, but competitions and additional features require a connection. This has not been tested here.\n\n### Does Township include purchases?\n\nYes. The description discloses real-money in-game purchases, including randomized items.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/township/id638689075?uo=4).",
