    "bodyMarkdown": "# Amaze GO!™: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nAmaze GO! makes the order of removal the puzzle. Each arrow needs a free lane to the edge, and sending one away can open the path for another.\n\n## Trace the exit before tapping\n\nThe described action is simple: find an arrow with an unobstructed route and tap it off the grid. Repeat until the board is empty. The challenge is reading dependencies in a crowded arrangement rather than moving every arrow immediately.\n\nThis is a useful distinction from a maze. You are not guiding one character through a continuous route; you are deciding which objects can leave and which still block one another. The board should become less cluttered as valid removals create opportunities, according to the advertised rules.\n\nLong-pressing activates Guidance to highlight directions, and hints are also mentioned. These tools could help with reading a dense layout, though the description does not establish their cost, availability, or exactly how much of a solution they reveal.\n\n## A quiet theme with a mistake budget\n\nWrong taps cost a heart for the level. That puts a consequence on probing an uncertain path and gives careful inspection a role even when the action itself is only a tap. Readers who prefer experimentation without penalties should consider that rule before accepting the description’s calm framing at face value.\n\nThe developer emphasizes a clean interface, reduced visual noise, and sound feedback. Those are presentation promises, not observations of touch accuracy or accessibility. In particular, a sparse visual style does not tell us whether the smallest arrows remain easy to distinguish on every supported screen.\n\n## Who should consider it\n\nAmaze GO! is a plausible choice for players who enjoy sequencing and progressive board clearance. It is less compelling for anyone who wants unrestricted movement, a story campaign as the main attraction, or a fully documented no-pressure ruleset.\n\nThe saved US download price is free, and offline play is advertised. Actual offline feature coverage, ad interruptions, and assistance pricing remain unverified. The listing’s examples of commutes and wind-down moments describe intended use, not measured session lengths or proof that the game meets a short-round filter.\n\n## Verdict\n\nAmaze GO! has an easily understood hook: free the arrows in an order that keeps lanes opening. Consider it for that dependency puzzle, while treating hearts and unknown assistance terms as meaningful limits.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How do you clear an arrow in Amaze GO!?\n\nTap an arrow whose path to the edge is free, according to the advertised rules.\n\n### What happens after a wrong tap?\n\nThe description says a wrong tap costs a heart for that level.\n\n### What does Guidance do?\n\nThe listing says long-pressing uses Guidance to highlight directions. Its limits and cost are not specified here.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/amaze-go/id6758326278?uo=4).",
