    "bodyMarkdown": "# Colony Flow!: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nColony Flow! makes a tiny workforce into a capacity puzzle. Color-matched ants remove blocks from a pixel picture, but every box you open occupies one of only five slots.\n\n## Your workforce can become the blockage\n\nEach level supplies a stack of ant boxes. Tap one to move it into an open slot and release workers of that color. They carry matching blocks from the picture back to the nest. A completed box frees its slot, creating room to open another.\n\nThe critical rule is that a poorly chosen color can occupy space without finishing its job. The listing explicitly warns that a box with nothing left to carry can clog a slot. Fill all five, and the colony stops. The puzzle is therefore not simply selecting every visible color: it is choosing an order that keeps the working area usable.\n\nBoxes deeper in the stack must also be unlocked. That adds a second reason to think about sequence, because the next useful workforce may not be available immediately. The picture, the accessible boxes, and the remaining capacity all belong in the same decision.\n\n## Pixel art is the working surface\n\nThe developer advertises hundreds of pictures covering animals, food, flowers, toys, and other subjects. Those images are made of the blocks your ants remove, rather than being only a decorative background. The description also promises a collection of completed pixel art.\n\nThe visual premise could appeal to players who like watching a large task shrink into manageable parts. The mechanical premise is more specific: you must be comfortable with a limited buffer and the possibility of blocking it. A cute ant theme does not make every tap harmless.\n\n## Pace, ads, and practical limits\n\nThe listing expressly advertises no timers and no lives. That distinguishes the stated rules from a countdown puzzle, although it does not eliminate the slot-based failure condition. These are developer claims, not independently observed needs-tag evidence.\n\nThe US download is free. The supplied description does not establish ad frequency, paid assists, or offline support. Consider it for planning around five slots, not because the soundtrack and relaxing language guarantee an interruption-free session.\n\n## Verdict\n\nColony Flow! has a concrete organizing idea: keep the right colors working so that scarce slots keep reopening. It is best suited to players who enjoy capacity management without an advertised countdown.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How many slots does Colony Flow! give you?\n\nThe listing specifies five open slots for ant boxes.\n\n### Can a box block progress?\n\nYes. The developer warns that a color with nothing left to carry can clog a slot; filling all five halts the colony.\n\n### Does Colony Flow! have a timer?\n\nThe developer advertises no timers and no lives. Those claims have not been playtested here.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/colony-flow/id6779167923?uo=4).",
