    "bodyMarkdown": "# Parking Loop: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nParking Loop’s text barely explains its rules, but its official screenshots provide a more useful introduction: colored cars enter a road around a tightly packed parking area.\n\n## What the screenshots actually show\n\nThe first saved image shows a rounded rectangular road, a central grid of cars, and several vehicles below the road. A hand points at a pink car under the caption “Send a Car!” A small indicator reads 1/5, but the image alone does not establish precisely what that counter limits.\n\nThe second image adds the instruction “Sort by Color!” and shows columns containing vehicles of different colors, with a blue car moving around the road. These visible elements support describing a vehicle-and-color organization puzzle. They do not prove detailed matching rules, an exact loss condition, or whether every stage uses the same capacity.\n\nSeveral central cars in the first image carry question marks. That is a visible presentation detail, not enough evidence to decide how or when concealed information becomes available. It would be easy to overexplain the screenshot by importing rules from a different parking game, and doing so would make this description less reliable rather than more helpful.\n\n## An ordering puzzle, at least in its stated pitch\n\nThe written listing asks you to plan moves, avoid getting stuck, and find the right order. It advertises hundreds of increasingly tricky levels, but never supplies a full play guide. Together with the images, the reasonable attraction is organizing traffic and colors while preserving useful space.\n\nThis may interest readers who prefer a graphical sequencing task to a word or number puzzle. It is a poor choice to select solely on the assumption of realistic driving: the supplied material points toward abstract sorting, not road simulation.\n\n## What remains unresolved\n\nThe saved US download is free. The listing and inspected images do not establish ad frequency, optional purchase terms, timers, offline operation, or the mechanics behind the visible 1/5 indicator. Anyone choosing specifically for those features needs more than the short promotional description.\n\n## Verdict\n\nParking Loop has a visually understandable cars-and-colors concept, but its rules are underdocumented. Consider the premise; do not treat screenshot counters or familiar-looking layouts as a verified instruction manual.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What kind of puzzle is Parking Loop?\n\nThe official images show colored cars, a surrounding road, and color-sorting instructions; the text emphasizes planning an order of moves.\n\n### What does the 1/5 indicator mean?\n\nIt is visible in the screenshots, but its exact function is not explained by the supplied description.\n\n### Is Parking Loop a driving simulator?\n\nThe saved material supports a car-sorting puzzle rather than realistic driving simulation.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/parking-loop/id6802383216?uo=4).",
