    "bodyMarkdown": "# Rotate Rings: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nRotate Rings is about finding an opening, literally. You rotate linked structures until their gaps line up, then free the loops one layer at a time.\n\n## Openings determine the route out\n\nThe listed controls are tapping and dragging rings to a target angle. You align gaps across multiple layers and untangle the interlocking pieces. The objective is therefore not matching colors or filling a grid; it is understanding how the shape of an opening permits release.\n\nThe developer names C-locks, D-rings, S-flex links, and triangle frames. Those shapes are the most informative part of the feature list because they suggest that the geometry varies beyond identical circular rings. The description does not specify exact movement restrictions for each shape, so the names should not be mistaken for a complete rulebook.\n\nSequence matters as well as alignment. Removing one layer can change which structure you should address next. That makes the central appeal a combination of angle finding and ordering, rather than merely spinning every ring until something happens. The advertised hints reveal a next move when a knot becomes difficult.\n\n## Who may enjoy the geometry\n\nConsider this if you like disentanglement toys, visible mechanical relationships, or spatial problems without vocabulary requirements. It is less obviously suited to someone who wants numerical deduction or a narrative reason to progress. Its stated variety comes from shapes and arrangements, not story chapters.\n\nThe store promises hundreds of handcrafted stages and a smooth difficulty curve. The first is an advertised content claim; the second is an evaluation that would require actual progression evidence. Similarly, “tactile feel” and fluid physics are developer descriptions, not impressions from hands-on use here.\n\n## Hints, ads, and unknowns\n\nThe saved US download is free, but the supplied description does not establish whether hints consume currency, require advertising, or are freely available. It also does not establish offline access or the presence of time limits. A relaxation-oriented pitch is not enough to answer those practical questions.\n\nThe best reason to investigate Rotate Rings is its specific set of linked shapes. The main unresolved issue is whether the implemented constraints make their release as readable as the marketing suggests.\n\n## Verdict\n\nRotate Rings has a distinct geometry-first premise and a useful variety of advertised lock shapes. Consider it for alignment and disentanglement, not on an unverified promise of smooth controls or effortless difficulty.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How do you solve Rotate Rings?\n\nThe listed method is rotating shapes to align their open gaps, then freeing linked pieces in an appropriate order.\n\n### Are there shapes besides circles?\n\nYes. The description names C-locks, D-rings, S-flex links, and triangle frames.\n\n### Does Rotate Rings have hints?\n\nHints that reveal a next move are advertised. Their cost and limits are not established here.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/rotate-rings/id6793924927?uo=4).",
