    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong Blast: Player Review Digest\n\n*Player-review digest, not a playtest. This analysis combines the US listing with 50 saved US App Store reviews dated 2026-11-07–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 2.2.1. This self-selected sample is not a representative survey; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMahjong Blast’s tile matching has enthusiastic fans, but its strongest store promise—completely ad-free play—is contradicted by multiple accounts in the saved review sample.\n\n## Solitaire matching, not table mahjong\n\nThe listing describes matching identical tiles, opening blocked pieces, and clearing boards. It targets adults and seniors with large tiles and no timers, and explicitly distinguishes itself from American mahjong. This is not evidence of teaching a four-player scoring card or table strategy.\n\nDaily puzzles and thousands of levels are advertised. The app is also identified as formerly Mahjong Wonders. Those are useful product facts; the broad claim of improved memory is marketing, not a demonstrated health benefit.\n\n## What supporters appreciate\n\nOne reviewer describes concentration practice that is easy on the eyes and relaxing. Another likes playing to destress. These are individual experiences supporting the stated relaxed audience, not proof that the game treats anxiety or suits every visual need.\n\nOther praise is short and general. It should not be stretched into detailed conclusions about progression balance, generous rewards, or accessibility testing that the comments do not actually contain.\n\n## When help gets in the way\n\nA distinctive complaint concerns automatic completion. One player wants to finish rounds personally rather than have the game take over after a few matches; another describes the same loss of control. This matters especially if the reward you want is the act of selecting all the pairs, not simply receiving a cleared board.\n\nA separate account describes a blank-screen problem followed by lost progress after reinstalling. That is a specific stability and recovery report, not evidence that reinstalling universally removes saves. Accusations about bots and cheating elsewhere in the sample remain player allegations rather than verified findings.\n\n## Advertising and purchase expectations\n\nThe developer advertises no ads or pop-ups. Reviewers instead describe frequent ads, including between-level interruptions. Some ask for a paid way to remove them, but that request does not establish that a suitable offer currently exists.\n\nOffline play is also a listing claim, not a tested finding. The saved download is free. Readers seeking solitaire may reasonably consider the visible premise; anyone choosing specifically for no forced ads should not rely on the listing promise without further verification.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong Blast’s accessible-looking solitaire concept and relaxed appeal have support in the sample. Advertising contradictions and unwanted automatic completion are substantial caveats for players who want uninterrupted, hands-on matching.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is Mahjong Blast ad-free?\n\nThe listing says it is, but multiple saved reviewers report ads. This digest cannot endorse a universal ad-free claim.\n\n### Does Mahjong Blast finish rounds automatically?\n\nSome reviewers say it completes boards for them and dislike losing control. The behavior and settings have not been tested.\n\n### Is it American mahjong?\n\nNo. The developer explicitly describes a different solitaire tile-matching experience.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-blast/id6747492600?uo=4).",
