    "bodyMarkdown": "# Candy Crush Saga: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nCandy Crush Saga is a continuing match-3 campaign built around changing obstacles and goals, not merely an endless exercise in lining up the same candies.\n\n## The board is a route through specific objectives\n\nThe listing names jelly, toffees, jam, Gummy Dragons, and Bubblegum Pop among the game’s obstacles and modes. It emphasizes creating combinations and using the results to clear challenges. These named targets are more informative than the generic promise of thousands of levels: they indicate that the purpose of a match changes with the stage.\n\nBoosters include Bonbon Blitz, Lollipop Hammer, and Jelly Fish. Extra moves are also advertised as help when stuck. Their presence does not establish how often a player needs them or whether a particular stage can be completed without assistance.\n\nDaily rewards, themed events, tournaments, and leaderboards provide goals outside an individual board. Sharing lives and comparing scores add a social layer, while connecting to Facebook is advertised for syncing progress and additional connected functions. None of that makes social participation a verified requirement for ordinary play.\n\n## Consider the campaign, not just the candy theme\n\nThe strongest audience is a match-3 player who wants many stages and recurring reasons to return. It is less obviously suited to someone who wants a compact puzzle book with a definite final page or who prefers no surrounding events and reward systems.\n\nThe listing still includes an All Stars promotion whose stated event dates are earlier in 2026. That should not be read as an invitation to an event currently open in this saved November context. A long-running game’s promotional description can contain historical material alongside current feature information.\n\n## Free play and strong claims\n\nThe description discloses optional paid in-game items. It also advertises offline play and ad-free fun. Those are developer statements, not observations from this analysis; they do not independently establish every feature’s offline availability or a universal absence of advertising.\n\nThe supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this entry. Its large store aggregate therefore cannot substitute for actual accounts of difficulty, spending pressure, or reliability. The useful case for consideration remains the named match-3 variety and ongoing campaign structure.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCandy Crush Saga is most relevant when you want match-3 as an ongoing campaign, with boosters, events, and social options surrounding the boards. Choose on that structure, not a presumed guarantee about ads or free progression.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is Candy Crush Saga free?\n\nThe saved download is free, and the developer discloses optional paid in-game items.\n\n### Which boosters are listed?\n\nThe description names Bonbon Blitz, Lollipop Hammer, and Jelly Fish, along with extra-move assistance.\n\n### Is the All Stars promotion currently open?\n\nThe saved description gives event dates earlier in 2026. It does not establish a currently open competition at this November evidence snapshot.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/candy-crush-saga/id553834731?uo=4).",
