    "bodyMarkdown": "# Water Sort Puzzle : Challenge: Player Review Digest\n\n*Player-review digest, not a playtest. This analysis combines the US listing with 29 saved US App Store reviews dated 2026-08-10–2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 1.2.0. This self-selected sample is not a representative survey; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWater Sort Puzzle : Challenge offers the familiar satisfaction of separating mixed colors into bottles. The saved reviews suggest the bigger obstacle for some players is getting from one puzzle to the next.\n\n## A readable rule with planning consequences\n\nThe listing permits pouring onto the same color or into an empty bottle. The goal is to sort the colors and clear each level. Empty bottles therefore provide working room, while the order of pours can either preserve options or leave colors trapped.\n\nHelpful tools are advertised, but the description does not name their current prices or limits. A reviewer’s mention of watching an ad for a free bottle adds one reported assistance route, not a complete inventory of the game economy.\n\n## Enjoyment is present, but qualified\n\nSome feedback is straightforwardly positive: a reviewer says the game helps them cool down, and another simply enjoys playing. A more detailed account likes that the puzzle is more challenging than many alternatives, while criticizing the surrounding ad experience.\n\nThat last distinction is useful. The sample does not say the sorting idea itself fails for everyone. It shows players who enjoy the challenge yet feel prevented from sustaining it. Some find the format boring, while others describe it as absorbing; neither preference should be converted into a universal judgment.\n\n## Ads and update prompts dominate practical concerns\n\nSeveral reviewers describe frequent ads, and one explicitly wishes for a way to buy them away. That wish does not prove a removal offer is currently available. Another says advertising time rivals playing time; it is a personal comparison, not measured timing.\n\nRepeated “update” prompts are a separate issue. One reviewer reports being blocked around level 96 after buying extras. Another says an apparent upgrade downloaded a different app. These are user reports, not verified findings that every prompt behaves that way. The supplied description does not explain the prompts.\n\nThe free download should not be mistaken for established uninterrupted access. A review mentioning cash-out expectations is not evidence that this listing offers legitimate earnings, and no payout recommendation follows from it.\n\nChoose only if the bottle-sorting premise interests you enough to inspect these access and advertising concerns carefully. Readers wanting dependable, uninterrupted logic practice have concrete reasons for caution.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe sorting rules are clear and some reviewers enjoy the challenge. Repeated reports about advertising and confusing update prompts are substantial caveats, especially before paying for extras.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What pours are allowed?\n\nThe listing says water can be poured onto the same color or into an empty bottle.\n\n### Can you pay to remove ads?\n\nOne reviewer asks for that option, but the supplied evidence does not establish a current ad-removal offer.\n\n### Why do reviews mention updates?\n\nSome report repeated update prompts or being unable to continue. Their exact cause and behavior have not been independently verified.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/water-sort-puzzle-challenge/id6787414784?uo=4).",
