    "bodyMarkdown": "# Hollywood Merge: Player Review Digest\n\n*Player-review digest, not a playtest. This analysis combines the US listing with 50 saved US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-26–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 0.0.950, 0.0.963, 0.0.975. This self-selected sample is not a representative survey; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nHollywood Merge’s strongest attraction is the combination of fashion and melodrama. Its most consequential trade-off, according to the saved sample, is how often resources interrupt that combination.\n\n## Merge a wardrobe, then enter the drama\n\nThe listing describes combining fabrics and clothing into gowns and accessories, with more than 1,000 clothing items advertised. Red carpets, yacht parties, and photo shoots give the wardrobe a setting. A newly discovered relationship to actor Peter Dake supplies an inheritance story, a jealous half-sister, and questionable romantic motives.\n\nThe central proposition is not merely merging higher-level objects. It is using that progression to style a protagonist and continue a story. Someone who likes both activities has a clearer reason to consider it than someone looking only for an abstract board puzzle.\n\n## The same story is a reward and a cost\n\nSeveral reviewers enjoy the drama or dressing-up options. Another likes the storyline and choices while still wanting easier collection of some items. That is qualified praise, not an endorsement of every progression system.\n\nA specific criticism is spending earned coins on conversations rather than outfits. One player wants skippable dialogue because those costs consume resources intended for styling. Another raises the same concern alongside perceived unfairness in fashion contests. Contest manipulation is not independently established; the useful finding is how some players experience the reward structure.\n\n## Energy is the bigger spending concern\n\nReviews describe a low energy cap and slow regeneration making higher-level orders more restrictive. Those accounts are not a current price list or proof that every player must pay. A contrasting review says energy bonuses have become more plentiful and welcomes adjustments, while asking for optional ad-based energy.\n\nSeveral reviewers report no ads; another describes few ads but many purchase pop-ups. No ads, few ads, and no commercial pressure are different claims. The sample supports keeping them separate.\n\nReported save conflicts and lost progress are additional cautions. They are specific accounts spanning older sample versions, not verified current defects. This matters because the sample covers multiple releases and should not be flattened into a single present-day behavior.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConsider Hollywood Merge if styling and soap-opera drama are both rewards for you. Skip it if resource waits or paying coins to advance conversations would undermine the wardrobe-building appeal.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What do you merge in Hollywood Merge?\n\nThe listing centers on fabrics and clothing that become gowns and accessories.\n\n### Why do reviews mention energy?\n\nSome describe slow regeneration and increasingly costly orders; another reports improved bonuses. The current balance has not been independently tested.\n\n### Is Hollywood Merge ad-free?\n\nSeveral sampled players report no ads, while another reports few ads and purchase pop-ups. That does not establish a universal ad-free guarantee.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/hollywood-merge/id6755455500?uo=4).",
