    "bodyMarkdown": "# Sudoku.com - Number Games: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nSudoku.com’s most useful distinction is not the familiar number grid, but how many solving aids it advertises—and the ability to solve without some of them.\n\n## Choose how much feedback you want\n\nThe listing describes classic Sudoku across easier and expert difficulties, with a single solution promised for each puzzle. It advertises hints, automatic checking, and highlighted duplicates in rows, columns, or blocks. Those are different kinds of assistance: finding a next step is not the same thing as being told an entry is wrong.\n\nNotes track possible numbers, and the developer says they update automatically when a cell is filled. This is a particularly relevant feature for readers who use candidate notation heavily. The description does not specify every note-management rule or whether each automatic behavior can be configured independently.\n\nAuto-Check is presented as an option rather than the only way to play. You can instead work out mistakes yourself, according to the listing. That distinction makes the app’s pitch relevant both to learners who want immediate feedback and to solvers who prefer checking their own reasoning.\n\n## More than a random-grid button\n\nDaily Challenges award trophies, Seasonal Events offer medals, and statistics track progress. These systems provide continuing goals around the puzzles. They are not evidence that any daily streak is compulsory or that the app restricts you to one grid per day.\n\nThe listing advertises thousands of puzzles and says every puzzle has one solution. Both are developer claims, not results of an independent catalog or uniqueness audit. Likewise, the comparison to pencil and paper is a marketing analogy rather than proof that digital controls feel identical.\n\n## Audience and cost boundaries\n\nConsider Sudoku.com if you want classic Sudoku with candidate notes and a choice of feedback tools. It is a less natural match if you specifically seek variant Sudoku rules, competitive real-time solving, or a completely bare paper-like interface without surrounding rewards.\n\nThe saved US download is free. The supplied description does not establish ad frequency, paid removal, offline availability, or hint costs. There are no saved customer review bodies for this entry, so the aggregate store rating cannot support a claim that players universally find the checking system fair or the controls comfortable.\n\n## Verdict\n\nSudoku.com’s advertised notes and optional checks offer clear reasons to consider it for classic Sudoku. The choice is about solving tools; uninterrupted access and the actual quality of assistance still need verification.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does Sudoku.com support pencil notes?\n\nYes. Notes for possible numbers are advertised, with automatic updates after filling a cell.\n\n### Can Auto-Check be disabled?\n\nThe description presents Auto-Check as optional and says players can work out mistakes without it.\n\n### Does every puzzle have one solution?\n\nThe developer says so. Puzzle uniqueness has not been independently audited here.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/sudoku-com-number-games/id1193508329?uo=4).",
