    "bodyMarkdown": "# Gardenscapes: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nGardenscapes gives match-3 a place to lead: a garden gradually restored through new areas, decorative choices, and a continuing story with Austin the butler.\n\n## Two rewards for puzzle progress\n\nThe core board activity is making match-3 combinations and using power-ups and boosters against obstacles. The listing advertises more than 16,000 levels. That is an extensive stated campaign, but the number alone cannot establish how varied or balanced those boards remain.\n\nRestoration supplies the other reward. Garden areas range from fountains to island landscapes, and new chapters bring friends, neighbors, and pets into Austin’s world. For a reader who likes improving a place over time, the setting may provide a stronger reason to continue than another isolated board clear.\n\nThe description also names Expeditions, competitions, and prize events. These are additional activity layers, not proof that every event is required for ordinary restoration. Nor does the garden theme mean the game is primarily freeform landscaping: the listing consistently connects progress to puzzle solving.\n\n## Choose the wrapper as well as the board\n\nGardenscapes is a plausible match for someone who enjoys both match-3 and the visible transformation of a shared setting. It is a weaker fit for readers who want to choose a puzzle and immediately solve it without characters, decorative decisions, or event prompts around it.\n\nThe broad “loved by millions” language does not replace player evidence. This supplied collection has no customer review bodies for the entry, so there is no basis here for claims about whether players find late stages generous, frustrating, or fair.\n\n## Offline access has an explicit exception\n\nThe developer says an internet connection is not required for play, then adds that competitions and additional features require one. Those statements should stay together. An offline claim for the main game is not a promise that every connected event remains available without service.\n\nThe US download is free. The description explicitly discloses real-money purchases, including randomized items. It does not establish a no-spend progression pace, exact offer prices, or ad frequency. Readers concerned about spending should treat those as separate questions rather than equating optional purchases with either mandatory payment or a completely cost-free experience.\n\n## Verdict\n\nChoose Gardenscapes if restoring a garden and following Austin’s chapters would make match-3 progression more rewarding. Skip it if you want a board-only experience or need all features to work without a connection.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is Gardenscapes only a decorating game?\n\nNo. The listing connects garden restoration and story progression to match-3 puzzles.\n\n### Can Gardenscapes work offline?\n\nThe developer says basic play does not require a connection, but competitions and additional features do. This has not been independently tested.\n\n### Are purchases randomized?\n\nThe description discloses that some purchasable in-game elements include randomized items.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/gardenscapes/id1105855019?uo=4).",
