    "bodyMarkdown": "# Royal Kingdom: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nRoyal Kingdom uses match-3 as both a building tool and a way to fight. Puzzle wins support new districts, while confrontations with the Dark King turn the same broad activity into a defense story.\n\n## A kingdom to expand, an enemy to overcome\n\nThe listing introduces King Richard, King Robert’s younger brother, alongside Princess Bella and the Wizard. With the Builder, you earn coins through puzzles and unlock districts such as Parliament Square, the University, and Princess Tower.\n\nThat creates a concrete progression framework beyond reaching the next board number. If you enjoy seeing a larger place take shape, the district names make the advertised goal more specific than a generic promise of adventure. The description does not establish the amount of freedom available in building layouts or decorative choices.\n\nDark King encounters are also resolved through match-3. The stated aim is destroying his castles and minions, not controlling a separate action-combat character. Readers attracted by battle imagery should keep the puzzle-based nature of those confrontations in mind.\n\n## Related branding does not establish identical systems\n\nThe store identifies the game as coming from the creators of Royal Match, but that does not justify copying assumptions about ad policy, life systems, or difficulty from another product. This entry needs its own evidence.\n\nThe supplied description emphasizes obstacles, leaderboard ranks, rewards, and uncovering new lands. It does not provide an exact level count, name all assist tools, or explain failure conditions. Claims of smooth animation and exceptional visuals remain developer evaluations rather than tested impressions.\n\n## Audience and spending boundaries\n\nRoyal Kingdom may suit a match-3 player who wants a fantasy campaign with both construction and opposition. It is less relevant for someone who wants realistic city management, direct combat, or a standalone logic puzzle unaffected by a larger progression layer.\n\nThe saved US download is free. Advertising frequency, purchase terms, offline access, and the pace of no-spend progression are not established by the supplied text. There are no saved customer review bodies for this entry, so it would be misleading to claim recent players endorse its fairness or complain about particular levels.\n\nThe useful decision rests on the two advertised rewards for solving: expand a kingdom and push back the Dark King. Those are specific enough to assess as a concept without inventing hands-on experience.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConsider Royal Kingdom if a kingdom-building and battle narrative would add purpose to match-3. Judge it on its own documented features, not assumed policies inherited from Royal Match.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Who is King Richard?\n\nThe listing identifies him as King Robert’s younger brother and the central royal character in this adventure.\n\n### How do you fight the Dark King?\n\nThe description says confrontations are resolved by solving match-3 puzzles to destroy castles and minions.\n\n### Is Royal Kingdom ad-free?\n\nThat is not established by the supplied description. Its connection to Royal Match does not prove an identical ad policy.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/royal-kingdom/id1606549505?uo=4).",
