    "bodyMarkdown": "# Arrows – Puzzle Escape: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nArrows – Puzzle Escape asks a simple question repeatedly: which arrow can leave without hitting another? Clearing the grid depends on reading that order, not just tapping everything you see.\n\n## Removal creates the next opportunity\n\nThe stated objective is extracting every arrow without a collision. That makes the layout a network of dependencies. An arrow that cannot move now may become available after another one leaves, so the useful move is one that respects the current paths.\n\nThis is different from drawing a route through a maze. You are removing objects from an arrangement, gradually changing what blocks what. The supplied description does not explain every arrow shape or how paths are checked, so a detailed universal solution method would go beyond the evidence.\n\nThousands of handcrafted levels and increasing complexity are advertised. Those claims establish the intended scale and progression, not an independently verified count or proof that later boards avoid repetition. A hint system is included in the pitch, but its cost and the amount of guidance it provides are not documented.\n\n## Untimed does not mean consequence-free\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises no timers. It also asks whether you can clear the grid without losing a heart. Those statements can coexist: the pressure may come from mistakes rather than a countdown.\n\nThe exact heart allowance and recovery rules are not given in the supplied description. Readers who like experimenting until a route works should therefore avoid assuming unlimited harmless taps. Readers who prefer careful inspection may find the collision-avoidance premise more naturally appealing.\n\n## Choosing on the actual puzzle\n\nThe minimalist presentation is intended to keep attention on the layout. It does not independently establish small-screen readability, color contrast, touch precision, or suitability for an individual accessibility need. The important reason to consider the game is its removal sequence, not a generic claim that minimal visuals are always easier to use.\n\nThe saved US download is free. Ad interruptions, optional purchases, offline availability, and hint pricing are not established. No customer review bodies are available here to say whether players find the puzzles fair or the assistance useful.\n\nConsider Arrows if collision-free sequencing is your preferred challenge. Skip it if you need a proven penalty-free environment or want the action of freely moving pieces rather than selecting their exit order.\n\n## Verdict\n\nArrows – Puzzle Escape has a focused dependency puzzle with an advertised untimed pace. The heart reference is an important counterweight to the no-pressure language, especially for experimental solvers.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is the goal in Arrows – Puzzle Escape?\n\nRemove every arrow without causing a collision.\n\n### Does it have a countdown?\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises no timers. No playtest was performed here.\n\n### Can mistakes cost hearts?\n\nThe listing refers to clearing a grid without losing a heart, but does not specify the complete allowance or recovery rules.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/arrows-puzzle-escape/id6748397500?uo=4).",
