    "bodyMarkdown": "# Pixel Flow!: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nPixel Flow! hides a resource puzzle inside a playful image: pigs ride a conveyor and fire at matching pixel cubes. Their remaining ammunition determines whether they leave or need somewhere to wait.\n\n## An unfinished pig is still part of the plan\n\nThe number over a pig’s head represents its ammo, according to the listing. Tap to send it onto the conveyor, where it hits only cubes of its own color. Exhausted pigs leave; those with ammo remaining move into one of five waiting slots and can be launched again with another tap.\n\nThat reuse rule is central. A pig may still be valuable even if its first pass does not consume all its ammunition. At the same time, retaining too many unfinished pigs creates a waiting-area problem. Color selection and available space are linked.\n\nThe conveyor itself also has a capacity. The developer says pushing past it means waiting, so launching every available pig is not presented as a solution. The intended challenge is managing when pigs enter, what useful work they can do, and where they go afterward.\n\n## Color matching without individual aiming\n\nThe description explicitly says pigs hit only their own color and removes the need to select individual targets. That makes the meaningful decisions closer to dispatch and flow management than conventional aiming. It is an important distinction for someone attracted by the shooting imagery but uninterested in reflex marksmanship.\n\nThe listing promotes timing and fast action-puzzle play. It does not specify a countdown or a complete failure condition, so neither should be invented. Equally, the mention of micro-breaks is not measured evidence that every round finishes within a particular number of minutes.\n\n## Audience and missing economy details\n\nConsider Pixel Flow! if you enjoy managing limited capacity and reusing resources until their job is finished. Skip it if you want unrestricted firing, freeform pixel drawing, or a puzzle with no queue to monitor.\n\nThe saved US download is free. Ad frequency, spending offers, assistance pricing, and offline operation are not established by the supplied description. The one-handed marketing claim remains untested; a one-tap action does not itself establish comfortable reach or readability on a particular phone.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPixel Flow!’s strongest feature on paper is the interaction between ammo, color availability, conveyor capacity, and five waiting slots. Choose it for that flow-management problem rather than assuming it is a simple shooting game.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What does the number over a pig mean?\n\nThe listing identifies it as ammunition: the number of hits that pig can make.\n\n### What happens if ammo remains?\n\nThe pig moves into one of five waiting slots and can be tapped back onto the conveyor.\n\n### Do you aim at individual cubes?\n\nThe description says pigs automatically hit cubes of their own color, without individual target selection.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/pixel-flow/id6751056652?uo=4).",
