    "bodyMarkdown": "# Cutie Sort - Magic Color Fever: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nCutie Sort - Magic Color Fever puts its color-sorting pieces on perches. The cute characters are the theme; limited space and the order of moves are the advertised logic challenge.\n\n## A spare slot is part of the solution\n\nThe listing says to tap and move characters, sorting them by color onto different perches. Limited slots prevent unrestricted rearrangement, so you need to think about what a move makes possible afterward. Grouping one color is useful only if the remaining arrangement still leaves a route to completion.\n\nThis is not described as matching three pieces to make them vanish. The stated endpoint is every character correctly sorted. That distinction matters if you enjoy organizing a stable set of objects more than continuously clearing and refilling a board.\n\nThe developer advertises increasingly complex layouts but does not provide a complete legal-move guide. It is not established whether a character can move onto any open perch, whether only certain positions are accessible, or how many slots every stage supplies. Those rules should not be borrowed from bird-sorting or bottle-sorting games with a similar look.\n\n## Recovery tools change the planning options\n\nUndo, Swap, and expanded slots are explicitly named. They address different problems: revisiting a move, changing an arrangement, or creating additional capacity. Their existence does not establish whether normal boards require them or how often they are available without spending.\n\nThe listing says there are no timers and no luck is required. That positions Cutie Sort as a deliberate spatial puzzle rather than a reaction test. It remains a developer claim, not a verified proof that every level is deterministic or solvable without assistance.\n\n## Audience and costs\n\nConsider this if you like color organization with a visible space limit and want time to consider your choices. Skip it if you dislike deadlocks or want unrestricted experimentation without needing to think about recovery tools.\n\nThe saved US download is free. Ad behavior, tool prices, purchase offers, offline access, and failure penalties are not established by the supplied material. There are no saved customer review bodies to show whether players find later arrangements fair or the helpers generous.\n\nThe useful attraction is the relationship between colors and available perch space, not a broad promise that cute visuals automatically make a puzzle calming.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCutie Sort has a clear deliberate-sorting pitch, with limited slots supplying the challenge and named tools offering recovery. Consider it for spatial ordering, while leaving tool costs and full movement rules unresolved.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What do you sort in Cutie Sort?\n\nThe listing describes moving cute characters by color onto different perches.\n\n### Does Cutie Sort have a timer?\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises no timers. No independent playtest was performed.\n\n### Which helpers are listed?\n\nUndo, Swap, and expanded slots are named, but their costs and availability are not established.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cutie-sort-magic-color-fever/id6796547048?uo=4).",
