    "bodyMarkdown": "# Jewel Coloring: iOS Puzzle Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. Based on the saved US App Store listing; the supplied collection contains no customer review bodies for this game (sample N=0; no review date or version window).*\n\nJewel Coloring frames pixel art as an organization task. Sort jewels by color, keep the working shelf useful, and place gems until a predetermined image emerges.\n\n## The shelf gives sorting a purpose\n\nThe listing describes tapping and arranging jewels into matching color groups, while planning the workspace to clear each puzzle. As gems are placed, a hidden pixel design becomes visible. The image is the result of the organizing process, not evidence of a freehand drawing canvas.\n\nShelf management is the most useful mechanical detail. It suggests that where pieces wait matters alongside their color. The description does not specify capacity, legal transfers, or a complete failure condition, so it would be misleading to invent a particular slot count or overflow rule.\n\nThe developer promises hundreds of levels and themes, along with limited-time collections. That gives two kinds of attraction: finishing a picture and collecting more designs over time. The evidence does not establish how those collections are accessed or whether any are tied to purchases or events.\n\n## Structured art rather than unrestricted creativity\n\nConsider Jewel Coloring if you enjoy watching a preset picture develop and prefer organization to choosing every artistic detail. It is less naturally suited to someone who wants to draw original images, select an unrestricted palette, or export custom designs. None of those authoring tools is established by the supplied description.\n\nThe text repeatedly presents the game as calm and pressure-free. It does not explicitly enumerate timer, energy, or mistake systems. Those should not be assumed absent merely because the visual treatment is bright and the marketing invites relaxation.\n\n## Access, resuming, and costs\n\nOffline play and pause-and-resume use are advertised. Those are practical promises worth checking before relying on the game during travel; they have not been verified through a disconnected session or a save-recovery test here.\n\nThe saved US download is free. Ad frequency, purchases, assistance costs, and the exact handling of limited collections are not established. There are no saved customer review bodies to support claims about players finding it soothing, repetitive, or stable.\n\nThe reason to consider it is the combination of gem sorting and picture completion. Claims of natural stress relief or improved focus are marketing, not independent health or educational findings.\n\n## Verdict\n\nJewel Coloring may suit readers who enjoy structured gem art with a workspace to organize. Its appeal is assembling preset images, while the shelf rules, spending model, and offline reliability remain open questions.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is Jewel Coloring a drawing app?\n\nThe description focuses on sorting and placing jewels to reveal preset pixel-art images, not unrestricted drawing.\n\n### Does the shelf have a fixed capacity?\n\nThe listing discusses shelf organization but does not specify a universal capacity or complete overflow rule.\n\n### Can it work without Wi-Fi?\n\nThe developer advertises offline play. Actual offline scope and save continuity have not been tested.\n\n[View the US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/jewel-coloring/id6759081967?uo=4).",
