    "bodyMarkdown": "# Jigsawcard Solitaire Puzzle: A Picture Puzzle With Hidden Information\n\nJigsawcard's interesting idea is not simply putting a picture back together. It is deciding what to uncover before you know exactly where everything belongs. That makes this a more intriguing listing for card-puzzle fans than its familiar solitaire label initially suggests. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Pictures, cards, and the information gap\n\nOakever describes a combination of sliding image cards, matching pieces, and flipping face-down cards. You swipe cards into position, reveal concealed images, and connect related pieces until the picture comes together. Maintaining connected sets is part of the stated strategy, rather than treating every tile as an isolated move.\n\nThe face-down cards are the important wrinkle. A conventional jigsaw gives you a large amount of information immediately; here, revealing a card is itself part of solving. Remembering what you have uncovered could matter alongside recognizing image fragments. The listing does not provide a complete rulebook for which cards can move or exactly when a flip becomes available.\n\n## What the design promises—and what it leaves open\n\nThe developer advertises nature, animal, and art pictures, connected-card chain reactions, and scenery unlocks. Those features give completion a visual destination beyond an abstract score. They are store-described content, not an assessment of the actual image quality or animation smoothness.\n\nThe listing explicitly says there are no timers. That is a useful distinction for someone seeking a patient picture puzzle, although timer-free behavior across modes remains untested. Nor should the word solitaire be read as a promise of Klondike rules, traditional suits, or a standard card deck. The described challenge is image reconstruction with cards.\n\nThere are no usable customer-review bodies in the saved evidence. Consequently, nothing here establishes whether players find the concealed cards clever, repetitive, or frustrating in later levels.\n\n## Ads and payment\n\nThe US listing records a free download. The saved description does not establish an ad schedule, a verified ad-removal offer, or the cost of continuing when stuck. Free entry and the developer's relaxing presentation do not settle those questions.\n\n## Verdict\n\nJigsawcard is a sensible prospect for picture-puzzle players who want an additional memory and sequencing layer. Skip it if your priority is either traditional solitaire or a fully visible jigsaw with predictable piece access. Its strongest evidenced selling point is the hybrid rule concept, not a proven interruption-free experience.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Jigsawcard traditional solitaire?\nNo. The listing describes sliding and flipping picture cards to assemble images, not conventional suit-and-rank solitaire.\n\n### Does it have a timer?\nThe developer advertises no timers; that claim has not been checked in play.\n\n### Are there player reviews in this analysis?\nNo usable review bodies were available in the saved evidence, so this is a store-listing analysis.\n\n### Is it free of ads?\nThat is not established. A free download does not demonstrate an ad-free experience.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/jigsawcard-solitaire-puzzle/id6754900673?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
