    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bus Crowd - Traffic Jam: The Seat Count Matters\n\nBus Crowd asks a better question than simply which bus can leave the lot: which bus can actually finish loading? Its stated mix of directional exits, passenger colors, and different seat counts gives the parking theme a practical scheduling problem. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## A bus is useful only if it can finish its job\n\nThe described loop begins with passengers waiting at a station. You choose an empty bus, move it into a boarding slot, and load passengers matching its color. Every bus has a seat capacity. Once full, it leaves and frees the slot for another vehicle.\n\nMovement is constrained by arrows. A bus can leave only in its indicated direction, so another vehicle may need to move first. This creates two linked decisions: unlocking a route and selecting a bus the visible crowd can fill. Sending something out just because it is available may occupy space without helping the queue.\n\nA useful way to understand the design is as temporary-storage management. A partly filled bus represents parking capacity you cannot currently reuse. Matching the right color is necessary, but the seat count determines how long that decision might tie up a slot.\n\n## Strategy instead of driving simulation\n\nThe listing emphasizes observing paths, comparing passengers, and sequencing departures. It does not describe steering vehicles manually around a city. Players looking for a driving game should not confuse the transportation theme with a vehicle simulator.\n\nBoosters are advertised for adjusting buses, guiding passengers, or opening paths. Their presence matters because they can change the available choices when a station becomes blocked. The saved text does not specify their exact limits or prove that every layout can be solved without them.\n\nNo customer-review bodies were available for this collection. The escalating challenge and satisfying flow are therefore developer promises, not verified player reactions. Later-level balance and the usefulness of the tools remain open questions.\n\n## The free-download boundary\n\nBus Crowd is listed as free, but the saved description supplies no reliable ad frequency or complete booster pricing. It would be premature to call it either a generous free game or a paywall-heavy one. Check the actual offers before treating a stuck station as a reason to spend.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBus Crowd is aimed at players who enjoy coordinating several constraints at once: direction, color, capacity, and parking space. It is less appealing if you want unrestricted vehicle movement or dislike puzzles where an early choice ties up a resource. The seat-capacity wrinkle is the clearest reason to investigate this particular bus puzzler.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What makes a bus leave?\nAccording to the listing, a bus departs after its seats are filled by matching-color passengers.\n\n### Can buses move in any direction?\nNo. The stated rule restricts each bus to its arrow direction.\n\n### Are there boosters?\nYes. The description mentions tools affecting buses, passengers, and blocked routes; their cost is not established here.\n\n### Does this review confirm ad-free play?\nNo. The saved evidence does not verify advertising behavior.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bus-crowd-traffic-jam/id6754059516?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
