    "bodyMarkdown": "# Woodoku Blast: Spatial Planning With an Advertising Caveat\n\nWoodoku Blast's appeal is easy to recognize: finding room for one more shape and turning a crowded board into breathing space. The saved player feedback suggests that this basic puzzle works for many people, while advertising can undermine the relaxed rhythm. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with saved player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Clearing lines while protecting future space\n\nThe listing describes placing colorful blocks on a grid, completing lines, and scoring through a sequence of levels. Its challenge comes from fitting shapes without leaving the board too awkward to use. Events and changing themes provide additional reasons to return, although the description does not fully document the differences between modes.\n\nThis is not enough information to assume every rule from another similarly named block game. The meaningful comparison is with spatial-placement puzzles: today's useful gap can become tomorrow's trap if a large piece no longer fits. One sampled reviewer specifically appreciates having to think about available spaces rather than simply being asked to spend.\n\n## What players actually report\n\nThe saved sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-25 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.31.00, 1.32.00, 1.32.01; the listing records version 1.32.01. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. The experiences below are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reports include bedtime relaxation, accessible early levels with a growing challenge, and enjoyment of the shape-planning decisions. A detailed four-star review praises the need to consider available board space. Another describes the levels as mostly easy but increasingly challenging, a combination that may suit someone seeking concentration without constant failure.\n\nThe praise is not uniform approval of the surrounding systems. A five-star review still objects to automatic failure when the game concludes that no pieces fit and asks for more control. Other players want clearer progress indicators or more flexibility with power-ups. Those comments point to agency and communication concerns, not just difficulty.\n\n## Advertising is the major qualification\n\nSeveral reviewers describe ads between levels; others report interruptions during play or ads that will not close normally. One says a revive attempt became stuck in an advertisement. These are user accounts, not a measured schedule applicable to every install.\n\nThe contrast is worth preserving: a player can genuinely enjoy the blocks and still abandon the app because the breaks are irritating. Some ask to pay for ad removal, but their requests do not prove that a particular removal product is currently available. Nor do reward-app references establish dependable earnings from Woodoku Blast.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConsider Woodoku Blast if spatial planning and gradual level progression are your priorities. Be more cautious if uninterrupted sessions are essential. The saved comments offer a credible case for the puzzle's appeal, but not for a consistently quiet or technically trouble-free experience.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the main objective?\nPlace blocks, clear completed lines, and score while managing the remaining grid space.\n\n### Do players find it relaxing?\nSome explicitly do, including a reviewer describing it as a bedtime game. Others find the ads frustrating.\n\n### Are ad interruptions independently timed here?\nNo. Advertising observations come from individual player reports, with differing experiences.\n\n### Is this a tested performance review?\nNo. Device performance, battery use, and stability were not tested.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/woodoku-blast-block-puzzle/id6608975929?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
