    "bodyMarkdown": "# Wordscapes: Enjoyable Anagrams, Less Agreement About the Extras\n\nWordscapes makes a compact vocabulary challenge out of a small set of letters. The strongest divide in the saved comments is not whether that idea is enjoyable, but how much advertising, animation, and side activity players want around it. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with saved player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Finding the word the board wants\n\nThe listing combines anagrams, letter connecting, and crossword-shaped answers. You connect letters to discover hidden words and complete the level's word pattern. It advertises more than 6,000 puzzles, unlimited tries, landscape backgrounds, and five language options.\n\nUnlike a conventional crossword, the central input is rearranging a supplied letter set rather than answering a page of written clues. Recognizing one short word may help reveal the next arrangement. The finite board also means a valid word is not necessarily one of the required answers—a distinction reflected in the sample's complaints about bonus words.\n\n## Players like the words; the surrounding experience divides them\n\nThe saved sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-11-01 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.14.20, 3.14.30; the listing records version 3.14.30. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. The experiences below are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers describe learning words, finding the puzzles calming, and enjoying a quick challenge in spare time. One appreciates the team aspect and an ad-removal purchase. These are concrete reasons the game can become a routine rather than a one-time download.\n\nOther comments come from people who still like the core but want fewer surrounding obligations. Requests include restoring Level Select and allowing an opt-out from tournaments. A player frustrated with bonus words describes finding many legitimate alternatives without filling the board. That does not prove the dictionary is wrong; it illustrates the difference between vocabulary discovery and guessing the puzzle's selected answers.\n\n## Ads, purchases, and conflicting accounts\n\nThe advertising reports vary substantially. Several describe black screens, difficult close controls, or freezes associated with ads. Another simply reports a relaxing, no-ad experience. A separate reviewer praises paying for permanent ad removal, while someone else asks whether an ad-free version exists.\n\nThose comments should not be flattened into a single universal claim. Account status, purchase history, and delivery conditions are not established for every reviewer. The evidence supports concern about reported interruptions, not an independently verified price or a guarantee that a purchase removes every promotional element.\n\nSome reviewers also dislike event animations and requests to participate in side activities. For an anagram fan, the practical question is whether the word loop remains the main attraction or feels crowded out by the wrapper.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWordscapes remains a plausible choice for people who enjoy extracting several words from the same letters. The sample is less reassuring for players seeking a bare-bones, distraction-free word game. Try it for the anagrams, not for an assumed absence of advertising or optional-event pressure.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Wordscapes a traditional crossword?\nNot exactly. It centers on connecting supplied letters to fill a crossword-style board.\n\n### Why can a real word fail to fill the board?\nThe game distinguishes required puzzle answers from bonus words; sampled players sometimes find that distinction frustrating.\n\n### Are all sampled players seeing ads?\nNo. Some report no ads, while others describe substantial interruptions. Purchase and account conditions are not fully known.\n\n### Is the sample current for every version?\nNo. It covers the named versions and dates, not every update or every player's experience.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wordscapes-word-game/id1207472156?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
