    "bodyMarkdown": "# Match Factory!: Sorting Objects Against a Clock\n\nMatch Factory's listing promises relaxation, but its most important rule is the countdown. If you are choosing an object-sorting game to escape time pressure, that detail matters more than the calming language surrounding it. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Identify, match, and clear\n\nThe developer describes connecting identical objects, sorting items, and clearing everything from the screen. This is a 3D object-matching presentation rather than a conventional grid of flat gems. Fruits, candy, and cake are among the advertised objects you can unlock.\n\nEach level is explicitly described as having a timer. The challenge therefore includes quick recognition and efficient action, not simply eventual completion. Finding a match is useful only if you can keep making progress before the clock expires.\n\nThe saved description does not provide a full account of selection-tray capacity, penalties, or the exact number of items required for every match. Those rules should not be borrowed from another matching app merely because the object piles look like a familiar genre. This analysis can identify the broad loop without inventing the missing details.\n\n## The tension behind the soothing pitch\n\nA timed visual search can be absorbing for someone who enjoys acting quickly. It can be a poor fit for someone who wants to stop, inspect several similar objects, and consider every option without consequence. Both preferences are legitimate; the timer is a design choice, not automatically a flaw.\n\nBoosters are advertised for difficult situations. Their usefulness and availability may influence the pace, but the listing alone does not establish whether they are optional conveniences or practically necessary later. There are no saved player-review bodies to resolve that question or assess long-term difficulty.\n\nThe description says play is available online or offline, while purchases require an internet connection. That is a useful developer claim, not a completed airplane-mode test or a promise covering every event and feature.\n\n## Purchases and other boundaries\n\nMatch Factory is free to download with optional in-game purchases explicitly disclosed. The supplied text does not verify ad frequency or an ad-removal product. Purchases and advertising are separate issues; the existence of one does not establish the absence of the other.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMatch Factory is most appealing on paper to players who like fast object recognition and visible cleanup goals. Avoid treating it as an untimed organizing toy. Its timer, booster system, and untested free-play balance make it a more qualified recommendation for anyone seeking completely pressure-free sessions.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are levels timed?\nYes. The saved description explicitly says each level has a timer.\n\n### Is offline play supported?\nThe developer advertises offline play, but this analysis has not independently verified it.\n\n### Does it include in-app purchases?\nYes. Optional purchases are disclosed, and the listing says they require an internet connection.\n\n### Are there saved player reviews here?\nNo usable review bodies were available, so player satisfaction and later-level balance remain unassessed.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/match-factory/id6449094229?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
