    "bodyMarkdown": "# Color Slide - Hexa Puzzle: Making Space One Color at a Time\n\nColor Slide is built around a pleasingly practical goal: organize the colors so the board has room to breathe again. Its listing describes a puzzle that becomes harder through geometry and additional colors, not through a faster countdown. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Sorting on a changing field\n\nThe developer says you sort and match colored hexagons, merge colors, and free space for more pieces. Progress introduces multicolored blocks, additional colors, and differently shaped playing fields. That makes usable space as important as recognizing a matching color.\n\nThe board-shape changes are the most distinctive detail in the description. A strategy that works with a generous open area may not transfer neatly to a constrained layout. The proposed challenge is to reconsider where pieces can go instead of repeating the same arrangement indefinitely.\n\nThe exact movement rules and clear thresholds are not fully specified in the saved text. This is not enough evidence to explain every merge or guarantee that a particular sequence will work. It does, however, distinguish Color Slide from falling-block games and from bottle-based liquid sorting.\n\n## Relaxation is a design aim, not a measured outcome\n\nThe listing advertises no timer and no penalties, along with repeat attempts and the ability to stop and return. Those claims make it relevant to players looking for patient color organization. They have not been checked through actual sessions, and they do not automatically verify every interruption or every mode.\n\nThe developer also describes restrained visual effects and limited vibration. Without inspecting the screenshots or running the app, it would be inappropriate to judge the actual motion, contrast, or sensory accessibility. Color-dependent rules may be important to someone with a color-vision difference; no particular assistive feature is established by this evidence.\n\nNo customer-review bodies were available in the saved collection. Consequently, the description's promises of low stress and progressive challenge cannot be presented as player consensus.\n\n## Ads and spending remain unresolved\n\nThe app is listed as free to download. The supplied description does not establish ad placement, paid assistance, or the existence of an ad-removal offer. An untimed rule set is not the same thing as an uninterrupted session.\n\n## Verdict\n\nColor Slide looks most relevant to players who like spatial organization and matching without an advertised countdown. It is less clearly suited to someone who needs verified color-accessibility options or complete information about the free-play economy. The changing geometry is its strongest documented reason to stand out.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What kind of pieces does it use?\nThe listing describes colored hexagons, including multicolored blocks as difficulty increases.\n\n### Does the board change shape?\nYes. Different playing-field shapes are specifically advertised.\n\n### Is it timed?\nThe developer says no timer or penalties; actual behavior has not been playtested here.\n\n### Is the app confirmed ad-free?\nNo. Timer-free language does not establish an absence of advertising.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/color-slide-hexa-puzzle/id6590876141?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
