    "bodyMarkdown": "# Number Match: Simple Arithmetic, More Interesting Connections\n\nNumber Match uses arithmetic you already know, then makes the arrangement of the numbers the real problem. It is not a calculation race or a Sudoku substitute: the aim is to remove compatible pairs until the entire board is empty. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Two kinds of pair, several ways to connect\n\nThe saved listing gives unusually concrete rules. Equal numbers can pair, such as two sevens, and different numbers can pair when their sum is ten, such as six and four. You tap the two numbers to cross them out and earn points.\n\nConnections are described horizontally, vertically, and diagonally between adjacent cells, with another useful case at the end of one line and the beginning of the next. That wrap between lines is worth remembering: a board can contain a legitimate connection that a quick left-to-right scan misses.\n\nWhen no moves remain, the listing says you can add lines containing the remaining numbers at the bottom. This does not immediately solve the grid; it changes the layout you must inspect. The goal is still to remove everything, not merely reach an arbitrary sum.\n\n## A small rule set with a spatial catch\n\nThe attraction is the balance between familiar numbers and changing relationships. Recognizing that eight and two belong together is easy. Deciding which available pair leaves the most useful connections is the more interesting part.\n\nHints, daily challenges, and seasonal events provide additional structure. The description also advertises no time limit. That positions the main puzzle as something you can study rather than solve under a clock, although event-specific behavior has not been independently checked.\n\nNo customer-review bodies were available in the saved evidence. It is therefore not possible to establish whether later boards feel elegant, repetitive, or overly reliant on hints. Nor does a brain-training pitch demonstrate an improvement in memory or concentration outside the game.\n\n## Free download, incomplete economy details\n\nThe metadata lists Number Match as free. The supplied description does not document the frequency of ads, how hints are replenished, or any current price for removing interruptions. A visible hint system should not be mistaken for proof of either generosity or a paywall.\n\n## Verdict\n\nNumber Match is a promising fit for players who want a clearly stated, number-based clearing puzzle without an advertised timer. It is a poor substitute for Sudoku if you specifically want digit-placement deduction. Its equal-or-ten rule is approachable; its connection geometry is what gives that simple rule room to become interesting.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Which numbers can be matched?\nIdentical numbers or two numbers that add up to ten, subject to the connection rules.\n\n### Can pairs cross between lines?\nThe listing permits a connection from the end of one line to the beginning of the next.\n\n### What happens when you run out of moves?\nYou can add extra lines with the remaining numbers at the bottom, according to the description.\n\n### Is there a countdown?\nThe developer advertises no time limit, but this has not been independently tested.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/number-match-numbers-game/id1545567989?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
