    "bodyMarkdown": "# Goods Puzzle: Shelf Sorting With a Timer in the Picture\n\nGoods Puzzle turns a cluttered shelf into a speed-and-recognition challenge. The saved feedback contains genuine enthusiasm for organizing the goods, but also a crucial warning: do not choose it on the assumption that every level is timer-free. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with saved player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Three matching goods, less clutter\n\nThe listing describes dragging items among shelves and collecting three identical goods to remove them. Clearing a set creates room for the next decisions. Different shelving arrangements and a claimed range of 300 object types supply variety beyond a single board layout.\n\nThis is not just a matter of finding three visible copies. Moving one object changes the available workspace, so sorting order and temporary placement are part of the appeal. The developer explicitly describes time limits and boosters that can freeze time. Its stress-relief language should be read alongside that countdown, not instead of it.\n\n## Players disagree about how calm it feels\n\nThe saved sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-25 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.12.0, 4.13.0; the listing records version 4.13.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. The experiences below are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nA five-star reviewer calls the game enjoyable but wishes it were not timed. Another says it has no timer, while a third reports timers appearing after early levels. The saved description supports treating timed play as part of the product. The contradictory reports cannot establish the exact experience of every level or account.\n\nPraise centers on an engaging challenge, colorful and distinct objects, and the satisfaction of sorting. One detailed critical review still likes the objects and side games. Those positive comments matter because the frustration is often with interruptions around an otherwise appealing loop.\n\n## The advertising concern is more than a pause\n\nSeveral reviewers say ads repeatedly opened a browser during play, sometimes without an intentional tap. Others describe ads that freeze or are difficult to close. These remain player reports, not a security diagnosis or independently reproduced behavior.\n\nPurchase experiences also differ. One player says ads remained after paying; another says rewarded assistance became more limited after buying removal. A different reviewer says they enjoy playing without purchases. The sample therefore does not justify saying everyone must pay, or that a payment reliably removes every interruption. Reviewers' quoted prices are not verified current offers.\n\nFor a game about quickly organizing shelves, losing the thread midlevel could be particularly irritating. That is the practical issue to watch, rather than assuming the high-level sorting idea itself is unpopular.\n\n## Verdict\n\nGoods Puzzle may suit players who like timed visual organization and triple matching. It is harder to recommend for someone seeking a guaranteed untimed or uninterrupted routine. The saved sample supports the sorting's appeal while leaving meaningful concerns about advertising and paid-removal terms unresolved.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How are items cleared?\nThe listing says to collect three identical goods by moving items among shelves.\n\n### Is the game timer-free?\nThe listing describes timed levels, despite conflicting player accounts about timers.\n\n### Are browser-opening ads confirmed by testing?\nNo. Multiple players report them, but this analysis has not reproduced or diagnosed the behavior.\n\n### Does paying remove all advertising?\nThat is not established; saved purchasers describe different outcomes.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/goods-puzzle-3d-sorting-games/id6499306806?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
