    "bodyMarkdown": "# Fruit Merge: A Clever Container Puzzle, Frequently Interrupted in Reports\n\nFruit Merge makes a simple promise: combine small fruits into something much larger without running out of room. The saved player accounts suggest the idea can be compelling, but many reviewers find it hard to settle into that loop because of interruptions. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with saved player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Bigger fruit changes the problem\n\nThe listing describes moving an incoming fruit left or right and dropping it into a container. Two identical fruits merge when they collide, producing the next fruit and increasing the score. The long-term target is a watermelon, while overflowing the container ends the game.\n\nThis is a spatial growth puzzle rather than a recipe or order-based merge board. A successful combination advances the fruit chain, but the larger result changes the space available for everything else. Planning where future matches can meet is therefore as important as making the match immediately in front of you.\n\nThe description's claim that only a tiny percentage of players reach the highest fruit is promotional and not supported by a disclosed dataset here. It should not be used to predict your chances or measure the game's fairness.\n\n## Players like merging; some question the feel\n\nThe saved sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-26 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 10.4, 10.5, 10.6; the listing records version 10.6. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. The experiences below are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive comments describe cute fruit, enjoyment of merging, and the desire to reach a better fruit. Some of those favorable reviews still object to the ads. A detailed mixed review says identical fruits sometimes appear to touch without combining. That is a report about collision sensitivity, not a reproduced physics defect.\n\nRelaxation is also not universal. One reviewer says the game felt stressful before bed, while others describe straightforward entertainment. The rising pile can create its own tension even without evidence of a countdown.\n\n## Reported interruptions are the central drawback\n\nMany comments describe ads appearing during an ongoing game, not merely after a failed run. Individual timing estimates vary widely, from very short gaps to much longer intervals. Those differences are a reason not to publish a single claimed ad frequency as though it were measured across the app.\n\nSome reviewers say the interruptions make them quit despite liking the core game; others continue to rate it highly. The evidence supports a substantial concern in this sample, not the conclusion that every player has exactly the same session.\n\nCurrent ad-removal terms and purchase prices are not established by the saved description. Nor does one review mentioning airplane mode verify reliable offline behavior.\n\n## Verdict\n\nFruit Merge is a reasonable concept match for people who enjoy spatial stacking, chain growth, and chasing a larger final object. It is a more cautious choice if sustained concentration is essential. The container puzzle has a recognizable hook; the saved sample gives much less confidence in an uninterrupted rhythm.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How do fruits merge?\nAccording to the listing, two identical fruits become the next fruit when they collide.\n\n### What ends a run?\nThe described failure condition is fruit overflowing the container.\n\n### Are ads only between games?\nSome sampled reviewers specifically report midgame ads; that behavior has not been independently tested here.\n\n### Is the collision complaint verified?\nNo. A player reports fruit touching without merging, but no physics test was performed.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/fruit-merge-match-game/id6471572249?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
