    "bodyMarkdown": "# Color Block: Combo Blast: Fixed Shapes, Flexible Planning\n\nColor Block: Combo Blast has a more specific strategic identity than its broad title suggests. The listing gives you an 8-by-8 grid, pieces that cannot rotate, and a combo rule that rewards keeping line clears close together. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## The shape you receive is the shape you place\n\nThe developer describes dragging blocks onto the grid and filling rows or columns to clear them. Because rotation is not allowed, a piece has to fit in its supplied orientation. You cannot automatically rescue an awkward space by turning the next shape sideways.\n\nCombos add another consideration. The listing says to clear lines within three moves to keep the combo going. That creates a tension between making a safe placement now and arranging the next clear before the scoring opportunity disappears.\n\nA practical way to read the rules is that empty space has two kinds of value: it can accommodate a difficult piece, and it can help complete a future row or column. Spending all your flexible space on a single attractive clear may leave fewer options afterward. This follows from the described constraints, not from a tested claim about the best scoring strategy.\n\n## Not simply another falling-block game\n\nAlthough the marketing mentions block-dropping, the actual instructions specify drag-and-drop placement. The description does not establish a continuously falling stack or a speed-based control scheme. Those familiar assumptions should not be imported from other block games.\n\nThe fixed orientation is the more meaningful differentiator. Players who like spatial planning may welcome it; people who instinctively expect rotation may find the restriction less comfortable. The supplied text also does not document every failure condition or how future pieces are selected.\n\nThere are no saved review bodies to show whether combos feel rewarding, whether the piece sequence seems fair, or how quickly difficulty rises. Claims about improved cognitive skills are marketing rather than evidence of an outcome beyond the puzzle itself.\n\n## Ads and purchase details\n\nThe app is free to download in the saved US metadata. The description does not establish ad frequency, assistance pricing, or an ad-free version. Nothing about the combo rule demonstrates that a session is uninterrupted or that paid features are unnecessary.\n\n## Verdict\n\nColor Block is a clear concept fit for players who enjoy fitting fixed shapes and planning a few moves ahead. It is less suited to anyone seeking traditional rotation-based action. Its strongest documented feature is the interaction between the nonrotating pieces and the short combo window; the surrounding economy remains unassessed.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How large is the grid?\nThe listing specifies an 8-by-8 board.\n\n### Can blocks rotate?\nNo. Nonrotatable blocks are an explicit rule in the description.\n\n### How are combos maintained?\nThe developer says to keep clearing lines within three moves.\n\n### Is the game confirmed timer-free?\nNo comprehensive timer behavior has been verified. A move-based combo rule is not an independent timer test.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/color-block-combo-blast/id6478063606?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
