    "bodyMarkdown": "# GOGO! Blast: A Clear Path Before the Next Tap\n\nGOGO! Blast is about knowing when not to tap. Every arrow has a fixed direction, and removing the available piece is useful mainly because it can open a route for the pieces still trapped behind it. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Solve the dependency, not just the symbol\n\nThe listing describes tapping arrows in the correct sequence until the board is clear. Each arrow moves only in the direction it points, so a blocked path must be opened before that arrow can leave. The end goal is complete clearance rather than matching colors or collecting a target score.\n\nThe strategic appeal is a visible chain of dependencies. One piece leaves, another route becomes available, and the board becomes easier to read. A crowded layout can be treated as several smaller route problems rather than an instruction to tap randomly.\n\nThat is an interpretation of the documented rules, not a claim that every level has a unique solution or that mistakes are always reversible. The supplied description does not specify undo behavior, failure penalties, or every obstacle type.\n\n## Untimed on the listing, not independently certified\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises no time pressure and says the game is fully playable offline. Those are useful distinctions if you are choosing among arrow puzzles that otherwise sound similar. A fixed-direction puzzle with time to study the board asks for different attention from one built around speed.\n\nNeither claim was tested on an installed copy. The saved text cannot establish disconnected first-launch behavior, event access, or the absence of any mode-specific exception. These remain advertised capabilities rather than independently verified filter tags.\n\nThe listing also promises many levels and increasing difficulty. More layouts could provide a lasting routine, but quantity alone says little about how varied the reasoning becomes. There are no saved player-review bodies to assess repetition or the later difficulty curve.\n\n## Ads and costs are separate questions\n\nThe US metadata lists a free download. The description does not document ads, paid help, or an ad-removal offer. A game can have untimed boards and still interrupt a session between them; nothing here establishes which pattern applies.\n\nIf your goal is a minimalist experience, distinguish the simplicity of the rules from the amount of interface and monetization surrounding those rules.\n\n## Verdict\n\nGOGO! Blast is a strong concept match for players who like visible sequencing and patient route clearing. It is less relevant if you want free movement, physics, or a complex scoring system. Its promise is straightforward directional logic; the saved evidence cannot yet confirm the quality of the entire free-play experience.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can arrows turn?\nThe stated rule is that each arrow moves only in its pointing direction.\n\n### How do you complete a level?\nClear all arrows by opening their routes in a workable sequence.\n\n### Is there a timer?\nThe developer advertises no time pressure, but that was not independently checked.\n\n### Can you play offline?\nOffline play is advertised; actual disconnected behavior remains untested here.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/gogo-blast/id6776522053?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
