    "bodyMarkdown": "# Angry Birds 2: Choosing the Bird Is Part of the Shot\n\nAngry Birds 2 is built around a satisfying question: where should the next impact land? Its listing adds another decision before that shot—choosing which bird to launch—then wraps the destruction puzzles in a broader progression and competition system. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Aim for the structure, not just the target\n\nThe developer describes using a slingshot to fling birds at towers occupied by pigs. Bringing those structures down and defeating the pigs is the core action. Choosing a bird for the next launch adds planning before the trajectory decision.\n\nLevels can have multiple stages, including bosses. That structure matters because the task is not necessarily finished after one tower falls. The saved description does not establish exact bird abilities, detailed physics behavior, or a guaranteed solution for any particular layout.\n\nThis is a physics-themed destruction puzzler rather than a matching grid. Its appeal is in predicting the consequence of a shot and adjusting to the result. Actual collision consistency, touch precision, and the feel of aiming cannot be assessed without playing.\n\n## A larger game around the slingshot\n\nFeathers increase birds' scoring power, hats provide collection goals, and clans offer a shared activity layer. Daily challenges, an arena, leaderboards, and Mighty Eagle's Bootcamp broaden the advertised routine beyond completing ordinary levels.\n\nThose systems may appeal to someone who likes returning for several kinds of progress. They can be less attractive to a player who simply wants a quiet sequence of self-contained physics puzzles. The listing does not establish how much participation in any specific system is necessary.\n\nThere are no saved customer-review bodies for this app. It would therefore be unsupported to say players praise the current controls, complain about difficulty changes, or agree that the progression is fair.\n\n## Ads, purchases, and connection requirements\n\nThe description explicitly discloses optional purchases, possible loot boxes or randomized rewards, and advertising for Rovio and third-party products. It also says the game may require internet connectivity and that the first play involves an additional content download.\n\nThat is not an ad-frequency measurement or a complete current catalog of paid items. It is enough to reject an assumption that a free download automatically means no commercial interruptions or wholly offline access.\n\n## Verdict\n\nAngry Birds 2 is most relevant to players who want slingshot destruction with collection and competitive goals around it. It is less clearly suited to someone seeking a minimal, disconnected physics puzzle. Bird selection and multistage levels are the strongest documented strategic hooks; the quality of current play remains untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you choose which bird to launch?\nYes. Bird selection is an explicitly advertised feature.\n\n### Does every level have one stage?\nNo. The listing includes multistage levels and bosses.\n\n### Are ads and purchases disclosed?\nYes. The description names advertising, optional purchases, and possible randomized reward mechanics.\n\n### Is offline play guaranteed?\nNo. The listing warns about possible connectivity requirements and an initial content download.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/angry-birds-2/id880047117?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
