    "bodyMarkdown": "# Make It Fit: Packing Starts Before You Put Anything in the Box\n\nMake It Fit's best idea happens before the packing begins. The listing says some objects need to be cut, folded, or combined before they will fit, turning a tidy arrangement puzzle into a small sequence of transformations. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Change the item, then solve the space\n\nThe developer gives concrete examples: slice a baguette, assemble a sandwich, and organize prepared objects into a container. The puzzle is not simply dragging every original shape into an empty grid. You first need to recognize which objects can become something more useful.\n\nThat creates two related questions. What form should each item take, and where does that resulting shape belong? Solving only the second question may leave you trying to force an unsuitable object into a space it was never meant to occupy.\n\nThe listing claims levels can have a single working arrangement. That is a developer statement, not an independently checked uniqueness guarantee. The supplied text does not establish whether pieces rotate, whether transformations are reversible, or how mistakes are handled.\n\n## Themes give the same idea different contexts\n\nAdvertised settings include lunch boxes, picnic baskets, suitcases, jewelry storage, makeup organizers, and shopping carts. That variety could make the transformation logic feel more contextual than fitting anonymous geometric blocks.\n\nIt also suggests an important audience distinction. Someone who likes discovering how everyday objects interact may enjoy the preparation step. A player who wants pure shape tiling with no hidden interaction rules may prefer a more abstract packing game.\n\nThe developer emphasizes short levels, background music, and ASMR-style satisfaction. No hands-on session or audio inspection supports a judgment about the actual click sounds, pacing, or calmness. There are no usable customer-review bodies to show whether players find the transformations intuitive or spend time guessing at them.\n\n## What the free label leaves unresolved\n\nThe metadata records a free download. The saved description does not establish ads, hint pricing, paid content, or the availability of an ad-removal purchase. Nor does the phrase bite-sized levels prove that every stage fits a predictable break length.\n\nIf you are choosing it specifically for quiet, brief sessions, those practical details remain worth checking before committing to a routine.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMake It Fit is an interesting prospect for players who enjoy arranging objects but want more than shape placement. It is less clearly suited to strict tiling purists who dislike discovering undocumented interactions. The combination of preparation and packing is the reason to look closer, not an independently established claim of flawless puzzle design.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this just dragging objects into a box?\nNo. The listing also describes slicing, folding, and combining items before packing.\n\n### What kinds of containers appear?\nExamples include bento boxes, suitcases, baskets, and jewelry or makeup organizers.\n\n### Is every puzzle proven to have one solution?\nThe developer claims a single working arrangement, but the levels were not audited here.\n\n### Are ads or hint costs known?\nNo. The saved description does not establish those details.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/make-it-fit-packing-puzzle/id6796574369?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
