    "bodyMarkdown": "# Wool Crush: Read the Rules, Not Just the Traffic-Jam Title\n\nWool Crush has a traffic-jam subtitle, but the saved description tells a different story: choose colored cannons, attack matching blocks on a dragon, and protect a cat. That mismatch makes reading the actual listing particularly important. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## A color-matching rescue, as described\n\nThe developer's instructions are brief. You select a cannon of the right color, blast matching colored blocks to slow a dragon, and keep a kitty safe. The game is presented as a casual 3D puzzle with increasing challenge and repeated rescues.\n\nThose rules support classifying it as color-based shooting or defense, not automatically as a vehicle-exit puzzle. The title alone is insufficient evidence for buses, passenger queues, limited parking spaces, or directional car movement.\n\nThe yarn theme is also not enough to infer a thread-unraveling mechanic. The supplied text actually emphasizes a dragon and cannons. A useful review has to preserve that distinction instead of filling in a more familiar game from the name.\n\n## Fast action rather than a documented sorting board\n\nThe listing explicitly describes fast-paced play. That may appeal to players who like making quick color choices with an immediate visual objective. It is less reassuring for someone choosing the game because a wool theme sounds like a slow, tactile organizer.\n\nThe limited description leaves important rules unanswered. It does not explain whether cannons have ammunition limits, whether they can be repositioned, how the dragon advances, or exactly what causes failure. No timer can be asserted simply from the word fast-paced, and no complete strategy guide can be built from the available instructions.\n\nThere are no saved customer-review bodies. Consequently, this analysis cannot establish whether players enjoy the rescue premise, find the pace fair, or encounter a different main mode after downloading. The discrepancy is between title and description, not independently verified deception.\n\n## Payment and interruptions are unknown\n\nThe US metadata records a free download. The saved description does not document advertising, paid power-ups, or a removal offer. Neither the rescue premise nor the promise of simple controls establishes a low-cost or interruption-free experience.\n\nIf the cannon idea interests you, confirm the actual installed gameplay and purchase terms before making assumptions based on the subtitle.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWool Crush is worth investigating for players attracted to colorful cannon choices and a dragon-rescue theme. It is not a clear recommendation for someone specifically seeking traffic management or yarn sorting. Its thin evidence supports a recognizable rescue loop, but leaves too many operational details open for a stronger judgment.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Wool Crush definitely a car-parking puzzle?\nNot from the supplied description, which emphasizes cannons, a dragon, and protecting a cat.\n\n### What does color matching do?\nThe listing says matching-color attacks clear dragon blocks and slow it down.\n\n### Is it described as slow-paced?\nNo. The developer explicitly presents it as fast-paced.\n\n### Are the full rules documented?\nNo. Cannon limits, movement, failure details, and many other mechanics remain unspecified.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wool-crush-escape-traffic-jam/id6743385713?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
