    "bodyMarkdown": "# Differences - Find & Spot It: A Reference Picture for Every Clue\n\nDifferences - Find & Spot It gives you something hidden-object games do not always provide: a reference image beside the puzzle. The challenge is to notice exactly where two apparently matching scenes stop matching. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Compare before you tap\n\nThe listing describes inspecting two nearly identical pictures and identifying changes such as missing objects, altered colors, or shifted shapes. You find the discrepancy rather than search for an unrelated object from a separate list.\n\nThat distinction makes the comparison itself the main tool. A scene can be scanned section by section, checking corresponding details instead of guessing what might be relevant. This is a natural implication of the paired-picture format, not a tested claim that any particular scanning strategy guarantees success.\n\nThe developer advertises more than 2,000 pictures, varied art styles, and difficulty ranging from easy to hard. The saved text does not document the exact number of differences per level or whether mistakes have a penalty. Those details should not be assumed from other apps in the same genre.\n\n## Zoom and hints are the practical attractions\n\nA zoom system is explicitly described, allowing closer inspection of small areas. Hints are also advertised for the last elusive difference. Those tools could matter more to a prospective player than the total image count, especially on a smaller screen.\n\nTheir actual usability is untested. The description does not establish maximum magnification, contrast options, hint replenishment, or the conditions attached to the phrase endless hints. That phrase does not automatically mean unlimited, unconditional free assistance.\n\nThe developer also promotes unlimited play time and a relaxed pace. That supports describing an untimed pitch, not certifying every challenge or optional mode as timer-free. There are no saved review bodies to assess whether the image details feel fair or whether players find the assistance sufficient.\n\n## Free is a starting price, not a complete model\n\nThe metadata records a free download, and the description claims no hidden fees. This analysis cannot independently confirm every content or purchase boundary from that marketing statement. Advertising frequency and a removal offer are not established in the supplied evidence.\n\nClaims about memory, focus, and cognitive improvement likewise do not demonstrate benefits beyond enjoyment of comparing pictures.\n\n## Verdict\n\nDifferences - Find & Spot It is a clear fit on paper for players who prefer careful visual comparison over abstract logic. It is less relevant to someone seeking a clue-driven mystery or verified brain-training outcomes. Zoom, hints, and a reference image are the strongest documented practical reasons to consider it.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What kinds of differences do you find?\nThe listing mentions changes to colors, missing objects, and altered shapes.\n\n### Can you zoom into pictures?\nYes. Magnification is an advertised feature, though its usability was not tested.\n\n### Are hints unlimited and free?\nThe listing advertises endless hints but does not establish all access conditions.\n\n### Does the app prove cognitive improvement?\nNo. The saved material contains promotional claims, not validated outcome evidence.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/differences-find-spot-it/id6504279468?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
