    "bodyMarkdown": "# Car Sort: Color Puzzle: Matching Garages, Managing Traffic\n\nCar Sort makes parking a color-matching problem rather than a precision-driving test. Its listing describes sending cars around a track and directing them into the right garages, with avoiding a jam as the central organizing concern. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## A tap starts a traffic decision\n\nThe developer says you tap to send colored cars around the track, match their colors, and park them in appropriate garages. The goal is to clear the board. Increasingly challenging levels are intended to build on that basic relationship between departure and destination.\n\nThe track matters because the game is not described as unrestricted vehicle placement. A car's usefulness depends on where it can go within the system, not simply whether it matches a visible color. The supplied text does not fully specify track capacity, garage capacity, collision rules, or whether cars can circulate more than once.\n\nThose omissions limit how confidently a detailed strategy can be offered. It is reasonable to identify traffic sequencing and color sorting as the broad mechanics; it would not be reasonable to invent a precise failure threshold or a guaranteed opening move.\n\n## Quick levels, unknown pace\n\nThe listing advertises hundreds of quick levels and a balance between relaxation and challenge. That could appeal to someone who likes a concrete cleanup objective and compact decisions. It is less clearly suited to players who want an open-world driving game or realistic parking practice.\n\nThe instruction to think fast is promotional rather than a documented timer rule. This evidence does not establish whether every level has a countdown. It also does not demonstrate one-handed comfort or measured session length.\n\nThere are no saved customer-review bodies to show whether the progression feels fresh, repetitive, fair, or demanding. Any judgment about later difficulty would therefore need more than the developer's increasing-challenge promise.\n\n## Internet is explicitly required\n\nUnlike many listings that loosely say play anywhere, this description specifically requires an internet connection and warns that network fees may apply. That makes it a poor evidence-based choice for someone seeking guaranteed disconnected travel play.\n\nThe app is free to download and explicitly includes optional in-game purchases. Some items may also be unlocked through gameplay, according to the description. Exact prices, earning rates, and ad behavior are not established; optional purchases do not imply an absence of ads.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCar Sort is a plausible fit for players who enjoy coordinating colored objects through constrained routes. It is less suitable if offline availability is essential. The garage-and-track setup gives the matching a clear purpose, but the saved evidence cannot establish long-term balance or how quiet the interface remains between moves.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the objective?\nSend colored cars around the track and park them in matching garages to clear the board.\n\n### Does it require internet?\nYes. That requirement is explicit in the saved description.\n\n### Are purchases included?\nYes. Optional in-game purchases are disclosed, with some items also unlockable through play.\n\n### Is there definitely a timer?\nThe supplied text does not establish a specific timer rule.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/car-sort-color-puzzle/id6758138698?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
