    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bubble Bus: Parking Space Is the Real Resource\n\nBubble Bus combines a transportation theme with a familiar sorting constraint: you can only hold so much unfinished work. Its listing makes a full parking lot, rather than steering skill, the important danger. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Park for the bubbles you can collect\n\nThe described loop is to drive buses into the right spots and collect bubbles matching their colors. You plan the order carefully, because a full lot combined with missing the needed bubbles can end the game.\n\nThat makes parking a resource rather than mere decoration. Sending a bus forward may help only if the matching bubbles are accessible; otherwise, it can occupy space needed for a more useful move. This is a direct implication of the listed failure rule, not a claim about an optimal solution to an unseen level.\n\nThe supplied description does not establish passenger queues, seat capacities, directional arrows, or detailed collision behavior. Those are common in related bus games but are not facts that should be transferred into this one without evidence.\n\n## Tools can change the constraint\n\nVIP parking and shuffle features are explicitly advertised. A parking-related tool could alter the amount of room available, while a shuffle suggests a way to reorganize the current situation. The exact effects, limits, and prices are not documented in the saved text.\n\nThat matters because tools in a constrained-storage puzzle can substantially change the choices. Their presence does not prove that the standard levels are unfair, nor does it establish that all help is freely earned. Both conclusions would require evidence the listing does not provide.\n\nThe developer calls the game relaxing and increasingly challenging. Those descriptions express an intended experience, not a demonstrated difficulty curve. There are no saved customer-review bodies to show whether players find the bubble collection satisfying or become frustrated with parking pressure.\n\n## Short description, significant practical unknowns\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, paid assistance, offline access, and current removal offers are not established. The short store text also does not describe a timer or an undo system.\n\nFor someone choosing a quiet logic game, the absence of those details should encourage a closer look at the installed rules—not be converted into an assumed absence of ads, time pressure, or penalties.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBubble Bus is a plausible fit for players who like color sorting with limited staging space. It is less clearly suited to people expecting a driving simulator or a fully documented puzzle system. The strongest evidenced hook is the relationship between matching bubbles and keeping parking capacity available; the rest of the session experience remains open.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What do buses collect?\nThe listing says they collect matching-color bubbles.\n\n### What can cause a loss?\nThe description warns about a full parking lot and missing the right bubbles.\n\n### Which assistance tools are named?\nVIP parking and shuffle features are advertised, without complete terms or prices.\n\n### Is there a timer?\nThe saved description does not establish a timer rule.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bubble-bus-parking-jam-puzzle/id6743441683?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
