    "bodyMarkdown": "# Words of Wonders: Crossword: Travel Framing for Letter Connections\n\nWords of Wonders uses a tour of landmarks to give its letter puzzles a sense of destination. The actual activity is more compact: connect letters in one continuous gesture and find the words needed by the board. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## A vocabulary hunt with a travel wrapper\n\nThe listing describes connecting letters without lifting your finger, finding hidden words, and progressing through levels associated with successive wonders. It advertises varied board designs and a growing challenge.\n\nThat puts it in the word-connect family rather than establishing a conventional clue-based crossword. The crossword label refers to the board-oriented word puzzle; the supplied text emphasizes forming words from letters, not solving a full page of written definitions.\n\nThe landmark progression can provide a reason to keep moving even when the underlying action stays familiar. Players who enjoy finishing sets of levels may find that framing more motivating than a standalone score. The evidence does not establish a detailed geography lesson or factual travel content for every wonder.\n\n## What the challenge can—and cannot—promise\n\nThe developer says the vocabulary database supports increasingly difficult, varied levels. That is not enough to assess dictionary quality, repetition, the handling of alternate spellings, or how often a plausible word is rejected.\n\nThose details are particularly important in a word game: a satisfying puzzle depends not only on recognizing a word, but also on understanding what the board accepts. No customer-review bodies were available in the saved evidence, so there is no basis here for a player consensus about fairness or variety.\n\nLikewise, the invitation to test vocabulary should not be inflated into a claim of validated language improvement. The documented benefit is an opportunity to play with words.\n\n## The weekly membership is explicit\n\nThe saved description lists a US Pro membership at $3.99 per week, with a doubled daily gift, two hints per level, and ad removal. It is described as auto-renewing. This is a captured listing price, not a guarantee of the offer currently shown to every account.\n\nThe free download therefore should not be confused with the absence of subscription decisions. The description does not establish free-version ad frequency or precisely which promotions disappear under Pro. Read the actual offer before accepting a recurring charge.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWords of Wonders is a sensible prospect for players who enjoy connecting letters and advancing through a themed sequence. It is less clearly suited to traditional-crossword purists or anyone seeking a one-time-price experience. The landmark wrapper gives the word hunt context, while the weekly membership deserves attention before a casual download becomes a recurring expense.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you solve written clues?\nThe saved instructions emphasize connecting letters to find words, not traditional written crossword clues.\n\n### What does the travel theme do?\nIt frames progression through levels and successive wonders; detailed educational content is not established.\n\n### What Pro price is listed?\nThe saved US description says $3.99 per week, subject to the actual current offer.\n\n### Is Pro a recurring subscription?\nYes. The description says it automatically renews unless turned off under its stated terms.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/words-of-wonders-crossword/id1369521645?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
