    "bodyMarkdown": "# Plants vs. Zombies: A Defensive Puzzle With a Moving Deadline\n\nPlants vs. Zombies asks you to build the right defense before an advancing threat reaches the house. Its listing makes clear that the strategy is not just placing more plants, but matching limited resources to enemies with different behaviors. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Choose a defense for the threat approaching\n\nThe developer describes using plants such as Peashooters, Wall-nuts, and Cherry bombs against zombie attackers. Your supply of plants and seeds is limited, so placement and selection are part of the challenge rather than unlimited construction.\n\nDifferent enemies can jump, swim, or otherwise complicate a basic defense. An Almanac is advertised as a source of information about both plants and zombies, giving the player a way to plan rather than rely only on trial and error.\n\nThis is not a turn-based logic grid. The description emphasizes thinking and planting quickly as enemies advance. Even without a conventional countdown display, that creates a moving deadline: failing to respond can let attackers reach their destination.\n\n## Changing environments broaden the planning\n\nThe listing advertises 50 Adventure levels spanning day, night, fog, a pool, and the rooftop, plus Survival mode. Different settings imply a changing context for defense, although the supplied text does not fully document every environmental rule.\n\nThat structure could appeal to someone who likes adapting a familiar toolkit to new conditions. It is less suitable for a player who wants unlimited time to consider each move. The evidence does not establish a pause system, difficulty settings, or accessibility options that might change that fit.\n\nThe description also advertises achievements and collectible plants. Its award claim is explicitly footnoted to the original PC downloadable game, so it should not be presented as an award earned by this exact iOS version.\n\n## Purchases and version-specific uncertainty\n\nThe app is listed as free and explicitly offers optional purchases of virtual currency for in-game items. Coin packs are mentioned in the description. Their exact current prices and practical importance to progression are not established here.\n\nThere are no saved player-review bodies. That means the available evidence cannot assess current ad behavior, performance, offline reliability, or whether the free mobile version feels balanced. Familiarity with the franchise is not a substitute for checking this particular build.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPlants vs. Zombies is a strong concept fit for players who enjoy planning defenses under active pressure and adapting to different enemy types. It is less suitable for someone seeking a completely untimed, consequence-free puzzle. The plant-versus-threat relationship is clearly documented; current mobile-version quality and monetization remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What kind of game is it?\nThe listing describes a defensive strategy game using plants to stop advancing zombies.\n\n### Is there an endless-style mode?\nSurvival mode is advertised alongside the 50-level Adventure campaign.\n\n### Are the awards for this iOS version?\nThe saved description footnotes its award claim to the original PC downloadable game.\n\n### Does it include purchases?\nYes. Optional virtual-currency purchases for in-game items are explicitly disclosed.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/plants-vs-zombies/id893677096?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
