    "bodyMarkdown": "# Loop Sort: The Decision Happens Before the Conveyor Moves\n\nLoop Sort's listing puts a small but important decision before an automatic process: choose which truck releases its cubes. The conveyor does the visible moving, but the player is responsible for creating a useful order. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Release, match, clear\n\nThe developer describes tapping a truck to send cubes onto a conveyor, where they sort automatically. Matching and clearing cubes advances the level. That makes the game sound more like managing input to a system than manually placing every block into a destination.\n\nThe difference matters. A tap can change what arrives on the line next, so the relevant question is not simply whether a truck is available. It is whether its contents help with the current obstacle or make the next stage easier to resolve.\n\nThe saved description does not specify the conveyor's capacity, exact match threshold, or complete failure condition. It also does not establish that every truck's contents are visible. Any detailed claim about those rules would go beyond the evidence.\n\n## Obstacles are the most informative part of the listing\n\nFour obstacles are explicitly named. Hidden blocks require revealing what lies behind them. Curtains lift after sorting a required color. Ice blocks must be broken to free cubes. Barriers prevent cubes inside from leaving while permitting new cubes to enter.\n\nThe barriers provide a particularly interesting asymmetry: entry and exit are not equivalent. A space can accept something without being able to release what it already holds. That could require planning around dependencies rather than assuming the entire line remains freely available.\n\nThese are developer-described behaviors, not tested explanations of individual levels. The supplied text does not say exactly how ice breaks or how hidden contents are previewed. It is enough to identify a more specific puzzle premise than the broad title alone provides.\n\n## Thin evidence around the system\n\nNo customer-review bodies were available. There is no basis here for claims about players finding the automatic sorting satisfying, the obstacles repetitive, or the later levels unfair.\n\nThe download price is recorded as free, but the supplied description does not establish ads, paid assistance, offline support, or an ad-removal offer. Automatic animation also does not prove that the game is a passive idle experience: choosing the release order is still the documented interaction.\n\n## Verdict\n\nLoop Sort is an interesting prospect for players who enjoy directing a process rather than moving every object themselves. It is less clearly suited to someone who wants fully specified rules before starting. The obstacle list supplies genuine mechanical identity, but the actual pacing and free-play economy remain unassessed.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What does tapping a truck do?\nIt releases cubes onto the conveyor, according to the listing.\n\n### Is sorting manual?\nThe description says the cubes sort automatically after release; the player still chooses when to send them.\n\n### How do barriers behave?\nThey are described as allowing new cubes in while preventing the cubes inside from leaving.\n\n### Are ads or offline play verified?\nNo. Neither is established by the saved evidence.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/loop-sort/id6751101794?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
