    "bodyMarkdown": "# Crossword Master: Sharing the Board, Competing for the Finish\n\nCrossword Master gives you an unusual incentive: help fill the crossword, but try not to hand the most valuable finish to your opponent. The saved feedback suggests that this shared-board competition is both its attraction and a source of frustration. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with saved player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Correct letters are not the whole score\n\nThe listing describes drawing a set of five letters and placing them into a board using clues embedded in cells, including picture clues. Correct letters earn points, and finishing words earns additional points. Using the full set in a turn also contributes to the scoring strategy.\n\nThat creates a tactical layer beyond knowing the answer. A letter that helps complete the crossword may also leave an easy word finish for the opponent. The developer explicitly suggests that holding back a useful letter can sometimes improve a future scoring opportunity.\n\nThere is no advertised time limit, and auto-save is described as supporting unfinished puzzles. Those capabilities have not been independently checked. The description also does not establish live friend matchmaking merely by using the word opponent.\n\n## Players like the challenge but debate the rewards\n\nThe saved sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-12 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.4.0; the listing records version 2.4.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. The experiences below are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive comments praise thought-provoking clues, a manageable challenge, and the fun of competitive vocabulary play. One reviewer requests a mode for friends and family after enjoying games against the computer. That is evidence of a desired feature, not confirmation that such a mode exists.\n\nThe sharpest scoring complaint comes from a player who places many letters but sees the opponent claim the word-completion points. That frustration is consistent with the listed bonus structure; it does not by itself prove an incorrect score calculation or manipulated outcome.\n\nAccessibility concerns are also concrete. A reviewer wants more magnification for picture clues, and several ask for a dark mode because they play before bed. Those requests matter to fit even if the word rules sound appealing.\n\n## Advertising and paid removal\n\nSeveral reviewers report ads during a puzzle, not just between boards. Some otherwise favorable accounts say the volume drives them away. Others mention ad-removal prices, but those amounts are individual reports rather than a verified current offer.\n\nPurchasing removal would not necessarily address every concern in the sample: clue readability, completion-bonus preferences, and restart behavior are separate issues. There are also reports of repeated puzzles or changed restart options, which remain unverified account experiences.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCrossword Master is a good concept match for players who enjoy clue solving with tactical timing. It is less suited to someone who wants a purely solitary crossword or expects points to reflect only the number of letters contributed. The scoring twist gives the game identity, while advertising and readability deserve attention before making it a nightly routine.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Why can finishing a word matter so much?\nThe listing awards additional points for word completion beyond individual correct letters.\n\n### Do clues appear inside the grid?\nYes. Embedded text and picture clues are part of the described format.\n\n### Can you definitely play with friends?\nThat is not established; one reviewer specifically asks for such a mode.\n\n### Is dark mode confirmed?\nNo. Sampled players request it, but the saved evidence does not establish current availability.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/crossword-master-word-puzzle/id6547858927?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
