    "bodyMarkdown": "# Knock Out: A Color Shot With a Structural Consequence\n\nKnock Out's listing combines two kinds of satisfaction: making a color match and watching a structure come apart. Its most interesting promise is that a well-chosen shot changes more than the block it directly hits. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Aim at the structure you want to change\n\nThe developer describes firing matching blocks, triggering chain reactions, toppling towers, and clearing every remaining piece to complete a level. Different structures, obstacles, and objectives are advertised across hundreds of levels.\n\nThat suggests the puzzle is not only recognizing the correct color. The target's position within a construction may matter because a successful shot can affect the pieces around it. A player attracted to cause-and-effect destruction may find that premise more appealing than a flat matching board.\n\nThe supplied text does not establish the exact match threshold, ammunition allowance, or every physical rule. It also does not explain whether unsupported blocks always fall or how obstacles interact. Those details are essential to a strategy guide, but cannot be invented from the phrase physics-driven.\n\n## Precision is the appeal, predictability is untested\n\nThe description emphasizes smooth controls and rewarding precision shots. Those are developer claims rather than a measurement of aiming accuracy or collision consistency. No installed session was used to assess whether a carefully lined-up shot behaves as expected.\n\nThat uncertainty matters more in a physics-style game than in one with fully discrete rules. The enjoyment may depend on how clearly a structure communicates its likely reaction. The listing provides the premise, not proof that every collapse is readable or satisfying.\n\nThere are no saved customer-review bodies to show whether players find the destruction clever, repetitive, or unpredictable. The advertised level count also does not establish mechanical variety by itself.\n\n## Offline play and the free-download label\n\nThe developer says no internet is required. That is an advertised capability, not an independently verified disconnected session, and does not establish whether every optional feature works offline.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, paid assistance, and a current ad-removal option are not documented by the supplied description. It would be inappropriate to infer no forced ads from the offline pitch or the game's simple controls.\n\n## Verdict\n\nKnock Out is a plausible fit for players who like color targeting with visible structural consequences. It is less clearly suited to anyone who wants a fully specified, deterministic logic puzzle before downloading. The combination of matching and collapse is the distinctive hook; the quality of the actual physics remains untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the level objective?\nThe description says to clear all blocks, with structures and obstacles varying by level.\n\n### Are chain reactions part of the game?\nYes. Matching shots and chain reactions are explicitly advertised.\n\n### Is the physics independently assessed?\nNo. Collision behavior, aiming precision, and structural consistency were not tested.\n\n### Can it work without internet?\nThe developer advertises that capability, but it has not been independently verified here.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/knock-out-physics-puzzle/id6782096197?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
    "chartRank": 93
