    "bodyMarkdown": "# Screw Out 3D: Nut Sort Jam: Dismantling as a Sorting Puzzle\n\nScrew Out 3D presents disassembly as a calm sorting activity. The listing's strongest mechanical idea is taking a structure apart layer by layer while directing the removed parts into suitable trays. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Taking apart is only half the job\n\nThe developer describes screws, bolts, bars, and plates forming miniature 3D structures. You twist and remove components, then sort them into the correct trays. Color and shape challenges are both advertised, suggesting the sorting is not limited to matching one color alone.\n\nThat gives each removal two consequences: it changes the remaining structure and adds something that needs a destination. The supplied text does not establish tray capacity, visibility rules, or every restriction on which piece can be removed next. Those details should not be borrowed from another screw game.\n\nThe description also mentions customizable trays and unlockable screw styles. Those features may provide a collecting or personalization layer, but the evidence does not establish whether they alter the rules or only the presentation.\n\n## A deliberate no-rush pitch\n\nThe developer explicitly says there are no timers and emphasizes offline play. It also advertises ASMR-style audio, smooth animation, daily challenges, and level packs. That makes the app relevant to players seeking a slower alternative to timed visual puzzles.\n\nThese remain listing claims. No audio was heard, no screenshot was visually inspected, and no disconnected session was performed for this analysis. It would be misleading to call the actual sound soothing, the motion comfortable, or every mode reliably timer-free.\n\nNo customer-review bodies were available. The description's assertions about mindfulness and mental resetting are not evidence that players experience those effects, let alone a validated health outcome.\n\n## Read the ad wording carefully\n\nThe listing says there are no annoying ads after every level. That statement is narrower than saying there are no ads at all, and annoying is subjective. It does not establish a measured frequency, the absence of midlevel ads, or the terms of any removal purchase.\n\nThe recorded price is free, but assistance pricing and paid content boundaries remain unspecified. A claim of offline access also does not independently verify no-forced-ads behavior when connected.\n\n## Verdict\n\nScrew Out 3D is a plausible prospect for people who enjoy methodical dismantling and matching objects to containers. It is less clearly suited to someone who requires independently verified interruption-free play. The layered structure concept is well described; the calmness and economy are promises that still need practical checking.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you only remove screws?\nThe listing also describes sorting parts by color or shape into trays.\n\n### Is it timed?\nThe developer advertises no timers, but behavior across modes was not tested.\n\n### Does the ad claim mean no ads at all?\nNo. The wording specifically concerns annoying ads after every level and is not a universal no-ad guarantee.\n\n### Is offline play verified?\nIt is advertised, not independently confirmed in this analysis.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/screw-out-3d-nut-sort-jam/id6747560860?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
