    "bodyMarkdown": "# Merge Cooking: Recipes Become a Production Puzzle\n\nMerge Cooking's useful twist is not just turning one food icon into a larger one. The listing adds cooking equipment, giving ingredient production another step before a customer receives the finished dish. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Merge ingredients, then put the kitchen to work\n\nThe developer describes tapping, dragging, and merging ingredients, then using seven pieces of equipment to prepare food. Named examples include a frying pan, blender, oven, and cocktail shaker. Finished dishes support serving customers and progressing through restaurants.\n\nThat equipment layer could make the recipe paths feel more connected to cooking than a generic item ladder. It also means the game should not be understood solely as matching identical pieces. The supplied text does not document exact ingredient requirements, machine capacities, or preparation durations for each dish.\n\nThe advertised journey includes food associated with New York, Bangkok, Tokyo, and Paris, alongside varied restaurant themes. This is thematic game content, not a verified guide to authentic preparation or practical culinary skill.\n\n## A restaurant project around the board\n\nAn assistant named Lea introduces the adventure, customers bring stories, and restaurants can be renovated. Those elements give the item chains a broader destination. Someone who likes collecting recipes and seeing a setting improve may enjoy that context more than an isolated score-based merge puzzle.\n\nThe description does not establish the degree of design freedom in renovation. It would be premature to call it a complete interior-design simulator or promise that every restaurant element can be chosen individually.\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises no time pressure. That does not automatically mean unlimited continuous play, no resource waits, or no timed optional event. Energy systems and generator behavior are not documented in the supplied text, and should not be inferred from other merge-cooking apps.\n\n## Missing player and economy evidence\n\nThere are no saved customer-review bodies for this app. Consequently, this analysis cannot say players find the machines satisfying, complain about energy, or agree that the recipe chains stay varied.\n\nThe US metadata records a free download, but the supplied description does not provide ad frequency, current item prices, or a verified removal offer. A relaxed cooking theme is not evidence of a generous economy. The actual pace and purchase decisions remain questions for a closer inspection.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMerge Cooking looks best suited to players who enjoy ingredient production chains and a restaurant-progression wrapper. It is less clearly suited to someone seeking hands-on cooking instruction or guaranteed uninterrupted play. The equipment layer is its most substantive documented distinction; long-term balance cannot be judged from the listing alone.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What makes the cooking system distinctive?\nThe listing adds seven pieces of equipment to the ingredient-merging loop.\n\n### Which machines are named?\nExamples include a frying pan, blender, oven, and cocktail shaker.\n\n### Does no time pressure mean unlimited play?\nNo. It does not establish the absence of energy limits, waits, or mode-specific conditions.\n\n### Are player complaints available here?\nNo usable review bodies were present in the saved evidence for this app.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/merge-cooking/id1643509748?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
