    "bodyMarkdown": "# Unscrew Frenzy 3D: Rotate First, Remove Second\n\nUnscrew Frenzy 3D makes inspection part of the puzzle. Its listing encourages rotating and zooming around a model before removing screws, a useful distinction if you enjoy understanding an object rather than working only from one fixed view. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Find a useful removal order\n\nThe described loop is to examine the model from different angles, remove screws of matching colors into corresponding boxes, and gradually dismantle the whole object. The developer warns that an unhelpful removal order can block progress.\n\nThat makes choosing the next visible screw a planning decision. Accessibility is not the same as usefulness: removing something now may leave the remaining model or the sorting destinations harder to manage. The supplied text does not fully define box capacities or the exact conditions that create a blockage.\n\nDrills, brooms, and hammers are advertised as assistance tools. Their existence does not establish how often they are needed, whether they are freely earned, or whether later puzzles depend on them. Those are economy and balance questions the listing cannot resolve.\n\n## A collection layer around the structures\n\nThe developer advertises models ranging from airplanes to houses and a collection for completed or unlocked objects. That gives dismantling a longer-term destination beyond clearing the current screen.\n\nThe description also explicitly promises no timers and promotes click sounds and smooth animation. These are attributed product claims, not the result of a sensory or performance evaluation. No screenshot was visually inspected and no audio was heard for this analysis.\n\nThere are no saved customer-review bodies. Consequently, the evidence cannot support claims that players love the models, find the tools fair, or complain about repeated layouts.\n\n## The VIP text contains a real ambiguity\n\nThe saved US description lists two weekly VIP prices—$7.99 and $4.99—each after a three-day trial. It does not clearly explain the relationship between them. That could reflect different options or inconsistent copy; this analysis cannot determine which.\n\nBoth lines describe recurring billing rather than a one-time unlock. The actual purchase sheet and renewal terms are the appropriate place to resolve the price before accepting an offer. The free download also does not establish an ad-free experience, and the description does not document ad frequency or every VIP benefit.\n\n## Verdict\n\nUnscrew Frenzy 3D is a plausible fit for players who enjoy rotating models and sorting parts in a deliberate order. It is less suitable if you want fully transparent pricing before inspecting the actual offer. The mechanical premise is clear enough to interest screw-puzzle fans; the contradictory subscription copy is the most concrete caution in the evidence.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you rotate and zoom the model?\nYes. Both are explicitly described as ways to inspect the puzzle.\n\n### Is it timed?\nThe developer advertises no timers; this has not been independently tested.\n\n### Which weekly VIP price is correct?\nThe saved text lists both $7.99 and $4.99 without clearly distinguishing them. Check the actual current offer.\n\n### Is the trial a permanent free unlock?\nNo. The listing describes a three-day trial followed by a recurring weekly subscription.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/unscrew-frenzy-3d/id6749300334?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
