    "bodyMarkdown": "# Zoodoku: Animal Placement With Rules Worth Learning\n\nZoodoku dresses a constraint puzzle in animal themes, but its most interesting quality is the rule set. You are not simply finding a cat in a picture: you are deciding where animals can legally go while several restrictions overlap. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Three constraints shape each placement\n\nThe listing says to place exactly one animal in each colored region. Animals cannot share a row or column, and they cannot touch diagonally. A legal position therefore has consequences beyond its own region.\n\nFor example, choosing a square can rule out other positions along the same row and column while also affecting nearby diagonal squares. This is a general explanation of the listed constraints, not a solution to a particular unseen board. The challenge is to use those exclusions together until the arrangement works.\n\nThe developer compares the game with Sudoku and Minesweeper, but the stated rules do not make it either one. There are no supplied digit-completion rules or mine counts. The more accurate category is region-based placement deduction with spacing restrictions.\n\n## Logic is the promise, uniqueness is unverified\n\nThe description claims every puzzle has one solution and requires no guessing. That is an attractive proposition for players who want each decision to follow from evidence. It has not been established by a solver audit or hands-on examination of the level set.\n\nThe listing advertises hundreds of handcrafted boards, daily puzzles, and additional animal themes beyond the starting cats. Those themes are described as preserving the same core rules. They may provide a collecting incentive without fundamentally changing the deduction task.\n\nThere are no saved customer-review bodies to show whether the later puzzles maintain a clear logical path or whether the interface helps track exclusions. Notes, undo behavior, error checking, and accessibility features are not fully documented in the supplied text.\n\n## Offline and cost boundaries\n\nThe developer advertises offline play. That remains an untested capability, not a verified statement about every daily or optional feature. The metadata records a free download, but the supplied description does not establish ads, paid hints, or a removal offer.\n\nAnimal themes are described as preserving the rules, so changing the theme should not be mistaken for an entirely different kind of puzzle.\n\n## Verdict\n\nZoodoku is an especially clear concept fit for players who enjoy constraint propagation without arithmetic. It is less suited to someone looking for a conventional Sudoku board or a casual hidden-cat hunt. The overlapping placement rules are substantive; the quality and uniqueness of the actual puzzle collection still need independent checking.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Zoodoku standard Sudoku?\nNo. The listed rules concern animals, colored regions, rows, columns, and diagonal spacing rather than digits.\n\n### How many animals go in each region?\nExactly one, according to the description.\n\n### Can animals touch diagonally?\nNo. Diagonal contact is explicitly prohibited.\n\n### Are unique solutions independently verified?\nNo. The developer makes that claim, but the level set was not audited here.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/zoodoku/id6781525746?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
