    "bodyMarkdown": "# Dot Link: A Simple Connection Rule With Competing Routes\n\nDot Link begins with an approachable instruction: connect matching colors without letting the paths cross. The challenge is that every successful connection changes how much room remains for the next one. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## A good route must leave room for the others\n\nThe developer describes joining matching-color dots with pipes and completing all the pairs on a board. Crossing pipes is prohibited. That makes a direct-looking connection potentially awkward if it blocks the only useful route for another color.\n\nThe strategic interest is in sharing limited space. A path can be valid by itself but still leave the board unsolved. Thinking about several pairs together is therefore more useful than treating each connection as an independent task.\n\nThe supplied description does not explicitly require every square to be filled, so that familiar rule from some connection games should not be imported here. Nor does the saved text establish whether all boards have unique solutions or how mistakes are signaled.\n\n## Difficulty choices and practical tools\n\nThe listing advertises five tiers: Beginner, Intermediate, Hard, Extreme, and Insane. Undo, restart, hints, and a daily puzzle provide additional structure. Those are useful options for someone who wants to experiment with a route and reconsider it.\n\nDay and Night modes and a Color Blind setting are also explicitly advertised. That last feature is especially relevant in a game whose pairs are defined by color. Its actual implementation has not been inspected, so this analysis cannot say which visual distinctions it adds or certify accessibility for a particular player.\n\nThe developer claims more than 10,000 levels. That is a catalog claim, not an independent count or a guarantee of varied reasoning. There are no saved customer-review bodies to establish whether players find the progression fresh or the hardest boards satisfying.\n\n## What the free label does not cover\n\nThe recorded download price is free. The description does not establish advertising frequency, hint replenishment, paid packs, or an ad-removal option. The presence of undo does not automatically mean every experiment has no cost, and a daily puzzle does not prove offline availability.\n\nThese are practical details to inspect if your main requirement is an uninterrupted logic routine rather than simply trying a new connection puzzle.\n\n## Verdict\n\nDot Link is a sensible prospect for players who enjoy planning routes and revising a board until every pair can coexist. It is less clearly suited to people who need independently verified accessibility or monetization details before downloading. Its noncrossing rule is clean; its advertised visual settings are worth investigating without treating them as tested accommodations.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the main restriction?\nConnect matching-color dots without allowing the pipes to cross.\n\n### Must every square be filled?\nThe saved description does not explicitly establish that additional rule.\n\n### Are undo and restart available?\nYes. Both are advertised, along with hints.\n\n### Is there a color-blind setting?\nThe listing says there is, but its implementation and effectiveness were not independently inspected.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/dot-link-connect-the-dots/id6444312485?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)",
