    "bodyMarkdown": "# Tile Match Ultimate: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nTile Match Ultimate offers two reasons to clear a tile layout: complete it at your preferred pace, or try to finish faster for more stars. Its official imagery explains more than its unusually brief description.\n\n## Read the pictures before assuming the rules\n\nThe inspected official screenshot shows a layered arrangement of illustrated tiles, including fruit and flowers. Three matching blossom tiles appear in the tray at the bottom, and a hand points toward another tile in the layout. The caption promotes matching tiles, while a star-progress bar appears above the board.\n\nThose details support a picture-tile matching concept, with a selection area separate from the main arrangement. They do not establish a universal tray capacity, the exact conditions for selecting a covered tile, or the penalty for filling the tray. Familiarity with another triple-tile game is not enough to supply this one’s missing rulebook.\n\nThe written description calls the game “Tile Match Pro,” despite the US listing’s Tile Match Ultimate title. That mismatch is worth noting when identifying the product, not treating as proof that two different apps are installed together.\n\n## Pace is framed as a choice\n\nThe description explicitly offers either playing at your own pace or completing levels quickly to maximize stars. That is a more specific proposition than simply describing the puzzle as relaxing. A completion-focused player and a star-chasing player may be interested in the same board for different reasons.\n\nHowever, the listing does not explain whether speed affects access to later levels, merely the rating awarded, or some other progression system. Nor does a pause icon in a promotional screenshot prove that every game system stops when paused.\n\n## Who should consider it\n\nThis is a candidate for readers who enjoy recognizing identical pictures in layered layouts and who like having a performance goal beyond simply clearing them. It is less convincing as a choice for someone who needs fully documented mistake rules or a verified penalty-free mode before installing.\n\nThe US listing shows a free download. The supplied evidence does not establish advertising frequency, paid removal, hints, or offline access. The screenshot contains an ad-related icon, but that alone does not explain what advertising appears during actual play.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConsider Tile Match Ultimate for illustrated tile matching with optional speed-oriented star goals. Its concept is visible, but the sparse instructions leave important tray and progression rules unresolved.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Tile Match Ultimate the same as Tile Match Pro?\n\nThe description uses the name Tile Match Pro, while the US listing identifies the app as Tile Match Ultimate. The naming inconsistency is unresolved.\n\n### Does speed matter?\n\nThe developer says faster completion can earn more stars, while also describing play at your own pace.\n\n### How many tiles fit in the tray?\n\nThe inspected image shows a selection tray, but the universal capacity is not established.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/tile-match-ultimate/id6499209500?uo=4)."
