    "bodyMarkdown": "# Homescapes: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nHomescapes makes the next room part of the reward for solving a puzzle. Match-3 levels support restoring a mansion, while Austin and the household provide reasons to care about the work.\n\n## The board supports a home project\n\nThe description asks you to swap and match pieces, use boosters and combinations, and beat levels to renovate and decorate. The larger setting is an expansive house whose secrets and family chapters unfold alongside the restoration.\n\nInterior design is explicitly part of the pitch: you decide how the house looks. The listing does not quantify the number of alternatives for each decision, so it would be misleading to promise unrestricted room planning or a full architectural editor. The supported attraction is making decorating choices within a progression-based game.\n\nAustin is joined by other characters who interact through an in-game social network, and a mischievous cat adds a domestic thread. Those details distinguish the game from a generic match-3 campaign with a decorative backdrop. Characters living around the renovation are part of what the developer is selling.\n\nAn official promotional image depicts a pin-and-pipe challenge above Austin. That image does not establish how often such a challenge appears; the written description identifies match-3 as the renovation engine.\n\n## Choose the story as well as the puzzles\n\nHomescapes is a natural candidate for someone who enjoys improving a persistent place and wants puzzle clears to lead into visible changes. It is less compelling for a player who sees dialogue and home tasks as obstacles between boards.\n\nFacebook friends are mentioned as a way to add social involvement. That does not establish that an account connection is mandatory, or that friends are needed for normal progress. Similarly, the heartwarming description says little about how difficult later match-3 stages become.\n\n## What free-to-play means here\n\nThe US listing describes the game as free to play and explicitly discloses real-money purchases, including randomized items. That is a meaningful spending consideration. It does not show that purchases are required to progress, but neither does it establish a comfortable no-spend pace.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample, so the store’s popularity cannot become evidence that players consider the economy fair or the story well paced. Advertising frequency, offline access, and current booster prices are also not established by the supplied description.\n\nThe useful choice is whether mansion restoration and a continuing family story would make match-3 more rewarding for you. Those features, rather than a claim of tested polish, provide the case for consideration.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConsider Homescapes if the house and its residents are rewards you actually want, not just a wrapper around match-3. Readers seeking only standalone boards should weigh the additional story and renovation structure.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What does Austin help you do?\n\nThe listing centers on renovating and decorating a mansion through match-3 progress.\n\n### Can you design the interiors?\n\nInterior choices are advertised, but the description does not establish unrestricted layout editing.\n\n### Are purchases randomized?\n\nThe developer discloses that purchasable in-game elements include randomized items.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/homescapes/id1195621598?uo=4)."
