    "bodyMarkdown": "# Jigsaw Puzzles - Puzzle Games: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nJigsaw Puzzles - Puzzle Games puts picture choice and adjustable complexity at the center of its pitch. You assemble images rather than chase points, with puzzle sizes advertised up to 400 pieces.\n\n## Pick the picture and the scale of the job\n\nThe listing advertises more than 30,000 free HD pictures across categories including flowers, animals, nature, landmarks, and art. The most meaningful choice is therefore not just what image appeals to you, but how many pieces you want to work through.\n\nIncreasing the piece count changes how much local shape and image detail you need to track. It does not prove a particular completion time or a uniform difficulty rating: a visually repetitive picture can be harder than a more varied image at the same size.\n\nMystery puzzles add another variation by asking you to uncover an image rather than starting with its full identity established. Daily puzzles and seasonal postcards provide reasons to return, while a gallery advertised as updated daily supplies the longer catalog framework.\n\nThe official Eiffel Tower image shows loose jigsaw pieces and gaps around the assembled picture. It presents recognizable picture assembly, not a demonstration of every workspace tool.\n\n## Assistance without a score chase\n\nA hint can place or match the next piece when you are stuck, according to the feature description. Custom backgrounds are also advertised. Those are practical tools, but the text does not specify hint cost, replenishment, edge filters, piece rotation, or every workspace control.\n\nThe developer explicitly presents the app without points. That makes it a plausible option for readers who want assembly to be the reward rather than a leaderboard result. It does not automatically establish the absence of ads, purchases, or time-limited events around the puzzles.\n\n## Who should consider it\n\nChoose on whether browsing a large picture collection and adjusting piece count sounds enjoyable. Someone seeking a close digital counterpart to a tabletop jigsaw has a clearer reason to investigate than someone who wants changing game rules or a story campaign.\n\nThe US listing offers a free download and advertises many free images. It does not, in the supplied material, document the entire purchase inventory or the access terms of every collection. Offline availability and advertising frequency are also unresolved.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample. Claims about improved short-term memory or calming effects remain the developer’s positioning rather than independently demonstrated outcomes.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe advertised image range and 400-piece ceiling make Jigsaw Puzzles a relevant catalog-first option. Consider it for traditional picture assembly, while checking workspace features and access terms rather than assuming every tool is included.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the largest advertised puzzle?\n\nThe listing specifies up to 400 pieces.\n\n### Are there daily puzzles?\n\nYes. A new free daily puzzle and a regularly updated gallery are advertised.\n\n### Does it have a hint tool?\n\nThe description says a hint can help match the next piece. Costs and limits are not specified.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/jigsaw-puzzles-puzzle-games/id1324604053?uo=4)."
