    "bodyMarkdown": "# Sandbox - Pixel Art Coloring: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nSandbox - Pixel Art Coloring offers more than filling a supplied picture. Its listing also includes free drawing and turning your own photos into pixel art, making creation tools a meaningful part of the proposition.\n\n## Move between coloring and making\n\nThe description connects tiny pixel art with number recognition and using a legend. That frames the coloring side as structured completion rather than choosing every color from scratch. Search helps locate subjects, and a round similarity button is advertised for finding related artwork.\n\nCollections let you organize art within the app. These are useful features for someone who wants to return to a type of image rather than repeatedly browse the whole catalog. The supplied text does not establish export formats or whether every collection is editable in the same way.\n\nFree Drawing Mode is a distinct feature, not merely another name for filling numbered squares. Photo conversion also adds a personal source for pixel images. The listing does not explain conversion resolution, palette control, or processing location, so those details should not be assumed.\n\nThe official heart image shows filled red pixels beside numbered gray cells and a numbered palette, making the structured coloring side visually explicit.\n\n## A family pitch is not an educational study\n\nThe developer emphasizes use by children, parents, and teachers, including motor skills and number recognition. Those statements explain its intended audience, but do not demonstrate measured learning gains or independently establish that every image suits every child.\n\n“No Ads” is also explicitly stated. That is a developer claim rather than a tested finding. It should not be expanded into a conclusion that the entire art catalog is free, since the same description offers membership for access to all pixel art.\n\n## Membership is a separate access decision\n\nThe listing describes a seven-day trial, followed by membership rates of $2.99 weekly, $7.99 monthly, or $39.99 yearly. It says subscriptions renew automatically unless renewal is disabled at least 24 hours before the period ends. These are described terms, not a checkout quote for a particular account.\n\nThe download itself is free. Offline access, the free-versus-member boundary for individual tools, and actual ad behavior have not been independently checked. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to resolve those questions.\n\nConsider Sandbox if personal photos and drawing matter alongside coloring. Skip it if you need unrestricted catalog access without a recurring membership.\n\n## Verdict\n\nSandbox stands out on paper for combining structured pixel coloring with original drawing and photo conversion. Its membership model is the key boundary to examine, separate from the developer’s no-ad promise.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you draw your own pixel art?\n\nYes. Free Drawing Mode is explicitly advertised.\n\n### Can photos become pixel art?\n\nThe listing advertises converting gallery photos, without specifying detailed conversion controls.\n\n### Does the trial renew?\n\nThe description says membership continues automatically after the trial unless renewal is disabled under the stated terms. Confirm the actual offer before starting.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/sandbox-pixel-art-coloring/id1237405816?uo=4)."
