    "bodyMarkdown": "# Collect Em All! Clear the Dots: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nCollect Em All! Clear the Dots rewards tracing a useful chain, not simply spotting any two matching colors. Connect adjacent balls of one color and work toward the level’s objectives before the move budget runs out.\n\n## A longer chain is only part of the decision\n\nThe listing describes tapping and dragging through adjacent matching balls. Longer chains produce larger effects and better scores, while completing each board requires meeting its particular goals. Those two aims need not be identical: a large clear is most useful when it advances the objective.\n\nThe description does not specify every adjacency rule, such as whether diagonal links are always legal. It also does not establish how new balls enter or whether every level uses the same board shape. Those details should not be inferred from another linking game.\n\nWhat is explicit is the move limit. The puzzle asks you to choose productive actions rather than continue linking indefinitely until the board happens to clear. This makes planning around the next few opportunities a more useful expectation than assuming a purely decorative swiping activity.\n\nThe first official image instead promotes mini-games using curved paths between colored dots. It is not a complete diagram of the main ball-linking rules described in the text.\n\n## Three advertised tools do different jobs\n\nRocket clears a row or column, Bomb removes a larger area, and Shuffle rearranges the board. Their effects address different obstacles: alignment, concentration of targets, and an unhelpful arrangement. The supplied description does not establish their costs or prove that a particular stage requires assistance.\n\nDaily rewards, missions, timed events, and global leaderboards provide additional goals. Timed events should not be confused with proof that every ordinary board has a countdown; the explicit board constraint in the play guide is running out of moves.\n\n## Who should consider it\n\nConsider Collect Em All if you enjoy drawing color chains and choosing where a limited action can do the most work. It is less relevant if you want traditional swapping match-3 or an unlimited-move clearing sandbox.\n\nThe US download is free, and offline play is advertised. Actual offline feature coverage, ad frequency, and purchase terms remain unverified. There are no customer review bodies in this sample, so the developer’s promises of satisfying effects and steadily increasing difficulty are not player consensus.\n\nThe supported appeal is the interaction between chain length, target goals, and a move budget. Those concrete rules are more informative than its general claims about addictive play.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCollect Em All is a candidate for goal-driven color linking with a finite move budget. Choose it for chain planning and distinct assist tools, not a presumed guarantee about uninterrupted or wholly offline access.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How do you select a chain?\n\nThe listing describes dragging through adjacent balls of the same color.\n\n### Are moves limited?\n\nYes. The play guide says to meet each level’s objectives before running out of moves.\n\n### What does Shuffle do?\n\nIt rearranges the board to create fresh opportunities, according to the description.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/collect-em-all-clear-the-dots/id1570641170?uo=4)."
