    "bodyMarkdown": "# FreeCell: Card Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nFreeCell: Card Game makes temporary storage part of the strategy. Four free cells help you reorganize the tableau while building the suit foundations in ascending order.\n\n## Holding a card is not the same as solving it\n\nThe listing describes a standard 52-card deck and four free-cell spaces used as placeholders. Cards ultimately move from the tableau to suit-based foundations. A holding cell can release access to another card, but an occupied cell is no longer available for a different detour.\n\nThat is the game’s recognizable planning tension: a move that looks like progress may reduce flexibility. The developer says most deals can be solved with the right strategy, not that every random arrangement is guaranteed solvable.\n\nRandom games and a million well-known games are advertised. The description does not provide a deal-selection walkthrough, so exact controls for choosing a numbered deal remain unverified. A step-by-step tutorial and highlighted movable cards are also listed for learners.\n\nThe official wood-table image shows the tableau, holding spaces, and foundations together, with score, time, and move displays above. A displayed time value is not itself proof of a countdown.\n\n## Tools can support deliberate practice\n\nUnlimited undo and hints are advertised, alongside auto-completion, statistics, and personal-best scoring. Undo is especially relevant if you enjoy comparing alternatives without treating a first mistake as the end of a deal. Hints and auto-completion serve different purposes: one suggests progress, while the other helps finish an already-resolved ending.\n\nBoth portrait and landscape layouts are listed, with tap-to-move and drag-and-drop controls. These are concrete interface options rather than proof of comfortable use on every device. The promise of saving an interrupted game is likewise a developer statement, not a recovery test performed here.\n\n## Daily goals and paid access\n\nDaily goals award XP and titles, adding a progression layer to the classic rules. Readers who prefer a plain deal can weigh that against those who like an extra reason to return.\n\nThe US download is free. The description offers an optional ad-free subscription at $1.99 monthly, $4.99 for three months, or $9.99 yearly, with auto-renewal and a trial for new subscribers. It also includes future Daily Challenges and exclusive themes. Those are listing terms, not a current personalized checkout quote.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample to establish ad frequency, purchase restoration, or save reliability in practice.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConsider this FreeCell for its clearly described classic framework and substantial practice tools. The main choice beyond the rules is whether daily progression and a recurring ad-free subscription fit how you want to play.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many free cells are available?\n\nThe listing specifies four temporary holding spaces.\n\n### Can you undo moves?\n\nUnlimited undo is advertised, along with hints and a tutorial.\n\n### What are the advertised subscription prices?\n\nThe description lists $1.99 monthly, $4.99 for three months, and $9.99 yearly, with auto-renewal. Confirm the actual offer before subscribing.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/freecell-card-game/id301987699?uo=4)."
