    "bodyMarkdown": "# Merge Dragons!: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-10-27–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 13.18.0, 13.18.3. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMerge Dragons! is most distinctive when the camp becomes a place you care about. Dragons hatch, evolve, and harvest while merged objects gradually reclaim a fantasy landscape.\n\n## Merging changes both objects and land\n\nThe description starts with three identical objects evolving into something rarer. Life Essence heals cursed land, dragon eggs hatch companions, and Gaia statues help resolve puzzle levels. The camp persists beyond those individual stages, giving accumulated objects and creatures a shared home.\n\nThe listing advertises hundreds of object types, quests, handcrafted levels, and Den communities. Those layers explain why this is not a minimalist merge board. A player who wants a growing collection and several parallel goals has a clearer reason to investigate than one looking for a finite puzzle book.\n\n## More to do can mean too much on screen\n\nOne supporter enjoys discovering dragon species; another likes visiting other players’ bases and the egg designs. A long-term player values having many activities available. These are concrete attractions in a collection-centered game.\n\nA different long-term account asks to hide floating event tokens because they obscure an already busy camp. Another says additional side activities drove them away. This is not a simple argument between players who like content and those who do not: the presentation of that content can interfere with caring for the camp itself.\n\n## Waiting and spending produce different experiences\n\nSome reviewers say patience makes the game enjoyable without spending. Others feel short events demand heavy grinding or purchases to reach their desired rewards. Both can be true for different goals: advancing a camp and completing every limited event need not require the same commitment.\n\nA reviewer specifically objects to scarce gems and long waits for chalices. Another reports missing rewards and unsatisfactory support, while launch or shutdown problems appear elsewhere. These are unverified individual accounts, not a demonstration that every reward is unreliable.\n\n## Ads and purchases are distinct systems\n\nSeveral reviewers describe no interrupting ads, or ads chosen for rewards. Another reports reward-ad glitches. That does not justify calling the entire product universally ad-free. The listing also discloses optional purchases, including random items, and says drop-rate information is available in-game.\n\nThe download is free, and offline play is advertised. Choose on whether managing a persistent camp and collection is appealing enough to accommodate waits and competing activities, rather than assuming all progress or event rewards come at the same pace.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMerge Dragons has meaningful depth for collectors who want a persistent camp. The sample’s best caution is the tension between that camp and the expanding event interface, with different spending expectations for different goals.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many objects make a merge?\n\nThe description gives three identical objects as the basic evolution rule.\n\n### Must you spend money?\n\nSome reviewers enjoy patient free progression; others feel event goals push purchases. The sample does not establish one universal requirement.\n\n### Are ads forced?\n\nSeveral reviewers describe optional reward ads or no gameplay interruptions, but there was no independent ad audit.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/merge-dragons/id1208952944?uo=4)."
