    "bodyMarkdown": "# Sort It 3D: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2024-11-19–2026-08-01 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.8.0, 1.9.0. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nSort It 3D is a tube-and-colored-ball puzzle with a very short store description. Its older review sample supplies useful experiences, but should not be presented as current-week feedback.\n\n## Sorting is established; detailed move rules are not\n\nThe US description asks you to sort colored balls into tubes and lists a wide palette. It does not explain the full transfer restrictions, available capacity, or victory conditions beyond organizing the colors. Those missing rules should not be replaced by assumptions from water-sort games.\n\nOne critical reviewer complains that placement is too permissive compared with the restrictions they expect. That is evidence of their difficulty preference, not a sufficient rule audit of the current release. Another describes using only one of two empty vials as a self-imposed challenge.\n\n## The attraction can be self-directed\n\nA long-term player values the absence of scoring and a time limit, instead trying to finish in fewer moves. Other accounts enjoy the simple, mellow sorting or find it too easy. That split makes the audience clearer: relaxing organization is the reward for some readers, while others need stronger constraints.\n\nThe sample also includes a report about a timed version freezing. It would therefore be inaccurate to turn praise for untimed play into a claim that the entire app has never contained a timed activity.\n\n## Historical ads and reliability reports\n\nThe sample covers November 2024 through August 2026, across versions 1.8.0 and 1.9.0. Multiple later entries report freezing during play. These accounts are worth noting, but do not establish whether a subsequent release resolved the problem.\n\nAdvertising complaints include a video after declining a reward prompt and between-level interruptions. A purchaser describes ad removal as worthwhile, while another says ads returned and restoration did not work. Different dates and purchase experiences prevent a definitive statement about current coverage or availability.\n\nA player also asks about syncing between phone and iPad. A question is not proof that no sync feature exists; the supplied evidence simply does not resolve it.\n\nThe US download is free. Consider the tube-sorting concept if self-directed organization sounds appealing, while checking current controls, ad terms, and stability rather than treating this historical sample as a live product report.\n\n## Verdict\n\nSort It 3D has supporters who enjoy low-pressure organization and invent their own challenges. Its evidence is older and its description sparse, so current mechanics and reliability deserve confirmation before stronger recommendations.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this a recent review sample?\n\nNo. The 50 included US reviews span November 2024 through August 2026, rather than the current week.\n\n### Is every mode untimed?\n\nSome reviewers praise untimed play, but another mentions a timed version. A universal no-timer claim is not supported.\n\n### Can you sync phone and iPad progress?\n\nA reviewer asks about it, but the supplied description and sample do not establish the answer.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/sort-it-3d/id1493125671?uo=4)."
