    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Crush® - Fun Puzzle Game: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-03-12–2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.0.5, 4.0.6, 4.0.7, 4.0.8, 4.0.9, 4.1.0, 4.1.2, 4.1.9, 4.2.0, 4.2.1, 4.2.2, 4.2.4, 4.2.5, 4.2.6, 4.2.7. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWord Crush® - Fun Puzzle Game makes a found word change the board. Correct answers remove letter blocks and let others cascade, so the next search happens in a different arrangement.\n\n## More than one activity under the word-game name\n\nThe listing describes searching, swiping, and connecting letters, with more than 18,000 levels advertised. Nature backgrounds frame the main activity. The falling-block consequence is the useful mechanical distinction: identifying an answer also reshapes what remains.\n\nThe app additionally advertises tile matching, sorting, screw puzzles, and other side activities. Those are not implied by the crossword label, and they matter when choosing an app for a focused word routine. Some readers want variety in the same place; others want fewer competing tasks.\n\n## Support for the words and the extras\n\nOne reviewer likes how the words flow together. Another enjoys both the word section and matching game, but objects when the latter becomes very small. That is a specific readability complaint about a side mode, not proof that the entire interface is unreadable.\n\nThe sample also contains disagreement with word acceptance and slang choices. Such criticism is relevant to dictionary expectations, but does not establish that every contested answer is invalid English. A preference for familiar vocabulary is different from a verified lexicon audit.\n\n## Crashes have reported recoveries too\n\nSeveral accounts describe opening failures or crashes around updates. Crucially, one reviewer says an update restored access and all their data; another explicitly updates an earlier complaint to say the app functions again. These later statements should not disappear from a summary simply because the original failure reports are more dramatic.\n\nOther reviewers still report obstructing black boxes or login difficulties. The sample spans March to November 2026 and many releases, so it cannot establish a single current stability state.\n\n## Paid removal versus purchase promotions\n\nMid-game advertising is a prominent complaint. One purchaser says paid removal was satisfactory, while another objects to purchase pop-ups despite paying to remove ads. Those experiences are not necessarily describing the same kind of interruption: an external ad and an in-game sales offer are different systems.\n\nThe US download is free, and offline play is advertised. Current offer coverage and prices remain unverified. Consider Word Crush if cascading word discovery and optional variety both appeal, while checking how much interface and promotion activity you want around them.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWord Crush’s shifting letters provide a concrete word-puzzle hook. Its expanding side modes and interruption reports make it a more conditional choice for readers who want a simple, quiet word routine.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Why do the letters move?\n\nThe listing says correct answers cause letter blocks to cascade down, changing the board.\n\n### Is it only a word game?\n\nNo. Tile matching, sorting, and screw-based side games are also advertised.\n\n### Were the crash reports resolved?\n\nSome reviewers explicitly report later recovery, while others describe continuing problems. The sample does not establish a universal current outcome.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/word-crush-fun-puzzle-game/id1446728841?uo=4)."
