    "bodyMarkdown": "# Blockudoku - Block Puzzle Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nBlockudoku - Block Puzzle Game borrows Sudoku’s board structure, not its number-filling objective. Place shapes on a 9×9 grid and clear complete lines or 3×3 squares to keep space available.\n\n## Two clearing targets share the same cells\n\nA block can contribute to a row, column, and smaller square at once. That makes placement more than finding any empty area large enough to hold a piece. A position that advances several possible clears may be more useful than one that merely avoids an immediate collision.\n\nThe listing distinguishes combos from streaks. Removing several elements together creates a combo; making successful clearing moves consecutively creates a streak. Those reward structures can pull attention in different directions: an immediate clear helps maintain a run, while arranging several targets can prepare a larger result.\n\nThe developer’s own guidance warns against waiting indefinitely for the perfect block. Preserving empty space is part of survival, so a promising setup can become a liability if the board fills before its key piece arrives. The description does not independently explain the piece-generation system or establish whether rotation is available.\n\nOne official promotional image shows a triangular grid rather than the classic square board. It signals additional visual variation without replacing the explicitly documented 9×9 rules or explaining that pictured mode.\n\n## Untimed does not mean unlimited space\n\nThe listing explicitly says there is no time limit. That allows consideration before placement, but it does not remove the risk of running out of moves. The pressure described here is spatial rather than a countdown.\n\nThis makes Blockudoku relevant to readers who like planning around several overlapping regions. It is less suitable if you want conventional Sudoku deduction using digits and candidate notes. Familiar 3×3 boxes do not make the rules interchangeable.\n\n## Additional goals and access\n\nDaily Challenges award trophies, Seasonal Events offer animated postcards, and tournaments add leaderboard competition. Those provide alternative goals around the same broad placement idea. Their existence does not prove every event has the same board restrictions or that social competition is mandatory.\n\nThe US listing offers a free download. The supplied text does not establish advertising frequency, paid removal, purchases, or offline support. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support claims about fair block sequences or comfortable controls.\n\nChoose on the actual combination of lines, squares, and limited space. The Sudoku branding is a helpful visual reference, not the reason to assume a familiar solving technique will work.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConsider Blockudoku for untimed spatial placement with several overlapping clearing goals. It is especially relevant if balancing immediate clears against bigger combinations sounds more appealing than filling a number grid.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Blockudoku ordinary Sudoku?\n\nNo. You place shaped blocks and clear lines or squares rather than entering digits.\n\n### How large is the board?\n\nThe listing specifies a 9×9 grid, with 3×3 squares as clearing targets.\n\n### What is the difference between a combo and a streak?\n\nThe description links combos to multiple clears at once and streaks to consecutive successful moves.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/blockudoku-block-puzzle-game/id1452227871?uo=4)."
