    "bodyMarkdown": "# Color Fill 3D: Maze Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nColor Fill 3D: Maze Game turns movement into painting. Guide a cube around a cell-based field and fill the board with color, rather than selecting colors for individual areas of a picture.\n\n## The route creates the result\n\nThe description puts you in control of a cube and gives the objective of coloring every cell. Swipe controls move it around the field. That means the puzzle’s subject is coverage through movement, not artistic palette selection.\n\nA route must eventually account for the whole available layout. Reaching one corner is not the same as completing a board if other cells remain unfilled. The developer advertises more complex fields as you progress, making spatial planning the stated source of increasing challenge.\n\nThe supplied text does not explain whether a swipe carries the cube until an obstacle, whether previously colored cells can be crossed freely, or which hazards exist. Those distinctions are important in a movement puzzle and should not be supplied from a superficially similar game.\n\nThe official image shows a purple trail beneath a partly colored pink grid. A face-like pattern supplies the picture, while the cube’s route supplies the action; the image does not explain every obstacle.\n\n## A maze label without a full rule diagram\n\nThe subtitle suggests navigating a constrained layout, but the description centers on filling cells. It does not establish a separate exit tile, a shortest-path scoring rule, or a requirement to avoid revisiting any square. The most reliable expectation is coloring the field through controlled cube movement.\n\nThis is a plausible choice for someone who likes seeing progress accumulate directly on the board. It is less suitable if the main attraction is designing an original image or solving written clues. The color is the visible trace of the route, not evidence of unrestricted drawing.\n\n## Pace and costs remain open\n\nThe developer describes a relaxed pace, but does not explicitly document timer or mistake rules. Its claim of an infinite number of levels should be treated as promotional scope rather than a verified count or a guarantee of endless unique layouts.\n\nThe US listing offers a free download. Advertising, purchases, offline access, and retry conditions are not established. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to tell whether players find later boards repetitive or the swipes comfortable.\n\nConsider Color Fill 3D for the coverage concept itself. Anyone choosing specifically for no timers, forgiving retries, or a particular sliding rule needs more information than the calming visual pitch provides.\n\n## Verdict\n\nColor Fill 3D has a clear movement-to-color premise, but a sparse rules description. It is worth considering for grid coverage, not on an assumption that every maze convention or no-pressure feature is included.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the main objective?\n\nThe listing says to guide the cube across the field and fill every cell with color.\n\n### How do you move the cube?\n\nSwipe controls are advertised; the exact stopping rules are not specified.\n\n### Is there a maze exit?\n\nThe description does not establish a separate exit objective. Its stated goal is full-cell coverage.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/color-fill-3d-maze-game/id1473024868?uo=4)."
