    "bodyMarkdown": "# Collect Cubes - ASMR Puzzle: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2025-04-12–2026-10-11 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.19.1, 4.19.2, 4.19.3, 4.19.4. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nCollect Cubes - ASMR Puzzle is a cleanup game with destruction at the start: move a scoop through cube-built structures, gather the loose pieces, and deliver them to the core.\n\n## Collection rather than rebuilding\n\nThe listing describes knocking over 3D buildings and bringing every piece back. That gives the action a loop with a destination, not just freeform demolition. The text does not establish exact steering gestures, scoop capacities, or universal failure conditions.\n\nIts ASMR positioning describes an intended sensory appeal. It does not prove a particular sound system, and some reviewers specifically mention missing audio. Actual haptics and sound quality have not been tested here.\n\n## The cleanup can satisfy despite disappointment\n\nReviewers describe relaxing or satisfying collection, with one also enjoying a decorative side activity. Those accounts explain why a very simple loop can remain appealing without needing to claim that everyone finds it challenging.\n\nA more distinctive complaint is the mismatch between promotions and actual play. Several reviewers expected to draw a scoop or change its size and say those controls were not present. The written listing does not promise a scoop-drawing tool, so it should not be advertised in this article as an established feature.\n\nThe sample spans April 2025 to October 2026. It is historical feedback, not a current-week survey. Some players report repeated shapes after many levels or ask for new content, which is relevant to readers seeking extended variety rather than a short-lived cleanup toy.\n\n## Cosmetics and interruptions\n\nSeveral reviewers cannot find how to change skins, including one who says they bought them. That is evidence of a discoverability or access concern, not proof that no cosmetic menu exists. The description offers no menu instructions to resolve it.\n\nAd experiences vary from frequent interruptions to a report of ads only after many levels and another describing none. A player’s five-star rating explicitly accompanies negative text intended to gain visibility; treating stars alone as sentiment would miss that criticism.\n\nThe sample also includes timed-level complaints and crash reports. Neither the calming pitch nor the sparse listing supports a universal no-timer or stability claim.\n\nThe US download is free. Current ad-removal terms, offline access, and cosmetic pricing remain unverified. Choose the actual scooping loop, not a promotional customization feature that the evidence does not establish.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCollect Cubes may suit readers who find gathering scattered pieces satisfying. The strongest cautions concern promotion expectations, cosmetic discoverability, and repeated layouts, with older reports that need current verification.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you draw your own scoop?\n\nThe listing does not establish that feature, and several reviewers complain that it was absent despite promotional expectations.\n\n### Is every level untimed?\n\nSome sampled reviewers describe timed levels, so a universal no-timer claim is not supported.\n\n### Where are the skins?\n\nReviewers report difficulty finding or changing them. The supplied description does not explain the menu location.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/collect-cubes-asmr-puzzle/id1465222322?uo=4)."
