    "bodyMarkdown": "# Color Maze - Puzzle Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nColor Maze - Puzzle Game asks you to think about which route should happen first. The advertised objective is drawing every path without a collision, while keeping your hearts intact.\n\n## A route can obstruct another route\n\nThe description frames the task around path order: which route blocks another, and which should be selected first. That makes the puzzle a sequencing problem as much as a question of tracing a line.\n\nTap controls select or draw the paths. The text does not specify whether every route is predefined, whether you freely choose its shape, or exactly how a collision is judged. Those details should not be imported from a familiar maze app. A title containing maze does not automatically establish a single character seeking an exit.\n\nThe useful premise is that one action changes the opportunities for later actions. Completing every individual route requires considering the collection as a whole, not simply finishing whichever one looks easiest in isolation.\n\nThe official image arranges colored paths into a chameleon silhouette on a black field. That gives the route puzzle a picture-shaped presentation without documenting all legal moves.\n\n## No advertised timer, but accuracy still matters\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises no time limits. It also encourages finishing tasks without losing a heart. Those statements describe different constraints: you may have time to think, while still facing consequences for a poor choice.\n\nThe exact heart budget and recovery process are not documented. Readers who like testing routes through trial and error should not assume unlimited harmless mistakes. Those who prefer studying dependencies before tapping may find the stated design more naturally relevant.\n\n## Hints and content claims\n\nThousands of handcrafted levels, progressive difficulty, and hints are advertised. The claim that the catalog avoids repetitive generation is the developer’s statement, not an independent check of every board. Likewise, the promise of gentle guidance does not explain whether hints consume currency or reveal one move versus a whole solution.\n\nThe minimalist presentation is intended to reduce distraction. That is not evidence of readable route markers or suitable contrast on every screen. The US download is free, but ad frequency, purchases, and offline access are not established.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample. Consider Color Maze for the interaction between routes and their order, rather than accepting general claims about calm play or measured five-minute sessions.\n\n## Verdict\n\nColor Maze offers an advertised untimed sequencing challenge with a heart-based accuracy goal. It is relevant to route-planning fans, but the exact drawing freedom and mistake rules remain important unanswered questions.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the objective in Color Maze?\n\nThe listing says to draw all paths without collisions and plan their order to avoid blocking one another.\n\n### Is it timed?\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises no time limits.\n\n### Can a mistake cost a heart?\n\nThe listing refers to completing tasks without losing hearts, but does not specify the full penalty or recovery system.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/color-maze-puzzle-game/id6755216191?uo=4)."
