    "bodyMarkdown": "# 2248 - Number Puzzle Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\n2248 - Number Puzzle Game asks you to trace matching numbers, including diagonally, and turn a chain into a higher value. It is not described as sliding an entire board in one direction.\n\n## Eight directions widen the search\n\nThe rules allow connecting equal-numbered blocks horizontally, vertically, and diagonally. That gives a tile more potential neighbors than a four-direction-only system, so a promising connection may bend around the board rather than form a straight line.\n\nMerging several matching numbers produces a higher value, and the stated goal is to keep reaching larger numbers. The description does not provide the exact arithmetic for chains of every length, so a precise scoring formula cannot be supplied here.\n\nNor does the title establish a fixed victory target of 2248. The wording emphasizes the highest possible number rather than ending at one named tile. Readers familiar with another numerical merging game should not assume identical movement, spawning, or game-over rules.\n\nThe official number grid highlights a bent connection of 2 tiles. That illustrates chain selection across neighboring cells, rather than moving every numbered tile with a single directional swipe.\n\n## No clock, but still choices\n\nThe listing explicitly advertises no time limit. That supports an expectation of considering possible chains before committing, not proof of unlimited undo or a board that can never become blocked. The complete failure conditions are not documented.\n\nA natural decision is whether to take a visible connection or search for a larger group. That follows from the advertised chain-merging concept, but the evidence does not establish that longer is always strategically superior. Without a full rule set, advice about guaranteed best moves would be speculative.\n\nGlobal leaderboards provide a competitive frame, and automatic saving is advertised for preserving a run. Saving has not been recovery-tested, and the description does not explain account linking or cross-device transfer.\n\n## Choose it for matching, not mathematics lessons\n\nThe numbers represent values to combine; the main stated task is spotting and linking matches. Claims about improving memory or reflexes remain marketing, not measured educational outcomes. Someone seeking arithmetic exercises with written calculations may want a different format.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, purchases, ad removal, and offline support are not established. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support conclusions about difficulty balance or long-run variety.\n\nConsider 2248 if diagonal number chains and open-ended value chasing sound appealing. The sparse description leaves important details about board renewal and mistakes unresolved, so genre familiarity is not a substitute for those rules.\n\n## Verdict\n\n2248 offers a clearly described eight-direction matching hook and an untimed score pursuit. It is a conditional fit for number-linking fans rather than a documented replica of any other merge game.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you connect diagonally?\n\nYes. The listing explicitly allows all eight neighboring directions.\n\n### Does the whole board slide?\n\nThe description says to slide through matching numbers to merge them; it does not describe whole-board sliding.\n\n### Is 2248 timed?\n\nNo time limits are advertised. Other loss conditions are not fully documented.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/2248-number-puzzle-game/id1487607878?uo=4)."
