    "bodyMarkdown": "# Cut the Rope: Physics Puzzle: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nCut the Rope: Physics Puzzle gives each cut a destination: guide a piece of candy into Om Nom’s mouth. The challenge comes from changing the candy’s path while also collecting stars.\n\n## A cut changes what happens next\n\nRopes support or constrain the candy, and removing one lets the physical setup evolve. The listed tools include bubbles, air cushions, valves, and bees, so the activity is not limited to choosing a single rope and watching a drop.\n\nThe description advertises 425 levels across 17 themed boxes. Several boxes are tied to distinct mechanics: Toy Box has bouncy platforms, Magic Box uses teleporting hats, Steam Box introduces valves and steam, and Cosmic Box includes gravity switches.\n\nThose examples make the progression more concrete than a general claim of increasing difficulty. A solution involving orientation and falling can become a different problem when transportation or gravity changes. Exact timing windows and control sensitivity have not been tested here.\n\nThe official image shows candy held by several ropes, stars along possible routes, and Om Nom below. It visually links the cutting action to the delivery and collection goals.\n\n## Feeding and collecting are related goals\n\nCandy delivery is the central objective, while stars and coins are also listed for unlocking boxes and rewards. That gives readers a reason to care about the route, not merely the final destination. The evidence does not establish the full unlock thresholds or whether every star is mandatory for every stage.\n\nSuperpowers are advertised as another intervention. Their presence should not be expanded into a claim of unlimited help or a complete solution system, because the description does not explain availability or cost.\n\nOm Nom Stories cartoons add non-puzzle material around the character. Readers seeking a physics collection may appreciate knowing that entertainment extras are part of the app, without assuming they are required for progress.\n\n## Offline claim and purchases\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises offline play. This is a listed feature, not a connectivity test of every box or entitlement. The US download is free, with real-money purchases including randomized items disclosed.\n\nThe listing markets the game to all ages, but that phrase does not remove the need to review purchase controls. Advertising frequency and current item prices are not established. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support claims about current monetization pressure or device stability.\n\nConsider Cut the Rope for causal experimentation with a candy route and a changing gadget set. It is less suitable if you want static clues with no moving elements or timing considerations.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCut the Rope’s description offers unusually concrete mechanical variety: different boxes change how candy travels. Its physics premise is the reason to consider it, while purchases and current access terms deserve separate attention.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the main objective?\n\nGuide candy to Om Nom by cutting ropes and using the available gadgets.\n\n### How many levels are advertised?\n\nThe listing states 425 levels across 17 themed boxes; that count has not been independently audited.\n\n### Are there purchases?\n\nYes. The description discloses real-money purchases, including randomized items.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cut-the-rope-physics-puzzle/id1024506959?uo=4)."
