    "bodyMarkdown": "# Fishdom: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-10-17–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 10.1.6, 10.2.0. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nFishdom offers a persistent reason to clear match-3 boards: build and decorate aquariums for animated fish. The sample’s central tension is affection for those tanks versus frustration with the work needed to expand them.\n\n## Matching funds an underwater home\n\nThe listing connects winning levels to new aquariums and decorations. Boosters support the match-3 boards, while expeditions, seasonal keys, competitions, and teams provide additional activities and rewards.\n\nOne reviewer likes the cute designs and reports playing offline. Another particularly enjoys expeditions and fish communicating their decoration preferences, even while describing missing resources and achievement-sync issues. These details explain attachment to the surrounding world rather than merely praising matching in the abstract.\n\nThe core described game is match-3. Several reviewers complain that promotional gameplay looked different, so a prospective player should use that stated mechanic as the expectation rather than assuming a rescue or arithmetic game is the main activity.\n\n## Difficulty complaints are about lost momentum\n\nMultiple accounts describe being stuck on a board for days, exhausting boosters, or feeling that diamonds are needed to continue. Some allege deliberately unfair sequences. The difficulty frustration is real as a report, but manipulation is not independently demonstrated.\n\nThere are also positive counterpoints: a reviewer enjoys winning levels and earning currencies to buy fish and decorations. The sample therefore does not justify saying free progress is impossible for everyone.\n\nThe aquarium economy is a separate concern. A long-term player says later decorations require much larger coin totals, making preferred designs harder to pursue. Another misses older interactive map quests and dislikes replacement cards. These are reasons the meta game can lose appeal even when the basic boards remain recognizable.\n\n## Ads, purchases, and connectivity\n\nTwo reviewers explicitly praise an absence of ads. That is useful firsthand testimony, but it does not independently establish a universal current ad policy. The listing discloses purchases, including randomized items; purchase availability alone says nothing about ads.\n\nThe developer advertises play without internet while reserving competitions and additional features for a connection. A reviewer’s offline account supports that distinction without verifying every mode.\n\nThe US download is free. Current diamond bundles and event offers are not established here. There are also black-screen and loading complaints, including after updates, but no device-level testing. Consider Fishdom for the aquarium investment only if repeated match-3 attempts are an activity you want in their own right.\n\n## Verdict\n\nFishdom’s fish and decoration systems have specific supporters, but progression and reward changes dominate the detailed criticism. It is a more natural fit for committed match-3 players than for someone who only wants to design a tank.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is Fishdom’s main puzzle type?\n\nThe listing identifies match-3 levels as the core activity used to develop aquariums.\n\n### Can Fishdom work offline?\n\nThe developer says basic play does not require internet, while competitions and additional features do. One reviewer also reports offline play.\n\n### Does Fishdom have purchases?\n\nYes. The description discloses real-money purchases, including randomized items.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/fishdom/id664575829?uo=4)."
