    "bodyMarkdown": "# Woodoku - Wood Block Puzzles: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-10-04–2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.15.02, 4.15.03. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWoodoku - Wood Block Puzzles turns a 9×9 board into a space-management problem. Drag wooden pieces into place, complete a row, column, or square, and preserve enough room for what follows.\n\n## Sudoku geometry without entering numbers\n\nThe grid creates several overlapping clearing opportunities. A useful placement may support a horizontal line while also contributing to a square, so surviving is not just a matter of fitting the current shape into the first open gap.\n\nThe listing’s objective is beating your high score before space runs out. Its occasional use of merge language should not be mistaken for a number-doubling system: the described rules are placing shapes and clearing filled regions.\n\nDaily puzzles and recurring new boards add alternatives to a score run. One reviewer specifically enjoys challenge and journey modes; another likes the need to think ahead. Those are concrete endorsements of the planning and content structure, not evidence of an IQ increase.\n\n## Visual simplicity is an active preference\n\nA detailed reviewer values the earlier simple presentation but dislikes pieces lighting up on every placement. Another independently asks for a way to disable colorful flashes. These accounts are important because the new effects affect the same quiet visual character that attracted them.\n\nThey do not establish a medical safety finding or prove no relevant setting exists in the current release. One account says vibration and points could be disabled, but lighting could not be found. That is a reported settings limitation rather than a complete interface audit.\n\n## Advertising can interfere with placement\n\nAd complaints include long closing flows and interruptions during a puzzle. One player says an interruption forces a held piece into an unwanted location. Other reviews raise concerns about misleading products advertised between rounds. Those product allegations are not independently verified endorsements or fraud findings.\n\nThere are positive counterpoints, including a reviewer who feels no pressure to buy. That does not mean the app is ad-free. Cash-reward references in some reviews also do not establish a reliable native payout system.\n\nThe developer advertises offline play, and one reviewer reports using it without internet. Separately, daily completion tracking and slowdown complaints appear in the sample. The US download is free; current ad-removal options and pricing are not established.\n\nConsider Woodoku if spatial planning is the attraction. Readers choosing it specifically for low visual stimulation or uninterrupted placement should take the detailed interface and ad reports seriously.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWoodoku’s grid-planning appeal is supported by specific players, but presentation changes are not a minor issue for everyone. It is a conditional choice for quiet-play seekers rather than an assumed distraction-free classic.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Woodoku a number Sudoku game?\n\nNo. The listed rules use shaped blocks and clear completed rows, columns, or squares.\n\n### Can you disable the flashing effects?\n\nSome reviewers say they could not find a setting for new lighting effects. The current settings have not been independently checked.\n\n### Can Woodoku be played offline?\n\nOffline play is advertised and supported by one sampled account, without testing every feature here.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/woodoku-wood-block-puzzles/id1496354836?uo=4)."
