    "bodyMarkdown": "# Solitaire by Staple Games: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2025-12-09–2026-11-04 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.9, 4.00. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nSolitaire by Staple Games sells restraint: familiar Klondike, readable cards, and no decorative world to maintain. Reviewers who like it tend to describe precisely that straightforward approach.\n\n## A recognizable card-table objective\n\nMove all 52 cards to four suit foundations in order from Ace through King. On the seven tableau columns, build descending sequences with alternating colors; empty columns accept Kings. The listing explicitly allows moving already ordered groups of cards.\n\nDraw 1 and Draw 3 change how the stock is dealt. Tap-to-play, unlimited undo, an Auto Helper, and intelligent auto-complete are advertised alongside statistics, left- or right-handed layouts, and dark mode.\n\nThose features offer different kinds of convenience. Undo reverses a decision, while a legal automatic move is not necessarily proof of an optimal strategy. Auto-complete is also different from a promise that every deal is winnable.\n\n## The simple presentation has real supporters\n\nOne beginner describes a clean, simple first solitaire app. Another says the app taught them about solitaire, and a later reviewer calls it simple with no distractions. That supports the appeal of the card-focused presentation without claiming every user encounters no interruptions.\n\nA request for more board and card-back choices shows the other side of restraint: fewer distractions can also mean less personalization. The sample does not establish the current complete customization menu.\n\n## Ad reports directly conflict\n\nSome reviewers report no ads or say ads are unobtrusive. Others describe long, hard-to-close commercials before games or after hands. The evidence spans December 2025 through November 2026 across versions 3.9 and 4.00; it does not explain why experiences differ.\n\nOne complaint describes inappropriate imagery, while another objects to flashing ad content. These are reported exposure concerns, not independently verified judgments about legality or the full ad inventory. No universal ad frequency can be responsibly assigned.\n\n## Offline reliability and deal fairness remain unsettled\n\nSeveral players report crashes when connectivity is weak or absent. A different account claims airplane mode changes win rates, but that is not evidence that the deal generator adjusts odds based on ad access. Likewise, reports of missing cards or lower win rates do not independently establish a defective deck.\n\nThe US download is free. Current ad-removal terms and purchase prices are not established, and offline support has not been tested. Choose the app for its documented Klondike tools, while recognizing that a minimalist table does not guarantee a consistently quiet commercial experience.\n\n## Verdict\n\nStaple Solitaire has a clear classic-card identity and specific support for its clean look. Mixed ad experiences and connectivity-related crash reports make it a conditional fit for readers whose main priority is distraction-free reliability.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Which solitaire rules does it use?\n\nThe listing explicitly documents Klondike, including alternating-color tableau sequences and Ace-to-King suit foundations.\n\n### Are Draw 1 and Draw 3 included?\n\nYes. Both stock-dealing modes are advertised.\n\n### Is it ad-free?\n\nThe sample contains both no-ad accounts and substantial ad complaints. Current removal options and universal behavior are not established.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/solitaire-by-staple-games/id1454538656?uo=4)."
