    "bodyMarkdown": "# Paint by Number Coloring Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nPaint by Number Coloring Game offers guided art completion rather than a blank canvas. Choose a supplied image, follow its numbers, and fill the assigned areas to reveal the finished picture.\n\n## The picture is the main choice\n\nThe listing advertises more than 30,000 artworks, including fantasy, nature, animals, pastoral scenery, mandalas, and geometric patterns. That range provides concrete subject categories, even though catalog size and the quality of individual works have not been independently audited.\n\nChoosing a subject is different from choosing every color. The numbered system supplies the palette structure, making the activity relevant to someone who enjoys completing details without making open-ended design decisions. It is not described as a general-purpose drawing or painting editor.\n\nThe developer emphasizes artists and varied cultural styles. That remains a claim about the catalog’s production and breadth, not verified authorship for each image. The supplied description does not identify individual artists or license details.\n\nThe official rose artwork shows unfilled numbered regions among colored petals, with numbered swatches below. The supplied design remains recognizable throughout completion.\n\n## Stories and collection goals\n\nPicture Book Story combines coloring with narrative material. Collection events add picture-search tasks and unlockable works, providing goals beyond finishing whichever image you opened first.\n\nThese are meaningful additions for someone who wants a sequence or collection to pursue. A reader looking only for a quiet image picker may care less about event participation. The description does not establish whether events are compulsory, how long they last, or which works remain available afterward.\n\nThe advertised touch-to-color process also does not establish the size of the smallest regions, zoom controls, or accessible contrast. General claims of user-friendliness and exceptional stability are not substitutes for those details or for actual testing.\n\n## Premium has explicit listing terms\n\nPremium is advertised as unlocking all pictures and removing ads and watermarks. The supplied description quotes a weekly plan at $4.99 after a three-day trial, a monthly plan at $9.99, and a yearly plan at $69.99. These are quoted listing terms, not verified checkout offers or a promise that every account receives a trial.\n\nThe text describes automatic renewal and a cancellation cutoff at least 24 hours before the period ends. Review the actual subscription screen before accepting any offer. The US download itself is free.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample. Ad frequency outside Premium, offline support, and the practicality of the coloring interface remain unverified. Consider the artwork categories and story format on their stated merits rather than assuming a clinically calming effect.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPaint by Number is relevant to readers who want guided pictures with stories and collection goals. Premium changes access and presentation, so the subscription decision should remain separate from the appeal of numbered coloring.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does Paint by Number include stories?\n\nYes. Picture Book Story is explicitly advertised alongside ordinary coloring and collection events.\n\n### What does Premium remove?\n\nThe listing says Premium removes ads and watermarks and unlocks all pictures. Actual offer terms need checking at checkout.\n\n### Is the weekly plan a one-time payment?\n\nNo. The supplied description identifies auto-renewing subscriptions, including a weekly plan.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/paint-by-number-coloring-game/id1420058690?uo=4)."
