    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Link - Word Puzzle Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nWord Link - Word Puzzle Game offers an unusual convenience for a level-based word app: the listing explicitly says you can return to earlier puzzles. A difficult board does not have to vanish after completion.\n\n## Connect letters, then keep looking\n\nThe core action is swiping to connect letters into words. Bonus words bring additional rewards, making the puzzle more than a search for the first obvious answer. The description does not document every dictionary rule or the exact arrangement of its letter pool.\n\nThat distinction matters when choosing a word game. Word Link is not described as a traditional newspaper crossword with authored clues, nor as a large word-search matrix where a complete list is supplied. Its stated hook is forming words through connected letters.\n\nThe developer advertises more than 9,000 nonrepeating levels. That is a catalog claim, not an audited guarantee of unique answers or distinct layouts. A level count alone also does not establish how difficulty grows.\n\nAn official image also promotes another activity: identifying labeled words in a room illustration. It suggests variety beyond the core letter-linking description without establishing how frequently that mode appears.\n\n## Replay changes how a puzzle can be shared\n\nReturning to previous levels lets you revisit a tricky answer or show the same board to someone else. The listing specifically suggests sharing difficult levels with friends, but does not establish real-time multiplayer, co-op solving, or a separate online sharing service.\n\nNo time limit is advertised. That makes unhurried vocabulary search part of the stated proposition, although no timer does not mean every other limitation is absent. The description does not fully explain mistake handling or repeated guesses.\n\nFree hints are also promised, but their availability conditions and replenishment rules are not described. It would be unsafe to translate that into unlimited hints on demand or a guarantee that no ad is required. Daily rewards provide another return incentive without establishing their exact contents.\n\n## A compact feature pitch with commercial gaps\n\nClassic graphics and light music frame the activity. Those are attributed presentation claims rather than measured sound quality or proof of readable text. Brain-training language likewise does not establish educational gains.\n\nOffline play without Wi-Fi is explicitly advertised, while the US download is free. Actual connectivity behavior, ad frequency, purchases, and ad-removal terms are not established. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support claims about dictionary fairness or interruptions.\n\nConsider Word Link if untimed word formation and revisiting earlier levels matter to you. It is less compelling as a documented choice for clue-based crosswords or a fully specified free-hint system.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWord Link’s most useful differentiators are its advertised lack of a timer and access to earlier levels. The basic letter-linking appeal is clear, while hint conditions and commercial behavior remain open.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you revisit completed levels?\n\nYes. The listing explicitly says previous levels can be opened again.\n\n### Is Word Link timed?\n\nThe developer advertises no time limit.\n\n### Are hints unlimited?\n\nFree hints are advertised, but the description does not establish unlimited use or explain their conditions.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/word-link-word-puzzle-game/id1347684152?uo=4)."
