    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong!!: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nMahjong!! describes the solitaire form of mahjong: remove matching pairs from a layout until no tiles remain. It is a one-player visual matching puzzle, not the multiplayer hand-building game.\n\n## Matching is necessary, but availability matters\n\nThe listing says tiles with the same picture disappear as a pair. Only free tiles that are not covered can be removed, so seeing two matching symbols does not automatically make them a legal move.\n\nThat is the source of a planning question: which available pair opens access to tiles that are currently blocked? Clearing the first visible match may change later opportunities. This follows from the stated layered-removal premise, not from a claim that a particular layout has been solved here.\n\nThe description does not define every aspect of a free tile, including the exact side-access rule. It also does not list board shapes or guarantee that every deal is winnable. Readers familiar with traditional mahjong solitaire should distinguish familiar conventions from rules actually documented for this app.\n\nThe official board shows tiles in stacked rows, with some symbols exposed and others partly covered. That makes availability, not just symbol recognition, visible as part of the matching problem.\n\n## Three tools serve different needs\n\nHint, undo, and shuffle are advertised. A hint can help locate a move, undo provides a way to revisit a decision, and shuffling changes the board arrangement. The description does not explain usage limits, costs, or whether shuffling preserves a guaranteed solution.\n\nThose tools make the app relevant to readers who want assistance rather than a strict no-reversal challenge. Their presence does not prove that every stalled game can be rescued for free.\n\n## Presentation choices are unusually explicit\n\nThe listing names 20 backgrounds and nine tile sets, plus a shadow option and large tiles. That gives some control over the table’s appearance rather than merely offering a single decorative theme.\n\nLarge tiles may be worth investigating for visibility, but they do not independently establish readable symbols on every phone, accessible contrast, or screen-reader support. The actual interface has not been tested here.\n\nAutomatic saving on exit or an incoming call is advertised. The developer also says the app does not interfere with a music player, a useful distinction for readers who prefer their own soundtrack. Neither background audio nor save recovery has been independently checked.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, purchases, offline support, and current removal offers are not established. No customer review bodies are available in this sample, so stability and commercial quietness should not be inferred from the classic presentation.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong!! is a focused concept match for solo pair-removal fans who want appearance options and standard assistance tools. The sparse listing leaves access conditions and detailed tile-availability rules unresolved.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Mahjong!! a multiplayer mahjong game?\n\nNo. The description explicitly identifies it as a matching game for one player.\n\n### Does it include undo and shuffle?\n\nYes. Hint, undo, and shuffle are all advertised, without detailed limits or costs.\n\n### Can you keep your own music playing?\n\nThe developer says it does not interfere with the music player. That behavior has not been independently tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong/id347878114?uo=4)."
