    "bodyMarkdown": "# Park Master: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2024-01-27–2026-10-21 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.7.10, 2.7.9, 2.8.0, 2.9.8, 3.0.2, 3.0.3, 3.0.4, 3.0.6, 3.2.1, 3.2.5, 3.2.6, 3.2.7. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nPark Master gives you a line to draw rather than a steering wheel. Trace a route with a finger, send a car toward its parking space, and avoid collisions with other vehicles.\n\n## Your route is the control\n\nThe listing describes crowded, awkward parking spaces and warns that cars can hit one another. That makes the shape and interaction of routes central to the premise. It does not establish precise departure timing, whether cars start simultaneously, or every obstacle type.\n\nA reviewer specifically likes drawing paths and the way route colors match the cars. Another enjoys the mental challenge enough to tolerate the advertising. Those are concrete points of appeal beyond generic praise for a free car game.\n\nThe sample covers January 2024 through October 2026 across many versions. It is historical evidence, not a snapshot of current-week player sentiment. Some entries are too brief or unrelated to support a detailed conclusion, and allegations of fake reviewers are not treated as verified facts.\n\n## Difficulty is not uniformly unwelcome\n\nSeveral reviewers say stages can become difficult while still describing the game as fun. Another says level 62 cannot be passed, but that isolated account does not prove an unsolvable board across releases.\n\nA longer complaint describes watching a video for a hint and then receiving a route that still hits a box. That is a reported hint failure, not a tested walkthrough. It is relevant because paid attention to an ad did not deliver usable assistance for that player.\n\nOne critical reviewer wants more to do and describes collecting keys to unlock cars. The written listing does not document that progression system in detail, so no current unlock thresholds are assumed.\n\n## Advertising affects family suitability\n\nRepeated ad complaints span the sample, including interruptions after parking and a parent who wanted a paid removal option for a toddler. Another reviewer explicitly says the gameplay makes ads worthwhile. That disagreement concerns tolerance, not evidence that one group saw no ads.\n\nSome reviewers also report progress resetting or unlocked vehicles disappearing. These are individual reports across older versions, not a verified current save-system diagnosis.\n\nThe US download is free. Current ad-removal availability, purchase terms, and offline support are not established. The listing mentions an Indonesian age classification, but that is not a substitute for verifying the US age rating or reviewing the actual ad environment before child use.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPark Master has a concrete draw-the-route appeal, supported by comments about colored paths and challenge. Its historical ad and progress-loss reports make current commercial and save behavior important checks rather than settled benefits.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How do you control cars in Park Master?\n\nThe listing says to draw a line with a finger to guide each car toward parking.\n\n### Is level 62 impossible?\n\nOne sampled reviewer says they could not pass it. That does not establish a universal unsolvable level across versions.\n\n### Can you buy ad removal?\n\nAn older reviewer requests that option. Current availability and pricing are not established by this evidence.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/park-master/id1481293953?uo=4)."
