    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block Puzzle ·: A High-Score Game With a Sparse Rulebook\n\nBlock Puzzle · has a modestly defined purpose: place shapes, complete lines, and try to improve your score. Its short listing is more useful for identifying that basic appeal than for choosing among every variation of the block-puzzle formula. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Keep the grid usable\n\nThe developer describes placing blocks on a grid and clearing a completed line across it. The goal is to clear as many lines as possible and beat a personal high score. The wooden presentation is part of the marketing, while the underlying decision is spatial.\n\nEach placement uses some empty cells and potentially contributes to a future clear. Those are competing needs. A placement that helps finish a line can be useful, but only if the remaining grid can still accommodate the next shapes.\n\nA helpful way to assess the premise is to imagine leaving a narrow, awkward gap beside a nearly completed line. That gap may be harder to use than a larger open area elsewhere. This is a general implication of fitting shapes, not evidence about the actual piece sequence or a proven optimal strategy.\n\n## Do not assume a particular block-game variant\n\nThe supplied text does not specify grid dimensions, whether blocks rotate, or whether rows and columns both clear. It also does not establish a falling-piece system, a move limit, or a countdown. Those are meaningful differences between games that otherwise share the same genre label.\n\nReferences to other wooden or hexagonal puzzles are promotional comparisons, not confirmation that this game uses a hexagon board. Its actual instructions describe blocks on a grid. Likewise, playing daily to improve a score does not automatically establish daily challenge levels or a streak reward system.\n\nThat limited rulebook is important for fit. If you mainly want an approachable shape-placement pastime, the concept is easy to understand. If a particular rotation rule or board size determines your enjoyment, this evidence is not enough to settle the choice.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you about cost\n\nThe US metadata records a free download. No usable review bodies are included, and the description does not document advertisements, purchases, or a removal product. It would be inappropriate to infer player enthusiasm from the rating aggregate or promise quiet sessions from the wooden theme.\n\nClaims about daily brain training are also not evidence of measurable benefits beyond practicing this kind of puzzle.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlock Puzzle · is most relevant to players seeking a simple score-chasing placement game. It is not a strong evidence-based choice for anyone who needs a fully specified variant before downloading. The line-clearing premise is clear; the rules that distinguish it from its neighbors remain thinly documented.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the main goal?\nPlace blocks, clear completed lines, and improve your high score.\n\n### Is the grid a confirmed size?\nNo. The supplied description does not give its dimensions.\n\n### Can pieces rotate?\nThat rule is not established in the listing evidence.\n\n### Is this a falling-block action game?\nThe text describes placement, not a verified continuous falling-piece system.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-puzzle/id1356820483?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
