    "bodyMarkdown": "# Match 3D: Pairing Objects, Not Collecting Triples\n\nMatch 3D's name can encourage the wrong assumption. The 3D describes the objects; the instructions describe matching pairs, not collecting three of a kind. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Two matching objects go to the circle\n\nThe developer says to pick one object, find its counterpart, and move both into the circle in the middle of the screen. Repeat until all objects are cleared, then progress to a level with more things to pair.\n\nAnimals, food, school supplies, household items, and emojis are among the advertised categories. The attraction is recognizing a matching object within a mixed field, not swapping symbols on a fixed match-3 grid.\n\nClearing a pair also changes what remains to inspect. As objects disappear, fewer candidates compete for attention. That gives the same scene a different character near the end: the early problem is sorting visual possibilities, while the last few matches may be easier to isolate. This follows from the clearing objective, not a judgment of actual object visibility.\n\n## Pause is more relevant than a vague relaxation promise\n\nA pause function and automatic saving are specifically advertised. For someone who may need to stop midway through a crowded screen, those are practical features worth checking. They potentially matter more than the description's broad claim that anyone can play.\n\nThe evidence does not establish whether pause freezes every mode or how progress behaves after an app interruption. The supplied instructions also do not explicitly document a timer, selection penalties, or all object-manipulation rules. It would be inaccurate to add those details from another 3D matching game.\n\nThere are no customer-review bodies for this app in the supplied collection. Consequently, the interface's responsiveness, later difficulty, and reliability of the resume system remain unassessed. The developer's memory-improvement claims are promotional, not validated outcomes.\n\n## Advertising and payment\n\nThe metadata lists a free download. The description does not establish ad placement, assistance prices, or an ad-removal option. Pairing two objects successfully says nothing about how often the larger session may be interrupted.\n\nLikewise, the object categories are described by the developer; no screenshots were visually inspected for an independent assessment of their size, detail, or contrast.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMatch 3D is a sensible prospect for players who enjoy visual recognition and gradually clearing a mixed collection. It is less suited to anyone specifically looking for triple matching or a traditional gem-swapping game. The matching circle gives the loop a clear destination, while the pause and save claims deserve practical verification.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you match two objects or three?\nThe instructions explicitly describe two matching objects.\n\n### Where do matched objects go?\nThey are moved into a circle in the middle of the screen.\n\n### Can you pause?\nThe developer advertises a pause function and auto-save; neither was playtested here.\n\n### Does the game prove improved memory?\nNo. The evidence provides a product claim, not a validated cognitive study.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/match-3d/id1502447854?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
