    "bodyMarkdown": "# Crossword Jam: Anagram Appeal, With Progress-Saving Concerns\n\nCrossword Jam offers the compact pleasure of making several words from a small letter set. The sample also shows why a word game needs more than good puzzles: interruptions and reliable progress saving can determine whether a daily routine survives. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Build words rather than answer written clues\n\nThe listing describes swiping between letters to discover words, with anagram puzzles using two to seven letters. Country-themed scenery, daily challenges, tournaments, and a reward spinner frame the progression.\n\nDespite the crossword name, the central input is forming words from supplied letters rather than solving traditional definition clues. A short letter set gives repeated opportunities to reconsider the same material: a combination that failed to appear at first may become recognizable once another answer is found.\n\nThe developer advertises offline play and Facebook-based progress synchronization. Those are listing capabilities, not independently checked guarantees across devices or every event.\n\n## Enjoyment persists alongside specific frustrations\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-20 through 2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.400.4, 1.402.0, 1.406.0; the listing records version 1.406.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers describe a rewarding challenge, easy-to-understand play, and relaxation. One says getting an answer right is particularly satisfying. Those comments support the core word loop, rather than a claim that the whole app works equally well for everyone.\n\nSeveral critical accounts concern completed puzzles appearing unfinished on the next visit. One long-time player describes losing daily progress repeatedly, while another says the game sends them back to earlier boards. These are user reports, not a measured persistence failure rate or proof that the current version always behaves this way.\n\nThe distinction between repetition and lost progress matters. A player may not object to revisiting a word set voluntarily, but can understandably dislike repeating it because completion was not retained.\n\n## Ads can obstruct more than the break between levels\n\nSome reviewers describe advertisements covering letters or appearing with unwanted sound. A purchaser says a remove-ads offer did not eliminate the interruptions they expected. Another player reports different ad behavior between a phone and tablet.\n\nThose accounts do not establish a universal frequency, current price, or exact entitlement attached to an offer. They do suggest checking whether removal applies across your devices and whether the playing area stays accessible.\n\nComplaints about competition outcomes are also present, but do not independently establish manipulated tournaments. The stronger, directly relevant caution is the reported disruption of ordinary word play.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCrossword Jam may suit anagram fans who like a travel-themed progression. It is a more cautious choice for someone whose main requirement is a dependable, interruption-free daily session. The sample supports the word challenge's appeal while raising concrete concerns about persistence and ad presentation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this a conventional crossword?\nIts listing emphasizes swiping letters to make words, not answering written clues.\n\n### Is offline play advertised?\nYes, though disconnected behavior was not independently tested.\n\n### Do repeated-puzzle reports prove a current bug?\nNo. They are individual accounts within the stated versions and dates.\n\n### Is ad removal verified to work everywhere?\nNo. The sample includes a dissatisfied purchaser and differing device experiences.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/crossword-jam-fun-word-search/id1269540854?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
