    "bodyMarkdown": "# ▻Sudoku: Classic Logic With a Score-Chasing Side\n\n▻Sudoku preserves the familiar nine-by-nine logic puzzle while adding scores and Runs. That creates two ways to approach it: quietly solve a grid, or treat the same deductions as part of a performance and streak challenge. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## A familiar board, optional assistance\n\nThe listing describes classic Sudoku with Easy, Medium, Hard, and Expert choices. Pencil notes, hints, undo, and auto-check can be used or disabled. Scores reflect speed, accuracy, and completed rows, columns, and boxes; Runs track consecutive completed puzzles.\n\nThose systems change the incentive without changing the basic digit-placement premise. A solver interested in careful reasoning may value notes and undo, while a score chaser may care about how quickly the same deductions are made. Speed-based scoring should not automatically be described as a countdown that ends the game.\n\n## What the sample says about the everyday experience\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-08 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 8.2.7, 8.2.8; the listing records version 8.2.8. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSeveral reviewers praise readable boards, straightforward controls, selectable difficulty, and a routine lasting years. One calls the interface intuitive while still finding it somewhat slow to respond. Another describes functional, legible software without glitches. Those favorable reports do not establish identical performance on every device.\n\nNot everyone wants the streak layer. A reviewer wishes the Run feature were absent, and another says a video-based attempt to preserve a Run failed to load. That illustrates the difference between enjoying Sudoku and enjoying the extra framework around it.\n\nThe description lists four named difficulties, while one reviewer mentions five. The listing is the clearer source for the documented choices; the account should not be used to invent an extra confirmed mode.\n\n## Ad-free subscription versus player access\n\nAn optional ad-free subscription is explicitly described in the US listing, with the actual price shown before purchase. Yet some reviewers say they cannot find an ad-removal option, while another mentions a paid alternative. That conflict may involve availability, navigation, or changing offers; this evidence cannot resolve it.\n\nOther comments describe full-screen interruptions during puzzles and difficulty returning from ads. These are player reports, not a measured ad schedule. A clean board interface does not necessarily mean a clean session around that board.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Sudoku is most appealing to players who want familiar logic with adjustable help and an optional score pursuit. It is less reassuring for someone who demands a quiet interface at all times. Readability draws specific praise, but the sample's interruption concerns deserve as much attention as the long-running affection.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does scoring mean a forced countdown?\nThe listing describes speed-sensitive scoring, not a confirmed failure countdown.\n\n### Can assistance be turned off?\nThe developer says tools such as notes, hints, undo, and auto-check can be disabled.\n\n### What are Runs?\nThey track consecutive puzzle completions, according to the description.\n\n### Is there an ad-free option?\nThe listing advertises a subscription, although some sampled players report difficulty finding removal.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/sudoku-classic-brain-game/id366247306?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
