    "bodyMarkdown": "# Jigsawscapes: Adjust the Puzzle Before You Start\n\nJigsawscapes offers several ways to change the task before placing the first piece. Its library is the headline, but difficulty selection, zoom, and an optional rotation challenge are the more useful details when deciding whether it fits your habits. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Choose an image, then choose the workload\n\nThe US listing describes selecting a picture, choosing the number of pieces, and dragging pieces into place. Custom backgrounds and zoom adjust the workspace. Rotate Mode adds orientation to the usual questions of shape and image matching.\n\nPiece count matters because it changes more than the amount of time a picture might take. Smaller fragments provide less image information per piece, and a fuller workspace can require more sorting. Rotation can add another reason a promising piece does not immediately fit.\n\nThose are design implications, not measurements of this app's difficulty. The supplied text does not specify all available counts or establish which controls are most comfortable on a phone versus a tablet.\n\n## A library with several reasons to return\n\nThe developer advertises more than 60,000 puzzles across over 20 categories and additional weekly content. Daily challenges, a completed-puzzle collection, leaderboards, and themed events create different reasons to choose another image.\n\nThe important fit question is whether you want those extra goals. A collection can preserve the satisfaction of finishing a picture; an event or leaderboard can shift attention toward a schedule or competition. The existence of those systems does not prove that participation is required.\n\nThe description's zero-missing-pieces promise is also narrower than a guarantee of flawless software. It refers to digital puzzle completeness, not independent evidence that saving, input, or downloads never fail. No customer-review bodies are available here to assess those practical issues.\n\n## Offline claims and free access\n\nThe listing advertises offline solving. It does not fully explain whether selected pictures must first be downloaded or which connected features remain unavailable. No airplane-mode session was performed, so offline access stays an attributed capability rather than a verified need tag.\n\nThe metadata records a free download, but the supplied description does not establish the complete content economy, ad frequency, or a removal offer. A large advertised catalog should not automatically be treated as unlimited free access to every item.\n\n## Verdict\n\nJigsawscapes looks strongest for players who want to tailor the size and handling of a picture puzzle. It is less clearly suited to anyone seeking a verified distraction-free digital jigsaw. The adjustable workspace and rotation option are concrete attractions; library quality and access conditions remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you choose the piece count?\nYes. Difficulty selection is described in terms of the number of pieces.\n\n### Is rotation required?\nRotate Mode is advertised as an additional challenge, not established as mandatory for all puzzles.\n\n### Can you zoom?\nYes. Zooming is explicitly included in the controls.\n\n### Is the whole library confirmed free offline?\nNo. The listing's broad claims do not establish every download and access condition.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/jigsawscapes-jigsaw-puzzles/id1589762792?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
