    "bodyMarkdown": "# Point Out: Color Escape Puzzle: Direction Is Something You Can Change\n\nPoint Out is not described as merely tapping arrows that already have a clear exit. Its listing includes rotating arrows and shifting tiles, suggesting a maze you actively reorganize rather than only peel apart. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Change the route before following it\n\nThe developer describes guiding the right arrow out of a maze through directional logic and spatial planning. Rotation and tile shifting are the stated interactions. The goal is escape, but the path may need to be changed before it becomes useful.\n\nThat distinction matters when comparing it with directional block-removal puzzles. In a fixed-arrow system, you look for the correct removal order. Here, the described ability to alter orientations means creating the route can be part of the solution.\n\nThe supplied text does not establish every move restriction or how many arrows must escape. It also does not explain whether all rotations are freely reversible. Those rules should not be invented from the title or from similar-looking games.\n\n## Several kinds of help, not one generic hint\n\nThe listing names Zoom, Hint, Magic Wand, Rubber, and Grid Scale. Zoom and Grid Scale address inspection and visibility; Hint suggests a move; the other tools are described as helping through difficult situations or erasing mistakes.\n\nThat range is potentially useful because a maze can be hard for different reasons. Not seeing a path clearly is not the same problem as understanding the next step. A larger view may help with one without solving the other.\n\nA dark mode is also advertised. Its actual contrast, text size, and effectiveness have not been inspected. The evidence supports saying the setting exists in the feature list, not certifying comfortable use for a particular person.\n\n## A brain-training pitch needs limits\n\nThe developer promotes thousands of handcrafted levels, offline-friendly play, and IQ improvement. No review bodies are available to assess level variety or player satisfaction. No outcome evidence supports treating the app as a validated intelligence-training tool.\n\nThe US metadata lists a free download. The supplied description does not establish ad frequency, tool prices, or removal terms. Offline-friendly is likewise not a complete guarantee that every feature works without a connection.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPoint Out is a plausible fit for players who enjoy reconfiguring a route instead of just recognizing an open one. It is less clearly suited to someone seeking a fully specified, pure arrow-removal puzzle. Rotation, shifting, and visibility tools are its best documented distinctions; the economy and actual puzzle quality remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can arrows rotate?\nYes. Rotation and tile shifting are explicitly described.\n\n### Is this definitely a fixed-direction removal game?\nNo. The listing suggests route reconfiguration as well as escape.\n\n### Does it advertise dark mode?\nYes, although the setting was not independently inspected.\n\n### Is IQ improvement established?\nNo. The supplied evidence contains marketing language, not validated training outcomes.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/point-out-color-escape-puzzle/id6754243057?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
