    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Stacks: Every Found Word Changes the Search\n\nWord Stacks gives word search a consequence: finding a word changes the arrangement you search next. The sample supports that satisfying loop, while showing how repeated vocabulary and surrounding promotions can wear on a long-term habit. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Read the clue, then reshape the letters\n\nThe developer describes words hidden in stacked letters, with a clue tying the answers together. Finding a word makes the stacks shift, so the field is not simply a static page to mark off.\n\nThat creates two connected tasks. You think about the category, then inspect how the remaining letters can support another answer. Removing a familiar word may make a less obvious one easier to see. It is a distinct rhythm from an anagram wheel or a conventional crossword.\n\nSpyglass, Light Bulb, and Shuffle tools are advertised, alongside bonus words, daily challenges, and unlockable themes. The evidence does not establish that a paid Shuffle is universally necessary to solve every board.\n\n## A good mechanism can outlast its vocabulary\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-08-02 through 2026-10-30 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.23.2, 1.24.0, 1.25.0, 1.26.0, 1.27.0, 1.28.0, 1.28.1; the listing records version 1.28.1. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPlayers describe approachable challenge, interesting daily words, attractive themes, and satisfaction when the pieces of a word finally become apparent. One reviewer specifically celebrates learning and examining letter patterns.\n\nThe reservations are more particular than a general dislike of word games. Several reviewers describe recurring words or category sets. Another objects to colloquial abbreviations, while a different account welcomes vocabulary variety. These preferences conflict: what feels accessible and recognizable to one solver can feel repetitive or irregular to another.\n\nA report of needing to buy a Shuffle reflects that player's experience, not a verified rule that progress is impossible without payment. It does identify a useful question for an impatient solver: will rearranging letters feel like a tool you enjoy, or an obstacle between you and the next answer?\n\n## Ads, rewards, and internal promotion differ\n\nSome reviewers accept videos as a way to keep playing for free; others report disruptive ads or rewards that do not arrive. One paid player distinguishes a bouncing internal promotion from ordinary external advertisements.\n\nThat distinction matters when evaluating removal. Eliminating third-party ads may not eliminate the game's own offers or animated prompts. The sample does not establish current product terms or a consistent interval between interruptions.\n\nThe broad date window also means these accounts should not all be read as descriptions of a single current release.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWord Stacks is a promising match for players who want a word search that changes under their answers. It is less clearly suited to solvers who need unusually fresh vocabulary or a completely quiet presentation. Its mechanical identity is strong; the sample's more persuasive reservations concern repetition and the systems around it.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What changes after a word is found?\nThe remaining letter stacks shift, according to the listing.\n\n### Are there category clues?\nYes. A clue helps connect the words in a puzzle.\n\n### Must every player buy Shuffle?\nNo such requirement is established. A sampled player reports feeling stuck without it.\n\n### Does ad removal necessarily remove internal offers?\nNo. A reviewer specifically distinguishes internal promotion from external advertising.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/word-stacks/id1413942319?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
