    "bodyMarkdown": "# Thief Puzzle: A Swipe Route, Not a Math Workbook\n\nThief Puzzle makes a mischievous objective into a route problem: get the item without triggering the danger around it. Its keyword-heavy listing sounds broader than that, but the strongest evidence points to short swipe-based visual challenges. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Plan the reach around the obstacle\n\nThe developer describes stealing objects while avoiding traps and guards. Swipes guide the action; a reviewer describes drawing a path around obstacles. The puzzle is therefore about finding a workable route, not just selecting a desirable item.\n\nThe description also mentions math, trivia, crosswords, and mental exercises in broad promotional language. It does not provide distinct rules for those supposed modes. Treating the app as a documented collection of arithmetic and crossword games would overstate the evidence.\n\nShort individual problems can be appealing when you want a quick resolution rather than an extended campaign commitment. Whether they offer enough reasoning depends on the actual difficulty, which reviewers assess differently.\n\n## Easy for one player, awkward for another\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-18 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.10.0, 2.3.4; the listing records version 2.10.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive comments call the game fun, relaxing, and occasionally challenging. One detailed account enjoys drawing the line but finds the puzzles too easy. Another reports getting stuck at an early stage.\n\nThose observations are not necessarily contradictory bugs. People differ in how quickly they recognize a visual trick, and a short challenge can seem obvious once the required route clicks. What the sample cannot provide is a calibrated difficulty curve or a guarantee that every level introduces a new idea.\n\nThe theme itself also divides opinion. One reviewer feels uneasy about the stealing premise even while finding some enjoyment in the game. Another raises content concerns. Those are individual reactions, not an independently established age-suitability judgment.\n\n## No single ad interval is supported\n\nThe sample includes a player reporting an early session without ads, others estimating interruptions every few levels, and several strongly negative accounts of frequent advertising. Those experiences should remain separate rather than become a universal claim about an exact schedule.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. This evidence does not establish a current removal price, entitlement, or an independently verified uninterrupted mode. A favorable report about a brief session is particularly limited as evidence for long-term ad behavior.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThief Puzzle is most suitable for players who enjoy brief route-finding tricks and do not mind the stealing premise. It is a weaker fit for someone seeking substantial arithmetic practice or a reliably demanding logic curriculum. The central interaction has clear fans, but difficulty and advertising expectations should stay modest.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it a math puzzle collection?\nThe listing uses math-related promotion but does not document a separate arithmetic mode.\n\n### What do you avoid?\nThe stated obstacles include traps and guards while trying to retrieve items.\n\n### Is every level easy?\nNo. Sampled reviewers disagree substantially about difficulty.\n\n### Is there an ad after a fixed number of levels?\nThe sample does not support a single verified interval.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/thief-puzzle-brain-games/id1549814091?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
