    "bodyMarkdown": "# Zen Match: Check the Room-Decorating Promise Before Committing\n\nZen Match's tile-matching premise is straightforward. The harder buying decision concerns its wrapper: the listing still promotes decorating rooms, while multiple reviewers say that feature was removed or replaced. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Clear tiles, then seek a reason to continue\n\nThe developer describes matching three tiles to clear a board, with daily challenges and room decoration using earned progress. Plants, themed interiors, and a personal design space feature prominently in the pitch.\n\nTile matching provides the immediate task. Decoration supplies a longer-term goal outside the board. For someone who enjoys both, the room can be the reason to finish another challenge rather than a disposable extra.\n\nThat distinction is important here because the source record does not line up neatly with player experience. It would be misleading to present the decorating feature as independently confirmed in the current app.\n\n## A substantial feature disagreement\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-05-23 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 220000.1.745, 220000.1.750, 220000.1.762, 220000.1.770; the listing records version 220000.1.770. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers enjoy the observation challenge and daily puzzles. But several specifically describe losing the ability to design rooms, seeing a map or journey instead, or no longer having a satisfying use for earned progress.\n\nThese accounts come from players, not an inspected feature history. Even so, the repeated, concrete disagreement with the listing deserves prominence. If room decoration is your main reason to download, confirm that it is available for your account before investing heavily in progression.\n\nOther criticism concerns numerous event prompts, offer screens, and animations. Those can interrupt a relaxing rhythm without being third-party ads. A player who tolerates advertisements may still dislike navigating repeated internal announcements.\n\n## Paid items and rewarded videos\n\nThe listing discloses optional purchases, including randomized items. That does not establish the probability or value of a particular purchase, nor prove that all boards require paid help.\n\nThe sample includes complaints about added external ads and reward videos failing to provide assistance. Those reports identify potential friction but do not establish a universal failure rate or a current ad-removal entitlement.\n\nThe strongest editorial caution is therefore not a sweeping claim that the game is impossible for free. It is that the motivating feature and the surrounding interruption load may differ from the quiet room-design experience a prospective player expects.\n\n## Verdict\n\nZen Match may still interest players who principally want tile triples and daily challenges. It is a more uncertain fit for someone choosing it specifically to decorate interiors. The listing-review contradiction is material, and it should be resolved in the actual app rather than hidden behind the relaxing theme.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is room decorating definitely still available?\nNo independent confirmation is available; multiple sampled reviewers say it was removed or replaced.\n\n### Are internal popups the same as external ads?\nNo. Reviewers raise both kinds of interruption, which may have different controls.\n\n### Does the listing disclose random paid items?\nYes. It explicitly says optional purchases can include randomized items.\n\n### Is every level proven to require payment?\nNo. The evidence does not establish a universal paywall.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/zen-match-relaxing-puzzle/id1560124228?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
