    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bus Out: Make Space Before You Make Progress\n\nBus Out describes traffic as a puzzle of blocked possibilities. Your immediate goal is not simply to choose a bus, but to determine which vehicle movement creates useful space for the others. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## The jam is the problem\n\nThe US listing describes moving buses out of gridlock through strategic planning. It presents increasingly challenging situations and a broader setting of bus-themed locations.\n\nThe general attraction of an unblocking puzzle is easy to understand. A vehicle that appears ready to leave may still be obstructed, while moving something less obvious can open a useful route. Progress is about recognizing dependencies rather than making every available move immediately.\n\nThat is an analysis of the stated premise, not a description of a specific level. The supplied text does not fully document movement directions, bus lengths, exit positions, or whether vehicles can reverse. Those rules materially affect the strategy and should not be borrowed from another traffic game.\n\n## Do not add an undocumented passenger system\n\nBus-themed puzzle listings often overlap in vocabulary, but the evidence here emphasizes unjamming vehicles. It does not establish a passenger-capacity calculation, a color-matched boarding queue, or the exact arrow-based behavior of other bus games.\n\nThe developer also advertises challenges and competing with friends. The supplied description does not explain the competition's rules, ranking system, or whether participation is necessary for ordinary play.\n\nIf you enjoy examining a shared space and choosing a useful sequence, the premise is relevant. If your favorite part of a bus puzzle is arranging passenger colors or optimizing seat capacity, this listing alone does not confirm that experience.\n\n## What remains unassessed\n\nNo usable review bodies are available for this app in the supplied collection. There is therefore no evidence-based player digest about difficulty, repeated layouts, controls, or the rate at which new mechanics appear.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. It does not establish the cost or availability of help, an ad-removal product, or how often interruptions occur. A strategic premise also does not imply offline support or the absence of a timer.\n\nThe developer's claims about challenging the brain describe intended appeal, not measured cognitive improvement. The relevant practical test would be whether the movement rules produce decisions you find satisfying, not whether the app's traffic theme sounds educational.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBus Out is best considered by players looking for a vehicle-unblocking concept. It is a less certain choice for anyone who requires a specific movement system or passenger mechanic before downloading. Its central idea is clear enough to compare at the genre level, while the details needed for a stronger recommendation remain undocumented.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the stated objective?\nMove buses out of a traffic jam through strategic vehicle movement.\n\n### Are passenger colors part of the confirmed rules?\nThe supplied description does not establish that system.\n\n### Are all buses locked to one direction?\nExact directional movement restrictions are not documented here.\n\n### Is the free download confirmed to have no ads?\nNo. Download price and advertising are separate questions.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bus-out/id6736378756?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
