    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Search: Crossword Game: Straightforward Searching, Split Expectations\n\nWord Search - Crossword Game offers the familiar act of finding a word already hidden among letters. Its sample is most useful for understanding differing expectations: a calm, readable search for some players can feel too simple for others. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Look across, down, and diagonally\n\nThe listing describes swiping through words vertically, horizontally, and diagonally. Clues, daily challenges, and trophies add structure, while English, German, and Russian are advertised language choices.\n\nDespite the crossword label, the documented action is searching a letter field rather than typing answers into intersecting clue entries. That distinction matters if you are choosing between vocabulary deduction and visual scanning.\n\nThe description does not fully specify grid sizes or timer behavior across modes. A daily trophy system is documented; a universal race against a clock is not.\n\n## The same simplicity draws praise and criticism\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-08-10 through 2026-09-26 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.29.0, 1.30.0, 1.31.0; the listing records version 1.31.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts mention a calm start to the day, large print, and an approachable level of challenge. Those are practical attractions for someone seeking a low-friction visual task.\n\nOther reviewers want harder words, larger fields, or less obvious parallel placements. Their criticism is not necessarily that the rules are unclear; rather, the search can feel insufficiently demanding. One reviewer likes a clock, while another dislikes being timed. Those reports should remain mode- and account-specific because the listing does not resolve the complete timer design.\n\nThe sample spans more than a year. It should not be summarized as a single recent verdict on the current release, particularly for interface or progression details that may have changed.\n\n## Ad presentation and progress are separate concerns\n\nSeveral reviewers describe substantial advertising, including one account of a browser-opening ad. Others report difficulty transferring progress after a device change. These are user reports, not independent measurements of current behavior or cloud-save guarantees.\n\nThe free download does not establish a removal offer or current assistance prices. Nor does large-print praise certify the entire interface for low vision; the actual controls and contrast were not inspected.\n\nA player who wants a familiar word search may accept a modest challenge. A player depending on years of progress moving intact to a new device has a different requirement that this evidence cannot certify.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Word Search app looks most suitable for players who value readable, conventional searching over complex word deduction. It is a less certain fit for someone seeking consistently difficult grids or verified progress transfer. The sample's disagreement about ease is more informative than a blanket claim that the game is relaxing for everyone.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it a traditional clue-answer crossword?\nThe main documented action is swiping hidden words in a letter grid.\n\n### Which directions are supported?\nVertical, horizontal, and diagonal searching are described.\n\n### Is every mode definitely timed?\nNo. Player comments mention a timer, but the full mode rules are not documented.\n\n### Is device-to-device progress confirmed?\nNo. Some reviewers report transfer problems, and no independent transfer was tested.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/word-search-crossword-game/id1471967529?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
