    "bodyMarkdown": "# Sudoku - Brain Puzzle Games: Tools Around a Familiar Grid\n\nSudoku - Brain Puzzle Games pitches a familiar logic puzzle with unusually explicit claims about distractions and privacy. Those promises are worth noting, but the most concrete basis for comparison is its collection of solving and editing tools. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Logic first, assistance by choice\n\nThe listing describes classic nine-by-nine Sudoku with four difficulties and more than 5,000 puzzles. Notes, hints, duplicate highlighting, undo, redo, and automatic saving support the solving process.\n\nFast Fill is advertised as a way to enter a selected number repeatedly. That is a practical distinction for someone who prefers scanning the board for one digit rather than repeatedly choosing a cell and then its value.\n\nHints and highlighting can reduce the burden of checking your work, while notes preserve candidates you are not ready to commit to. Their presence does not establish whether every hint teaches a logical step or merely reveals an answer. That distinction matters for a player choosing an app to learn deduction rather than finish boards quickly.\n\n## Several modes, not one fully documented ruleset\n\nThe developer also names Killer Sudoku, Letter Sudoku, daily challenges, and multiplayer. Those labels broaden the pitch, but the supplied text does not give complete rules for each variation or explain how competition works.\n\nA dark mode and a magnifying feature are advertised. They may be relevant to visual comfort, but no inspection establishes their contrast, text sizing, or usability for a particular visual need.\n\nThere are no customer-review bodies available in this evidence set. It is therefore not possible to assess whether players find the puzzles well graded, the tools responsive, or the extra modes worthwhile. More puzzles do not automatically mean better difficulty calibration.\n\n## Three claims to keep attributed\n\nThe description explicitly promotes ad-free play, offline use, and no tracking. These are developer statements, not independent certifications. No disconnected session or privacy audit was performed.\n\nThe metadata lists a free download, while the supplied evidence does not fully document purchases or the scope of access to every mode. The absence of review bodies should not be turned into either a complaint or a clean bill of health.\n\nFor someone specifically choosing on privacy grounds, the distinction is especially important: a reassuring sentence in a listing is not equivalent to verified data behavior.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Sudoku is worth considering for players who want substantial input and checking tools around classic logic. It is less conclusive as a privacy-certified or accessibility-certified recommendation. Fast Fill, undo/redo, and notes are concrete differentiators; broader claims should remain attributed until checked directly.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is Fast Fill?\nThe listing describes repeatedly entering the same selected number across cells.\n\n### Are notes and undo available?\nYes. Notes, undo, redo, hints, and duplicate highlighting are advertised.\n\n### Is ad-free play claimed?\nYes, explicitly, but the claim was not independently verified.\n\n### Does no-tracking language constitute an audit?\nNo. It is a developer statement rather than inspected technical evidence.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/sudoku-brain-puzzle-games/id1442749804?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
