    "bodyMarkdown": "# Sand Balls: Digging Feels Different From Sorting\n\nSand Balls asks you to create a route rather than aim a shot. The sample's strongest praise concerns the tactile idea of digging and guiding, while criticism often targets repetition or what happens between those short puzzles. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Make a channel to the truck\n\nThe listing describes removing sand so balls can reach a truck. Obstacles and additional objectives complicate the route, while keys and collected progress connect the puzzles to an island-building layer.\n\nThe central decision is how to shape the path. An opening needs to lead somewhere useful; simply removing more sand is not necessarily a plan. This differs from sorting balls into tubes or launching them at bricks, despite the shared ball imagery.\n\nThe supplied text mentions special balls and obstacles without fully documenting all their behavior. It would be inaccurate to assign every object a precise interaction from the listing alone.\n\n## The sensory loop has specific fans\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-03-21 through 2026-11-01 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.3.48; the listing records version 2.3.48. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers describe digging, collecting, sound effects, and visible progression as satisfying. One explicitly enjoys the sounds even as an adult. Another likes the basic game but reports getting balls caught around a fan obstacle.\n\nSome accounts find the levels easy or the content repetitive, with complaints about a lack of fresh island destinations. These are assessments of the amount and variety of challenge, not proof that every current level repeats.\n\nA low-pressure puzzle can be attractive precisely because it is easy. The question is whether you want a relaxing routing activity or a sustained difficulty curve with new mechanics. The sample supports different answers for those two audiences.\n\n## The weekly VIP terms are unusually concrete\n\nThe listing describes a three-day trial followed by a $5.99 weekly VIP subscription. Advertised benefits include removing ads, effects, gems, and building-related access. Those are the supplied terms, not a checkout verified in the app.\n\nThey should not be confused with a one-time purchase or a separate pass mentioned by a reviewer. Subscription renewal is a recurring cost, even when the initial download is free.\n\nAdvertising reports differ: some reviewers describe relatively few ads, while others report frequent interruptions or ads after restarts. Accounts of lost purchases or activation trouble remain unverified individual experiences, not a finding of deliberate misconduct.\n\n## Verdict\n\nSand Balls may suit players who like short, visually guided tasks and the satisfaction of making a route work. It is a less certain fit for someone seeking steadily expanding complexity or minimal recurring offers. The digging mechanic has clear supporters; the weekly subscription and repetition concerns deserve equally clear attention.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the main interaction?\nDig through sand to guide balls toward a truck.\n\n### Is this a ball-sorting tube puzzle?\nNo. Its central rule is route creation through digging.\n\n### What VIP price is in the listing?\nA three-day trial followed by $5.99 per week is described.\n\n### Do all reviewers report the same number of ads?\nNo. The sample includes both lighter-ad experiences and frequent-interruption complaints.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/sand-balls-digger-puzzle/id1474046667?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
