    "bodyMarkdown": "# Cube Solver 3D: A Solving Assistant, Not a Level Campaign\n\nCube Solver 3D is best understood as a companion to twisty puzzles. Unlike most puzzle games, its starting point is a state you supply, not necessarily a level the app has designed for you. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Enter a puzzle, receive a route to completion\n\nThe listing describes inputting the current state of a puzzle and receiving a step-by-step solution. Multiple cube and other twisty-puzzle types are advertised, with different move-count estimates depending on the puzzle.\n\nThat workflow changes what success means. The app can be useful for getting a physical puzzle back to a solved state, but following a sequence is not automatically the same as understanding a reusable solving method.\n\nAccurate input also matters. A solution is only useful if the entered state corresponds to the puzzle in front of you. The supplied description does not establish every validation message or how impossible states are diagnosed, so those details should not be invented.\n\n## More than an answer button\n\nA speedcubing timer, statistics, tutorials, patterns, and colorblind-oriented design are also listed. Those features address different needs: timing supports practice, statistics track sessions, and tutorials may help someone move beyond copying a single solution.\n\nThe distinction between solving assistance and practice tools is the central reason to consider this app. A beginner may want help recovering a scrambled cube, while an experienced solver may be more interested in timing or experimenting with patterns.\n\nThe evidence does not establish that every advertised puzzle type has the same tutorial depth or that every generated sequence is mathematically shortest. Move-count claims should remain the developer's estimates rather than a blanket optimality guarantee.\n\n## Support claims are not independent validation\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in this collection, so there is no evidence-based assessment of input convenience, solution correctness, or how well the timer fits a training routine.\n\nThe colorblind design claim is relevant, but no interface was inspected to verify which alternatives supplement color or how they work. It should not become an accessibility certification.\n\nThe US metadata records a free download. The supplied text does not fully establish advertising, paid access, or offline availability for each tool. This is not evidence of a problem; it is simply a limit on a stronger recommendation.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCube Solver 3D is most relevant to people who already have an interest in physical twisty puzzles and want practical assistance or timing tools. It is a poor comparison target for someone seeking a conventional campaign of original logic levels. Its utility depends on the accuracy and usefulness of the solving workflow, which was not independently tested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it mainly a level-based puzzle game?\nNo. The listing emphasizes entering puzzle states and generating solutions.\n\n### Does following a solution teach a general method?\nNot necessarily. Tutorials are separate from simply following generated steps.\n\n### Are timing tools included?\nA speedcubing timer and statistics are advertised.\n\n### Are all solutions proven shortest?\nNo independent optimality verification is available.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cube-solver-3d/id1673039373?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
