    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong City Tours: Familiar Pairs, a Longer Travel Framework\n\nMahjong City Tours connects tile-pair puzzles to collecting souvenirs and visiting cities. Its sample contains appreciation for varied layouts, but also reminders that automatic help and progression gates can change the feel of a familiar solitaire game. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Pairs serve a wider journey\n\nThe listing describes Mahjong-style tile matching, city travel, obstacles, powerups, and weekly tournaments. The objective is solitaire-style clearing rather than a documented four-player Mahjong ruleset.\n\nThe travel framework supplies a destination outside the current board. Souvenirs and challenges can make another puzzle feel purposeful, while powerups offer ways around a difficult arrangement. The supplied description does not fully establish tile-access rules or all stage restrictions.\n\nThat matters when comparing a traditional self-contained board with a progression game. Here, the wider journey is part of the proposition, not just an interchangeable background.\n\n## The sample includes genuinely older experiences\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2024-08-31 through 2026-10-19 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 61.2.0, 61.3.0, 61.4.0, 61.6.0, 61.6.1, 62.0.0, 62.1.0, 62.2.0, 63.0.0, 63.1.0, 63.2.0, 64.2.2, 65.0.0, 65.0.1, 65.1.0, 65.2.2, 65.4.0, 65.5.0, 65.6.7; the listing records version 65.6.7. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPlayers praise layout variety, rewards, and a satisfying challenge. One older reviewer particularly likes the souvenirs and reports no forced ads at the time. That is not interchangeable with newer comments describing interruptions or banners covering controls.\n\nSome players dislike automatic hints or the game completing the last pairs for them. Their objection is not excessive difficulty but reduced control over finishing the board. Other reviewers describe becoming stuck at high stages or encountering ticket-based limits.\n\nThose experiences suggest two different audiences: people who welcome assistance and a guided journey, and people who want to solve every pair themselves. The sample does not establish that all accounts receive identical help settings or restrictions.\n\n## Ads and paid items need chronological context\n\nThe listing discloses optional purchases, including randomized items. That is distinct from whether a session contains forced advertising or whether a specific powerup is good value.\n\nAd accounts vary across the broad date window. An older no-forced-ad report should not become a current guarantee, and a banner-overlap complaint is not proof that every device has the problem.\n\nThe practical distinction is between accepting an occasional break and losing access to the controls themselves. That matters more to uninterrupted solving than a broad claim about whether the game contains ads at all.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong City Tours may suit players who like tile pairs with a collection-driven travel goal. It is less clearly suited to those seeking a minimal board and complete manual control over every finish. The sample supports genuine affection, but its older and newer accounts should not be flattened into one timeless verdict.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this four-player Mahjong?\nThe listing describes a tile-pair solitaire experience, not a documented multiplayer Mahjong ruleset.\n\n### What exists beyond the boards?\nCities, souvenirs, obstacles, powerups, and tournaments are advertised.\n\n### Are all reviewers describing the current version?\nNo. The sample spans multiple years and versions.\n\n### Are random paid items disclosed?\nYes. The US listing explicitly includes randomized in-app items.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-city-tours-tile-match/id1217925171?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
