    "bodyMarkdown": "# Twisted Tangle: Untie the Knot by Moving Its Anchors\n\nTwisted Tangle gives knot solving a set of movable anchors. Its listing emphasizes pin manipulation and special constraints, making it more specific than simply dragging ropes until they look untangled. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## The pin determines what can move\n\nThe developer describes a three-dimensional knot puzzle built around moving pins. Fixed pins, octopus pins, and keys and locks introduce variations, with boss, Hard, and Epic challenges alongside regular puzzles.\n\nThat suggests the puzzle is about changing relationships between strands under constraints. The immediately tempting move may not be useful if an anchor is fixed or another part of the arrangement must be addressed first.\n\nThis is a reading of the advertised system, not a tested model of the physics. The supplied text does not fully explain crossing rules, move limits, camera controls, or how a knot is judged solved. It would be inaccurate to promise that every strand can be manipulated freely.\n\n## Special pins are the concrete differentiator\n\nThe named pin types give a prospective player more to compare than the general promise of relaxation. Fixed anchors imply a constraint you must work around; locks and keys suggest a dependency beyond simply moving an endpoint.\n\nThe exact behavior of an octopus pin is not fully documented in the supplied description. Its name should not be expanded into an invented ability or a precise number of attached strands.\n\nWeekly new puzzles and more demanding challenge labels are advertised. The evidence does not establish an actual current update cadence, whether the harder variants are optional, or how difficulty is calibrated across them.\n\n## For patient rearrangement, not a verified stress treatment\n\nSomeone who enjoys spatial rearrangement and trying a sequence of anchor moves may find the premise appealing. Someone who wants all constraints explained in a conventional grid may prefer a less tactile puzzle format.\n\nNo usable customer-review bodies are available here, so there is no basis for saying players find the knots satisfying, the physics fair, or the difficulty frustrating. The developer's relaxing language is not a guarantee of emotional response.\n\nThe US metadata records a free download. Advertising, purchase terms, offline behavior, and stability are not independently established by the supplied description.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTwisted Tangle is a plausible fit for players attracted to spatial untangling with rule-changing anchors. It is less conclusive for someone seeking freely simulated rope manipulation or a verified interruption-free pastime. The pin types and locked dependencies are its clearest documented distinctions; their execution remains untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What do you manipulate?\nThe listing emphasizes moving pins to untangle a three-dimensional arrangement.\n\n### Are all pins movable?\nNo. Fixed pins are explicitly advertised as one constraint.\n\n### What does an octopus pin do exactly?\nThe supplied description names it but does not fully specify its behavior.\n\n### Are weekly updates independently verified?\nNo. Weekly puzzles are a developer claim, not an inspected release schedule.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/twisted-tangle/id6447757125?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
