    "bodyMarkdown": "# Nut Sort: Color Sorting on Bolts, Not Dismantling Plates\n\nNut Sort: Color Sorting Game uses hardware imagery for a familiar sorting constraint. The task is grouping colored nuts on bolts, not taking a wooden construction apart. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Match the top color and leave enough room\n\nThe developer describes moving colored nuts between bolts until each bolt holds a single color. A legal move requires the matching color at the destination and sufficient space.\n\nThat makes temporary capacity central. A nut may belong with its matching group, yet reaching it can require moving the nuts above it somewhere else. An apparently spare bolt can therefore be useful as working space, not just an unfinished container.\n\nThe supplied text sometimes uses tube and pouring language, but the described subject is nuts on bolts. That wording should not be taken as evidence of a second liquid-sorting mode.\n\nThe exact capacity of each bolt, empty-bolt exceptions, and the availability of undo are not established in the supplied description. Those rules materially affect the flexibility of a sorting puzzle and should not be filled in from another app.\n\n## The appeal is organizing access\n\nSomeone who enjoys a color sort may like the way visible information can still be temporarily inaccessible. You can see the nut you want, but have to build a sequence that frees it without blocking something else.\n\nSomeone who dislikes backtracking may find that same premise less appealing. The listing does not establish that every mistaken move can be reversed one step at a time or that extra storage is freely available.\n\nNo usable review bodies are present in the supplied evidence. There is no basis for claiming players find the sorting easy, the later stages fair, or the controls satisfying.\n\n## Treat relaxation language carefully\n\nThe description uses therapeutic language, but no clinical evidence supports presenting this app as treatment. It can be described as a sorting pastime without promising relief from a health condition.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, hint costs, paid extra capacity, and offline access are not fully documented. The mere use of a calm sorting premise does not establish an uninterrupted or no-pressure economy.\n\nNo screenshot was visually inspected, so color contrast and hardware detail also remain unassessed rather than assumed accessible.\n\n## Verdict\n\nNut Sort is most relevant to players who like planning around top-piece access and limited temporary space. It is not a substitute for a bolt-removal physics puzzle or a validated therapeutic tool. The basic sorting rule is clear; the assistance system and practical session conditions are not.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are you unscrewing plates?\nNo. The documented objective is sorting colored nuts onto bolts.\n\n### Can you move a nut onto a different color?\nThe stated rule requires a compatible color and available room.\n\n### Is the exact bolt capacity documented?\nNo. The supplied description does not establish it.\n\n### Is therapeutic benefit proven?\nNo. The evidence supports a game description, not a treatment claim.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/nut-sort-color-sorting-game/id6476144780?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
