    "bodyMarkdown": "# Crossmath: Arithmetic Order Matters More Than Fast Guessing\n\nCrossmath turns arithmetic into an intersecting placement problem. The important rule is not a trick: multiplication and division take precedence over addition and subtraction, so reading an equation correctly comes before choosing its missing numbers. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Balance the arithmetic across a grid\n\nThe listing describes filling arithmetic puzzles using the four basic operations. Difficulty choices, daily challenges, notes, hints, and an Endless mode broaden the format.\n\nThe grid asks you to keep more than one relationship in mind. A number can look promising in one equation while causing trouble in another. That makes it different from a sequence of unrelated flash-card calculations.\n\nThe description indicates that Endless checks errors on submission, but its full mistake structure is not clear enough to reproduce as a detailed rules guide. The safest documented advice is to verify operation order rather than assume every expression is evaluated strictly left to right.\n\n## The sample appreciates calculation, not always the controls\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-08-19 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.15.0, 4.16.0, 4.17.0; the listing records version 4.17.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nOne reviewer enjoys holding several calculations in mind. Others describe motivation to practice arithmetic or satisfaction from different difficulty levels. A less enthusiastic player wants larger numbers or fractions and finds the existing challenge too easy.\n\nA detailed critique likes the concept but reports accidental placement and confusing undo behavior. That is a different concern from arithmetic difficulty: the solver may know the answer while still struggling to enter it as intended.\n\nAnother player reports losing progress after reinstalling. No independent save or restore test was performed, and the account should not become a universal claim that all progress is unprotected.\n\n## Keep ad reports and health claims separate\n\nAdvertising experiences conflict. Some reviewers say ads are absent or manageable, while others describe frequent or difficult-to-close interruptions. The free download does not establish a uniform ad experience or the current price of removing it.\n\nThe listing includes promotional health language about keeping a neurologist away. There is no evidence here to support disease prevention or a medical recommendation. Enjoying mental arithmetic is a legitimate reason to play without attaching an unsupported health promise.\n\nThat distinction also applies to positive player comments about feeling mentally active: they describe personal experience, not validated clinical outcomes.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCrossmath may suit players who enjoy checking intersecting arithmetic relationships and using standard operation order. It is less clearly suited to someone seeking advanced mathematics or a verified friction-free input system. The core exercise earns specific praise, while control complaints and conflicting ad reports keep the recommendation qualified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Which operations come first?\nThe listing specifies multiplication and division before addition and subtraction.\n\n### Is this a conventional word crossword?\nNo. The relationships are arithmetic rather than vocabulary clues.\n\n### Are all puzzles difficult?\nNo. At least one sampled reviewer finds them too easy and wants more advanced content.\n\n### Does the app have proven medical benefits?\nNo. The supplied evidence does not substantiate prevention or treatment claims.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/math-puzzle-games-crossmath/id1671991909?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
