    "bodyMarkdown": "# Cryptogram: Decoding Appeal, With a Contested Card Update\n\nCryptogram's appeal is the moment a partial message becomes readable. The sample suggests that core remains attractive, but a card-based change and mid-puzzle advertising have made the surrounding experience contentious for some long-time players. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Turn partial information into a message\n\nThe listing emphasizes decoding hidden words, symbols, and quotations, alongside word-scramble and logic challenges. The central attraction is using known information to make an uncertain word or phrase increasingly plausible.\n\nUnlike a conventional word search, the answer is not simply sitting intact in a visible grid. Progress comes from interpreting the incomplete message. The supplied description does not fully document every substitution or special-level rule.\n\nThat gap matters because several reviewers describe a changed input system that is more restrictive than the classic decoding routine they expected.\n\n## Solvable challenge versus imposed card choices\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-15 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.7.2, 3.8.1; the listing records version 3.8.1. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts call the puzzles engaging, difficult but solvable, and less repetitive than some alternatives. One reviewer also praises an uncluttered layout while asking for dark mode.\n\nOther players object to mandatory card-style stages. One says they can no longer enter any letter they know because the available cards constrain the choice. Another describes those levels as taking longer and interrupting an established nightly routine.\n\nThose are reported experiences, not an independently inspected feature history. Still, the objection is more specific than simply resisting change: limiting which letters can be entered can alter the relationship between knowing an answer and being allowed to record it.\n\n## Mid-puzzle ads target the moment of concentration\n\nSeveral reviewers accept ads between levels but object to breaks inside a puzzle. That distinction is especially relevant to decoding, where an interruption can disrupt a developing thought.\n\nA purchaser says ad-free access still leaves videos tied to continuing or completing play. Other comments question changing removal duration. The evidence does not establish the current offer, its price, or precisely which advertisements it covers.\n\nIt would be inappropriate to generalize a claimed interruption every few seconds into a verified schedule. The better-supported caution is the recurring report that interruptions occur during the solving process, not only around it.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCryptogram may suit players drawn to message reconstruction and a meaningful word challenge. It is a more cautious choice for someone seeking a stable classic format without constrained card stages. The sample supports the concept's strength while identifying changes that may matter deeply to how a particular player likes to think.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this mainly a hidden-word grid?\nThe listing emphasizes decoding messages and words rather than simply locating intact answers.\n\n### What concerns reviewers about cards?\nSome say card choices restrict letter entry and that special stages cannot be skipped.\n\n### Are mid-level ads verified by testing?\nNo. They are recurring player reports within the stated sample.\n\n### Does paid removal cover every continuation video?\nThat is not established; one purchaser reports a mismatch with their expectations.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cryptogram-word-brain-puzzle/id1644099880?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
