    "bodyMarkdown": "# Jigsaw Puzzle: Daily Puzzles: Piece Counts Are Device-Specific\n\nJigsaw Puzzle: Daily Puzzles makes a useful distinction many listings leave vague: its maximum piece count depends on the device. That matters more to a serious puzzle session than simply promising thousands of pictures. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Pick the image and the scale\n\nThe developer advertises 9–1,024 pieces on iPad and 9–400 on iPhone. Rotation, multiple puzzles in progress, daily pictures, and personal-photo puzzles add ways to tailor the activity.\n\nMore pieces can increase both the sorting work and the demand on the workspace. The device-specific limits acknowledge that a phone and tablet do not provide the same canvas. This is not an independent assessment of comfort at the maximum settings.\n\nThe listing also describes a large image library, coins, puzzle packs, and an animated completion feature. The evidence does not establish whether every image supports every piece count or which content is included without an additional purchase.\n\n## A personal picture can change the motivation\n\nUsing your own photo may make completion more meaningful than selecting an unfamiliar library image. It also shifts the comparison from a pure puzzle catalog to a tool for making a familiar image into a task.\n\nThe supplied description does not establish how accurately every imported photo is processed or what image adjustments are available. No customer-review bodies are present to assess those details or the quality of the collection.\n\nSomeone who wants to keep several pictures underway may value the advertised multi-puzzle feature. Someone who finishes one image at a time may care more about piece handling and sorting controls, which are less fully documented here.\n\n## Subscription and premium upgrade are not interchangeable\n\nThe listing describes an optional Puzzle Club subscription. It separately describes a $9.99 premium upgrade associated with removing ads, personal-image access, and a set of 95 puzzles. Optional coin-earning videos are still mentioned.\n\nThose descriptions should not be collapsed into a single subscription price or a promise that every video disappears. The supplied terms are not a current in-app checkout, and complete eligibility or offer variations are not established.\n\nThe initial download is free. That does not imply unrestricted access to every pack, while the existence of paid packs does not itself establish forced-ad frequency.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Jigsaw Puzzle app is most relevant to players who want documented piece-count options and the possibility of using personal photos. It is less conclusive as a recommendation for a completely free, unlimited catalog. Device limits and separate paid products deserve attention before choosing it for a long-term puzzle routine.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the listed maximum on iPhone?\nThe description gives a range up to 400 pieces.\n\n### What is the listed maximum on iPad?\nThe description gives a range up to 1,024 pieces.\n\n### Are Puzzle Club and premium the same offer?\nThey are separately described; the $9.99 amount belongs to the premium upgrade text.\n\n### Does removing ads eliminate optional coin videos?\nThe listing still mentions optional coin videos, so that broader promise is not supported.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/jigsaw-puzzle-daily-puzzles/id495583717?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
