    "bodyMarkdown": "# ColorPlanet Oil Painting: Guided Color, With a Long Content History\n\nColorPlanet Oil Painting uses an oil-painting look for guided coloring. It is not a documented simulation of mixing paint or controlling brush pressure; the activity is completing prepared, numbered regions. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Choose the subject, follow the palette\n\nThe listing describes categories, daily pictures, editor selections, tips, and sharing. The player selects an image and fills matching numbered areas until it is complete.\n\nThe realism pitch concerns the intended appearance of the result rather than unrestricted technique. Someone who wants a finished image without drawing may find that appropriate. Someone seeking a digital studio for creating original compositions should not assume those tools are included.\n\nNo screenshot was visually inspected, so the actual realism or visual quality of particular pictures is not independently assessed.\n\n## Praise and criticism span almost two years\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2024-12-14 through 2026-10-28 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.6.4, 1.6.5, 1.6.6; the listing records version 1.6.6. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers describe relaxation, enjoyable color, and satisfaction from completed pictures. One especially likes the Buildings category and wants names or locations attached to the subjects. Another praises the range of shades.\n\nSeveral long-time accounts complain about repeated images or fewer new selections. Those claims concern a continuing catalog experience, not just the quality of one picture. They matter most to someone planning frequent use rather than an occasional coloring session.\n\nOther practical concerns include tiny regions, limited highlight-color choice, and a desired landscape view on iPad. Those reports do not establish current accessibility settings, but they identify questions a prospective player can check early.\n\n## Payment does not have one consistent story\n\nOne reviewer thanks the app for a no-ads option, while another says advertisements remain after payment. A different account describes a video for saving without the app name that did not deliver the expected result.\n\nThese are individual experiences, not a verified current offer or failure rate. The sample also includes preferences for one-time payment rather than subscription, but those preferences do not establish the products currently available to every account.\n\nBecause the sample stretches back into 2024, it would be misleading to call every complaint recent or assume all offers remained unchanged. The free download and historical payment accounts need to stay separate.\n\n## Verdict\n\nColorPlanet Oil Painting may suit players who enjoy a guided picture with a realistic-style ambition. It is less clearly suited to someone seeking free painting tools or a dependable stream of novel images every day. The sample supports enjoyment of the finished pictures while raising concrete questions about catalog repetition and everyday handling.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this a free-form oil-painting simulator?\nNo. The documented activity is color by number.\n\n### Do reviewers all praise the catalog's freshness?\nNo. Several long-time reviewers describe repeated pictures or fewer new selections.\n\n### Is landscape support independently confirmed?\nNo. A reviewer requests it, but no interface inspection was performed.\n\n### Is current ad-removal pricing verified?\nNo. Historical purchaser reports do not establish a current universal offer.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/colorplanet-oil-painting-game/id1487291128?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
