    "bodyMarkdown": "# Tap Away 3D: Rotate Your View, Not the Exit Rule\n\nTap Away 3D turns a solid-looking structure into a sequence problem. You can change your view, but each block still has a direction it needs to travel before it can leave. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Find the block with a clear way out\n\nThe listing describes tapping blocks that fly away in a specific direction. Rotating the structure lets you inspect other sides, and progress depends on removing blocks in an order that opens further routes.\n\nThat makes visibility and access separate questions. A block may be easy to see but unable to leave, while an initially hidden block may be the useful next move. Rotating the view can reveal the latter without changing the former's directional constraint.\n\nThe supplied description does not establish that rotation changes a block's permitted movement. It also does not fully document penalties for a blocked tap or whether every mode uses the same limits. Those details should not be invented from the general removal concept.\n\n## Larger forms can change the inspection task\n\nThe developer advertises growing structures, changing block forms, skins, and themes. A larger object can require more deliberate inspection, but this evidence does not establish that every later stage introduces a new rule rather than simply more blocks.\n\nCustomization affects presentation, while the core puzzle is the dependency between exits. Someone who likes methodically exposing the next useful move may enjoy that process. Someone seeking long-range arithmetic or word deduction is looking for a different kind of challenge.\n\nNo screenshots were visually inspected. Specific colors, materials, or structure shapes should therefore not be described as independently observed features.\n\n## Offline is advertised, not tested\n\nThe listing promotes offline play, and the metadata records a free download. Neither fact establishes current ad frequency, paid help, or the scope of an ad-removal product.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in this collection. There is no evidence-based player account of responsiveness, later-level repetition, or stability. The relaxing pitch should remain a statement of intended appeal rather than a promise that all players find the process calming.\n\nThe useful comparison is with other directional removal games: here, inspecting a three-dimensional arrangement is explicitly part of the documented interaction.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTap Away 3D is a clear concept fit for players who enjoy discovering an order of removals through changing viewpoints. It is less conclusive for someone who needs fully specified penalties or verified uninterrupted offline use. The contrast between movable camera view and fixed block direction is its most useful documented idea.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can each block leave in any direction?\nNo. The listing describes blocks moving in a specific direction.\n\n### Why rotate the structure?\nRotation lets you inspect other sides and identify accessible removal paths.\n\n### Does rotating the view change the rules?\nThe supplied description does not establish that it changes a block's allowed direction.\n\n### Is offline play verified?\nIt is advertised, but no disconnected session was performed.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/tap-away-3d/id1568058543?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
