    "bodyMarkdown": "# Car Match: Traffic Puzzle: Access First, Color Match Second\n\nCar Match: Traffic Puzzle turns a traffic scene into a color-matching selection problem. The important restriction is that you cannot simply collect any car you can see: it needs an open side. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Reach the useful car before filling the holder\n\nThe developer describes collecting accessible cars and matching three of the same color. A limited holding area creates the risk of running out of space before enough matching cars are collected.\n\nAccess and color therefore have to be considered together. Taking an available car may expose something useful, but it also occupies a slot until its match can be completed. An attractive color group may be inaccessible behind another vehicle.\n\nThis is a general implication of the stated rules, not a tested solution sequence. The supplied text does not establish the exact number of holding slots or every interpretation of an open side.\n\n## Obstacles change which choice is available\n\nTunnels, boxes, walls, and elevators are advertised. Those features suggest that the set of accessible cars can change as the arrangement develops, rather than remain a flat, fully selectable collection.\n\nTheir exact behaviors are not fully documented. It would be inaccurate to assign an elevator a fixed cycle or a tunnel a particular vehicle sequence from its name alone.\n\nUndo, Shuffle, Super Undo, and Magnet are listed as assistance tools. Their presence may provide ways to recover from a crowded holder, but the description does not establish their costs, limits, or exact effects. In particular, similar names are not enough to define the difference between the two undo tools.\n\n## This is not a documented boarding simulator\n\nThe evidence centers on collecting and matching cars. It does not establish sorting passengers onto buses, calculating seats, or navigating roads in real time.\n\nSomeone who likes planning around a limited buffer may find the concept appealing. Someone who dislikes inaccessible pieces and temporary storage pressure may prefer a more open matching game.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available here. The metadata records a free download, but there is no supported player digest about ads, fairness, or difficulty. Offline play, removal offers, and stability also remain unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCar Match is a clear concept fit for players who enjoy triple matching constrained by spatial access. It is less useful as a recommendation for a passenger-management or driving game. The open-side rule gives the selection process a specific identity; assistance costs and actual level variety are not established.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you collect any visible car?\nThe listing says a car needs an open side to be collected.\n\n### How many matching cars form a group?\nThree cars of the same color are described.\n\n### Is the exact holder capacity documented?\nNo. The supplied description establishes limited space, not a verified slot count.\n\n### Are all assistance tools free?\nTheir availability and costs are not fully established.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/car-match-traffic-puzzle/id6504421808?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
