    "bodyMarkdown": "# Merge Studio: Fashion Makeover: Merging Can Outlast the Clients\n\nMerge Studio ties item merging to a visible makeover result. The sample's most consequential concern is that some long-time players say they can keep earning coins after running out of clients to spend them on. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Build the tools, then change the look\n\nThe listing describes combining items into beauty tools and cosmetics, completing requests, and using progress toward client makeovers, clothing, and stories. Events and a sensory makeover presentation add other activities.\n\nThe merge board and makeover are related but separate rewards. One supplies the repeated item-management task; the other supplies a reason for completing it. If a player mainly wants transformations, that second step matters as much as the merging itself.\n\nThe developer advertises offline play, but its scope was not independently tested. The evidence does not establish that every event or reward works disconnected.\n\n## A clear concept, a reported content ceiling\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-06-17 through 2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.4.6, 4.4.7, 4.5.0; the listing records version 4.5.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nA positive reviewer praises the understandable interface and the way combining items supports a makeover goal. Another calls the game relaxing and enjoyable when it works, while also reporting crashes and videos failing to load.\n\nSeveral accounts describe no new characters, a persistent coming-soon state, or large coin balances without a makeover to purchase. These are player histories, not an independently inspected content inventory. Still, they directly affect the central promise for someone choosing the game because of clients rather than merging alone.\n\nOne reviewer explicitly cares less about the makeover layer. That is an important counterpoint: reaching the reported ceiling may matter less to someone who enjoys the board as the main activity.\n\n## Board clutter and interruptions affect the same rhythm\n\nA detailed account describes many generators consuming limited space. Others report event clutter, random midgame ads, or forced closing. Those concerns are specific to maintaining an orderly session, not proof that every player encounters the same board configuration or technical issue.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Sampled comments mention removal and spending pressure, but current prices and entitlements are not verified. No exact interruption interval should be treated as universal.\n\nThe combined caution is practical: before investing heavily, determine whether the current content goal and session behavior match the part of the game you actually enjoy.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMerge Studio may suit players who enjoy fulfilling item orders and seeing them lead to a transformation. It is a more uncertain fit for someone whose main attraction is an ongoing stream of new clients. The sample supports the basic concept while identifying a possible disconnect between continued merging and the makeover payoff.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What do merged items support?\nThe listing connects tools and cosmetics with requests and client makeovers.\n\n### Is an endless supply of clients confirmed?\nNo. Several reviewers say they reached a coming-soon state with no new client.\n\n### Does everyone prioritize the makeover?\nNo. One sampled reviewer says the merge activity matters more to them.\n\n### Are offline features independently tested?\nNo. Offline play is an attributed listing claim.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/merge-studio-fashion-makeover/id1615964753?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
