    "bodyMarkdown": "# Connect Balls: Line Puzzle: Simple Connections, Different Difficulty Readings\n\nConnect Balls gives a familiar visual problem a minimal instruction: join matching balls without crossing the lines. The sample is divided less about understanding that objective than about how much challenge and interruption accompany it. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Every connection shares the same space\n\nThe listing describes drawing between matching balls and connecting all pairs without crossing existing paths. A route that works for one pair still has to leave a viable arrangement for the others.\n\nThat is the central planning appeal. The shortest-looking connection is not automatically the most useful if it prevents another pair from being joined. The supplied description does not establish that every board cell must be filled, so that common rule from some neighboring path games should not be added here.\n\nNor does the evidence document every drawing tolerance or whether all connections snap to a fixed grid. Those details affect the actual feel of entering a solution and remain untested.\n\n## Easy entry does not settle the whole difficulty curve\n\nThe review sample contains 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-08-02 through 2026-10-24 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.5.2, 2.6.0, 2.6.3, 2.6.5; the listing records version 2.6.5. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers describe enjoyable visual reasoning and puzzles that make them think. One says the game is harder than expected; another praises the challenge and smoothness of play.\n\nOther accounts call it elementary or insufficiently demanding. Those observations can coexist, particularly when reviewers have spent different amounts of time with the game. An easy beginning is not proof of a challenging end, and a harder board is not proof that the entire progression is consistently difficult.\n\nA player also reports difficulty distinguishing some colors. That is a relevant accessibility concern for a color-pairing game, though no independent color-vision assessment was performed and the available alternative cues are not established.\n\n## Offers can compete with a short puzzle\n\nSeveral comments describe advertising or other offers drawing attention away from play. Some estimate very frequent interruptions, but the sample does not establish a single verified schedule.\n\nFor short individual puzzles, even a moderate break may feel prominent relative to the time spent solving. That is a design tradeoff, not a measured ratio for this app.\n\nOne reviewer reports no-Wi-Fi play. That remains an individual experience, not a certified offline mode across every feature. The free download also does not establish current removal prices or assistance terms.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConnect Balls may suit players who enjoy concise spatial routing with an immediately understandable objective. It is less clearly suited to someone seeking consistently demanding paths or verified color-accessibility options. The sample supports enjoyment of the core connection task while preserving meaningful disagreement about its difficulty and distractions.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What makes a valid set of connections?\nMatching pairs must be joined without crossing lines, according to the listing.\n\n### Must every cell be filled?\nThat rule is not established in the supplied description.\n\n### Is the game uniformly easy?\nNo. Sampled reviewers disagree about its challenge.\n\n### Is offline play certified?\nNo. A reviewer reports it, but no independent disconnected test was performed.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/connect-balls-line-puzzle/id1626781939?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
