    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Cookies!®: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-09-24–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2619.0.1–2622.1.0. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWord Cookies!® turns anagrams into a baking-table activity. Swipe letter cookies into answers, then keep looking for extra words that may contribute to rewards beyond the required board.\n\n## The letter pool is the puzzle\n\nThe listing advertises more than 30,000 levels, daily challenges, hints, shuffles, offline mode, and optional purchases. The official image shows a circular letter pool beneath answer slots, making word construction clearer than the broad word-search label.\n\nA shuffle changes how the available letters are presented; it is not a substitute for recognizing a missing answer. The description does not establish every accepted-word rule or current hint cost.\n\n## Bonus vocabulary is not a minor extra\n\nOne supporter likes receiving credit for valid words that do not appear in the puzzle. Another values access to hints through videos rather than only spending. These accounts explain why the side reward system can matter to the core solving experience.\n\nSeveral November reviewers, however, say previously accepted bonus words disappeared after an update. One also objects to obscure required answers. This is evidence of dissatisfaction with dictionary consistency, not an independent ruling on the complete lexicon or a claim that every rejected submission is appropriate.\n\nReward changes create a related split. A detailed account misses a timed supply of honey dippers and dislikes receiving a much smaller item allowance. Others object to collectible cards or excessive event screens. The issue is not simply whether rewards exist, but whether they help with the word puzzles a player actually wants to solve.\n\n## The no-interstitial promise needs context\n\nThe listing explicitly promises no forced full-screen interstitials. Reviewers nevertheless describe long ads, unclosable promotions, and ads persisting after paid removal. Some accounts concern voluntary videos or game-event pop-ups, so not every interruption is necessarily the same ad format.\n\nThe evidence does not resolve the current policy for every account. It also does not establish the terms of every removal purchase. The US download is free, with purchases disclosed; offline play remains an advertised rather than tested feature.\n\nCrash and reward-delivery complaints appear alongside a positive support account. Consider Word Cookies for repeated anagram solving, while checking the current bonus dictionary and interruption pattern before committing to a daily collection routine.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWord Cookies has specific support for letter building and bonus discoveries. Dictionary and reward changes are especially relevant to returning word-game fans, while the no-interstitial claim should not be expanded into a universal no-ad guarantee.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How are words entered?\n\nSwipe through letter cookies; the official image shows a circular letter pool and answer slots.\n\n### Does the listing promise no ads at all?\n\nIt specifically promises no forced full-screen interstitials. That is narrower than no advertising of any kind, and reviewers report interruptions.\n\n### Are bonus words always accepted consistently?\n\nSome reviewers report that familiar bonus words were removed. The full dictionary has not been independently checked.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/word-cookies/id1153883316?uo=4)."
