    "bodyMarkdown": "# Killer Sudoku by Sudoku.com: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nKiller Sudoku by Sudoku.com adds arithmetic boundaries to the familiar number grid. Each dotted cage has a target sum, while ordinary row, column, and 3×3-region restrictions still apply.\n\n## Two kinds of constraint meet in one cell\n\nA digit must fit its row, column, and region without repeating. It must also contribute to the sum printed in its cage, where digits cannot repeat either. A placement that satisfies one condition can still violate another.\n\nThat makes the central activity different from merely calculating a total. You compare possible combinations with the surrounding Sudoku restrictions. A sum can narrow the digits a group contains, while a row or box can narrow where each digit belongs.\n\nThe description explicitly points out that a complete row, column, or 3×3 region totals 45. This gives a second arithmetic reference alongside the printed cage totals. It does not establish that a particular advanced technique is taught or that every board has a unique solution.\n\n## Notes are part of the advertised toolkit\n\nNotes and hints are listed for difficult positions. Notes are particularly relevant when several combinations remain possible and you want to retain candidates instead of guessing. The description does not explain automatic candidate updates or exactly how a hint reasons through a step.\n\nThe official image shows a number keypad, dotted cages, Notes and Hint controls, and a mistake counter. It visually supports the hybrid arithmetic-and-Sudoku premise, but does not settle mistake limits or the effect of reaching one.\n\nThousands of levels, daily trophies, and seasonal medals provide progression options. Their existence is not evidence that every difficulty suits beginners, despite the developer’s broad accessibility pitch.\n\n## Choose for deduction with sums\n\nConsider this app if you enjoy Sudoku’s overlapping constraints and want cage arithmetic to contribute additional information. It is less suitable if arithmetic is precisely what you want to avoid or if you are seeking a freeform number-merging game.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising frequency, purchases, offline availability, and hint replenishment are not established by the supplied description. There are no customer review bodies in this sample, so no player consensus about fairness or instruction quality is available.\n\nThe strongest reason to investigate is the documented interaction between sums and nonrepetition, not the promise of becoming a master quickly or a measured improvement in general intelligence.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Killer Sudoku has a clearly explained rules foundation and practical candidate-note support. Its fit depends on enjoying arithmetic deduction; access terms and the depth of its teaching remain open questions.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can a digit repeat inside a cage?\n\nNo. The listed rules prohibit repetition within a cage as well as rows, columns, and 3×3 regions.\n\n### What do cage numbers mean?\n\nThe digits inside the dotted cage must add to the printed target sum.\n\n### Why is 45 mentioned?\n\nA completed row, column, or 3×3 region contains the digits 1–9, which total 45.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/killer-sudoku-by-sudoku-com/id1505735640?uo=4)."
