    "bodyMarkdown": "# Pull Him Out: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2023-08-25–2026-10-15 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.4.2–2.0.4. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nPull Him Out is explicitly built around the pin-rescue concept often seen in mobile promotions. Remove barriers in the right order to protect a trapped man; the wrong sequence produces a comic disaster.\n\n## Read what each barrier is holding back\n\nThe listing describes multiple choices and different outcomes, making sequence the central decision. A pin is not simply an object to tap: removing it changes which hazards or escape routes can reach the character.\n\nThe official image shows a man suspended above a saw with several horizontal and vertical pins. It illustrates the danger-and-barrier premise, but does not establish every later hazard or the full rules of physical interactions.\n\nOne reviewer says the actual pin game resembles its promotion and enjoys it, while wanting longer levels. Another likes the story and point of the game. Those comments provide qualified support, not a reason to imply that the sample is mostly enthusiastic.\n\n## The historical sample is unusually ad-focused\n\nThe 50 reviews span August 2023 to October 2026. Many describe interruptions after very short puzzles, sometimes concluding that watching takes longer than solving. Those reported ratios are not independently timed measurements and should not become a universal current schedule.\n\nA player who likes the underlying game still says each level is followed by advertising. Another cannot work out the museum section while ads continue to appear. That distinction matters: interruptions can obscure both the puzzle and surrounding progression.\n\n## Payment and hints have unresolved reports\n\nSeveral purchasers say ads persisted after paying to remove them. The latest included reviewer also says an ad covers a decline option, preventing progress. These are concrete reports of coverage or interface problems, not verified findings about every purchase entitlement.\n\nTwo accounts describe being unable to pass level 147 despite following a hint or a known sequence. One explicitly revisits the complaint a year later without success. The sample does not establish the technical cause or prove every release has that problem.\n\nA separate reviewer reports trouble in airplane mode. Offline behavior, current prices, and the complete ad-removal terms remain unverified; the US download is free.\n\nConsider Pull Him Out only if the pin-order idea itself is what you want. The sample gives little reassurance for a reader whose priority is uninterrupted play, and its broad historical window prevents a confident claim about the present release.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPull Him Out offers the advertised rescue-puzzle premise, but detailed feedback is dominated by interruptions and progression problems. Its appeal is easy to understand; its current commercial and technical behavior needs confirmation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this actually a pin-pulling game?\n\nYes. The description and official image both center on choosing pins to rescue the character.\n\n### Is level 147 broken?\n\nTwo reviewers report being stuck despite following solutions. That is not an independently reproduced defect across releases.\n\n### Does paying remove every ad?\n\nSeveral purchasers report remaining ads. Current offer scope and entitlement behavior are not verified.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/pull-him-out/id1515233303?uo=4)."
