    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong Club - Solitaire Game: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-09-01–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 4.3.7. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMahjong Club - Solitaire Game offers more than one definition of success. Clear identical tile pairs, then choose whether the surrounding experience emphasizes points, time, or no score at all.\n\n## The solitaire board comes first\n\nThe listing advertises over 5,000 setups, a maximum of 144 tiles, landscape backgrounds, and boosters. Removing every tile completes a level and opens the next. The official image shows a layered tile arrangement, not a four-player hand.\n\nScore options include combination points, stars and time, an explicitly pressure-free choice, and no scoring. That is a useful distinction for readers who want matching without treating every board as a race. Exact availability and selection controls have not been tested.\n\n## A pleasant habit can become a competitive commitment\n\nReviewers describe relaxing puzzles and regular evening play. A detailed supporter likes the graphics and considers the ads short enough to tolerate, although they object to misleading update-style messages within them.\n\nClub chat and competitions add a different reason to return. Several invested players, however, say personal tournament results or rewards disappear while team rewards remain accessible. These reports concern the persistence of competition outcomes, not merely losing a difficult board.\n\nTwo reviewers also allege unequal speed-reward access between Android and iOS. No cross-platform test was performed, so that remains a reported fairness concern rather than a verified feature comparison.\n\n## Recovery and visibility deserve nuance\n\nOne player describes losing their status and later explicitly thanks the developers after it returned. That update prevents a blanket claim that account recovery never succeeds, even though other reviewers report unanswered support requests.\n\nA separate account finds tile highlighting too subtle. Calm landscapes and clear-looking promotional tiles do not guarantee readable availability cues for every viewer. Health-related testimonials are not treated as medical evidence.\n\nThe sample also includes a comment about calling tiles against opponents that does not match the documented solitaire rules. It is not used to invent a multiplayer hand-building mode.\n\n## Ads and access\n\nSome reviewers find the ad amount acceptable; others call it excessive or report unclosable screens. The US download is free, and offline play is advertised, while online access is recommended for the fullest experience. Purchase prices and current removal options are not established.\n\nConsider Mahjong Club for its scoring choices and social layer. Readers seeking only a dependable solitary tile routine should weigh the competition and reward reports separately from the basic matching appeal.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong Club has specific support for its presentation and relaxing boards. Its optional competitive framework carries more unresolved reward and fairness concerns than the simple tile-removal premise might suggest.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you play without a score?\n\nYes. No score system is an advertised option alongside points and time-based choices.\n\n### Are lost accounts always unrecoverable?\n\nNo. One sampled reviewer explicitly reports their status returning, while other support complaints remain unresolved.\n\n### Does it support offline play?\n\nThe listing says offline play is possible, while recommending online access for the best experience.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-club-solitaire-game/id1565374299?uo=4)."
