    "bodyMarkdown": "# Redirect It! - Logic Puzzle: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-09-11–2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.13.0–1.15.0. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nRedirect It! - Logic Puzzle makes release order the puzzle. Find an arrow with a clear exit, remove it, and use the space it leaves to free the next route.\n\n## A maze made of occupied paths\n\nThe listing describes colorful arrows tangled on a dark background, with larger stages adding more routes. Music and haptics can be toggled, boosters are available, and no countdown clock is advertised.\n\nThe official image shows bent arrows packed together with a three-heart display. That supports a visually traceable exit puzzle without establishing the exact mistake allowance. A clock-free board can still have other constraints.\n\nOne reviewer likes interesting silhouettes and meaningful challenge early on. Another enjoys the different colors. A contrasting account finds dark purple arrows difficult to see, showing why colorful does not automatically mean accessible.\n\n## Assistance is not always welcomed\n\nSeveral reviewers object to rainbow or win-streak arrows that remove other arrows automatically. One says the feature reduces the very challenge they came for; another asks whether it can be disabled. These comments are not complaints that the game lacks help, but that help can displace their own solving.\n\nA detailed reviewer also objects to zoom behavior changing after a restart. Another says zoom gestures can accidentally select arrows. Those are reported interface problems, not proof of a universal current zoom policy.\n\n## Moves and advertising sit outside the calm pitch\n\nReviewers describe finite moves, paying or watching videos for more, and unused purchased moves not carrying forward. The official no-countdown claim does not establish unlimited actions.\n\nAds draw reports of long waits, mid-puzzle interruptions, and lost bonuses after a forced restart. A positive counterexample describes fewer ads. One purchaser distinguishes paid removal from videos still offered for extra moves, so these systems should not be conflated.\n\nThe US download is free. Current offer scope, prices, and offline support are not established. Comments about Fetch, WeWard, or another reward app do not prove Redirect It has a native or reliable cash-out system.\n\nConsider it for tracing dependencies through vivid routes. If control over every solution step matters, investigate automatic streak assistance and zoom behavior as carefully as the advertising.\n\n## Verdict\n\nRedirect It has a concrete arrow-planning appeal, but the sample’s most distinctive concern is unwanted automation. It is a conditional fit for deliberate solvers rather than an assumed unlimited, unobstructed maze.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does it have a countdown?\n\nThe developer advertises no countdown clock. Reviewers still describe move budgets and other constraints.\n\n### What are rainbow arrows?\n\nSampled reviewers describe arrows that automatically remove others and sometimes reduce the challenge they want.\n\n### Can you earn cash directly?\n\nReviews mention third-party reward services. A native reliable cash-out feature is not established.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/redirect-it-logic-puzzle/id6754450031?uo=4)."
