    "bodyMarkdown": "# Merge Hotel Empire－Design Game: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-10-21–2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.1.44–1.1.46. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMerge Hotel Empire－Design Game gives matching objects a renovation purpose. Merge pairs into better tools, complete tasks, and turn Elsa’s damaged manor into a hotel while following a rivalry and a curse mystery.\n\n## Tools connect the board to the building\n\nThe listing explicitly calls this a merge-2 game. Upgraded objects supply renovation tasks, and completed work opens rooms ranging from bedrooms to lobbies. That is a different loop from clearing three matching tiles or freely furnishing a complete floor plan.\n\nAlthough the developer promises design control, the text does not quantify choices or establish unrestricted object placement. Its strongest supported proposition is a task-driven makeover with story progression.\n\nThe opening official artwork emphasizes characters outside a snowy building rather than showing the merge board. A reviewer also calls the app photos somewhat misleading while still enjoying the game, making the written mechanic especially important.\n\n## Story and extra activities have supporters\n\nOne reviewer likes the graphics, storyline, and side quests. Another says mini-games provide energy and diamonds. Those are concrete attractions for someone who wants variation rather than continuous merging.\n\nA different player dislikes the persistent pointing hand because finding matching objects independently is part of the fun. That is an autonomy concern, not evidence that beginners receive no guidance. The sample does not establish whether the current interface can disable it.\n\n## Generosity depends on the goal\n\nSome reviewers feel energy and rewards are plentiful or compare the costs favorably with other merge games. Others mention waiting for energy or feeling payment becomes necessary. These accounts do not establish one universal no-spend pace.\n\nSeveral reviews also mention playing through a third-party reward offer. That may influence who reviewed and why they continued; it does not establish native cash earnings or guaranteed payout from this game.\n\n## Ads are not the same as opening promotions\n\nOne player reports minimal ads and another none so far. A reviewer seeking a voluntary reward video instead finds none available. Separately, a player counts many game-generated pop-up tabs on opening. These experiences can coexist: few external ads does not mean a quiet interface.\n\nThe US download is free. Current purchase prices, ad-removal terms, offline support, and later task costs are unverified. Consider the hotel-and-story framework if it adds value to merging, not because the free download proves unlimited access.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMerge Hotel Empire has specific support for story, side activities, and perceived early generosity. It is less clearly suited to readers who want an uncluttered board with no prompts or resource waits.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you merge two or three objects?\n\nThe listing identifies merge-2 gameplay: matching pairs upgrade into useful objects.\n\n### Is it free of interruptions?\n\nSome reviewers report few ads, but another describes many in-game promotion tabs. Those are distinct systems.\n\n### Does the game pay real money?\n\nSome comments concern outside reward offers. A native or guaranteed cash-earning feature is not established.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/merge-hotel-empire-design-game/id1577970257?uo=4)."
