    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block Puzzle Woody Origin: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2025-12-28–2026-11-01 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.2.7–3.3.3. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nBlock Puzzle Woody Origin is most appealing when the board itself is enough: place shapes, preserve usable space, and try to improve a personal score without a narrative campaign.\n\n## A wood-themed placement board\n\nThe official image shows pale blocks on a wooden square grid with spare shapes below. The developer’s description relies heavily on selected testimonials, including one describing three-piece batches and no rotation. Those curated quotes are not the same source as the independent 50-entry review sample.\n\nThey suggest a line-placement framework, but the listing does not supply a complete current rulebook. A sampled reviewer even recalls being able to flip pieces on a previous device and wants that option restored. Rotation or flipping should therefore not be treated as a universally established present feature.\n\n## Restraint has specific supporters\n\nOne player likes the wood color and lack of fuss. Another values simple blocks with no timer. Those reports explain the appeal without claiming a measured stress benefit or universally accessible interface.\n\nOther reviewers want a more forgiving piece supply. One objects to receiving three shapes that cannot fit the remaining space. That is a complaint about the survival model, not independently demonstrated evidence that the generator targets a player unfairly.\n\n## Interruptions can become unintended moves\n\nThe most distinctive ad report is not just frequency. An August account says a pop-up during a held placement causes the block to land wherever it was hovering, potentially ruining the run. Another independently objects to ads appearing during block placement.\n\nA May reviewer reports no ads during gameplay, and a March account says there are few ads and offline play works. These historical counterpoints should remain visible alongside later complaints; no single current ad policy follows from them.\n\nSeveral accounts also mention freezing, lost high scores, loading trouble, or missing sound effects. They are reports across versions, not reproduced defects. A suspicious-pop-up warning is not proof of a verified infection.\n\n## Subscription terms are separate\n\nThe US download is free. The listing describes auto-renewing plans lasting one month, six months, or a year, without a universal price or complete benefit inventory. Offline behavior and current removal coverage have not been tested.\n\nConsider Woody Origin for a visually restrained score chase, while treating interruptions during placement as a more consequential concern than a simple wait between games.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWoody Origin has concrete support for simple blocks and wood-toned presentation. Its strongest caution is reported advertising that interferes with a move itself, alongside unresolved piece-option and subscription details.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can pieces rotate or flip?\n\nThe description contains an older no-rotation testimonial, while a sampled reviewer recalls flipping on another device. Current behavior is unresolved.\n\n### Are there ads during a run?\n\nSome reviewers report none, while later accounts describe interruptions during placement. No universal schedule is established.\n\n### Is there a subscription?\n\nThe listing describes auto-renewing monthly, six-month, and yearly plans, without a universal price.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-puzzle-woody-origin/id1078160821?uo=4)."
