    "bodyMarkdown": "# BlockPuz -Blocks Puzzles Crush: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nBlockPuz -Blocks Puzzles Crush presents block fitting through a jigsaw-like outline. The official image gives a clearer example than the brief description: pale pieces fill a unicorn-shaped recess on a wooden surface.\n\n## An outline supplies the target\n\nThe pictured pieces have square-cell shapes rather than conventional interlocking jigsaw tabs. A few already occupy the silhouette, while loose shapes sit below. That makes the visible problem one of fitting supplied geometry inside a boundary.\n\nThis is not enough to establish every mode in the app. The description combines classic block-puzzle language with innovative jigsaw gameplay and mentions comparing scores. It does not document whether some activities clear lines, whether pieces can rotate, or how a level ends.\n\nReaders should therefore distinguish the pictured silhouette activity from a complete universal rulebook. Assuming a continuously falling Tetris-style system would go beyond the evidence, as would promising ordinary tabletop-jigsaw assembly.\n\n## Fit can matter more than speed\n\nFor the visible shape puzzle, a piece that occupies convenient space may leave a remainder that does not suit the others. That is the natural spatial reasoning appeal of filling a bounded outline, not a tested solution for this pictured board.\n\nThe image contains an IQ-labeled progress graphic. That is promotional interface text, not a validated intelligence measurement. The description’s brain-relaxation claims likewise do not establish clinical benefits or a universal no-pressure experience.\n\nA timer, move allowance, wrong-placement penalty, and hint system are not documented. Simple-looking wooden pieces do not independently prove unlimited experimentation or accessibility for every viewer.\n\n## Offline is the strongest explicit access claim\n\nThe developer says the game can be played without internet. That is a useful stated feature, though actual downloaded-content coverage and account requirements have not been tested.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising frequency, purchase offers, paid removal, and the free-versus-paid boundary of individual activities remain unspecified. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support claims about fair difficulty or uninterrupted controls.\n\nConsider BlockPuz if fitting blocky pieces into illustrated outlines sounds appealing. Readers who need a particular line-clearing mode, piece-rotation rule, or verified penalty-free setup should not infer it from the broad block-puzzle label.\n\nThe concrete visual concept is more informative than the listing’s general assurance that the rules are easy to master.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlockPuz’s pictured silhouette fitting gives it an identifiable spatial hook. Its abbreviated description leaves enough mode and control questions that choosing it for a specific classic block-game rule set would be premature.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it an ordinary jigsaw?\n\nThe inspected image shows square-cell block pieces filling a silhouette, not conventional interlocking jigsaw pieces.\n\n### Can pieces rotate?\n\nThe supplied description and image do not establish rotation controls.\n\n### Is offline play advertised?\n\nYes. The developer says it can be played without internet; this has not been tested here.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/blockpuz-blocks-puzzles-crush/id1547762178?uo=4)."
