    "bodyMarkdown": "# Water Sort Puzzle: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nWater Sort Puzzle makes empty capacity a resource. Pour layered colors between glasses until each finished glass holds a single color, while avoiding an arrangement with no useful transfer left.\n\n## A legal pour needs both color and room\n\nThe listed rules say to tap a glass and pour into another when the colors match and there is space. That creates two constraints at once: a destination may have the right color but insufficient capacity, or enough room but an incompatible top layer.\n\nThe text does not separately explain the exception for a completely empty destination. The official image shows an empty tube among mixed ones, but a precise empty-glass transfer rule should not be invented from another water-sort app.\n\nThe pictured liquid layers make the ordering problem visible. A desired color can sit below another, so reaching it depends on finding a temporary home for what is above. The core activity is reorganizing existing contents, not mixing paints into new colors.\n\n## Restarting is explicitly allowed\n\nThe developer says you can restart freely when stuck. That is a substantive distinction from a puzzle where each failed attempt consumes a purchased retry. It remains a product claim, not a tested account of every mode or prompt.\n\nNo timer policy or undo system is documented. A free restart and an undo button are not equivalent: one begins again, while the other would reverse only a recent choice. Readers who like comparing branches should check that difference.\n\nHundreds of levels and increasing complexity are advertised, with more glasses and involved layouts mentioned as progression. The description does not establish a measured difficulty curve or independently verify every board’s solvability.\n\n## Ignore the broad genre comparisons\n\nThe listing references several other water and color games, but those names do not establish shared mechanics. It also uses holiday language without providing a complete inventory of seasonal content. The reliable premise remains layered-liquid sorting.\n\nOffline play is explicitly advertised, while the US download is free. Advertising, purchases, ad-removal terms, and actual offline coverage are not established. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support claims about quiet play or technical polish.\n\nConsider this Water Sort Puzzle if planning temporary storage and exposing buried colors sounds enjoyable. Skip it if you want freeform pouring physics, unrestricted color mixing, or a fully documented no-timer mode.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWater Sort Puzzle offers a clearly stated color-and-capacity challenge with advertised free restarts. Its strongest fit is deliberate sorting, while exact empty-glass rules, undo, and commercial behavior remain open.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What completes a level?\n\nThe listing says to finish with each glass holding a single color.\n\n### Can you restart when stuck?\n\nYes. Free restarts are explicitly advertised.\n\n### Does restarting mean undo is included?\n\nNo. The description promises restarts but does not establish a step-by-step undo tool.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/water-sort-puzzle/id1514542157?uo=4)."
