    "bodyMarkdown": "# Colors by Number® – No.Draw®: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nColors by Number® – No.Draw® replaces brushwork with numbered pixel cells. Find a matching number, apply its assigned color, and gradually turn a grid into a recognizable image.\n\n## Completion is structured rather than freehand\n\nThe official heart image shows a rainbow section already colored beside unfilled numbered squares. A numbered palette below links the small cells to their assigned colors, making recognition and completion the visible activity.\n\nThe description promises daily artwork updates. It does not provide a verified catalog count, photo import, or unrestricted drawing tools. The No.Draw name fits the supported premise: the design is supplied rather than sketched from scratch.\n\nThis can appeal if you enjoy seeing small repeated actions accumulate into a picture without deciding on composition or palette. It is less suitable if your priority is original illustration or changing every hue to your own preference.\n\n## Free tools have an explicit boundary\n\nThe listing names Tips and Shields among limited tools for free users. Some features can be accessed through rewarded videos. Their precise effects and replenishment rates are not documented, so neither unlimited assistance nor mandatory viewing is assumed.\n\nThe developer also acknowledges interstitial, banner, and native ads in the free version. That is a direct disclosure, not an inference from the presence of purchases. Actual frequency and closing behavior have not been tested.\n\nVIP is described as unlocking all coloring content, providing unlimited tools, and removing ads. Daily additions are said to include both free and subscriber-only art, so a growing catalog does not mean every new picture is freely accessible.\n\n## Subscription terms are part of the choice\n\nThe supplied US description lists $3.49 weekly, $7.99 monthly, and $39.99 yearly. The weekly plan is described with a three-day trial, followed by automatic renewal. These are quoted listing terms, not a verified checkout offer for a particular account.\n\nThe text also describes a 24-hour renewal cutoff. Review the actual subscription screen before accepting a trial, especially when a weekly price can sound like a one-time unlock. The download itself is free.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample. Claims about learning, coordination, stress relief, or therapeutic effects remain developer positioning rather than demonstrated outcomes. Offline access and device performance are also unresolved.\n\nConsider Colors by Number for guided pixel completion with clearly disclosed commercial layers, not because the family-oriented artwork proves unrestricted or clinically beneficial use.\n\n## Verdict\n\nColors by Number is unusually direct about free-version ads and tool limits. Its appeal is the numbered-pixel process; VIP is a separate recurring-access decision rather than part of the free-download promise.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does the free version have ads?\n\nThe developer explicitly says it may contain interstitial, banner, and native ads, plus rewarded videos.\n\n### What does VIP unlock?\n\nThe listing says all coloring content, unlimited tools, and ad removal.\n\n### Is the weekly plan a one-time payment?\n\nNo. It is described as auto-renewing after an offered trial; verify the actual checkout terms.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/colors-by-number-no-draw/id1317690198?uo=4)."
