    "bodyMarkdown": "# Construction Set - Toys Puzzle: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nConstruction Set - Toys Puzzle is closer to following a toy-kit instruction booklet than designing a structure from a blank workspace. Find the requested brick, tap it into place, and develop a finished 3D scene.\n\n## Recognition drives the assembly\n\nThe description explicitly provides an on-screen guide. The main task is selecting the right part from available pieces, not independently engineering the model’s structure. That makes it relevant to readers who enjoy orderly assembly and visible progress.\n\nThe official image reinforces the process with a Find panel showing a requested part above a tray of pieces and quantity labels. A highlighted placement area appears separately. It does not establish free placement or a complete sandbox editor.\n\nMore than 200 interlocking part types and dozens of sets are advertised. Characters, vehicles, a castle, ancient Rome, a spaceship interior, and landmarks are among the examples. Those themes indicate variety of subject, not a verified count of currently unlocked models.\n\n## The unpacking ritual is included\n\nThe developer describes opening boxes and bags before building and arranging finished scenes afterward. That gives the activity a toy-set presentation rather than just a list of abstract fitting problems.\n\nOptional vibration is advertised to imitate a brick snapping into place. This is a specific stated setting, not a hands-on assessment of haptic quality. Readers who prefer no vibration have a concrete option to check.\n\nThe claim that instructions ensure you never get stuck is not independently established. The text does not document every selection rule, mistake response, hint, or accessibility option. A familiar kit metaphor does not guarantee that every part is easy to identify on a small screen.\n\n## Gold packages add an access question\n\nFinishing sets unlocks special gold packages described as supplying new elements and harder builds. The description uses surprise language but does not establish purchase prices, contents, probabilities, or whether money is involved. A package is not automatically evidence of a paid randomized sale.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, paid removal, offline support, and any broader purchase catalog remain unspecified. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support claims of reliable progress saving or uninterrupted building.\n\nConsider Construction Set if selecting pieces and watching a guided model take shape is the attraction. Skip it if you mainly want unrestricted architectural design or a physics-based construction challenge where stability determines success.\n\n## Verdict\n\nConstruction Set has a clear guided-assembly identity, with part recognition and toy-unpacking presentation doing the work. Its access terms and creative freedom should not be inferred from the nostalgic brick theme.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you design freely?\n\nThe described core activity follows on-screen instructions to select parts for supplied models; unrestricted building is not established.\n\n### Can vibration be disabled?\n\nVibration is advertised as optional.\n\n### Are gold packages paid loot boxes?\n\nThe description says sets unlock them but does not establish a paid randomized purchase system.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/construction-set-toys-puzzle/id1544659730?uo=4)."
