    "bodyMarkdown": "# WordBrain: classic word puzzle: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2025-12-07–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.49.5–1.50.1. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWordBrain: classic word puzzle asks not only which words are present, but which order solves the board. That sequencing requirement gives it a different appeal from unrestricted letter-bank anagrams.\n\n## A word path changes the remaining problem\n\nThe listing says to connect letters and find hidden words in the right order. The official image shows a compact square letter grid and answer spaces, with a traced word highlighted. It is a path-finding word puzzle rather than a clue-led crossword.\n\nMore than 1,700 handcrafted levels, 15 languages, daily challenges, hints, and no time limits are advertised. The claim that every puzzle is solvable remains a developer statement rather than an audit of the full catalog.\n\nOne reviewer specifically likes persisting through different words until a less obvious path works. Another praises boards that grow larger and more complex. These are concrete reasons to value the mechanic.\n\n## Daily play can be an alternative route\n\nA reviewer who became stuck in the main campaign says they now enjoy the daily puzzle’s challenge level. That distinguishes losing interest in one progression point from finding nothing worthwhile in the app.\n\nOther reports mention daily progress disappearing or an event ending earlier than the displayed expectation. Those are individual persistence and scheduling complaints, not evidence that every daily challenge fails.\n\nThe sample spans December 2025 through November 2026. It should not be presented as a single current-week consensus.\n\n## Ads can interrupt the reasoning chain\n\nPlayers describe mid-board advertising and new ads when returning from another app, making a quick message check disruptive. A separate account says rewarded videos sometimes fail to deliver hints.\n\nReports of removal options conflict: one quotes a price, while another says they cannot find a paid way to remove ads. Current availability and terms are unresolved. Claims about fake health-product ads or alarming device messages are exposure reports, not proof of an infection or an independent fraud investigation.\n\nTwo reviewers also object to a particular pairing of words they find racially insensitive. Their concern is retained as a content-selection issue without treating it as proof of developer intent.\n\nThe US download is free. Offline behavior and current purchase prices have not been verified. Consider WordBrain for its path-and-order challenge, while recognizing that interruption costs can be especially noticeable when holding a planned sequence in mind.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWordBrain has specific support for satisfying word paths and a useful daily alternative to campaign difficulty. Advertising, missing rewards, and reported word-pair concerns make it a more qualified recommendation than the brain-workout pitch alone.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Why does word order matter?\n\nThe listing explicitly requires solving hidden words in the right order; it is not just finding any valid anagram.\n\n### Can daily puzzles suit a stuck player?\n\nOne reviewer describes enjoying daily puzzles after becoming stuck in the main campaign.\n\n### Is ad removal available?\n\nThe sample contains conflicting reports about paid removal. Current offer availability and scope remain unverified.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wordbrain-classic-word-puzzle/id708600202?uo=4)."
