    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong Solitaire: Match Game: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-09-29–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 1.8.2. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMahjong Solitaire: Match Game centers on removing identical pairs from a board of up to 144 tiles. Its promised conveniences are auto-fit display, statistics, and adjustable sound rather than a new hand-building ruleset.\n\n## Pairs are the documented requirement\n\nThe listing describes randomly dealt tile arrangements, with every removed pair bringing the board closer to completion. Clearing all tiles advances the level. It does not fully explain covered-tile and side-access restrictions, so those details should not be invented.\n\nThe official image emphasizes large matching symbols above a layered board. Its claim that larger tiles benefit seniors is marketing, not an accessibility certification. Auto-fit similarly does not establish readable symbols on every screen.\n\nOne review says an update changed a former triple-match experience into pairs and reset progress. That account is a reported transition, not evidence that triples are the current stated rule. The present description is explicit about matching two.\n\n## Straightforward play has supporters\n\nA reviewer calls it a good beginner game, while another likes the low-pressure feel. Many other positive entries are brief, so they support enjoyment without supplying detailed evidence about long-term balance or varied layouts.\n\nPersonal claims about focus or sleep are not treated as clinical outcomes. Nor does a positive rating prove that the reviewer found advertising acceptable: one enthusiastic player separately objects to interruptions.\n\n## Interruptions occur within the board in some accounts\n\nA detailed reviewer says ads appear multiple times while clearing a single board, not merely between levels. Another describes an ad that will not disappear. These are more specific concerns than a generic objection to free-app advertising.\n\nSeveral reviewers also report freezes that require closing and reopening, sometimes losing progress. The sample does not reproduce those defects or establish their cause. All included reviews list version 1.8.2, which narrows the reported version span without proving identical device conditions.\n\nSome comments concern Freecash linking or failure to receive money. An external tracking problem is not proof that this app itself offers reliable cash payouts. It is also not a gameplay score.\n\nThe US download is free. Offline play is advertised, with online access recommended for features and progress, but actual coverage has not been tested. Current purchase and removal terms remain unverified.\n\nConsider this app for straightforward pair finding, while checking whether mid-board interruptions undermine the quiet routine you want.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong Solitaire has specific support for accessible-seeming simplicity, not verified accessibility. Its detailed sample cautions concern ads during boards and freezing, with external reward expectations kept separate from the tile game.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does it match two or three tiles?\n\nThe current supplied description explicitly says identical pairs.\n\n### Are ads only between levels?\n\nSome reviewers report interruptions within a single board; no universal placement policy is verified.\n\n### Does it guarantee cash rewards?\n\nNo such native guarantee is established. Some reviews refer to an external reward service.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-solitaire-match-game/id6479984405?uo=4)."
