    "bodyMarkdown": "# Water Sort Puzzle: Get Color: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nWater Sort Puzzle: Get Color offers a spatial sorting job disguised as colorful liquid. Reorganize the contents of flasks, using an additional flask when needed, rather than blending colors into new shades.\n\n## Temporary space is the meaningful resource\n\nThe written instructions are unusually brief: sort colors across flasks and use one additional flask if necessary. They do not specify all legal-pour rules, how much liquid moves at once, or exactly how the extra vessel is unlocked.\n\nThe official image shows many narrow containers filled with distinct horizontal bands, one tilted above another. That supports a layered-transfer concept. It does not prove that every level uses that many containers or that players begin with the pictured configuration.\n\nA buried band cannot be directly reached without addressing what sits above it. That makes temporary storage relevant to the advertised task: organizing one color may create or consume room needed for another. This is an implication of the visible arrangement, not a tested solving guide.\n\n## More room is help, not a documented entitlement\n\nAn additional flask can change how much rearrangement is possible. However, the listing does not establish whether it is always available, earned, purchased, or supported by advertising. It should not be described as an unlimited free rescue tool.\n\nMore than 1,000 puzzles and broad level variety are advertised. Those are developer content claims rather than a count of unique boards or proof of steadily increasing difficulty. Undo, move limits, timers, and restart rules are not documented.\n\nThe relaxation and anxiety-relief pitch also needs a boundary. A repetitive sorting activity may be appealing on its own, but the supplied description does not establish therapeutic effectiveness or replace treatment advice.\n\n## No subscription is a narrower claim than no ads\n\nThe developer explicitly promotes free puzzles without subscriptions or hidden fees. That wording does not establish an absence of all optional purchases, nor does it say the game has no advertisements. Current checkout terms and ad behavior have not been tested.\n\nThe US download is free. Offline support and progress syncing are not established. Social sharing to Facebook or Instagram is mentioned, but that does not prove those services are required for ordinary play.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample. Consider Get Color for layered-liquid organization and the stated extra-space option, not a manufactured player consensus or a guarantee of interruption-free calming sessions.\n\n## Verdict\n\nGet Color has a recognizable flask-sorting premise, with extra space as its most specific listed aid. Exact transfer rules and the conditions of that help remain more important than the broad therapy language.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does the listing offer an extra flask?\n\nYes. One additional flask is mentioned, but its unlock conditions are not explained.\n\n### Is a subscription advertised?\n\nThe description explicitly promotes no subscription. That is not proof of no ads or no optional purchases.\n\n### Can colors mix into new ones?\n\nThe image shows separate bands and the task is sorting; color blending is not established as a mechanic.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/water-sort-puzzle-get-color/id1569776448?uo=4)."
