    "bodyMarkdown": "# Tetris®: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-10-30–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 7.7.12. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nTetris® on iOS offers two different expectations under one familiar name: a classic Marathon run and hand-designed levels completed before moves run out. Knowing which you want matters more than recognizing the blocks.\n\n## The mode changes the rhythm\n\nThe listing describes rotating Tetriminos, clearing lines, and tackling objectives across hundreds of levels. Coins can supply rewards and additional moves, while teams and customization provide surrounding progression.\n\nMarathon is explicitly advertised as the longer-form alternative. One reviewer says it retains the familiar experience, while another cannot find the endless format they expect. The sample does not justify claiming Marathon is absent simply because a frustrated player misses it.\n\nThe official opening image emphasizes the Tetris logo rather than a mode menu. It confirms the product branding but does not resolve mode discoverability or control layout.\n\n## Removed favorites are a distinct complaint\n\nA long-term reviewer misses Quick Play and Royal, explaining that a short timed run and live competition served different needs from Marathon. That is more informative than a generic claim that the new app is not Tetris.\n\nOther players still enjoy the game, and one specifically likes the retained theme music. Some find the level format too easy. These accounts support different preferences, not a measured comparison of every available difficulty.\n\nReports of unintended rotations or inaccurate placement also appear. They are control complaints, not independently reproduced input defects.\n\n## Ad closing is the immediate practical concern\n\nSeveral reviewers describe ads extending beyond the screen or lacking a usable close control. One says support contacted them, but hopes for a future fix; contact is not proof the defect has been resolved.\n\nAnother purchaser reports ads after paying. Separate accounts describe a 30-day ticket or recurring removal period rather than a permanent unlock. Current price and entitlement terms are not verified, and Apple Arcade membership should not be assumed to cover this particular app’s ads.\n\nA positive counterpoint considers ads limited for a free game. That disagreement prevents a single universal schedule. Audio complaints are more specific: one player says commercials interrupt an audiobook despite their preference for quiet play.\n\nThe US download is free, and offline play is advertised. A reviewer explicitly updates their account to confirm airplane-mode play, but not every connected feature has been tested. Consider the actual mode selection and commercial layer, not just nostalgia for the brand.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Tetris has recognizable line-clearing appeal and a documented Marathon option. The sample makes mode changes and ad-closing reliability central selection issues, particularly for players returning for a familiar short-run format.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does it include Marathon?\n\nYes. The listing advertises Marathon, and a reviewer explicitly describes playing it.\n\n### Did support fix the oversized ads?\n\nOne reviewer says support reached out and hopes for a fix. That is not a confirmed resolution.\n\n### Can it work in airplane mode?\n\nOne reviewer explicitly reports that it can, alongside the developer’s offline claim. Complete feature coverage remains untested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/tetris/id1491074310?uo=4)."
