    "bodyMarkdown": "# Nonogram.com - Number Games: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nNonogram.com - Number Games turns numerical clues into a picture. Fill cells that belong to the image and mark excluded cells with an X, using the row and column information together.\n\n## Numbers describe the picture’s structure\n\nUnlike a coloring page that directly labels every area, this format asks you to infer which squares are shaded. The description tells you to read column clues top to bottom and row clues left to right.\n\nThe official image shows groups of numbers beside a grid containing both filled cells and X marks. It makes the two-state reasoning task visible: deciding where not to color is part of solving, not merely an absence of action.\n\nA useful conclusion in one direction can constrain the other. That intersection is the central deductive appeal, rather than arithmetic addition or finding matching numbers. The supplied text does not provide a full tutorial on every run-spacing rule, so exact advanced techniques are not promised here.\n\n## Assistance and mistakes are separate systems\n\nHints are advertised when stuck, and auto-crosses help fill exclusions on correctly colored lines. Auto-crosses are a convenience for recording known information, not proof that the app solves the entire board for you.\n\nThe listing refers to obtaining extra lives after mistakes, and the screenshot shows three hearts. That makes a blanket no-penalty recommendation inappropriate. The precise failure budget, replenishment method, and cost of extra lives are not documented.\n\nThe distinction matters if you prefer exploring possibilities freely. A deduction puzzle can look quiet while still imposing consequences for an incorrect fill.\n\n## Daily and competitive goals\n\nThousands of puzzles, different difficulties, and recurring updates are advertised. Daily challenges offer monthly trophies, seasonal events provide postcards, and tournaments reward completing pictures, with harder puzzles yielding more points.\n\nThose layers supply reasons to return beyond the final image. They do not establish that the ordinary board has a countdown; a time-limited event is a different constraint from a timed individual solve.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, purchases, removal offers, offline support, and hint limits remain unspecified. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to verify fairness, readable clues, or technical polish.\n\nConsider Nonogram.com if crossing numerical information into a picture sounds rewarding. Readers seeking freeform coloring or a confirmed unlimited-mistake mode should choose on the actual listed rules rather than the relaxed art presentation.\n\n## Verdict\n\nNonogram.com offers a clearly pictured fill-or-exclude deduction task with daily and competitive layers. Lives and assistance conditions deserve attention, especially for solvers who want experimentation without penalties.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What does an X mean?\n\nThe listing says to mark a square with X when you determine it should not be shaded.\n\n### Does the app have lives?\n\nThe description mentions extra lives after mistakes, and the image shows hearts. Exact recovery rules are not documented.\n\n### Are all puzzles timed?\n\nThe listing describes time-limited events but does not establish a countdown for every ordinary puzzle.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/nonogram-com-number-games/id1452992954?uo=4)."
