    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong Delight: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-04-27–2026-11-02 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 5.6.2–5.8.0. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMahjong Delight by Dogmelon presents mahjong solitaire as a straightforward matching activity: find free tiles and eliminate matching pairs. Its most useful review debate is about preserving that uncluttered experience.\n\n## A solitaire board, not four-player mahjong\n\nThe description explicitly identifies Mahjong Solitaire, also known as Shanghai or Taipei. The inspected image shows a layered tile arrangement on a plain background, with small controls rather than a character-driven adventure.\n\nOne reviewer likes the original tile format and the fact that play is not organized as conventional levels. Another praises the available backgrounds, colors, and tile setups. These are specific presentation preferences, not a complete catalog of current customization.\n\nA long-term player enjoys improving their best time and appreciates the textured parchment and tile sounds. Their account of relief during personal hardship remains a personal experience, not clinical evidence of treatment.\n\n## Zoom can matter before the final tiles\n\nA detailed reviewer wants manual zoom restored, saying automatic enlargement occurs only near the end, when they need it less. Another dislikes the newly expanding tiles. Those comments show that simply making tiles larger at some point does not solve every visibility preference.\n\nFireworks introduced around late July also draw objections from players wanting a quiet display. A reviewer’s migraine concern is not a medical finding, but the animation preference is relevant to choosing a visually calm matching game.\n\n## Version timing prevents a blanket bug verdict\n\nSeveral July entries on version 5.7.0 describe black screens when starting or restarting a deal. An early August account on 5.7.1 reports slow tile population and poor touch response.\n\nLater reviews on 5.7.2 and 5.8.0 include positive reactions. They do not specifically confirm that those defects were fixed, and the older reports do not independently establish that they persist on the current release.\n\n## Ads and clock language are mixed\n\nOne player reports no commercials, while another accepts an ad between games and a third complains of too many. Current frequency and paid-removal terms are unresolved. A review titled Subscription contains only a single character in its body, which is not enough to establish billing terms.\n\nTiming accounts also vary: one praises no timer, others race personal bests or criticize timekeeping. An elapsed record and a countdown are different systems, and the supplied evidence does not settle all mode behavior.\n\nThe US download is free. Consider Mahjong Delight for classic matching and customization, while checking current zoom, animation, and technical behavior rather than assuming the simple presentation guarantees them.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong Delight has meaningful support for its classic tile format, but visibility changes deserve close attention. Dated technical complaints and contrasting ad accounts should remain contextual, not become universal current-state claims.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this traditional multiplayer mahjong?\n\nNo. The developer describes mahjong solitaire: matching and removing free tiles.\n\n### Is manual zoom confirmed available?\n\nA reviewer asks for it to return and criticizes automatic endgame zoom. Current availability is unverified.\n\n### Were July’s black-screen problems fixed?\n\nLater positive reviews exist, but they do not explicitly verify a fix to those particular reports.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-delight/id490142440?uo=4)."
