    "bodyMarkdown": "# Drag n Merge: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2024-02-11–2026-10-15 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.9.56–2.9.84. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nDrag n Merge makes its central action clear: drag a numbered block onto another with the same value to create a larger number. The surrounding review history is more complicated than that simple instruction.\n\n## A merger rather than a placement-only board\n\nThe listing advertises equal-number merging, online rankings, and a wooden presentation. The official image shows a finger dragging one numbered tile toward its match above a stack of different values.\n\nA reviewer enjoys chasing larger results, while another likes the pressure of the timer. Other accounts describe blocks continuing to arrive. Those reports and the pictured clock are reasons not to market the whole experience as universally untimed.\n\nExact spawn rules, maximum values, and the current mode structure are not supplied. A request to raise the number ceiling is not proof of a current universal cap.\n\n## Long-term affection survives many complaints\n\nSeveral critical reviewers explicitly say they have played for years. One values the clicking sounds when tiles merge and is disappointed when they disappear. Another describes an immersive puzzle when playing in airplane mode.\n\nThat support identifies a real attraction in the numerical routine, even when the same reviewers reject the commercial experience. Claims of improved intelligence are not independently measured results.\n\nThe sample spans February 2024 through October 2026. Older update complaints should not automatically be described as a current defect affecting all devices.\n\n## Purchases and rewarded continues need separating\n\nMany players report ads returning after a purchase, a new phone, or reinstallation. A lengthy review reproduces support correspondence distinguishing removable pop-ups from rewarded revive videos and describing a restored revive button.\n\nThat is correspondence quoted by a reviewer, not a directly authenticated current policy. It provides context without proving that every purchaser’s complaint was only a misunderstanding. Some explicitly describe forced interruptions or unsuccessful restoration.\n\nThe US download is free. Historical dollar amounts are not treated as current checkout prices, and the scope of any present removal offer is unverified.\n\n## Continuity matters in a score chase\n\nOne player reports losing a score when pausing and resuming. Another says blocks kept dropping behind an obstructing ad. These are specific threats to maintaining a run, not reproduced defects.\n\nMultiple 2024 reviews also describe the app pausing podcasts or music. Those conflict with the needs of readers using a familiar puzzle alongside audio. Later complaints about missing game sounds concern a different direction of audio failure.\n\nOffline play is advertised and supported by one airplane-mode account, but full feature coverage remains untested. Consider the merge loop only alongside current checks of sound, score persistence, and purchase restoration.\n\n## Verdict\n\nDrag n Merge has long-term supporters of its numerical loop, but the historical sample raises unusually important continuity issues. A pleasant merger is less useful if ads, audio behavior, or saved scores interrupt the run.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What combines into a larger block?\n\nThe listing says to drag a block onto another with the same number.\n\n### Does no-ads payment remove rewarded revives?\n\nA reviewer quotes support saying it does not. That historical correspondence is not independently authenticated current policy.\n\n### Can it be played without Wi-Fi?\n\nThe developer says Wi-Fi is not required, and one reviewer describes airplane-mode play. Online rankings naturally remain a separate feature.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/drag-n-merge/id1447871511?uo=4)."
