    "bodyMarkdown": "# Screw Out: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2025-07-26–2026-10-10 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.61.23, 1.61.25. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nScrew Out places colored fasteners across overlapping shapes, with a matching-looking collection area above. Its official image is more concrete about the board than the description’s repeated nuts-and-bolts language.\n\n## Plan removals without inventing the whole rulebook\n\nThe inspected promotional board is a large radial arrangement of translucent pieces held by colored screws. A purple three-hole box and a row of spare-looking positions sit overhead. This supports a color-oriented screw-removal family, not a wooden block-placement recommendation.\n\nThe written description advertises boosters, bonus levels, coins, and a win-streak reward system. It does not clearly specify collection-box rotation, exact storage capacity, or which exposed screws are legal to remove.\n\nOne reviewer says the game makes them think ahead, and another enjoys the balance of challenge at a high level. Those comments support a planning attraction without substituting for complete verified rules.\n\n## Bombs complicate the relaxed pitch\n\nAn invested player objects to bombs, while another describes two countdowns starting together on level 222. That board-specific report is not independently reproduced and does not prove that all levels are impossible without help.\n\nAnother reviewer reports a free-looking screw that will not come out. The complaint is relevant to interpreting visible availability, but its cause is unresolved.\n\nA brief color-vision warning also appears. Given how prominently differently colored screws feature in the image, readers who struggle to distinguish hues should inspect the current noncolor cues rather than assume broad accessibility.\n\n## A quiet opening may not represent later ads\n\nSeveral reviewers say commercial breaks begin or become frequent around level 20. Their accounts differ in duration and placement; one tolerates a commercial after each level, while others reject ads interrupting play.\n\nThose are reported patterns, not a measured schedule for every installation. An assertion that early ratings are manipulated is not treated as a verified explanation of the store’s overall score.\n\nOne purchaser says bonus rewards remain behind videos after payment. That is a different issue from whether a paid offer removes automatic pop-ups. Other reviews disagree about whether a permanent removal option exists, so no current price or permanence is promised.\n\nThe US download is free. Offline support and ad-closing reliability have not been tested. A reviewer’s warning about a supposed mandatory update to a different app is not adopted as a legitimate update instruction.\n\nConsider Screw Out if visual fastener planning is appealing. Readers avoiding countdown elements, hue dependence, or video-linked bonuses should check those details before assuming the calm-looking board meets their needs.\n\n## Verdict\n\nScrew Out has credible supporters of its think-ahead challenge. Bomb, color-visibility, and commercial-layer reports prevent a simple relaxing-puzzle recommendation, while the exact removal rules remain incompletely documented.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does every level have a timer?\n\nThat is not established. Reviewers specifically mention bomb countdowns, which are not proof of a universal level timer.\n\n### Does payment unlock all bonuses without videos?\n\nOne purchaser explicitly says bonuses remain video-linked. Current offer coverage is not independently verified.\n\n### Are color-vision accommodations confirmed?\n\nNo. A reviewer warns about color vision, and the image prominently uses colored screws; alternative cues were not tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/screw-out/id6502940202?uo=4)."
