    "bodyMarkdown": "# Water Sort Puzzle - Sort Color: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nWater Sort Puzzle - Sort Color gives unusually explicit instructions for a familiar sorting scene. Move layered liquid among bottles until each color is organized, while obeying space and top-color restrictions.\n\n## Match the receiving color and preserve room\n\nTap a source bottle, then a destination. The description says their top colors must match and the receiving bottle must have enough space. Full containers cannot accept more liquid.\n\nThe inspected image shows a tilted bottle over a colored band in another, several mixed containers, and an outlined empty bottle. It supports the transfer-and-storage concept. The text does not separately explain every empty-bottle exception, so that detail is not invented as a complete legal-move tutorial.\n\nCapacity is the useful planning constraint. Moving a band may uncover a needed color while occupying the room required for another transfer. Merely finding a color match is not necessarily the same as preserving a productive sequence.\n\n## Restarting is explicitly part of the offer\n\nUnlike sparse descriptions that only say sort water, this listing promises no timer, no penalties, and the ability to restart when stuck. Those are developer claims rather than observed test results, but they are specific enough to help distinguish the intended pace.\n\nAn untimed board can still be difficult. The developer says higher levels require more care, but the actual progression has not been audited. No claim is made that every board has one solution or that careless moves cannot create a dead end.\n\nThe screenshot includes a curved-arrow control. Its presence does not establish unlimited undo, the cost of using it, or a guarantee that every mistake can be reversed without restarting.\n\n## Simple input is not an accessibility test\n\nThe developer promotes tap-based, one-finger control. That describes an input method, not independent proof of comfortable one-handed reach on every device. It also does not settle contrast or whether the color bands have alternative labels.\n\nThe promises to train critical thinking are similarly broader than the evidence. The rule set involves planning, but no cognitive improvement has been measured here.\n\nThe US download is free, and offline play is expressly advertised. Ad frequency, optional purchases, removal terms, and complete offline feature coverage remain untested. No customer review bodies are available in this sample to add player observations.\n\nConsider this Water Sort for its documented no-countdown design and explicit transfer conditions. Do not interpret no penalties as a guarantee of no ads, unlimited helpers, or effortless success.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Water Sort listing provides a comparatively clear sorting contract: top-color compatibility, limited capacity, and restarts without a timer. Its commercial layer and assistance allowances remain separate unknowns.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### When can you pour into another filled bottle?\n\nThe description requires matching top colors and enough receiving capacity.\n\n### Is there a timer?\n\nThe developer explicitly says there is no timer. That claim was not independently playtested.\n\n### Can you restart after getting stuck?\n\nYes. Restarting at any time is explicitly offered; unlimited undo is not established.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/water-sort-puzzle-sort-color/id1597897013?uo=4)."
