    "bodyMarkdown": "# Wood Block Puzzle: Brain Games: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nWood Block Puzzle: Brain Games uses a smaller 8×8 playing field for a familiar task: place shaped pieces, complete lines, and preserve enough room for what comes next.\n\n## Space, not a countdown, ends the run\n\nDrag and drop pieces into the grid. Completed rows or columns disappear, and combos raise the score. The game ends when there is no space for a new block, according to the description.\n\nThat loss condition matters alongside the developer’s unlimited, stress-free wording. No time or step limit does not mean a single run cannot end. Placement errors can still narrow the board until no incoming shape fits.\n\nThe inspected image shows a wood-toned grid, a highlighted piece being positioned, a score above, and additional shapes beneath. It supports the placement premise without establishing the future-piece sequence or a guaranteed fair generator.\n\n## Clear lines while preserving useful gaps\n\nThe compact board puts different shape footprints into direct competition. Completing one row can open room, but leaving fragmented holes may make later pieces awkward to fit. That follows from the listed spatial task rather than a tested high-score strategy.\n\nRotation, piece replacement, undo, and the ability to select shapes in any order are not documented. They should not be imported from other block games or guessed from the presence of a settings icon.\n\nThe description calls for combos but does not give their multiplier formula or the timing needed to maintain them. No particular score target or maximum run length is promised.\n\n## A Journey alongside the score chase\n\nA new themed Journey each week is advertised as a way to collect jigsaw pieces. That gives the app a progression layer beyond the board score, but it does not establish a standalone jigsaw mode or whether all Journey content is permanently available.\n\nThe developer also promotes wood-carving sounds. That is a presentation claim, not an independently assessed ASMR benefit. The IQ and brain-training language does not establish cognitive testing or measured improvement.\n\nThe US download is free. Offline play is explicitly advertised, while ad behavior, optional purchases, paid removal, and complete offline event access remain unspecified.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample. Consider this title for its defined grid size and no-countdown placement goal. Readers seeking rotation, penalty-free experimentation, or a confirmed ad-free experience should not assume those features from the soothing wooden theme.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWood Block Brain Games makes its core board and failure condition clear. The 8×8 score chase and weekly collection layer are concrete attractions, with helpers and commercial behavior still undocumented.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How large is the board?\n\nThe developer specifies an 8×8 grid.\n\n### Does no time limit mean you cannot lose?\n\nNo. The listing says a game ends when no space remains for a new block.\n\n### Is Journey a confirmed separate jigsaw game?\n\nThe description says weekly themed Journeys collect jigsaw pieces; it does not fully describe a separate jigsaw mode.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wood-block-puzzle-brain-games/id6464458481?uo=4)."
