    "bodyMarkdown": "# Words of Wonders: Guru: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-06-12–2026-11-05 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.1.27–3.5.0. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWords of Wonders: Guru gives its letter wheel an additional prompt: a clue pointing toward the answer. It is neither a plain word-search grid nor simply every possible word from a random letter bank.\n\n## The clue narrows the scramble\n\nThe listing describes connecting letters, trivia, and crossword-style progression against nature and historical backgrounds. The official image asks for something imaginary, displays five answer spaces, and shows DREAM traced from the wheel.\n\nThat example makes the hybrid clear: consider meaning, choose the available letters, and fit the result into the crossing layout. It does not establish whether every level supplies the same amount of clue information.\n\nOne reviewer specifically reminds other players to check the question when stuck. Another enjoys figuring out scrambled letters, while a separate account likes hints that guide them toward guessing rather than immediately remove the task.\n\n## Support is specific, even when reviews are short\n\nMany positive entries simply say the game is fun. More informative ones praise calming play, soothing sound, interesting clues, or making it part of an evening routine. These explain the attraction better than treating repeated star ratings as a feature audit.\n\nOne player values the absence of cash-earning pop-ups, while another asks how to be paid. Neither establishes a real-money reward feature. The supplied description offers a word game, not a verified earning program.\n\nThe developer advertises native content in multiple languages. Language availability and translation quality have not been checked, and educational enthusiasm does not prove measured spelling improvement.\n\n## Ads can interrupt the clue-solving loop\n\nCritical reviewers describe commercials between rounds or after completed words. One says ads eventually require restarting the app, and another reports an iPad lockup. These are reported failures, not reproduced defects or a universal ad schedule.\n\nA detailed account alleges fraudulent destinations in advertisements. Those destinations were not independently investigated, so the accusation is not presented as an established legal or security finding.\n\nAnother player says completed daily puzzles are erased and must be repeated. That is a progress-persistence complaint; their suggestion that it is intentional remains unverified.\n\nThe US download is free. Present removal prices, purchase coverage, and offline support are unresolved. The sample spans multiple releases from June through November 2026, so not every report describes the same build.\n\nConsider Guru when a semantic clue makes letter connecting more interesting. Readers wanting uninterrupted vocabulary practice should weigh the specific ad-closing and daily-progress accounts rather than assuming the scenic presentation guarantees continuity.\n\n## Verdict\n\nGuru’s clue-and-letter combination gives it a useful identity, with reviewers supporting the hints and calming routine. Interruptions and daily-progress reports are the practical reservations, not an unsupported judgment of every puzzle’s quality.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does Guru provide clues as well as letters?\n\nYes. The official image pairs a meaning prompt with an answer grid and letter wheel, and reviewers discuss the questions and hints.\n\n### Does it pay real money?\n\nNo verified cash-reward feature is established. A review asking about payment is not proof of one.\n\n### Are daily completions reliably preserved?\n\nOne reviewer reports erased daily progress. Persistence has not been independently tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/words-of-wonders-guru/id1540919737?uo=4)."
