    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block Crush: Place the Blocks, Do Not Wait for Them to Fall\n\nBlock Crush is a placement puzzle, not a documented falling-block action game. That distinction is central to this review because several players downloaded it expecting a different style of block game. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Space is the resource\n\nThe US listing describes dragging shapes onto a board and completing full rows or columns to clear them. You continue while useful space remains, with a separate daily challenge offering refreshed layouts. No time limit and offline play are advertised.\n\nThe immediate question is whether a shape fits; the more interesting question is what that placement leaves behind. A nearly completed row can be valuable, but so can an open area that accommodates several future shapes. Chasing one clear without considering the remaining gaps can work against the longer run.\n\nThe description does not establish board dimensions, piece rotation, or a falling-piece mode. A familiar genre label should not fill in those omissions.\n\n## The expectation gap is unusually specific\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-22 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.2.2, 4.2.3, 4.2.4, 4.2.5, 4.2.6, 4.2.7; the listing records version 4.2.8. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers in this sample describe engaging, relaxing play and a game enjoyable to share with a family member. Those positive accounts concern the placement activity itself.\n\nSeveral others say the advertising suggested blocks falling from above, while the downloaded game instead asks them to place shapes. One explicitly contrasts its behavior with a Tetris-style presentation. These are reports about their expectations, not an independent inspection of the promotional material.\n\nThat difference helps explain why the same app can be enjoyable to one person and disappointing to another. Someone choosing deliberate spatial planning may welcome the actual premise; someone seeking fast falling-block reactions may not.\n\n## No Ads Mode is not independently confirmed\n\nThe listing names a No Ads Mode. Yet one reviewer says there is no way to pay for removal, while others report advertisements during levels or after successful and failed boards. The evidence does not resolve whether the named mode has different availability or access conditions.\n\nA mode label is therefore not a guarantee of universally ad-free play. Exact ad intervals and current prices also remain unverified. The sample includes much stronger claims about advertising than about the game's basic line-clearing logic.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlock Crush may suit players who want untimed shape placement and a high-score-style space-management challenge. It is a weaker fit for anyone specifically seeking falling blocks or relying on the No Ads Mode phrase without checking it. The central puzzle has supporters; the mismatch between expectation and access deserves equal attention.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do blocks continuously fall from the top?\nThe listing documents placement, and several reviewers say it differs from the falling-block game they expected.\n\n### What clears space?\nCompleting full rows or columns, according to the developer.\n\n### Can pieces rotate?\nThat rule is not established in the supplied description.\n\n### Is No Ads Mode verified?\nNo. It is advertised, but some player reports conflict with an uninterrupted experience.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-crush/id1638139403?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
