    "bodyMarkdown": "# Fillwords: Trace the Word Instead of Typing It\n\nFillwords describes word finding as a tracing activity. You connect letters with a line to uncover answers, rather than type a guess or work through a conventional crossword clue list. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Find the path through the letters\n\nThe listing describes a grid containing hidden words and allows connecting letters in any direction. It advertises more than 2,000 levels, ranging from easier tasks to more demanding puzzles.\n\nThat gives the format a visual and vocabulary component. A likely word has to be recognized in the available letters, but recognizing a word and seeing a valid path to trace it are not identical tasks.\n\nThe description does not fully explain whether paths can bend within one answer, whether cells can be reused, or whether every cell must belong to a solution. Any-direction language should not be turned into a precise rulebook beyond what it actually says.\n\n## Choosing a level is part of the appeal\n\nThe developer says every level is available immediately. If implemented as described, that distinguishes the app from a strictly gated sequence where one difficult puzzle prevents trying anything else.\n\nIt may suit someone who prefers choosing a challenge or revisiting a familiar board. It does not independently establish how progress is recorded, whether solutions are replayable, or which assistance is available when a player is stuck.\n\nTen languages are advertised, including English, French, German, Spanish, and Ukrainian. Language support can broaden the audience, but it does not prove that every dictionary is equally comprehensive or that a particular regional spelling will be accepted.\n\n## No player digest is available\n\nThere are no usable customer-review bodies in this evidence set. It is therefore not possible to report whether players find the vocabulary fresh, paths intuitive, or later levels well graded. The description's claims about attention and thinking speed are not measured outcomes.\n\nOffline play is explicitly promoted. No disconnected session was performed, so the exact scope of that capability remains unverified.\n\nThe metadata lists a free download, while the supplied text does not establish ad frequency, purchases, hint pricing, or removal terms. Immediate level access is a progression claim, not a statement that every surrounding feature is free.\n\n## Verdict\n\nFillwords is a plausible fit for players who like finding words by tracing letters and prefer flexibility over strict level gating. It is less conclusive for anyone who needs a particular path rule or verified dictionary behavior. The input style and advertised immediate access are its most useful documented distinctions.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you type the answers?\nThe listing emphasizes connecting letters with a line.\n\n### Are all levels advertised as available immediately?\nYes. That is a specific developer claim.\n\n### Can a word path bend?\nThe text says any direction but does not fully define within-word turning rules.\n\n### Is offline play verified?\nIt is advertised, not independently tested.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/fillwords-word-search-puzzles/id1197961218?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
