    "bodyMarkdown": "# Brain Who?: The Clue Hunt Competes With the Ad Break\n\nBrain Who? asks players to notice a clue that makes a small mystery click. Its sample suggests that the interruption between mysteries can matter as much as the quality of an individual answer. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Inspect the words and the picture\n\nThe listing emphasizes questions about identities, liars, and culprits, with clues hidden in pictures, words, and prompts. Some decisions are described as a choice between two options.\n\nThe basic attraction is reinterpretation: an apparently ordinary detail may change which explanation makes sense. It is not a documented standardized IQ test, regardless of how often the promotional language invokes intelligence.\n\nThe supplied description does not establish every gesture or the exact answer tolerance. Those details matter when knowing what should happen and successfully making it happen are different problems.\n\n## Some praise survives a strongly critical sample\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-05-19 through 2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.4.5, 2.4.6, 2.4.7, 2.4.8, 2.5.0, 2.5.1, 2.5.2, 2.5.3; the listing records version 2.5.4. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSeveral reviewers describe the puzzles as fun or say the game could be enjoyable with fewer interruptions. One calls the early rounds cool before losing interest in the ad load. That supports a limited attraction to the riddle concept, not broad approval of the whole app.\n\nA more specific critique says the game fails to recognize an attempted solution, then uses a hint to recommend the same action. This is a player report, not a verified input defect, but it identifies a meaningful risk for visual riddles: rejection can feel arbitrary if the intended action was already understood.\n\nOther accounts find the rounds too easy or uninteresting. The sample does not support a calibrated claim about improving IQ or reasoning ability.\n\n## A weekly package does not resolve every ad question\n\nThe listing describes an auto-renewing weekly premium package with ad-free access and extra features, but does not give a current price in the supplied text. Reviewer-mentioned prices are historical reports, not a verified universal offer.\n\nSome paid players say advertisements remain. One distinguishes bonus-prize videos from moving to the next level. That distinction matters because an optional reward ad may fall outside a removal entitlement, though the actual terms were not independently inspected.\n\nNumerous comments describe ads after rounds or hints. Their exact estimated durations should not become a measured schedule.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBrain Who? may interest players who enjoy quick visual deduction and unusual interpretations. It is a more cautious choice for someone who wants sustained concentration or predictable answer recognition. The sample contains some enjoyment of the riddles, but its much stronger theme is frustration with the time spent reaching the next one.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are the puzzles mainly visual clues?\nThe listing combines clues in pictures, words, and questions.\n\n### Is this a validated IQ test?\nNo. No standardized assessment evidence is provided.\n\n### What premium period is described?\nAn auto-renewing weekly subscription, without a verified current price here.\n\n### Does removal necessarily eliminate bonus videos?\nThat is not established; a reviewer specifically distinguishes them from ordinary progression.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/brain-who-tricky-riddle-tests/id1526842079?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
