    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong Solitaire Card Games: Pairs With a Reversible Path\n\nMahjong Solitaire Card Games offers several practical claims around a familiar pair-clearing objective. Free unlimited undo and tiles that grow as the board empties are more useful distinctions than its broad promise of a mental workout. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Remove a pair to reveal another possibility\n\nThe listing describes layouts containing up to 144 tiles. Match identical symbols or matching themes to remove a pair, then clear the arrangement to complete the stage.\n\nThe stated availability rule says a tile must not be covered by another. It does not fully document a side-access restriction, so the usual rules of other Mahjong solitaire apps should not be added without checking.\n\nRemoving a pair can matter beyond that immediate success because it exposes more of the structure. The interesting decision is which available match reveals the most useful next choice. This is an interpretation of the described layered format, not a tested strategy for a particular layout.\n\n## Undo can support learning through alternatives\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises free, unlimited undo. That could let a player compare branches after discovering an unhelpful match rather than immediately abandoning the entire board.\n\nIt does not establish that every other booster is also free or unlimited. Nor does the claim of more than 10,000 solvable levels prove that every sequence of available pairs reaches a solution.\n\nDaily challenges, event badges, three tile/background styles, and adaptive tile sizing are also advertised. Enlarging the remaining tiles may help inspection near the end, although no screenshot or interface test establishes actual readability.\n\n## A useful feature list, not a player verdict\n\nNo usable review bodies are available here. The evidence cannot establish whether players find the layouts varied, the undo controls convenient, or the resizing comfortable. A senior-friendly or relaxing presentation would not by itself answer those questions.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, removal offers, and the price of additional assistance are not established in the supplied description. Free undo should remain the specific claim it is, not expand into a promise that every aspect of access is unrestricted.\n\nThe app is described as tile-pair solitaire, not a documented competitive four-player Mahjong game.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Mahjong Solitaire app is most relevant to players who like revisiting a decision and trying another route through a layered board. It is less conclusive for someone requiring independently verified readability or a completely specified traditional ruleset. Unlimited undo and adaptive sizing are concrete claims worth examining first.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many tiles can a board contain?\nThe listing says up to 144.\n\n### Is undo advertised as unlimited?\nYes. It is specifically described as free and unlimited.\n\n### Are side-access restrictions fully documented?\nNo. The text clearly mentions uncovered tiles but does not fully explain side access.\n\n### Is this four-player Mahjong?\nThe documented objective is solitaire-style matching and clearing.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-solitaire-card-games/id6504851426?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
