    "bodyMarkdown": "# Traffic Puzzle: Match Cars to Open the Road\n\nTraffic Puzzle combines matching with traffic clearance rather than merely asking you to slide vehicles out of a parking lot. Its description makes the three-car match the central action. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Clear a color to serve a mission\n\nThe listing describes swiping and matching three or more cars of the same color. Stages have limited moves, and clearing cars contributes to objectives involving police cars, fire trucks, ambulances, trains, helicopters, and trams.\n\nThe useful match is therefore not necessarily the biggest available one. A smaller action that opens the required route may serve the mission better than clearing an unrelated section. That is a design implication of the stated goals, not a tested account of a particular level.\n\nThe supplied text does not fully explain how new cars enter the board or whether every interaction uses a conventional adjacent swap. It should not be expanded into an exact movement system beyond the documented swipe-and-match concept.\n\n## Different objectives can change the priority\n\nHelping an emergency vehicle reach its destination asks a different question from clearing a broad area. The developer also mentions planting grass as part of improving the city, suggesting that the campaign uses more than one visual goal.\n\nThose objectives may appeal to someone who enjoys a familiar matching action with a concrete reason for each clear. Someone who wants a pure unblocking puzzle without color matching may prefer another traffic format.\n\nDespite the car-jam vocabulary, the supplied description does not establish a bus passenger queue, seat capacities, or a limited matching holder. Those are different puzzle systems and should not be imported from nearby titles.\n\n## Limited moves do not prove paid difficulty\n\nBoosters and progression are advertised, but their costs and availability are not fully documented. A move limit establishes a constraint; it does not establish that every hard stage requires payment.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in this collection. There is no supported player digest about mission variety, fairness, repetition, or control responsiveness. Awards and best-game language in the description remain promotional rather than independent editorial findings.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, removal terms, offline support, and stability are not verified by the supplied evidence.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTraffic Puzzle looks most suitable for players who want matching decisions tied to opening routes and completing specific transport missions. It is less clearly suited to someone seeking a straightforward parking-lot slider or passenger-sort game. The mission-driven match-3 identity is clear; its actual balance and access conditions remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What forms a match?\nThree or more cars of the same color, according to the listing.\n\n### Are moves limited?\nYes. Limited moves per stage are explicitly described.\n\n### Is this a bus-boarding puzzle?\nNo passenger-boarding or seat-capacity system is established in the supplied text.\n\n### Do boosters prove payment is required?\nNo. Their presence alone does not establish a universal purchase requirement.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/traffic-puzzle-car-jam-escape/id1471999779?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
