    "bodyMarkdown": "# Drop The Number: A Merge Game That Keeps Falling\n\nDrop The Number is not simply a board where numbered tiles wait for your next move. Player accounts describe falling blocks, making interruption behavior especially important to its merge-and-placement loop. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Check the next number before committing\n\nThe listing describes dropping and merging numbered blocks toward values such as 2,048 and beyond. A preview shows the next block, while scores and global rankings provide a continuing goal.\n\nThe preview matters because the current move can create a useful landing opportunity for what follows. A merge that looks attractive now may leave an awkward arrangement for the next number.\n\nThe description does not fully document every merge direction or failure threshold. One reviewer contrasts the actual falling action with an advertisement that allowed more leisurely merging; that report should not become an independently measured fall speed.\n\n## Relaxation and responsiveness do not always align\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-09-06 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.5.0, 2.5.2, 2.5.3, 2.5.4, 2.5.5, 2.5.6, 2.5.7, 2.6.0, 2.6.1, 2.6.3, 2.6.4, 2.6.6, 2.6.8, 2.6.9; the listing records version 2.6.9. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts describe a relaxing challenge and a background activity suitable for listening to something else. Several critical reviewers still say they like the core game.\n\nTheir reservations concern freezing, progress resetting, and advertisements that appear without pausing the falling blocks. If that reported behavior occurs, the issue is not merely waiting through a break: pieces can land where the player did not intend.\n\nThese are unverified experiences across a sample extending into 2025, not a current device test or proof that every account is affected. The date range matters particularly for claims tied to an update.\n\n## Membership terms are more concrete than removal outcomes\n\nThe US listing describes a $1.99 weekly Diamond membership after a three-day trial, a $5.99 monthly option, and $49.99 yearly. These are auto-renewing source-listed terms, not a verified checkout.\n\nSome purchasers say they still encounter ads, including videos associated with claiming points. Others report missing rewarded tools. The evidence does not settle which products eliminate which interruptions or whether optional videos remain outside removal.\n\nOffline play is advertised, but no disconnected test establishes its scope. It should not be used to dismiss the sampled interruption concerns.\n\n## Verdict\n\nDrop The Number may suit players who like merging under a falling-block rhythm and planning with a preview. It is a more cautious choice for someone who wants every move to wait indefinitely or depends on uninterrupted control. The sample supports the concept's appeal while raising practical concerns precisely where timing matters.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is there a next-block preview?\nYes. The developer explicitly advertises it.\n\n### Is this definitely a turn-only board?\nNo. Sampled players describe blocks falling during play.\n\n### What weekly membership price is listed?\n$1.99 after a three-day trial, with monthly and yearly alternatives.\n\n### Are ad-related lost moves independently verified?\nNo. They are player reports, not findings from a controlled test.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/drop-the-number-merge-puzzle/id1454637812?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
