    "bodyMarkdown": "# Screw It Out!: Remove the Fastener, Find Its Box\n\nScrew It Out! gives its hardware puzzle a second destination. Removing a fastener is not enough by itself: the listing says the removed nuts must go into the right boxes to win. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Dismantling and assignment share the objective\n\nThe developer describes untwisting screws and placing nuts into appropriate boxes across hundreds of levels. A wrong move can create a jam, suggesting that the order of removal matters to continued progress.\n\nThe general appeal is a dependency puzzle. An available piece may be easy to remove but not necessarily the most useful next choice if its destination or the remaining arrangement becomes difficult.\n\nThat is a reading of the stated premise, not a tested account of the game's storage system. The supplied description does not specify box capacities, matching colors, temporary slots, or exactly how a jam occurs. Those familiar rules from other screw games should not be imported here.\n\n## Hardware imagery does not define the physics\n\nThe description uses pins, nuts, bolts, and screws somewhat interchangeably. It gives a clear dismantling theme without providing a technical simulation of real fasteners.\n\nSomeone who enjoys deciding what to remove next may like that premise. Someone looking for accurate mechanics, construction education, or a free-form tool simulator should not assume those features from the hardware language.\n\nLikewise, a claim of bright, satisfying visuals is promotional rather than an independent visual assessment. No screenshot was inspected, so specific materials, colors, or plate arrangements are not described as observed details.\n\n## A thin rulebook leaves important choices open\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available here. There is no supported player assessment of puzzle variety, responsiveness, or whether a failed sequence gives useful feedback.\n\nThe supplied text also does not establish hints, undo, a timer, or how a stage can be restarted. Those details affect whether experimentation feels forgiving or costly.\n\nThe metadata records a free download, but advertising frequency, purchases, removal offers, and offline support are not documented in the description. The promise of stress relief is an intended mood, not proof of an interruption-free session or a health benefit.\n\n## Verdict\n\nScrew It Out! is most relevant to players interested in removal order combined with a box-assignment goal. It is less conclusive for someone choosing based on a specific color-tray system or forgiving recovery tools. The basic concept is identifiable; the constraints that would make a stronger recommendation remain thinly documented.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is removing every screw the only stated task?\nNo. The listing also says to put the nuts into the right boxes.\n\n### Are box colors and capacities confirmed?\nNo. Those exact rules are not provided.\n\n### Is this a realistic tool simulator?\nThe description presents a puzzle, not a documented mechanical simulation.\n\n### Are undo and hints established?\nThe supplied description does not explain those tools.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/screw-it-out/id6667100711?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
