    "bodyMarkdown": "# Water Sort Puz: A Calm Loop With Color-Visibility Questions\n\nWater Sort Puz offers a clear endpoint for each bottle: one color. Reviewers in this sample often value that simple organization task as a break, while disagreeing about its challenge and presentation. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Give each liquid a home\n\nThe listing describes tapping bottles and sorting their contents until each contains a single color. Rollback, Restart, Color Inversion, and extra tubes provide different kinds of recovery or assistance.\n\nThose tools should not be treated as interchangeable. Restart returns to an earlier setup, while extra capacity changes the space available for solving. The exact effect of Color Inversion and the complete pouring restrictions are not fully explained in the supplied text.\n\nBottle sets, backgrounds, coins, and a progression map add customization and longer-term goals. The developer's catalog counts remain claims, not an independently inspected inventory.\n\n## A useful break is not necessarily a deep challenge\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-01 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.9.1, 4.0.2; the listing records version 4.0.2. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers describe short sessions between tasks, thinking ahead, and a calming sorting rhythm. One finds the levels increasingly challenging; another finds the app boring compared with other sorting games.\n\nA reviewer also says a green shade is too light for older eyes. That is a specific visibility concern, not an assessment of every color or proof that all older players will struggle. It matters because distinguishing the layers is part of understanding the puzzle, not just an aesthetic preference.\n\nNo screenshots or interface controls were visually inspected, so contrast and alternative cues remain unassessed.\n\n## Keep payment claims separate from puzzle feedback\n\nSeveral reviewers complain about ads, including interruptions before finishing short early boards. The sample does not establish a universal frequency or a current removal offer.\n\nSome comments also refer to earning money or payouts. The supplied listing describes a sorting game and coins, not a verified cash-reward program. Those comments cannot support a promise of real-money income or identify the terms of any outside promotion.\n\nThe initial download is free. Assistance costs, removal terms, and the fairness of every later stage are not independently established.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWater Sort Puz may suit players who like incremental color organization and a choice of visual themes. It is less conclusive for someone with specific color-visibility needs or expectations of cash rewards. The sample supports the sorting loop's appeal while giving concrete reasons to check presentation and access early.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the bottle objective?\nFinish with one color in each bottle, according to the listing.\n\n### Are recovery tools advertised?\nYes, including Rollback, Restart, Color Inversion, and adding tubes.\n\n### Are real-money payouts verified?\nNo. Reviewer claims do not establish a cash-reward product or its terms.\n\n### Is every color easy to distinguish?\nThat is not established; one sampled reviewer specifically criticizes a light green shade.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/water-sort-puz-color-game/id1615960653?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
