    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block Buster - No Timer: A Different Kind of Block Problem\n\nBlock Buster - No Timer uses the vocabulary of several block-puzzle families. Its listing mentions sliding, sorting shapes, reaching the top, and finding a way out, without providing a complete rulebook that reconciles every phrase. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Start with the actions that are actually documented\n\nThe developer describes sliding blocks through a grid and planning around shapes and obstacles. A magnet clears blocks of the same color, while a punch removes blocks or obstacles.\n\nThose are more concrete facts than assuming the game is a conventional row-and-column placement puzzle. The supplied description does not explicitly document line-clearing rules, an endless high-score loss condition, or a standard board dimension.\n\nThe title emphasizes no timer, and the broader text promotes patience. That is a useful positioning claim, but it does not establish every mode's constraints or how a blocked position is handled.\n\n## Assistance can change the shape of the problem\n\nA same-color magnet and a targeted removal tool potentially address different obstacles. The former changes a broader color set; the latter may be more relevant to one obstruction.\n\nTheir exact selection controls and resource costs are not documented, so those potential roles should not become a step-by-step strategy. Nor does the existence of boosters prove they are required for difficult stages.\n\nA black mode and offline-friendly play are advertised. These may interest someone who wants a subdued workspace or disconnected play, but no interface or offline session was tested.\n\n## Judge the premise without accepting the superlatives\n\nThe description claims thousands of handcrafted levels and benefits for IQ and problem solving. The level count is an attributed product claim; the cognitive benefits are not validated outcomes.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available here. It is therefore not possible to assess whether players find the movement intuitive, the goals clear, or the progression varied.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising, removal offers, purchases, and the availability of boosters remain unspecified. A no-timer title does not imply a no-ad session.\n\nFor a prospective player, the main distinction is comfort with an incompletely described movement puzzle. If a specific classic block ruleset is essential, the listing alone is not enough to establish it.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlock Buster may interest players who enjoy sliding shapes and using different obstacle-clearing tools. It is a less certain recommendation for anyone expecting standard line placement from the name. The magnet, punch, and subdued-mode claims are concrete; the complete objective and access economy remain unverified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is row clearing explicitly documented?\nNo. The supplied text emphasizes sliding and navigating blocks instead.\n\n### What does the magnet do?\nThe listing says it clears blocks of the same color.\n\n### What does the punch do?\nIt is described as removing blocks and obstacles.\n\n### Does no timer prove no ads?\nNo. Timing and advertising are separate questions.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-buster-no-timer/id6745272388?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
