    "bodyMarkdown": "# Wordus: Six Guesses, Two Useful Kinds of Feedback\n\nWordus offers a familiar deduction structure: submit a word, study the feedback, and make the next guess better. Its listing is specific about six attempts and color meanings, but not every rule surrounding them. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Separate a correct letter from a correct position\n\nThe developer says green identifies a correct letter in the correct place. Orange identifies a letter that belongs in the answer but occupies the wrong position. The target must be found within six guesses.\n\nThat creates an information problem as well as a vocabulary problem. A candidate word may seem plausible yet repeat letters or positions you already know do not work. The useful next guess is one that respects what the feedback has actually established.\n\nThe supplied description does not specify word length, how duplicate letters are handled, or what feedback means when a letter is absent. Those are important details and should not be filled in merely because the format resembles another word game.\n\n## Unlimited words change the daily rhythm\n\nThe listing advertises unlimited words, score sharing, and leaderboards among friends, within a country, or worldwide. That positions it differently from a strictly one-puzzle-per-day routine.\n\nFor someone who likes practicing deduction repeatedly, the ability to continue is potentially valuable. Someone who prefers a single shared daily answer may care more about whether that exact mode exists, which is not established here.\n\nCompetition is also not the same as cooperative play. Sharing a result or comparing rankings does not prove that friends solve a board together in real time.\n\n## Color feedback raises a practical question\n\nBecause the documented feedback relies on green and orange, readability and alternative cues may matter to some players. No screenshot was inspected, and the listing does not establish a colorblind mode or noncolor labels.\n\nThere are no usable customer-review bodies in this collection. There is therefore no supported account of dictionary quality, repeated answers, interface clarity, or ranking fairness.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. The description calls it an online game; it does not establish a verified offline mode. Advertising, paid help, subscriptions, and removal terms are not documented in the supplied text.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWordus is a plausible fit for players who enjoy reducing uncertainty through letter-position feedback and want more than one attempt at the format each day. It is less conclusive for anyone needing a specific dictionary, word length, or accessibility option. The six-guess rule is clear; several consequential details remain unspecified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many guesses do you get?\nThe listing specifies six.\n\n### What does orange mean?\nThe letter is correct but in the wrong position.\n\n### Is the word length confirmed?\nNo. The supplied description does not specify it.\n\n### Is there a verified colorblind mode?\nNo such option is established in the available evidence.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wordus/id1602940346?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
