    "bodyMarkdown": "# Brainium Sudoku: Hints That Aim to Explain the Deduction\n\nBrainium's ⋆Sudoku makes its clearest promise about help: a hint should explain why a number belongs, not merely reveal it. That is a meaningful distinction for someone who wants to become a better solver rather than only finish another grid. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Keep the logic, adjust the support\n\nThe listing describes classic Sudoku with five difficulties, daily puzzles, statistics, notes, and optional error checking. Explanatory hints use puzzle-specific instructions, animations, and visual guidance.\n\nAn explanation can potentially bridge the gap between seeing an answer and understanding a repeatable technique. The supplied evidence does not establish which advanced methods are covered or how effectively the instruction works for an individual learner.\n\nAuto-Fill Notes, Auto-Clear Notes, and Number Painting provide additional input choices. Number Painting is described as rapidly placing a selected number or note across cells, which may suit solvers who scan for one digit at a time.\n\n## Comfort options are unusually detailed\n\nAdjustable font size, light or dark grid fonts, automatic theme changes, two grid styles, and nine backdrops are advertised. The description also names portrait and landscape play, left- or right-handed landscape options, and iPad keyboard compatibility.\n\nThose features address different practical needs. Font size affects inspection, while handedness and keyboard input affect how answers are entered. Their presence in the list does not certify the whole interface for a particular visual or motor requirement.\n\nNo screenshot or device interface was inspected. The developer's claims of a clean, readable board remain attributed rather than independently evaluated.\n\n## Competition and learning can coexist\n\nStatistics, achievements, daily checkmarks, and leaderboards add goals around the grids. A player may value those records or ignore them in favor of careful deduction.\n\nThe description does not establish a universal countdown or require speed as the only measure of success. Likewise, a daily challenge is not proof that ordinary play is restricted to one puzzle per day.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available here. There is no supported player digest about hint quality, difficulty calibration, stability, or advertisements. The metadata records a free download, while current purchases and removal terms are not specified in the supplied description.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBrainium Sudoku looks most relevant to players who want explanatory assistance and several ways to customize input. It is less conclusive as a verified teaching or accessibility recommendation. The concrete tool list is strong; its actual instructional effectiveness and session conditions remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do hints only reveal a number?\nThe developer says they explain the logic behind the answer.\n\n### What is Number Painting?\nIt lets you rapidly fill cells with a selected number or note.\n\n### Is landscape play advertised?\nYes, including handedness options and iPad keyboard compatibility.\n\n### Are learning benefits independently measured?\nNo. No learning study or hands-on assessment is available here.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/sudoku/id418044512?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
