    "bodyMarkdown": "# Puzzle Fuzzle: Build a New Object From Old Pictures\n\nPuzzle Fuzzle asks you to stop seeing an object only as itself. A picture contains useful components, and the challenge is repurposing them into something different. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Take apart the meaning as well as the image\n\nThe developer describes disassembling two pictures and combining selected parts into a new object. Its examples include making a toaster from a suitcase and mitten, or finding components of a wine glass in a plunger and candle.\n\nThose examples come from the listing, not from inspected levels. They illustrate the central skill: notice a shape that could serve a different purpose once separated from its original context.\n\nThis is not a conventional jigsaw where every piece returns to its original place. Nor is it entirely free-form collage software. The puzzle supplies a target idea and expects the relevant components to be arranged appropriately.\n\n## Feedback may help with an imperfect arrangement\n\nA hot-and-cold meter is advertised to show whether the assembled picture is moving closer to the intended result. That could be useful when recognizing the components is easier than positioning them precisely.\n\nThe supplied description does not establish how the meter evaluates placement, whether rotation is always allowed, or how many alternate constructions are accepted. A resemblance-based premise can be engaging without having a fully documented answer-tolerance system.\n\nThe developer advertises more than 100 combination challenges and no time limit. That supports a deliberate experimentation pitch rather than a race, although neither the count nor the timing behavior was independently checked.\n\n## Creativity within a prepared answer\n\nSomeone who enjoys visual analogy may like searching for a new use hidden inside an everyday object. Someone who wants strict deduction from explicit rules may be less comfortable interpreting the designer's intended resemblance.\n\nThose are potential tradeoffs, not player feedback. No usable review bodies are available, and no screenshot was visually inspected. There is no supported judgment of the illustrations, hint usefulness, or variety across the full collection.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, purchases, removal offers, offline access, and stability are not established by the supplied text. The no-timer claim should not expand into an unrestricted-access promise.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPuzzle Fuzzle is a distinctive prospect for players who enjoy reinterpreting familiar shapes and assembling an unexpected object. It is less suited to someone seeking a traditional jigsaw or unrestricted drawing tool. The component-reuse idea and directional feedback are concrete; input precision and content quality remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do the pieces return to their original picture?\nNo. The aim is to create a different object from selected components.\n\n### What does the hot-and-cold meter indicate?\nThe listing says it shows whether the arrangement is moving toward the intended answer.\n\n### Is a timer advertised?\nThe developer explicitly says there are no time limits.\n\n### Are all alternate constructions accepted?\nThat is not established in the supplied description.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/puzzle-fuzzle/id1460555504?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
