    "bodyMarkdown": "# Tile Craft: Triple Matching With a Very Long Review Window\n\nTile Craft's basic decision is which tile to collect before the holder fills. Its review evidence is unusually spread out in time, so historical complaints deserve context rather than being presented as fresh findings about the listing version. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Three match; seven can end the attempt\n\nThe developer describes tapping tiles into a collection box. Three identical tiles disappear, while filling all seven slots without completing a match ends the attempt. Clearing the original board completes the stage.\n\nThat turns selection into capacity planning. A visible tile is not automatically a useful choice if it leaves several unrelated symbols occupying the holder. The next complete triple matters more than collecting quickly for its own sake.\n\nNew tile sets, landscapes, and offline support are advertised. Those remain listing claims rather than visually inspected or disconnected-test findings.\n\n## Older praise and older criticism both matter\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2021-12-21 through 2026-06-05 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 6.6, 6.8, 6.9, 7.1.1, 7.2, 7.5, 7.7, 7.9, 8.1, 8.2, 8.3, 8.7, 9.0, 9.1, 9.2; the listing records version 9.3. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nThe sample reaches back to 2021 and ends before the listing version. Some reviewers praise music, colorful presentation, and challenge. A detailed 2023 account says patience and retries can work without powerups, while other older accounts feel forced toward assistance.\n\nA 2024 reviewer reports missed taps, and a 2023 comment objects to a timer-related aspect. Those observations cannot establish current input behavior or a universal current timer. The listing's clearest documented loss rule is the seven-slot holder.\n\nThe useful takeaway is not that every old issue persists, but that the format's appeal and its surrounding systems have drawn different reactions over several years.\n\n## Removal and rewarded videos are separate expectations\n\nHistorical purchasers report ads remaining after removal. One specifically says between-level ads disappear while optional boosters and second chances still involve videos. Another distinguishes a paid version from voluntary reward viewing.\n\nThat is more informative than saying removal either works perfectly or never works. The exact current offer and its scope were not inspected, and the older prices or terms should not be treated as current.\n\nFree download does not establish free unlimited assistance. Equally, a player's frustration at a hard board is not proof of an impossible or deliberately rigged arrangement.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTile Craft may suit players who like planning triples in a small holder and are comfortable retrying a board. It is less conclusive for someone choosing based on current stability or advertising guarantees. The rules are clearly described, but the long historical sample cannot substitute for verification of the latest release.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many tiles form a match?\nThree identical tiles in the collection box.\n\n### What is the holder limit?\nSeven tiles; filling it without a match ends the attempt.\n\n### Are these all recent reviews?\nNo. The sample extends back to 2021 and predates the listing version.\n\n### Does removal necessarily remove reward videos?\nHistorical reviewers distinguish ordinary ads from optional videos for assistance.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/tile-craft-triple-crush/id1447399252?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
