    "bodyMarkdown": "# Triple Match City: The Clock Changes the Object Hunt\n\nTriple Match City is an object hunt with a deadline. Its listing's relaxation language should not obscure the explicit rule that matching must be completed before time runs out. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Search the map and complete triples\n\nThe developer describes finding objects across broad maps, collecting three of a kind, and clearing the objective within a timer. Zoom, panning, and hints support the search, while optional purchases provide boosters.\n\nThat combines recognition with pace. Zooming can make a target easier to select, but it also reduces how much of the larger map is visible at once. The player has to balance inspection against the remaining clock.\n\nThe description does not establish an untimed alternative. A reviewer requesting one is asking for a feature, not confirming it exists.\n\n## Affection for the search, concerns about its limits\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-29 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.0.4, 3.1.1, 3.2.0, 3.2.2, 3.2.3, 3.2.5; the listing records version 3.3.1. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts praise variety, an engaging challenge, active teams, and helpful support during a phone transfer. Those specific service and community experiences are useful counterpoints to the more critical reports.\n\nOther reviewers describe very hard stages, repeated maps, or selection difficulty when objects sit close together. One explicitly says zoom helps tapping but makes items harder to locate in the larger scene. Another reports losing stylus support after an update.\n\nThese accounts do not establish mathematically impossible levels or a measured control failure rate. They do show why someone wanting leisurely exploration may react differently from a player who likes time pressure.\n\n## An interruption is more consequential during a timer\n\nSeveral reviewers describe ads appearing inside a timed stage rather than only between attempts. Some also report reward-time options freezing or failing to become available.\n\nThose are player reports, not independently observed timer behavior. The evidence does not establish whether the clock pauses during every interruption, and no exact interval should be generalized.\n\nThe free download and optional booster disclosure do not establish a required spending amount. Complaints about needing assistance coexist with positive accounts of the game, and current prices were not verified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTriple Match City may suit players who enjoy fast visual searching and a team framework around it. It is less suited to someone specifically seeking a quiet, untimed scavenger hunt. The timer is an explicit part of the proposition; sampled concerns about interruptions and crowded selections matter because they compete with that same limited time.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is the game timed?\nYes. The listing repeatedly describes completing objectives before the clock expires.\n\n### How many matching objects are needed?\nThree of a kind, according to the developer.\n\n### Is an untimed mode confirmed?\nNo. A sampled player requests one, but its availability is not established.\n\n### Do difficulty complaints prove forced payment?\nNo. They describe individual experiences, not a universal mathematical or payment requirement.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/triple-match-city/id6450110217?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
