    "bodyMarkdown": "# Flashback: Rewind the Scene Before Choosing the Explanation\n\nFlashback gives a visual riddle an extra dimension: you can revisit what happened before the moment on screen. That is the clearest reason to choose it over a collection of static trick questions. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Watch, rewind, inspect again\n\nThe developer describes controlling time with a slider, looking for clues, and using them to resolve a scenario. The useful information may be in an earlier moment rather than the apparent aftermath.\n\nThat changes the act of observation. A detail can matter because of when it appears, not only where it sits. A player may need to compare stages of an action before deciding which explanation fits.\n\nThe supplied description does not fully document every answer-entry rule or whether alternate interpretations are accepted. It also does not support treating the puzzles as a validated IQ test.\n\n## Mystery appeal does not settle content suitability\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-12 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.9.7, 1.9.8; the listing records version 1.9.8. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts enjoy going back to see what happened, finding answers slowly, and the mystery format. Difficulty reports differ: some find the early rounds easy, while others struggle to answer or want clearer instructions.\n\nSeveral reviewers also describe unsettling scenarios, including death and self-harm themes. Those are player reports, not a scene-by-scene independent content review. They are nevertheless relevant for anyone expecting an entirely lighthearted riddle collection or choosing content for a younger audience.\n\nThe time-control concept can be appealing without every scenario being suitable for every player.\n\n## Weekly premium and conflicting ad experiences\n\nThe listing describes an auto-renewing weekly premium package offering ad-free access and extra features. It does not give a verified current price in the supplied text.\n\nSome reviewers complain about ads after each puzzle or videos that loop without an exit. Another reports no commercials, while a different player finds the ads present but less overwhelming. Those differences should not be flattened into one universal interval.\n\nA reviewer mentioning a different billing duration does not override the listing's weekly description or establish the offer currently shown to every account. Free download remains separate from optional recurring access.\n\n## Verdict\n\nFlashback may suit players who enjoy reconstructing a scene by moving through time. It is less clearly suited to someone wanting only gentle subject matter or uninterrupted deduction. The rewind mechanic earns specific praise; sampled concerns about content and access are substantial qualifications rather than reasons to invent a single consensus.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What makes the interaction distinctive?\nThe listing describes sliding back through time to inspect clues.\n\n### Is every scenario lighthearted?\nThat is not established; reviewers report some disturbing themes.\n\n### What subscription duration is listed?\nAn auto-renewing weekly package.\n\n### Is the current premium price verified?\nNo. Reviewer-mentioned amounts are not a current checkout.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/flashback-tricky-fun-riddles/id1630338576?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
