    "bodyMarkdown": "# Screw Puzzle: A Good Sequence Can Matter More Than a Free Screw\n\nScrew Puzzle describes dismantling interwoven metal parts rather than sorting colored fasteners into boxes. Its sample's affection centers on the mental sequence, while repetition and daily-challenge problems limit that appeal for some long-time players. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Remove the constraint that frees the next piece\n\nThe listing describes unlocking screws, disentangling iron sheets and rings, and working around ropes. Some stages advertise a handsaw that creates more holes for bolts. No time limit and separate easy and hard challenges are also promoted.\n\nThe useful next move may be the one that creates a better future position rather than simply removing an obvious fastener. A spare hole can become working space, while a poorly chosen relocation can obstruct another step.\n\nThe supplied description does not fully document every physics interaction or the availability of the saw. Those features should remain attributed rather than presented as tested controls.\n\n## The mental task has fans, but not endless novelty\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-02-18 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.1, 3.2, 43, 45; the listing records version 45. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers enjoy the challenge and describe the game as relaxing or mentally engaging. A purchaser who likes the reasoning nevertheless complains about repeated puzzles at high levels.\n\nSeveral accounts say a daily challenge is already marked complete before they can play it. Others want new levels or a darker surrounding background. These are specific practical complaints, not proof that the entire game is broken or visually uncomfortable for everyone.\n\nMany detailed comments come from 2025 or an older version. They should not be generalized as fresh findings about version 45.\n\n## An extra hole can have an access condition\n\nSome reviewers describe watching a video to unlock a spare hole and feeling constrained without it. That is relevant to a puzzle where available space affects the sequence, but it does not prove every board requires paid help.\n\nOther accounts report requiring an internet connection or difficulty leaving ads. Those experiences remain unverified; the supplied description does not establish a universal offline guarantee or current removal terms.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. A player calling an arrangement impossible is not a mathematical proof, and allegations about harmful ads do not establish a security finding.\n\n## Verdict\n\nScrew Puzzle may suit players who like carefully dismantling a structure and reusing available space. It is less clearly suited to someone expecting continually novel layouts or dependable daily challenges. The sample supports a real reasoning appeal while identifying repetition and access to working space as meaningful qualifications.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this mainly a color-box sorting game?\nThe listing emphasizes metal plates, screws, rings, ropes, and dismantling instead.\n\n### Is a time limit advertised?\nThe description explicitly says there is no time limit.\n\n### What daily issue do reviewers report?\nSome say challenges appear completed before they have played them.\n\n### Are extra holes always free?\nThat is not established; some reviewers describe video-based access.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/screw-puzzle/id6468194355?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
