    "bodyMarkdown": "# Water Match: Hidden Colors Add an Information Problem\n\nWater Match adds uncertainty to an already constrained sorting puzzle. Hidden colors and covered bottles mean you may have to decide what to expose before you can decide where everything belongs. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Sort the liquid and reveal what is missing\n\nThe developer describes pouring colors into matching bottles, which transform into gift bags when completed. Mysterious colors, covered bottles, and limited-time events broaden the basic sorting loop.\n\nThe information constraint is the distinctive part. With fully visible layers, planning is largely about access and space. Concealed layers can also make the next discovery relevant to the plan.\n\nThe supplied description does not fully specify legal-pour restrictions, bottle capacity, or how every covered state is revealed. Those familiar rules should not be borrowed automatically from another water-sort app.\n\n## Recovery tools solve different problems\n\nUndo is described as reversing a move while retaining revealed information. Shuffle rearranges layers in a bottle, including mysterious ones. Adding an empty bottle supplies more working space.\n\nThat is a useful distinction: reversing an action, changing a local arrangement, and increasing capacity are not the same kind of assistance. Keeping revealed information could make experimentation less costly, if it works as advertised.\n\nThe evidence does not establish tool prices, replenishment, or whether every stage permits all three. Their presence is not proof that difficult puzzles are either generous or paywalled.\n\n## Social competition changes the relaxation pitch\n\nThe listing advertises leaderboards, real-time contests, guild activity, and offline play. Those claims need separate treatment: disconnected sorting does not imply that connected competition also works without a network.\n\nThe developer also promotes pouring sounds, haptics or sensory effects, relaxation, and improved sleep. No screenshot, audio session, or clinical evidence supports independently validating those benefits. In particular, sleep-aid language should not become a health recommendation.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available here. The metadata records a free download, but ad frequency, purchase terms, and stability remain unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWater Match is most relevant to players who enjoy color sorting with an added discovery element and several recovery tools. It is less conclusive for someone who chiefly wants a clinically supported sleep aid or verified quiet offline use. The hidden-information mechanics are concrete; their balance and surrounding economy remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What makes it different from fully visible sorting?\nThe listing advertises hidden colors and covered bottles.\n\n### Does undo erase revealed information?\nThe developer says revealed secrets are retained.\n\n### What does Shuffle change?\nIt is described as rearranging one bottle's water layers.\n\n### Are sleep benefits established?\nNo. The supplied evidence does not validate the sleep-aid claim.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/water-match-asmr-water-sort/id6751788348?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
