    "bodyMarkdown": "# ZEN Block: Fixed Orientations, a Finite Frame, and a Long History\n\nZEN Block asks you to fill a frame with pieces that cannot rotate. Its sample suggests the concept can be relaxing and satisfying, but the amount of challenge and the finite supply of puzzles divide expectations. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Fit the orientation you are given\n\nThe listing describes dragging blocks into a frame, with no rotation and no time limit. That distinguishes it from endless line clearing and from tangrams that let you turn a promising piece.\n\nA piece that looks useful in another orientation must still fit as supplied. The reasoning therefore includes considering which shapes can occupy a region without leaving an incompatible gap elsewhere.\n\nMore than 200 stages, minimal graphics and sound, and offline play are advertised. Those are listing claims, not an independently inspected catalog or interface.\n\n## Easy beginnings and finished collections\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2021-04-03 through 2026-09-03 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.4.21, 2.5.1, 2.6.1, 2.6.2, 2.6.3; the listing records version 2.6.3. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers enjoy the logic, music, and peaceful pace. Others stop early because the opening puzzles feel too easy. A more detailed account says later packs are harder and hints feel expensive.\n\nSeveral long-time players say they have finished all available content and want additional levels. That is not proof of an exact current catalog count, but it is relevant to someone expecting an endless stream of new challenges.\n\nThe sample reaches back to 2021. Historical opinions about packs, prices, and ad behavior should not be presented as a single current consensus.\n\n## Advertising is explicitly disclosed\n\nThe listing says the game contains banners, interstitials, and videos, with optional purchases for ad-free access and hints. That is clearer than inferring advertising from a generic in-app-purchase label.\n\nPlayer experiences still differ. One older reviewer reports rare ads, others describe frequent breaks, and purchasers disagree about removal. Another specifically says paid access leaves optional coin videos.\n\nThe current price and exact entitlement were not verified. An ad-free product may not eliminate optional reward videos, but the sample alone cannot establish every condition.\n\n## Verdict\n\nZEN Block may suit players who enjoy untimed fitting puzzles with fixed piece orientations. It is a less certain fit for someone seeking immediate expert difficulty or unlimited fresh content. The rule is compact and distinctive; the long sample's strongest qualifications concern easy starts, a reported content endpoint, and interruptions around short boards.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can the blocks rotate?\nNo. The listing explicitly forbids rotation.\n\n### Is this an endless line-clearing game?\nNo. The stated objective is fitting pieces into a frame.\n\n### Are ads disclosed?\nYes, including banners, interstitials, and videos.\n\n### Is fresh content guaranteed indefinitely?\nNo. Several reviewers say they reached the end and want more stages.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/zen-block-tangram-puzzle-game/id1393910125?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
