    "bodyMarkdown": "# Matchington Mansion: Decorating Competes With the Side Activities\n\nMatchington Mansion gives a match-3 board a home-improvement payoff. Its sample shows that the relationship between the puzzles and the decorating matters: players do not necessarily want every side activity competing for the same attention. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Match pillows to move the renovation forward\n\nThe listing describes matching pillows, combining boosters, opening rooms, and choosing decorations for a mansion and garden. Tiffany and other characters provide a story involving a troublesome cousin and hidden areas.\n\nThe matching task and the renovation are connected but distinct. A player might enjoy the boards, the design decisions, or the anticipation of uncovering the next room. The supplied text does not establish the exact conversion between each level and every decorative action.\n\nDespite a stray jigsaw reference in the marketing, the detailed description identifies match-3 as the central puzzle format.\n\n## What counts as meaningful progress?\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-20 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.211.0, 1.212.0, 1.213.0; the listing records version 1.213.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy decorating, multiple themes, and the merge mini-game. One says difficult boards remain beatable and describes ads as optional for extra help. Another longtime player reports comfortable play without spending.\n\nOther reviewers want more decoration and fewer side games. One dislikes spending stars on meeting a character or watching a scene rather than changing a room. Another wants to bypass familiar mini-game tutorials.\n\nThose are concrete differences in what feels rewarding. A narrative action can be welcome to a story-focused player and an unwanted delay to someone who only wants to decorate.\n\n## Assistance and difficulty produce conflicting accounts\n\nSome reviewers describe getting stuck for days or feeling pushed toward coins. Those experiences do not prove manipulated outcomes or that every stage requires payment.\n\nA player also reports losing reward-video options after making a purchase. That is an account-specific observation, not proof of a universal purchasing consequence. Other comments describe long or freezing videos, while favorable accounts describe ads as voluntary.\n\nThe listing discloses optional paid items but does not establish current assistance prices or a universal removal entitlement. The evidence supports reporting those differences rather than certifying either completely free progress or forced spending.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMatchington Mansion may suit players who enjoy both match-3 and a continuing renovation story. It is less clearly suited to someone who wants every earned resource to translate directly into decor. The sample supports affection for the setting while showing how tutorials, side events, and resource priorities can change the pace of the promised makeover.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this primarily a jigsaw app?\nNo. Its detailed listing describes match-3 pillow puzzles and renovation.\n\n### Do all players enjoy the merge mini-game?\nNo. The sample includes both enthusiasm and requests for less emphasis on it.\n\n### Does every star necessarily buy decoration?\nA reviewer reports stars being used for story actions as well.\n\n### Is free progression universally guaranteed?\nNo. Comfortable free-play reports coexist with substantial difficulty and spending concerns.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/matchington-mansion/id1216575026?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
