    "bodyMarkdown": "# Redstone Crossword: The Puzzle Library and the Keyboard Both Matter\n\nRedstone's appeal is straightforward: a large library of authored crosswords rather than a word game borrowing the crossword label. The sample also shows why a small keyboard change can matter enormously to an established solving routine. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Choose the constructor and the scale\n\nThe listing advertises more than 30,000 human-made puzzles, four difficulties, and mini, midi, and regular 15-by-15 formats. Both themed and themeless puzzles are included.\n\nError checking, reveal assistance, multiword-entry hints, lookup, and completed-word locking support different solving styles. These can make a puzzle more approachable without changing the underlying clue-and-crossing structure.\n\nThe developer also names iPad landscape mode, dark mode, offline solving, and a range of contributors. Those are concrete features, not a promise that every constructor uses identical vocabulary or cultural references.\n\n## A strong catalog meets a disputed interface\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-07-07 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.7.5, 1.7.6; the listing records version 1.7.6. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers praise customization, thoughtful clues, difficulty variety, and the ability to search for preferred authors. One initially criticizes limited author variety, then notes that search reveals a much broader collection.\n\nSeveral players welcome landscape mode. Others dislike the changed keyboard, misplaced navigation buttons, or thinner text. Those accounts are not mutually exclusive: a new layout can improve device positioning while making familiar typing less comfortable.\n\nThese comments span multiple releases. Appreciation for landscape mode should not be read as approval of every related keyboard revision.\n\n## No subscription does not mean no advertising\n\nThe listing explicitly promotes free puzzles without subscriptions. That is different from claiming there are no ads or no optional removal purchase.\n\nSome reviewers find ads tolerable because a crossword takes long enough that breaks are infrequent relative to solving. Others report freezing, blocked letters, or needing to restart after a video. Current removal prices are not independently verified; reviewer-mentioned amounts remain historical account reports.\n\nThe useful comparison is the total solving experience: puzzle quality, typing comfort, and the ability to return to another board all matter.\n\n## Verdict\n\nRedstone may suit crossword fans who want authored puzzles, multiple sizes, and control over assistance. It is a more cautious choice for someone sensitive to keyboard changes or thin text. The sample's appreciation for the library is substantial, while the interface debate deserves more than being dismissed as resistance to change.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are these conventional clue-based crosswords?\nYes. The listing describes authored American-style grids.\n\n### Are multiple sizes available?\nMini, midi, and regular 15-by-15 puzzles are advertised.\n\n### Does no subscription mean no ads?\nNo. Reviewer accounts clearly discuss advertising and optional removal.\n\n### Is landscape mode universally praised?\nNo. Several welcome it, while others dislike related layout and keyboard changes.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/crossword-puzzle-redstone/id957848865?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
