    "bodyMarkdown": "# Fairway Solitaire: A Simple Card Rule, a Changed Round Rhythm\n\nFairway Solitaire needs no virtual golf swing. Its core action is playing a card one rank above or below the active value, while golf supplies the course structure, hazards, and recovery tools. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Clear the hand with fewer leftovers\n\nThe listing describes removing cards through those up-or-down choices, with irons and Midas Clubs acting as wild cards. Shuffle and Preview provide other ways to inspect or change a difficult situation.\n\nA useful sequence can clear several cards before drawing again. Choosing one playable card over another can therefore influence what remains accessible, although the supplied text does not establish every layout's exact rules.\n\nCourses, stamps, mini-games, events, and Country Clubs add a broader collection and team layer. The developer advertises no lives or play limits; that does not independently establish unlimited free assistance.\n\n## Long-term affection is visible inside the criticism\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-08-30 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.60.0, 1.61.0, 1.61.1, 1.61.2; the listing records version 1.61.2. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nMany reviewers describe years of enjoyment, a favorite solitaire format, or a relaxing travel routine. These are not merely complaints from people who never liked the game.\n\nThe sample's dominant concern is a changed rhythm: more advertisements and a prompt questioning whether to end a hole with cards remaining. One reviewer says that prompt appears even after earning the relevant perfect-score badge.\n\nOthers criticize changed water-card visuals or the relationship between rewards and extra-card costs. No visual inspection or economy test establishes those changes independently, but the reports are specific enough to explain the frustration.\n\n## Optional rewards and forced breaks feel different\n\nOne long-time player says voluntary ads for stamps or tools were acceptable, while new breaks between rounds are not. Others describe interruptions during a hand and difficulty exiting.\n\nSome purchasers say advertisements now appear despite earlier payment. Those histories do not establish the current removal product or the exact entitlement attached to a past purchase.\n\nThe free download and no-lives claim should therefore remain separate from questions about recurring interruptions and optional assistance. Player allegations of unfair dealing also do not prove manipulated outcomes.\n\n## Verdict\n\nFairway Solitaire may suit players who enjoy rank-sequence planning with golf-themed tools and team collections. It is a more cautious choice for someone seeking the quiet routine described in older memories. The sample's lasting affection strengthens, rather than cancels, its specific reservations about prompts and advertising around each hand.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you swing a golf club in real time?\nThe central puzzle is card sequencing, with golf-themed tools and framing.\n\n### What is the basic card rule?\nPlay one rank up or one rank down, according to the listing.\n\n### Does no lives mean all help is free?\nNo. It does not establish the cost of extra cards or tools.\n\n### Are current removal terms verified?\nNo. Purchaser reports do not establish a current universal offer.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/fairway-solitaire-card-game/id887640616?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
