    "bodyMarkdown": "# Ball Paint: One Color to Finish, Different Views on the Challenge\n\nBall Paint ends where many color games begin: with a figure in one consistent color. Its challenge is using aimed shots to reach that state, while the historical sample disagrees about how much effort ordinary levels demand. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Paint the figure rather than sort its contents\n\nThe listing describes aiming colored balls, shooting at a figure, and making the whole result one color. Geometric shapes and changing color combinations provide the advertised variety.\n\nThat is not a tube-sorting puzzle or a standard bubble-clearing objective. The task is changing the color state of the figure, not simply removing every matching group.\n\nThe supplied description does not fully explain how paint spreads, how the next color is selected, or the exact shot allowance. Those details should not be invented from a familiar color-conversion format.\n\n## Boss stages split the audience\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2020-06-19 through 2025-09-15 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.91, 2.06, 2.11, 2.12, 2.13, 2.14, 2.17, 2.18, 2.19, 2.33, 2.81; the listing records version 2.81. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nThe newest sample entry dates to 2025, with many detailed accounts much older. Some reviewers enjoy easy, satisfying play. Others want a boss-only option because ordinary stages feel repetitive or too simple.\n\nAnother reviewer explicitly does not want boss levels. That disagreement is useful: more difficulty is not automatically an improvement for someone choosing the game as a low-demand break.\n\nHistorical complaints also concern a retry button that does not respond, or receiving colors a player believes are no longer useful. Those are reports, not verified current defects or evidence of intentionally unfair color selection.\n\n## Internal extras can interrupt like ads\n\nA 2025 account dislikes leaderboards, bonuses, and other popups, distinguishing them from the simple gameplay they still enjoy. A later positive comment welcomes the return of older features.\n\nOther historical accounts describe external ads interrupting music, while some find the load manageable. No current interval, removal price, or complete entitlement is independently established.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Older advice about disconnected play should not become a certified current offline mode. Nor should reports about data permission screens be treated as a completed privacy audit.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBall Paint may suit players who like aiming toward a visually clear one-color finish. It is less conclusive for someone seeking a steadily escalating challenge or verified current responsiveness. The sample supports satisfaction with the concept while preserving a real disagreement about whether easy stages or harder bosses are the main attraction.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the goal?\nPaint the figure entirely one color, according to the listing.\n\n### Is this water sorting?\nNo. The central action is aiming and shooting color at a figure.\n\n### Does everyone want harder boss stages?\nNo. Historical reviewers explicitly disagree.\n\n### Is the retry issue confirmed in the current version?\nNo. It is a historical player report, not a current test finding.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/ball-paint-color-puzzle-3d/id1460696485?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
