    "bodyMarkdown": "# Find Differences: Inspect Two Pictures, but Read the Timing Claim Carefully\n\nFind Differences offers one of the clearest visual puzzle premises: compare two nearly identical pictures and identify what does not match. Its listing is less consistent about whether time ever limits the task. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Compare corresponding areas, then tap\n\nThe developer describes inspecting paired pictures and tapping each discovered difference. Zoom supports closer inspection, while hints help locate a detail that has remained elusive.\n\nThe interest is not simply recognizing objects. You need to compare the same region across two images and decide whether an apparent mismatch is meaningful. Moving methodically can prevent repeatedly overlooking one area, although that is a general approach rather than a tested strategy for this app.\n\nNature, animals, food, and architecture are named categories. No screenshots were visually inspected, so individual differences, composition, and picture quality are not independently described.\n\n## A contradiction in the timing language\n\nThe opening description explicitly says there is no time limit. A later feature line says hints help solve pictures within the time limit. Those statements do not provide a consistent rulebook.\n\nThe responsible conclusion is not to invent a timed mode or certify every puzzle as untimed. A player specifically avoiding clocks should check the actual instructions and relevant mode.\n\nThe listing also advertises more than 20,000 levels and unlimited hints. These are developer claims, not an inspected inventory or a guarantee that hint access has no other condition.\n\n## Zoom helps inspection, not automatically accessibility\n\nA zoom feature may be useful for finding small differences, but the evidence does not establish its maximum scale, image sharpness, or controls. Someone who needs reliably large details should not treat zoom alone as a visual-accessibility certification.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available here. There is no supported player assessment of repeated pictures, hint usefulness, difficulty, or the accuracy of accepted taps.\n\nThe metadata records a free download, and offline use is advertised. Advertising frequency, paid options, removal terms, and stability are not independently established. Unlimited hints should remain an attributed claim rather than expand into a completely unrestricted session promise.\n\n## Verdict\n\nFind Differences is a plausible fit for players who enjoy patient side-by-side observation and want zoom support. It is less conclusive for someone requiring a verified no-timer rule or specific accessibility controls. The core comparison task is clear; the inconsistent timing language is the most important detail to resolve before choosing it for stress-free play.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What do you compare?\nTwo nearly identical pictures, tapping the differences you find.\n\n### Is zoom advertised?\nYes, along with hints.\n\n### Is every puzzle confirmed untimed?\nNo. The listing contains conflicting time-limit language.\n\n### Are hints independently confirmed unlimited?\nNo. Unlimited hints are a developer claim with incomplete access terms.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/find-differences-puzzle-games/id1522840765?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
