    "bodyMarkdown": "# Shanghai Mahjongg: Clear Tiles, Conflicting Changes and Ad Accounts\n\nShanghai Mahjongg promises familiar solitaire matching with large, clear tiles. The sample suggests that presentation and changes to the surrounding format are at least as important to some players as the matching itself. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## A simple pair-clearing pitch\n\nThe developer describes traditional Mahjong solitaire, tap-to-match controls, hints, undo, customizable themes, and fresh daily puzzles. No time pressure and offline use are explicitly advertised.\n\nThe core attraction is recognizing a pair while considering what removing it makes available next. The supplied description does not provide a complete tile-access rulebook, so every standard side-blocking rule should not be prescribed as independently confirmed.\n\nNor do memory and brain-training claims establish a medical outcome. The senior-focused positioning is an intended audience, not a guarantee of accessibility for every player.\n\n## Readability praise has an important counterpoint\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-08 through 2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.6.0, 3.7.0, 3.8.0; the listing records version 3.8.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers describe distinguishable tiles, enjoyable challenge, and an easy-to-understand activity. One specifically praises the possibility of fast play.\n\nAnother says tiles become smaller during progression. Other players dislike added special stages, a buffer-related change, or the disappearance of a daily calendar activity. Those reports suggest experiences more varied than the listing's simple classic pitch, but they do not provide enough detail to reconstruct every current mode.\n\nA reader choosing specifically for a plain, familiar format should therefore check more than the first board.\n\n## The ad conflict cannot be solved by choosing one review\n\nSeveral reviewers report no ads. Others describe breaks between games, close failures, or advertisements despite paying for removal. One purchaser says ads initially remained but later disappeared.\n\nThose differing histories are exactly why neither a no-ads guarantee nor a claim that removal never works is justified. Current prices and account-specific entitlements were not independently verified.\n\nThe sample also includes suspicions about automated opponents and references to payouts. Neither establishes a verified competition system or cash-reward program. The listing evidence supports a tile game, not a promise of real-money earnings.\n\n## Verdict\n\nShanghai Mahjongg may suit players who want a senior-oriented tile presentation and familiar solitaire goals. It is a more cautious choice for someone requiring consistently large layouts or a permanently unchanged classic format. The sample's readable-tile praise is useful, while changes and conflicting ad experiences keep the recommendation qualified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is no time pressure advertised?\nYes, along with offline play.\n\n### Do all reviewers find the tiles consistently large?\nNo. One reports smaller tiles as progression continues.\n\n### Is the daily calendar independently confirmed present?\nNo. A reviewer says an update removed it.\n\n### Are cash rewards or bot opponents verified?\nNo. Player claims do not establish either system as fact.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/shanghai-mahjongg-puzzle/id6740228508?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
