    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bus Frenzy: Clear the Traffic and Match the Passenger Colors\n\nBus Frenzy describes a combination of traffic clearance and passenger color sorting. That makes it more than a pure parking-lot slider, although the listing leaves several decisive constraints unspecified. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## The useful bus needs a useful route\n\nThe developer describes tapping and moving buses out of congestion while arranging passengers toward matching destinations. Clearing traffic and seating passengers are presented as connected parts of the objective.\n\nThat combination can create a dependency: the bus you want may need another vehicle moved first, while the order of available passengers can influence which destination is useful next. This is a design implication of the stated premise, not a verified sequence from an actual stage.\n\nThe description does not establish exact movement directions, seat counts, waiting-space capacity, or how passengers choose a bus. Those common mechanics should not be borrowed from another traffic title.\n\n## Quick-thinking language is not a complete timer rule\n\nThe listing emphasizes quick reflexes, timing, routes, and an evolving queue. It does not clearly specify a countdown or what happens when time expires.\n\nSomeone who enjoys managing several spatial relationships may like the combined concept. Someone who wants a fully untimed game or one clean unblocking rule has less certainty from the description alone.\n\nThe promise that no two levels feel the same is promotional rather than evidence of verified layout diversity. The same applies to the broader relaxation pitch: a puzzle can be intended as a casual break without every player finding its constraints calming.\n\n## What is not documented matters to fit\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available for this app in the supplied collection. There is no supported player assessment of control responsiveness, later-stage difficulty, or the relationship between bus movement and boarding.\n\nNo screenshot was visually inspected. Specific arrow positions, traffic layouts, or passenger designs therefore cannot be described as independently observed.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. The text does not establish assistance prices, ad frequency, removal products, or offline support. Its privacy-policy reference also does not independently resolve those gameplay questions or constitute a privacy audit.\n\nThe right comparison is with other combined traffic-and-assignment games, not an assumption that every bus puzzle follows identical rules.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBus Frenzy is a plausible fit for players who want to coordinate vehicle access and passenger colors. It is less conclusive for someone whose preference depends on a specific arrow, capacity, or timer system. The combined objective is identifiable; the detailed constraints and practical access economy still need direct confirmation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this only about moving vehicles?\nNo. Passenger color sorting is also described.\n\n### Are seat capacities confirmed?\nThe supplied description does not specify them.\n\n### Is a countdown definitely part of every stage?\nNo complete timer rule is documented.\n\n### Is offline play verified?\nNo. It is not established by the supplied text or a disconnected test.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bus-frenzy-traffic-jam/id6654917975?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
