    "bodyMarkdown": "# Screw Away: The Listing and the Box-Sorting Reports Do Not Fully Align\n\nScrew Away needs a qualified explanation of its rules. The listing emphasizes screwing pins in under time pressure, while sampled players discuss exposed screws, collection boxes, and unlocking space. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Two sources describe different emphases\n\nThe developer describes pins of different shapes and sizes, timed goals, minimizing errors, and increasing complexity. It also names achievements and competitive scores.\n\nA more detailed reviewer instead describes boxes that do not match the exposed screws, while other players discuss revives, a hammer, and unlocking spaces. Those accounts suggest removal-and-collection constraints not adequately explained in the listing.\n\nThe evidence does not resolve a complete current ruleset. It would be misleading either to ignore the player descriptions or confidently substitute the rules of another screw-sorting game. The broad shared idea is choosing fastener actions under constraints.\n\n## Creative appeal survives reservations\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-27 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.2.0; the listing records version 2.2.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers describe enjoyable distraction, engaging difficulty, and cute or creative puzzles. One long-time player still likes the art and ideas while deciding the advertising has become excessive.\n\nOther accounts report weekly quests failing to register a rating task, a shift to portrait-only play, or stars without a satisfying use. These are specific usability and progression concerns, not merely dissatisfaction with a hard board.\n\nA player calling a level impossible does not establish a mathematical proof or deliberate manipulation. The stronger evidence concerns their experience of needing help or restarting.\n\n## Loading a stage can also mean loading another ad\n\nOne detailed account distinguishes ads between stages from ads when a stage loads again after closing or restarting. Other reviewers report videos hanging, lost progress on reopening, or advertised space not being granted.\n\nThose reports remain unverified. The metadata records a free download, while current purchase prices and removal scope are not established in the supplied description.\n\nThe listing's timer language also should not be discarded just because the reviews emphasize boxes. Someone who specifically avoids time pressure needs direct confirmation of the relevant mode.\n\n## Verdict\n\nScrew Away may interest players who like fastener puzzles and are willing to check the actual instructions first. It is a less conclusive choice based solely on the store description because the rule emphasis conflicts with detailed player accounts. The sample supports creative appeal, but access, recovery, and format changes are substantial qualifications.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does the listing describe a timer?\nYes. It says pins must be handled within a specified time.\n\n### Why discuss boxes?\nA sampled reviewer describes collection boxes and exposed screws, which the listing does not fully explain.\n\n### Are all space-unlock rewards verified?\nNo. Some reviewers report missing or unreliable rewards.\n\n### Is portrait-only play independently confirmed?\nNo. It is a reported change, not an inspected current interface.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/screw-away-3d-pin-puzzle/id6547835725?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
