    "bodyMarkdown": "# Colorwood Sort: Sensory Sorting Does Not Guarantee an Untimed Session\n\nColorwood Sort emphasizes how sorting should feel: taps, haptic feedback, matching colors, and a sensory rhythm. The sample suggests that rhythm can be appealing, while a later timer or interruption can change the experience considerably. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Align colors through repeated decisions\n\nThe listing describes tapping blocks, sorting by color, and combining matching shapes through progressively harder levels. Haptic feedback is repeatedly promoted as a defining feature.\n\nThe supplied text does not fully explain the exact movement restrictions, container capacity, or every success condition. A review comparing the thinking to a Rubik's Cube is an analogy, not proof of cube-turning rules.\n\nSomeone drawn to organizing a color arrangement may like the premise. Someone who dislikes tactile feedback would need to inspect the available controls; an advertised effect does not establish whether it can be disabled.\n\n## A relaxing start is not the whole ruleset\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-03-24 through 2026-11-02 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 5.10.26640, 5.11.26937, 5.12.27616, 5.13.27872, 5.14.28560, 5.5.23696, 5.6.24705, 5.7.25360, 5.8.25914, 5.8.26057, 5.9.26409; the listing records version 5.15.29403. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy the music, simple interface, and soothing color matching. One reports few annoying popups even after continued play.\n\nAnother likes the same clean presentation but is disappointed when a timer appears after early levels. Other accounts describe freezing that consumes the remaining time or ends a streak. These are player reports, not independently verified current timing rules or a measured crash rate.\n\nThe important distinction is between a calming presentation and an absence of time pressure. The supplied listing does not promise that every stage is untimed.\n\n## Keys and advertising affect the pace\n\nSome reviewers describe keys, undo, or restarts linked to videos. One says the number of keys earned per view changed; that is a reported economy change, not a verified current exchange rate.\n\nAd accounts conflict, ranging from few popups to breaks during or after stages. A purchaser also reports continued ads. Current removal terms are not established, and criticism of advertisements for the game elsewhere should not be confused with interruptions inside it.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Real-money reward complaints in the sample do not establish a supported cash-payout program or its terms.\n\n## Verdict\n\nColorwood Sort may suit players who like color alignment accompanied by music and tactile feedback. It is a more cautious choice for someone specifically avoiding timers or depending on reliable long streaks. The sample supports the sensory appeal while showing how practical constraints can work against the relaxed mood.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is haptic feedback advertised?\nYes. It is a central feature in the listing.\n\n### Is every stage confirmed untimed?\nNo. Reviewers report timers appearing after earlier play.\n\n### Are key rewards fixed and verified?\nNo. Current video-to-key terms were not independently checked.\n\n### Is this a Rubik's Cube game?\nNo such rules are documented; a reviewer uses that comparison as an analogy.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/colorwood-sort-puzzle-game/id6475673897?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
