    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block! Triangle Puzzle: The Daily Wait Is Not a Solving Countdown\n\nBlock! Triangle Puzzle gives fitting pieces a fixed-orientation constraint. The sample also reveals an easily misunderstood issue: complaints about waiting for the next daily puzzle are not the same as a countdown while solving one. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Fill the target with the triangles as supplied\n\nThe listing describes dragging geometric blocks into a board, with no rotation. The aim is filling a shape rather than maintaining an endless line-clearing run.\n\nThe interesting decision is which region a piece can occupy without leaving an incompatible gap elsewhere. A piece that would fit after turning still has to work in the orientation provided.\n\nMore than 2,000 puzzles, no time limits, and offline support are advertised. These remain developer claims rather than an independently inspected catalog or disconnected test.\n\n## Satisfaction and difficulty vary by player\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-05-22 through 2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2604.0.4, 2605.0.2, 2606.0.1, 2606.1.0, 2607.0.1, 2607.0.3, 2607.0.5, 2607.0.8, 2607.0.9, 2607.1.0, 2607.1.3, 2608.0.0, 2609.0.11, 2609.0.9, 2610.0.0, 2610.0.2; the listing records version 2610.0.2. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers enjoy completing complex geometric arrangements and describe the task as satisfying or relaxing. One likes the increasing challenge but finds coins slow to accumulate. Another says even hard levels are too easy.\n\nSeveral accounts criticize daily rewards that say to choose a box but automatically grant a result instead. Others want hints to identify a more useful piece rather than placing one the player already understands.\n\nA recurring daily-access complaint concerns a 24-hour reset after the previous play, rather than access changing at midnight. That is a scheduling cooldown, not evidence that the ordinary puzzle violates the listing's no-time-limit claim.\n\n## Advertising is disclosed, but its practical load differs\n\nThe description explicitly lists banners, interstitials, videos, and house ads, with optional paid removal. That is direct advertising evidence, not an inference from in-app purchases.\n\nReviewers disagree about the amount, with some reporting few breaks and others finding them excessive relative to short boards. No exact universal interval or current removal price is verified.\n\nReports of lost history after reinstalling also should not be generalized into a tested backup policy. The evidence does not establish that every progress issue is caused by the same update.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlock! Triangle Puzzle may suit players who enjoy deliberate shape fitting without rotation. It is less clearly suited to someone seeking freely rotatable tangrams or effortless daily access at any hour. The core geometry earns specific praise, while hint selection and the daily reward framework deserve attention of their own.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can pieces rotate?\nNo. The listing explicitly says they cannot.\n\n### Is a daily reset wait the same as a solving timer?\nNo. They are different constraints, and reviewers specifically discuss a cooldown.\n\n### Are ads explicitly disclosed?\nYes, including external formats and house ads.\n\n### Are all hints considered useful?\nNo. A detailed reviewer wants more control over which piece a hint addresses.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-triangle-puzzle-tangram/id1415559267?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
