    "bodyMarkdown": "# Lily's Garden: The Garden Is the Reason to Solve Another Board\n\nLily's Garden connects a flower puzzle to a continuing renovation. The garden is not simply a background: restoring it and following Lily's relationships are the stated reasons to keep making progress. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Puzzles lead toward a visible makeover\n\nThe developer describes flower-matching blast puzzles, themed boosters, and renovating a great-aunt's garden. Decorations, hidden areas, and a cast of characters connect the boards to a romantic story.\n\nThat gives the loop two kinds of payoff. A board offers an immediate objective, while a renovation choice changes the larger project. A player interested in one may not value the other equally.\n\nThe supplied description uses match-3 and blast terminology without fully specifying the gesture rules. It does not establish a conventional neighboring swap or the exact number of adjacent flowers required for every action. Those details should not be invented from a genre label.\n\n## Story continuity is a design commitment\n\nThe listing emphasizes dialogue, secrets, twists, and choices among flowers or decorations. Someone who wants a light narrative between boards may find that a stronger motivation than an isolated score.\n\nSomeone who wants to move directly from one abstract puzzle to the next may prefer fewer intervening story beats. That is a potential tradeoff, not a complaint attributed to players: no usable review bodies are available here.\n\nReferences to hidden objects and areas also do not establish a separate, fully documented hidden-object game. They describe exploration in the garden framework unless more specific rules are supplied.\n\n## The scale and pace remain untested\n\nThe developer advertises hundreds of puzzles and continuing story updates. Those are product claims, not a verified current content inventory or release schedule.\n\nNo screenshots were visually inspected, so specific garden layouts, character expressions, and decoration designs cannot be presented as independently observed. The app's romantic tone is documented; the effectiveness of its writing is not evaluated.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. The supplied description does not establish ad frequency, assistance costs, removal terms, or offline operation. Nor does a booster system prove either generous free help or mandatory spending.\n\n## Verdict\n\nLily's Garden looks most suitable for players who want puzzle progress to support a renovation and continuing character story. It is less conclusive for someone whose preference depends on exact swap rules or verified economy terms. The garden-and-romance combination is clear; the actual pacing, writing quality, and access conditions remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What are you renovating?\nLily's great-aunt's garden, according to the listing.\n\n### Is the exact matching gesture documented?\nNo. The description uses blast and match-3 language without a complete action rulebook.\n\n### Is a separate hidden-object mode confirmed?\nThe text mentions hidden objects and areas but does not fully define another mode.\n\n### Is offline use established?\nNo. It is not documented in the supplied description or independently tested.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/lilys-garden-match-design/id1437783446?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
