    "bodyMarkdown": "# Words of Wonders: Search: Subject Clues Within a Field of Letters\n\nWords of Wonders: Search wraps word finding in a trip through cities and landmarks. Despite frequent crossword language in the listing, its more specific instructions describe hidden words within a board containing extra letters. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Use the subject to narrow the search\n\nThe developer describes finding words connected to a level's subject, dealing with distracting letters, and choosing whether to look for longer or shorter words first. Boards grow larger as progression continues.\n\nThat combines two kinds of thinking. The subject suggests likely vocabulary, while the arrangement still has to reveal where an answer lies. Knowing a plausible word is not the same as spotting it among the decoys.\n\nThe supplied description does not fully specify search directions, path turning, or whether letters can be shared. It should not be rewritten as a conventional clue-and-entry crossword or as the rules of another Words of Wonders title.\n\n## Travel supplies the next destination\n\nCities, monuments, rewards, and animated hints provide a progression layer around the boards. A player who likes collecting destinations may enjoy that larger purpose even when the main action stays familiar.\n\nThe text promotes a mix of common, modern, and more challenging vocabulary. It does not independently establish dictionary quality, regional spelling policies, or how consistently subjects relate to every accepted answer.\n\nSomeone seeking vocabulary discovery may find the premise relevant. Someone who dislikes topic-dependent guesses may prefer an explicit word list or a conventional clue format, neither of which is fully documented here.\n\n## Hints have incomplete access terms\n\nThe developer says hints can help with difficult words and describes creative animations. The supplied evidence does not establish their cost, replenishment, or whether every assistance option is available immediately.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available. There is no supported player digest about repetition, word selection, visual clarity, or later difficulty. Promotional claims of rapid improvement should not become measured learning outcomes.\n\nThe metadata records a free download, and offline play is advertised. Advertising frequency, removal offers, stability, and the full disconnected scope remain unverified. No screenshot was visually inspected, so specific landmark imagery is not independently described.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWoW Search may suit players who enjoy subject-guided word finding and a travel-themed progression. It is less conclusive for someone expecting a traditional crossword or a precisely documented search-path system. The combination of topic clues and decoy letters is the clearest stated attraction; its execution and access economy remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this definitely a traditional crossword?\nThe detailed text emphasizes finding hidden words on a board rather than filling clue entries.\n\n### Do level subjects matter?\nYes. The listing connects subjects with the words you need to find.\n\n### Are word directions fully specified?\nNo. The complete path rules are not documented.\n\n### Is offline play independently confirmed?\nNo. It is an attributed developer claim.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/words-of-wonders-search/id1483222663?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
