    "bodyMarkdown": "# Math Crossword: Arithmetic Satisfaction, With a Move-Count Debate\n\nMath Crossword replaces verbal clues with numerical relationships. The sample's strongest praise concerns getting the equations to work, while its sharper reservations concern entering answers and the constraints added around them. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Solve the relationship, not just one blank\n\nThe listing describes a crossword-like grid whose answers come from equations or mathematical problems. Difficulty progression, hints, and offline play are advertised.\n\nThe grid format encourages checking how a value participates in the wider puzzle rather than treating every blank as unrelated. The supplied description does not fully document the available operations, precedence tutorial, or answer-entry method.\n\nIt should therefore not be turned into a precise lesson based on another arithmetic app's rules. The documented concept is an equation grid, with assistance when progress stalls.\n\n## Challenge is welcome until the controls compete\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-05-08 through 2026-10-14 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 5.3.0, 5.4.0, 5.4.1; the listing records version 5.4.1. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy mental arithmetic and an increasing challenge they still consider manageable. Another finds even difficult boards elementary or repetitive. Those reactions reflect different mathematical familiarity rather than one universal difficulty rating.\n\nSome reviewers object to added move counting. Others describe poor placement response, a reward multiplier not adding expected points, or an equation they believe contains an incorrect operator. These are user reports, not independently verified faults or proof of intentional interference.\n\nThe move-count complaints are especially useful for someone expecting unlimited experimentation: the listing alone does not fully explain that constraint.\n\n## Standard and Premium have specific stated benefits\n\nThe description says Standard disables banners and popup ads. Premium adds a gold avatar border while also disabling those formats. The plans renew automatically, but current prices and durations are not fully supplied here.\n\nSeveral reviewers describe ads during puzzles or after rounds. A purchaser says reward videos remain despite paid access, which is not necessarily the same category as the specifically named banners and popups.\n\nThe evidence does not establish a current universal removal price or every entitlement. Reviewer-reported dollar amounts should remain historical reports rather than a verified checkout.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMath Crossword may suit players who enjoy checking numerical relationships in a grid. It is less clearly suited to someone who wants unlimited trial moves or verified precision in answer placement. The sample supports the arithmetic premise while identifying practical constraints that can matter as much as the calculation itself.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are the clues words?\nThe listing describes equations or mathematical problems instead.\n\n### Is move counting fully documented?\nNo. Reviewers discuss it, but the listing does not provide complete rules.\n\n### What extra benefit does Premium list?\nA gold avatar border, in addition to disabling banners and popups.\n\n### Are reward videos necessarily covered by removal?\nThat is not established; the listing names particular ad formats.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/math-crossword-number-puzzle/id6469306922?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
