    "bodyMarkdown": "# Vita Jigsaw: Senior-Friendly Claims Need More Than Large Buttons\n\nVita Jigsaw promises a comfortable digital puzzle for older players. The sample suggests picture choice and adjustable difficulty are appreciated, while piece size and placement tolerance still deserve individual scrutiny. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Choose the picture and the piece count\n\nThe developer advertises more than 10,000 images, daily additions, zoom, large controls, and difficulties from 36 pieces to more than 400. The task remains assembling a picture rather than solving a line-clearing grid.\n\nPiece count changes the workload, but it can also change how much visual information each fragment provides. A larger image collection does not necessarily mean a wider variety of piece-cut layouts.\n\nThe listing's senior-focused label is a design intention, not a certification for every visual or motor need. No screenshot was inspected to independently judge button size or image clarity.\n\n## Comfort is not one universal setting\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-05-29 through 2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.1.29, 1.1.30, 1.1.31, 1.1.32; the listing records version 1.1.32. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy the pictures, selecting difficulty, and a comforting routine. One couple describes playing after dinner, while also reporting that an update stopped the app opening on their iPad.\n\nAnother player asks for larger pieces despite the listing's oversized-piece pitch. A different reviewer wants more cut layouts because successive puzzles feel mechanically similar. Someone else dislikes pieces not snapping into place when brought close to their destination.\n\nThose are distinct usability questions: seeing a piece, recognizing its location, and getting the app to accept placement are not the same task.\n\n## Ads can obstruct the intended quiet routine\n\nSome reviewers report fewer ads than expected, while others describe breaks before or after pictures, repeated interruptions, or difficulty proceeding past a video. A free download does not establish an ad-free experience.\n\nThe current removal offer is not verified; a reviewer saying they cannot pay to remove ads does not establish every account's options. Likewise, a request to import personal photos is not confirmation that the feature exists.\n\nThe listing's references to improved sleep are not supported here by clinical evidence. Enjoying a peaceful picture is a legitimate reason to play without attaching a health promise.\n\n## Verdict\n\nVita Jigsaw may suit players who want a picture-led pastime with advertised zoom and piece-count choices. It is less conclusive for someone requiring specific snapping behavior or reliably large fragments at every difficulty. The sample's comfort praise is meaningful, but the detailed accessibility and interruption concerns deserve equal weight.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What piece-count range is advertised?\nFrom 36 pieces to more than 400.\n\n### Does the senior label prove accessibility?\nNo. Actual visual and motor comfort depends on individual needs and the implementation.\n\n### Can you import personal photos?\nThe supplied listing does not establish it; a reviewer requests that feature.\n\n### Are improved sleep outcomes verified?\nNo. The evidence does not substantiate a clinical sleep benefit.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/vita-jigsaw-for-seniors/id6451403685?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
