    "bodyMarkdown": "# Satisdom: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nSatisdom is a collection of small interactive tasks rather than one puzzle rule stretched across a campaign. Sorting, tidying, and playful objects are its advertised route to a satisfying result.\n\n## The gesture changes with the task\n\nThe description names tapping, dragging, sliding, and drawing. That variety is the proposition: inspect the current object, infer what needs adjusting, and complete the interaction rather than repeatedly solve the same grid.\n\nThe official image shows a hand holding a striped object above a pink drink with an animal-like face. A progress bar and skip-looking control appear above. It establishes a toy-like interaction, not the precise solution or a universal set of level controls.\n\nThe broader catalog includes anti-stress puzzles and cute objects. Exact tasks, total level count, and whether categories can be selected freely are not documented. A mini-game collection does not automatically mean every stage is available in any order.\n\n## Sound and vibration are part of the pitch\n\nASMR effects, background music, and vibration are advertised as central presentation elements. Those claims are relevant to readers who specifically enjoy sensory feedback, but the quality and intensity have not been tested.\n\nThe supplied text does not establish separate audio controls, haptic switches, or compatibility with background podcasts. Readers seeking silent interaction should check those settings rather than assume a relaxation label guarantees quiet operation.\n\nThe pictured progress display also does not establish a countdown. Conversely, soothing language is not proof that every task has unlimited time or no failure condition. Assistance and restart rules remain unspecified.\n\n## Entertainment is not established treatment\n\nThe listing invokes art therapy, brain health, and potentially easing OCD. None of the supplied material establishes clinical testing, a treatment protocol, or an independently measured health effect. The case for interest is the interaction collection itself.\n\nConsider Satisdom if the pleasure of arranging, cleaning, and manipulating small objects is enough. It is less appropriate as a choice for sustained deductive depth or a defined therapeutic program.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, purchases, removal terms, and offline access are unresolved. No customer review bodies are available in this sample to establish whether the tasks feel satisfying, whether gestures register well, or how often commercial interruptions occur.\n\n## Verdict\n\nSatisdom offers varied tactile-looking mini-games with an explicit sensory presentation. Its fit is a preference for that style of interaction, not a verified health benefit or a fully documented no-pressure mode.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does every level use the same gesture?\n\nNo single gesture is promised. The description names tapping, dragging, sliding, and drawing across different activities.\n\n### Are sound and vibration advertised?\n\nYes, but their settings and actual behavior were not tested.\n\n### Is Satisdom a verified OCD treatment?\n\nNo clinical effectiveness is established by the supplied listing.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/satisdom/id6480585904?uo=4)."
