    "bodyMarkdown": "# Rubix Cube Solver: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nRubix Cube Solver is primarily a solving aid, not just a virtual cube to scramble. Its stated purpose is to show the moves needed to resolve a cube you cannot finish.\n\n## The solution is presented as a sequence\n\nThe developer promises step-by-step movements and explanations of algorithm details. That makes the app relevant to someone who wants guidance rather than another unassisted attempt at a twisty puzzle.\n\nThe supplied description does not explain how the initial cube state is entered. Camera scanning, automatic color recognition, and manual face entry should therefore not be promised. The first official image is a cube-and-branding panel, not a demonstration of the input screen.\n\nFollowing a move sequence and learning a general solving method are also distinct goals. Explanations are advertised, but the listing does not establish a complete beginner curriculum or guarantee that following one solution teaches independent solves.\n\n## The free boundary is unusually clear\n\nOnly the 3×3 solver is described as free. Solvers for 2×2, 4×4, Void Cube, Tower, and Domino are also offered, but the description explicitly excludes them from that free entitlement.\n\nThe US download itself is free. Current prices, whether access is purchased separately or through a bundle, and any recurring billing terms are not established. A free app icon is not a promise of free support for every puzzle size.\n\nThe listed variants are useful distinctions for a mixed collection. They do not establish support for every twisty puzzle or for arbitrary custom shapes.\n\n## Treat the move ceiling as a product claim\n\nThe developer advertises an optimal solution of 22 moves or fewer for the 3×3. The material does not define its move-count convention or supply an independent algorithm benchmark. That wording should remain attributed rather than become a verified universal guarantee.\n\nThe large solved-cube and satisfied-user totals on the promotional panel are also marketing counts, not customer review bodies. No independent review sample is available here.\n\nAdvertising, offline operation, invalid-state detection, and error recovery are unresolved. Consider Rubix Cube Solver for guided assistance with its explicitly supported puzzles. Readers who require camera input, a guaranteed teaching course, or unrestricted free 4×4 solving need confirmation beyond the supplied evidence.\n\n## Verdict\n\nRubix Cube Solver has a clear aid-oriented purpose and a useful distinction between the free 3×3 solver and other variants. Input methods, prices, and the strength of its optimality claim remain unverified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Which solver is described as free?\n\nOnly the 3×3 solver is explicitly described as free.\n\n### Does it scan a cube with the camera?\n\nThe supplied description does not establish a camera-input feature.\n\n### Does it explain moves?\n\nStep-by-step movements and algorithm explanations are advertised; their teaching quality was not tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/rubix-cube-solver/id784085951?uo=4)."
