    "bodyMarkdown": "# Daily Mahjong Match: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-08-31–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.0.20–1.0.22. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nDaily Mahjong Match uses mahjong imagery for a different matching system. Find identical tiles along rows or columns, use the gaps between them, and drag a tile horizontally or vertically to create a match.\n\n## Do not import classic solitaire restrictions\n\nThe developer explicitly says mahjong refers to the visual tile style, not traditional mahjong or another solitaire ruleset. The inspected image shows a flat arrangement with gaps and an enlarged matching pair, rather than a conventional stacked pyramid.\n\nEasy, Normal, and Expert settings are advertised, along with free hints, daily content, no timer, and offline play. These are product claims rather than tested entitlements or network findings.\n\nOne reviewer enjoys the twist, while another specifically likes Expert difficulty. A newcomer finds it approachable without a separate tutorial. Those are useful reasons to consider the matching mechanic beyond the familiar tile symbols.\n\n## Readability and guidance are not settled by large tiles\n\nA supporter appreciates clear tiles and little excess animation. Another says the characters remain too small for their eyes despite the senior-oriented marketing. This is a preference and visibility split, not an independently verified accessibility outcome.\n\nA separate player cannot find the rules and remains unsure when a strategy works. The written listing explains movement, but that does not establish how well the in-app introduction communicates it.\n\n## Coins are not the same as usable rescue tools\n\nTwo detailed accounts describe accumulating coins without understanding or accessing their purpose. One says a stuck Expert board will not shuffle and progress resets instead. These are reported discoverability or state problems, not proof that the currency is universally useless.\n\nOther reviewers also describe lost progress after closing an unclosable ad. Such reports matter especially when the next attempt begins at an easier entry point rather than preserving a chosen challenge.\n\n## Ad removal accounts conflict\n\nReviewers repeatedly dispute no-ads promotions. One supporter tolerates a single commercial per match; others report mid-game interruptions. Some request a permanent removal option, while another quotes an offer already visible to them.\n\nThat disagreement prevents a universal current price or availability claim. The US download is free, but the actual removal terms, offline coverage, and technical behavior have not been independently checked.\n\nChoose for sliding-pair reasoning if that departure from stacked solitaire interests you, while treating readability and commercial continuity as separate selection questions.\n\n## Verdict\n\nDaily Mahjong Match has a specific and supported movement-based twist. Expert fans and newcomers both appear, but progress loss, confusing coins, and conflicting removal offers keep the recommendation conditional.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it traditional mahjong?\n\nNo. The listing explicitly limits mahjong to the tile imagery and describes sliding pair matching.\n\n### Are there difficulty choices?\n\nEasy, Normal, and Expert are advertised; one reviewer specifically praises Expert.\n\n### Can coins always buy a shuffle?\n\nA detailed reviewer reports being unable to use accumulated coins that way. Current tool rules are unresolved.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/daily-mahjong-match/id6741703805?uo=4)."
