    "bodyMarkdown": "# Unpuzzle: Tap Away & Car Out: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nUnpuzzle: Tap Away & Car Out packages vehicle unblocking and directional block removal together. The shared idea is to free an exit before trying to release what is trapped behind it.\n\n## Direction supplies the constraint\n\nThe description says cars slide out in an indicated direction, while blocks and cars can be tapped or moved to clear paths. View rotation is explicitly offered to inspect another approach.\n\nThe first official image shows a flat-looking collection of colored arrow-marked blocks under a Tap to Move caption. It supports directional release, but does not demonstrate the full parking mode or establish that every stage uses a freely rotatable 3D cube.\n\nA useful conceptual question is which piece has a clear route now and what removing it will expose. This follows from the stated escape objective, not a tested solution strategy or proof that every layout is uniquely solvable.\n\n## The catalog pitch is broader than the pictured board\n\nCars, wooden blocks, skins, and thousands of layouts are advertised. Those are product claims, not an audited inventory of modes or an assurance that all customization is free.\n\nThe description repeatedly shifts between parking and tap-away language. Readers should not assume a realistic driving simulation, steering controls, or real traffic rules. The concrete premise is puzzle-based unblocking.\n\nBombs, hammers, and other boosters are listed for harder stages. Their availability, costs, and exact effects are not explained. Calling them important in the description is not proof that an ordinary board cannot be completed without purchasing assistance.\n\n## Calm language coexists with a competitive clock\n\nThe developer promotes stress relief but also says to race against time and other players. That makes a blanket no-timer recommendation inappropriate. The text does not specify whether timed competition is separate from ordinary levels or whether every board has a deadline.\n\nOffline play is advertised too. That claim does not establish how leaderboard features behave without a connection, and the full network boundary has not been tested.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, purchases, paid removal, retries, and failure conditions remain unspecified. No customer review bodies are available in this sample to support claims about fair challenges or comfortable view controls.\n\nConsider Unpuzzle if identifying release dependencies across vehicles and blocks sounds appealing. Readers who need one precise, stable mode specification should recognize the breadth and ambiguity of the supplied presentation.\n\n## Verdict\n\nUnpuzzle has a recognizable directional-escape foundation, with rotation and boosters explicitly listed. Timer scope and mode boundaries remain less clear than the bright arrow-block image might suggest.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can the view rotate?\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises rotating the view for another approach.\n\n### Is the whole app untimed?\n\nNo such finding is supported. The description specifically promotes racing against the clock.\n\n### Does the first image show cars?\n\nIt shows arrow-marked blocks; the car-escape mode is described in the text rather than demonstrated there.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/unpuzzle-tap-away-car-out/id1596319487?uo=4)."
