    "bodyMarkdown": "# Merge Hexa™ Number Puzzle Game: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2025-11-28–2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.8.7–3.0.1. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMerge Hexa™ Number Puzzle Game builds a score by placing numbered hexagons and combining matching values. Preserving useful board space matters alongside reaching a larger number.\n\n## Placement is the decision\n\nThe listing says to drag blocks onto the board, match equal numbers, and keep room available. The official image shows a populated hex grid, a paired incoming piece, and a curved rotation-looking control below.\n\nIt does not provide a complete merge threshold or placement guide. The family is number merging, not a verified clone of falling-block Tetris, despite one reviewer expecting that from an advertisement.\n\nSupporters describe repeated daily play and an enjoyable strategic challenge. Another finds the game easy after discovering a reliable method. Difficulty is not a single agreed quality in the sample.\n\n## Larger numbers do not guarantee endless novelty\n\nSeveral invested reviewers say progression reaches 999 or an infinity stage and then becomes repetitive. One otherwise satisfied purchaser wants additional rounds rather than merely more time with the same endgame.\n\nThose are historical reports across versions, not an independently audited current cap. They are still relevant to someone choosing specifically for indefinitely rising numerical targets.\n\nA restart request also deserves precision: one player cannot find a new-game control and explicitly allows that it may exist unnoticed. That uncertainty should not become a definitive missing-feature claim.\n\n## An accidental placement can spoil planning\n\nTwo accounts describe tiles jumping onto the board while the player tries to rotate or taps unintentionally. Another finds Pencil input inaccurate on an iPad. These are reported control concerns, not reproduced device findings.\n\nA separate reviewer describes locked cells needing diamonds later in progression. This is a spending-pressure account, not proof that every possible path requires cash.\n\n## Advertising experiences diverge\n\nOne supporter says ads do not follow every round, while another reports interruptions after a few moves. Purchasers also disagree: one has no regrets about removal, and another says ads continued after payment.\n\nThe US download is free. Present prices, removal coverage, and stability are unresolved. Offline play is advertised and supported by a reviewer’s account, without full feature testing.\n\nConsider Merge Hexa for spatial number building rather than an assumed infinite campaign. Readers who dislike accidental input or care about a permanent removal offer should inspect those independently.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMerge Hexa has supporters of its strategic number growth, but reported endgame repetition and placement mishaps are meaningful qualifications. Contrasting ad accounts prevent a simple free-versus-paid experience guarantee.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this a falling-block Tetris game?\n\nThe supplied rules describe dragging hexagons into a merging board, not a documented Tetris ruleset.\n\n### Does it continue indefinitely beyond 999?\n\nSeveral reviewers describe a cap or repetitive infinity stage. Current progression was not independently audited.\n\n### Is offline play supported?\n\nThe developer advertises it and one reviewer reports it working; complete feature coverage remains untested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/merge-hexa-number-puzzle-game/id1497489224?uo=4)."
