    "bodyMarkdown": "# Blossom Match: Puzzle Game: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-01-20–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.72.04–2.72.12. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nBlossom Match: Puzzle Game places flower recognition inside a layered matching board. Choose matching tiles, clear sets, and expose what remains underneath.\n\n## A tray makes selection consequential\n\nThe description says to match three or more tiles. The official image specifically promotes matching three and shows flower tiles stacked over one another above a holding tray. It is not a grid of gems swapped with adjacent pieces.\n\nThe tray separates selecting a tile from completing its set, although its universal capacity and failure rules are not fully documented. A visible flower may also be unavailable because another layer covers it.\n\nOne reviewer enjoys the strategy, while another appreciates a challenge that remains manageable. These comments identify a planning attraction beyond the decorative art.\n\n## Similar flowers complicate the search\n\nA detailed May reviewer finds flowers easy to confuse and says the visual depth sometimes makes a desired tile appear more accessible than it is. That is a specific readability complaint, not simply a request for easier boards.\n\nTwo other accounts say cream-colored flowers that look alike stop matching. That could involve symbol distinction or a defect; neither explanation was independently tested. Reinstalling and losing progress is a reported workaround, not advice endorsed here.\n\nThe sample also contains a highly positive rating whose author explicitly says they never played. It is not used as firsthand evidence of the game’s quality.\n\n## A finished board can still lead to frustration\n\nSeveral reviewers describe commercials between levels, while one also reports interruptions during a complicated sequence. A July counterpoint finds few annoying ads. These differing accounts prevent one universal schedule.\n\nA particularly consequential complaint says closing the app before an ad ends requires replaying the completed board. Another reports a blank ad and repeated failure to move beyond level eight. These are progress-flow reports, not verified proof that every transition behaves that way.\n\nSome players request a permanent removal option, while others mention tickets or payment. Present prices and entitlement coverage remain unresolved. References to Scrambly or a survey offer do not establish a native cash-paying feature.\n\nThe US download is free. Offline support and current technical reliability have not been tested. Consider Blossom Match if planning around layered floral tiles appeals, while checking symbol readability and transition behavior before building a long routine.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlossom Match has support for approachable strategy and attractive flowers. The sample’s most useful cautions are distinct: reading the layers, distinguishing similar symbols, and retaining progress through ad transitions.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you swap adjacent flowers?\n\nThe official image shows selecting layered tiles into a tray, rather than an adjacent-swap board.\n\n### Are similar cream flowers definitely the same tile?\n\nReviewers report apparent matching failures, but symbol identity and the cause were not independently verified.\n\n### Does an outside reward offer mean the game pays cash?\n\nNo. Those accounts involve third-party offers, not an established native cash feature.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/blossom-match-puzzle-game/id6503251921?uo=4)."
