    "bodyMarkdown": "# Ball Sort - Color Games: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-03-23–2026-11-05 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.3.6–1.4.2. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nBall Sort - Color Games uses stacked balls rather than liquid bands for a color-organizing task. Transfer balls among tubes until each contains a single color, while preserving enough capacity to continue.\n\n## The top of a stack matters\n\nThe description restricts transfers by color and available tube capacity. Hints, an extra-tube option, and undo are advertised, but explicitly described as limited. Their presence is not a guarantee of unlimited free rescues.\n\nThe inspected image shows mixed stacks, three empty tubes, and assistance icons beneath. It illustrates temporary storage without establishing the number of spare tubes on every board or the exact cost of adding another.\n\nA useful move can expose a buried color as well as organize the transferred ball. That is the planning attraction in the stated rules, not a tested optimal solution for the pictured level.\n\n## Accessible-looking is not universally difficult\n\nOne reviewer likes the progression from easy to more demanding boards. Another wants more difficulty, while a third values a task that is not obvious yet remains satisfying. These are different preferences rather than a measured difficulty curve.\n\nA player appreciates larger graphics for their own visual needs, and another enjoys the beat sounds. The comments identify concrete presentation benefits without establishing clinical or accessibility certification.\n\nMany other endorsements are brief. They support enjoyment but cannot establish a generous tool economy, varied late-game obstacles, or a verified number of unique stages.\n\n## Keeping progress is a separate issue\n\nOne player says changing phones returned them to the beginning. Another associates a new version with a reset before difficulty becomes interesting. Those are reported continuity problems, not proof that every update erases data.\n\nThis matters if the appeal is working toward tougher sorting arrangements rather than replaying introductory boards. Account linking, cloud recovery, and current save behavior are not established by the supplied description.\n\n## Ads and purchase coverage\n\nA reviewer describes commercials after rounds, and a purchaser says ads continued despite a no-ads payment. The refund outcome in that account is unresolved; it is not evidence of a completed refund or universal entitlement failure.\n\nThe US download is free. Current removal prices, helper costs, offline support, and technical stability have not been tested. Consider this Ball Sort for its approachable stack organization, but check progress transfer and assistance allowances before treating it as a long-term interruption-free routine.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBall Sort has specific support for satisfying, readable color organization. Its best-supported reservations concern continuity and commercial terms, not an independently demonstrated lack of solvable puzzles.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are undo and extra tubes unlimited?\n\nNo such promise is made. The description explicitly calls the assistance options limited.\n\n### Does progress automatically transfer to a new phone?\n\nOne reviewer reports starting over; current linking and recovery behavior remain unverified.\n\n### Does payment guarantee no ads?\n\nA purchaser reports remaining ads, without enough evidence to establish current offer coverage.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/ball-sort-color-games/id6499139132?uo=4)."
