    "bodyMarkdown": "# Woodber - Classic Number Game: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-05-23–2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.12–2.23. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWoodber - Classic Number Game removes numbers instead of stacking wooden shapes. Clear equal digits or pairs adding to ten, then use added rows when the remaining arrangement offers no useful match.\n\n## Arithmetic and position both matter\n\nThe rules include adjacent horizontal, vertical, and diagonal pairs, plus the end of one row and the beginning of the next. The official image highlights equal digits inside a wood-toned number grid.\n\nThat makes this a pairing puzzle, not a block-placement game despite the wooden theme. The supplied text does not fully document every long-distance gap rule, so a complete advanced matching guide is not invented.\n\nOne reviewer likes the wood feel and the ability to change the number appearance. Others want more Journey variety. A comment about difficulty recognizing legal pairs suggests that understanding connectivity can matter as much as finding a sum of ten.\n\n## Journeys provide a second reason to return\n\nDaily challenges, statistics, auto-save, seasonal trophies, and new weekly puzzles are advertised. The listing also promises no time limit and offline play. Those are product claims, not independently tested conditions.\n\nAn enthusiastic player values the calm routine. Another says puzzles repeat, while several invested reviewers request more interesting Journeys. Requests for variety are not evidence that the feature was removed everywhere.\n\n## Free hints are not necessarily video-free hints\n\nThe developer advertises unlimited free hints. Purchasers nevertheless describe watching ads for hints and extra rows after paying. Those statements can coexist if free means no additional cash rather than no commercial viewing.\n\nOther accounts report mid-board advertising, audio interruptions, or leaving the app for an unexpected destination. A June counterexample finds ads short and acceptable. No universal frequency or current policy follows from the mixed evidence.\n\nCurrent removal price and coverage remain unverified; the US download is free. A reported policy violation is not independently established as a legal or platform-enforcement finding.\n\n## Saving needs more than a promise\n\nSeveral players describe progress resetting after an update or closing the app. Others report crashes in later versions. These are individual technical accounts, but they directly qualify the listed auto-save promise.\n\nConsider Woodber if number relationships and board connectivity appeal. Readers who need dependable pause-and-resume play or quiet background-audio use should examine those separately from the soothing wood presentation.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWoodber has a well-defined equal-or-ten objective and supporters of its number presentation. The important caveats are the assistance economy and reported save failures, not a claim that the underlying arithmetic puzzle has no appeal.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Which number pairs clear?\n\nEqual digits and pairs summing to ten, subject to the listed positional rules.\n\n### Does unlimited free help mean no ads?\n\nNot necessarily. Reviewers describe videos for hints and extra rows, including after payment.\n\n### Is auto-save advertised?\n\nYes, but multiple reviewers report progress loss after closing or updating; current reliability is untested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/woodber-classic-number-game/id1635289326?uo=4)."
