    "bodyMarkdown": "# Gallery: Coloring book & decor: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-05-02–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.7.2–4.8.1. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nGallery: Coloring book & decor gives completed coloring pages a practical destination: stars for renovating Mia and Leo’s house and abandoned art gallery. The story is part of the reason to finish another picture.\n\n## Art and decor reinforce one another\n\nThe listing advertises numbered coloring in geometric, flat, pixel, and low-poly styles, with decor choices and 3D characters. The official image juxtaposes partly numbered artwork with completed colored regions.\n\nA detailed supporter likes the composition and palette choices and finds the decor easy to change. Another explicitly says earning renovation stars makes this more engaging than a plain coloring book. These are specific benefits of the hybrid, not evidence of freehand drawing.\n\n## Energy is not experienced uniformly\n\nOne October returner finds energy refills reasonable and describes unlocking pictures in advance to color later. Another enjoys the art but feels limited to only a few pictures before resources run out.\n\nThese accounts preserve a real suitability split. A catalog of pictures does not necessarily mean immediate unrestricted access, while resource limits do not prove nobody can enjoy the game without subscribing.\n\nSeveral long-term players report reaching the end of available story chapters and waiting for more. That is a dated content-exhaustion concern, not proof the narrative was removed for everyone or that the developer has abandoned updates.\n\n## Corner ads affect the coloring space\n\nReviewers describe small commercial windows and unprompted audio during painting, as well as ads after completed pictures. One supporter tolerates the corner ads; others find them distracting or accidentally activate them while moving the picture.\n\nThe listing quotes a $7.99 weekly Premium subscription with a three-day trial, ad removal, daily resources, free decor choices, and a doubled energy bar. Those are attributed listing terms, not verified account-specific checkout benefits. Premium renews automatically.\n\nA purchaser reports losing subscription advantages, but the entitlement cause is unresolved. Current pricing and removal coverage were not tested.\n\n## Fixes should keep their dates\n\nSeveral May reviewers explicitly say picture-loading problems were fixed and thank support. A November account cannot get beyond the loading screen. Both belong in the analysis: an older repair does not guarantee present stability, and a newer failure does not erase the reported recovery.\n\nThe US download is free; offline operation remains unresolved. Consider Gallery when renovation gives supplied coloring a goal, while weighing energy and story availability separately from art preference.\n\n## Verdict\n\nGallery has unusually specific support for both its pictures and renovation purpose. Its fit depends on accepting resource pacing and commercial overlays, with loading recovery and later failures kept in their actual context.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What do completed paintings provide?\n\nThe listing links coloring to stars used for house and gallery renovation.\n\n### Is Premium a lifetime unlock?\n\nNo. The supplied offer describes a weekly auto-renewing subscription with a trial.\n\n### Were loading problems fixed?\n\nSeveral May accounts say so, while a November reviewer reports a loading failure. Current universal stability is not established.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/gallery-coloring-book-decor/id1456596043?uo=4)."
