    "bodyMarkdown": "# Onet 3D - Zen Tile Puzzle: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2024-04-01–2026-09-03 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.9.36–2.9.73. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nOnet 3D - Zen Tile Puzzle clears identical picture pairs from a board. Its official imagery adds a connection-path presentation, while the description leaves the exact legal-link geometry surprisingly brief.\n\n## Matching includes a visible route\n\nThe inspected image highlights two food tiles joined by a bent line through open space. The text confirms identical-pair removal, hints, shuffles, and multiple themes, but does not specify a maximum number of turns or every obstruction rule.\n\nThat distinction matters if you know other Onet games. A familiar title is not sufficient evidence to import their complete path restrictions.\n\nOne supporter likes easy recognition, and another finds the challenge just right. A 2024 reviewer specifically appreciates enlarged fonts and characters after an update. These comments support aspects of the presentation without establishing universal accessibility.\n\n## Theme choice has a reported recovery\n\nA detailed reviewer initially dislikes rarely receiving the Pirate icons bought with in-game coins. Their update explicitly says the preferred icons now appear regularly and thanks the team.\n\nThe purchase was not described as cash, and the later improvement should not be omitted. It is evidence of that person’s resolved selection issue, not proof that every customization concern is fixed.\n\n## Historical feedback cannot establish today’s whole app\n\nThe 50 reviews run from April 2024 through September 2026 across many versions. Reports of lost points, hints, or event results belong to that historical window rather than a supposed current-week consensus.\n\nOne player dislikes being moved to an easier puzzle after failing instead of retrying the original. That identifies a preference for persistence over automatic relief; it is not a demand for fewer hints.\n\n## Closing an ad is the persistent practical question\n\nSeveral accounts describe ads without a usable exit, requiring the app to close and sometimes replaying a board without points. The latest included September account raises that concern too.\n\nThe listing describes automatically renewing access at $4.99 weekly, $9.99 monthly, or $69.99 annually. These are supplied listing amounts, not verified current checkout prices. Unlimited content access does not independently establish every removal benefit.\n\nThe US download is free, and offline play is advertised but untested. Consider Onet for visual pair-linking if its themes appeal, while checking current ad control and subscription terms rather than assuming zen means interruption-free.\n\n## Verdict\n\nOnet 3D has supporters of its recognizable images and measured challenge. The historical sample requires careful dates, especially around ad-closing problems and one explicitly resolved theme-selection complaint.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What clears from the board?\n\nIdentical picture pairs; the official image also shows a bent connection path.\n\n### Is a two-turn connection limit confirmed?\n\nNo. The supplied description does not give a complete legal-path specification.\n\n### Was the Pirate icon complaint resolved?\n\nThat reviewer explicitly updates the account to say the icons appear regularly and the experience improved.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/onet-3d-zen-tile-puzzle/id1502227743?uo=4)."
