    "bodyMarkdown": "# WordWhizzle Search: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2022-01-15–2026-08-05 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.7.6–1.9.6. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWordWhizzle Search adds a category prompt to a grid of letters. Find the words that fit the theme and trace their paths, using both vocabulary association and visual search.\n\n## The category is more than decoration\n\nThe official image asks for words related to the body and highlights EAR in a compact grid. Unlike a conventional word search with a complete printed list, that example asks you to identify a relevant answer as well as locate it.\n\nThe description supports swiping vertically, horizontally, and diagonally, with more than 2,700 levels and daily puzzles that award hints. It does not fully specify bending-path restrictions or every accepted category answer.\n\nA detailed supporter likes larger, more demanding grids and the lack of unrelated filler. Another finds big grids fiddly on a phone but says reversing the tracing direction helps. These are useful design and input observations rather than a universal device verdict.\n\n## Enthusiasm can end at the catalog boundary\n\nThe latest included reviewer has finished available levels and still enjoys daily and weekly puzzles while requesting more. Similar requests appear years earlier. That supports a long-run content concern, not proof that no new puzzle was ever added.\n\nThe sample stretches from January 2022 through August 2026. It must not be treated as a single recent version cycle or a current campaign count.\n\nSpecific spelling complaints also appear, including a reviewer reporting PALE where a water-container clue should indicate PAIL. That is an attributed content report, not an independently audited error rate or a complete dictionary judgment.\n\n## A daily habit can become punitive\n\nOne long-term player dislikes losing a daily streak after illness and being unable to make up the missed date. Another reports resets despite not missing days. These are different problems: an unwelcome streak rule and an alleged tracking failure.\n\nA separate device-sync complaint says progress cannot be carried to an iPad. The listing’s Facebook social claim does not independently establish reliable cross-device saves.\n\n## Ads are mixed, not absent\n\nSome players report a commercial after every puzzle, while another considers the waits tolerable. An older supporter mentions discounted removal offers, without establishing a current price or entitlement scope.\n\nThe US download is free. Current checkout terms, offline support, and save recovery are untested. Consider WordWhizzle for category-driven searches, while weighing late-catalog novelty and streak flexibility separately from the word mechanic.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWordWhizzle Search has committed fans of its themed grids. Its historical evidence highlights content exhaustion and fragile daily or device continuity more usefully than a generic brain-training recommendation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does it give category clues?\n\nYes. The official example uses a body-related prompt and a traced answer.\n\n### Can missed daily puzzles be made up?\n\nOne detailed reviewer says they could not; current make-up options were not tested.\n\n### Does Facebook support guarantee device syncing?\n\nNo. The listing’s social feature does not settle save transfer, and a reviewer reports difficulty syncing to an iPad.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wordwhizzle-search/id1105834924?uo=4)."
