    "bodyMarkdown": "# Puzzledom: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nPuzzledom offers a shelf of logic formats rather than one evolving rule. Move from shape fitting to connections, number puzzles, or routes while keeping the developer’s no-time-limit premise.\n\n## Variety comes from changing the task\n\nThe description names Tangram, Shikaku, Plumber, Sudoku, Maze, One Stroke, Lazors, and other families. Their inclusion is more specific than a generic claim of thousands of brain teasers.\n\nThe first official image shows colored square-cell pieces filling an irregular outlined recess. It illustrates one shape-fitting activity, not the rules or control quality of every category in the collection.\n\nA reader who tires of one mental operation may value switching formats inside a single app. Someone seeking deep instruction in a particular puzzle should not assume the breadth establishes a complete tutorial or advanced-solving curriculum.\n\nThe supplied text does not provide an inventory of variant rules. Exact rotation, mistake, hint, and restart behavior may differ between games and remain undocumented here.\n\n## Offline and untimed are explicit claims\n\nMore than 8,000 offline levels and no time limit are advertised. Those are useful stated features, not independently audited content counts or network tests.\n\nNo time limit does not settle every failure condition. A shape can still be placed badly or a route can still block another even without a countdown. The description does not establish unlimited undo or penalty-free mistakes across all categories.\n\nLikewise, offline access does not establish that every future update, purchase restoration, or connected feature is available without a connection. Downloaded-content boundaries were not tested.\n\n## Subscription access is temporary access\n\nThe listing quotes $7.99 weekly for unlimited content access during an automatically renewing subscription. That is a supplied description amount, not a verified personalized checkout price or a lifetime unlock.\n\nThe US download is free. The exact free catalog, advertising, paid-removal benefits, and assistance economy are not fully described. A large total level count should not be assumed to mean every listed activity is unrestricted without membership.\n\nThe leaderboard wording is a future promise: the developer says online rankings will be supported soon. It should not be listed as a verified current competitive feature.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample. Consider Puzzledom for documented genre breadth and the offline pitch, not an invented player consensus about fair access or reliable saving.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPuzzledom has a substantial advertised range of logic formats. Its recurring unlimited-access offer and future-tense ranking promise need to stay distinct from the free download and current feature set.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Which puzzle types are named?\n\nExamples include Tangram, Shikaku, Plumber, Sudoku, Maze, One Stroke, and Lazors.\n\n### Is the leaderboard already confirmed?\n\nNo. The supplied text describes support as coming soon.\n\n### Is the weekly plan a permanent unlock?\n\nNo. Unlimited access is described for the subscription period, with automatic renewal.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/puzzledom/id1261300725?uo=4)."
