    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong - Tile Matching Puzzle: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nMahjong - Tile Matching Puzzle by Brainium gives a clear definition of a playable tile: uncovered from above, with either its left or right side open. Matching symbols alone is not sufficient.\n\n## Availability creates the puzzle\n\nRemove identical exposed pairs to clear the board. Flower tiles may pair with other flowers and seasons with other seasons, so those categories have explicitly broader matching rules than exact-image duplication.\n\nThe official image shows traditional tiles scattered and layered on green felt. It supports a solitaire presentation rather than four-player hand building, but does not demonstrate a complete solution.\n\nRemoving a pair can expose another tile or release a side. That makes the order of otherwise valid matches consequential. The developer’s assertion that every generated game is winnable does not mean every sequence of legal moves preserves a win.\n\n## Undo is a distinctive convenience\n\nAn undo slider is advertised for retracing a blocked route, alongside hints. That is more specific than simply saying the game offers help: readers can expect a history-navigation concept rather than only a single hint button.\n\nThe allowance and cost of using assistance are not explained. Neither the slider nor the solvability claim proves the app automatically teaches why a previous pair was strategically poor.\n\nA playable-tile option can be toggled for more casual play. It may reduce the burden of identifying available tiles, while leaving matching order as the activity. Its contrast and exact visual cues were not tested.\n\n## Timing is offered as a choice\n\nThe listing calls the timer optional. This is different from a mandatory countdown, though the supplied text does not explain every timed-mode rule or scoring consequence.\n\nTwenty-six board shapes, eight tilesets, twelve backgrounds, dark-mode compatibility, and a daily puzzle are advertised. Crowns and leveling unlock content, giving personalization a progression role rather than guaranteeing all designs are available immediately.\n\nThe Color Tileset is specifically named as an additional visual approach. Its accessibility or suitability for particular color-vision needs is not established by that name.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, purchases, paid removal, and offline support remain unresolved. No customer review bodies are available in this sample to support claims about animation quality, fair hints, or technical reliability.\n\nConsider Brainium Mahjong for its explicitly documented solitaire rules and retracing tools, not a manufactured consensus based on the studio’s reputation.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBrainium Mahjong has a clear rules foundation and an unusually specific undo interface. Its stated solvability and optional timer are useful distinctions, while access terms and real-world usability remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### When is a tile playable?\n\nIt must be uncovered above and open on at least its left or right side.\n\n### Must flower tiles look identical?\n\nNo. The developer says any flower tile can pair with another flower; the same category exception applies to seasons.\n\n### Does every legal match preserve a solution?\n\nThat does not follow from the developer’s claim that each generated starting board is winnable.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-tile-matching-puzzle/id1454698138?uo=4)."
