    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bus Fever - Car Parking Jam: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-06-03–2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.18.11–1.19.10. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nBus Fever - Car Parking Jam combines colored passenger loading with vehicle release. Its most important listing caveat is internal: the text promotes beating a deadline and separately promises no time limit.\n\n## The vehicle premise is better supported than the filler\n\nThe play guide says to match passengers and vehicles by color and remove all vehicles. The official image shows colored buses packed into a circular formation below a waiting area and road.\n\nOther parts of the description discuss fruit tiles and connecting cars to buses. Those inconsistencies should not become a fabricated hybrid mechanic. The pictured vehicle-and-passenger task is the stronger foundation for choosing the game.\n\nOne reviewer values focusing and strategy; another likes retrying after mistakes. A separate account describes predicting the ending as difficult unless they count passengers. These support a sequencing attraction, not a realistic transport simulation.\n\n## Do not label the entire app untimed\n\nBecause the written sources contradict themselves about a clock, neither a universal countdown nor a universal no-timer finding is established. Mode-specific timing has not been tested.\n\nBooster use and offline play are advertised. A June reviewer also reports offline operation, but that does not verify every event, purchase, or connected feature without internet.\n\n## Ads can obstruct the selected move\n\nA detailed player says touching a car causes an ad to expand over the screen. Others describe long interruptions or frozen ads requiring the app to close. Those reports affect planning and input, not just time between puzzles.\n\nA purchaser says no-ads payment did not remove ads. Another player believes accumulated points help avoid them. The accounts do not establish current entitlement terms or a universal removal price.\n\nOne review alleges sophisticated security attacks and offers network-setting advice without independently verified evidence. Those claims are not treated as findings or repeated as instructions.\n\n## Challenge and access are separate judgments\n\nA player reports an early ending despite an open spot; another describes limited levels. Neither account proves all boards are impossible or establishes the present campaign size across versions.\n\nThe US download is free. Current checkout terms, stability, and ad-closing behavior remain untested. Consider Bus Fever if coordinating colors and release order is appealing, while keeping its contradictory timing pitch and commercial-control reports in view.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBus Fever has support for strategic passenger loading, but the listing’s mixed instructions limit precise promises. Ad expansion and removal complaints are stronger cautions than its broadly relaxed marketing suggests.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is Bus Fever definitely untimed?\n\nNo. The supplied description both promotes racing a deadline and lists no time limit; the contradiction is unresolved.\n\n### Is offline play reported?\n\nYes. The developer advertises it and one reviewer reports it, without complete feature testing.\n\n### Does the fruit-tile text define another confirmed mode?\n\nNo. That wording conflicts with the stronger vehicle evidence and is not enough to establish a separate mode.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bus-fever-car-parking-jam/id6670540076?uo=4)."
