    "bodyMarkdown": "# Number Match: 2048 Puzzle: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-06-30–2026-11-04 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 572–601. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nNumber Match: 2048 Puzzle is about linking matching numbered blocks into larger values. It is not the equal-or-ten pair-clearing activity offered by other similarly named number games.\n\n## Follow a number chain\n\nThe listing describes connecting same-number blocks, starting from two and working toward checkpoints. The official image shows several 4 tiles joined by a traced path across a numbered board.\n\nThat supports individual chain selection rather than a verified rule where every tile shifts with one directional swipe. Exact adjacency, chain thresholds, and new-tile behavior are not fully explained.\n\nOne reviewer enjoys learning how to maximize points per move. Another likes the absence of time pressure while still finding the task mentally engaging. These are specific attractions of the scoring loop, not measured improvement in mathematics.\n\n## The board can be interrupted during a gesture\n\nSeveral reviewers describe ad breaks within play. A particularly useful September account says a commercial interrupts a number-merging sequence and causes unintended plays. That is a direct input concern, not merely a preference for fewer between-round ads.\n\nOther players report being sent to a shopping page without intending to leave the game. Claims about malware or platform-policy violations are not independently established by those accounts.\n\nThe reported intervals differ substantially. They should not become a single measured schedule, even when many detailed reviews find the interruption burden unacceptable.\n\n## Auto-save is promised but challenged\n\nThe developer advertises automatic saving and offline play. Reviewers nevertheless describe crashes, a blank board, lost scores, and a high-value tile reverting after an ad.\n\nOne support account says the team initially investigated but did not provide a subsequent resolution. A statement that support is looking into an issue is not a confirmed fix. None of the device failures were independently reproduced.\n\nThese reports matter in a high-score game because a useful run is cumulative. Returning to an earlier state changes more than the convenience of reopening a menu.\n\n## Leaderboard doubts are not a technical audit\n\nA detailed reviewer distrusts a repeated comparison message and dislikes reward pop-ups. The sample does not verify the ranking system’s implementation or prove opponents are fabricated.\n\nThe US download is free. Current purchase offers, removal terms, and complete offline coverage remain unresolved. Consider Number Match for chain-based number growth, while treating reliable resumption and unobstructed input as questions separate from enjoying the mechanic.\n\n## Verdict\n\nNumber Match has a concrete score-building attraction, but continuity is central to its usefulness. Mid-gesture ad and save-loss reports are the strongest cautions, without turning speculative security or ranking claims into established facts.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it the same as clearing pairs totaling ten?\n\nNo. This listing describes connecting matching values to merge toward larger numbers.\n\n### Is it untimed?\n\nNo time limits are advertised, and a reviewer praises that aspect; actual mode behavior was not tested.\n\n### Does auto-save guarantee a preserved run?\n\nNo. It is advertised, but reviewers report resets and lost progress that were not independently reproduced.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/number-match-2048-puzzle/id6446084109?uo=4)."
