    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bead Sort: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2022-04-05–2026-09-14 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.32 and 1.34. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nBead Sort makes organization the activity: sort small colored pieces into their matching bins. It is closer to tidying a container than solving a formal tube-transfer puzzle.\n\n## A tray of colors supplies the goal\n\nThe developer gives only two short instructions, emphasizing matching bins and an easy, relaxing activity. The official image shows a circular divided container, mixed blue and yellow pieces, a central collector-like tool, and percentage displays.\n\nThat presentation supports color separation without establishing every gesture, collection radius, or completion threshold. It would be misleading to borrow legal-move restrictions from a ball-sort app simply because both involve small colored objects.\n\nOne invested reviewer likes changing what is sorted, naming food, fruit, presents, and jewels. Another enjoys levels with many tiny pieces and wishes they occurred more often. Those are concrete attractions beyond a generic statement that sorting is relaxing.\n\n## Sensory enjoyment does not require deep difficulty\n\nA historical supporter likes the sounds and vibration despite minor glitches. Another finds the activity repetitive yet still enjoyable and describes it as impossible to lose. That remains their experience, not a verified universal failure model.\n\nA different long-term player exhausts the object themes and wants more variety. The appeal may depend on liking the organizing action itself rather than expecting a continually evolving strategic system.\n\nThe sample stretches from April 2022 through September 2026. Its older enthusiasm and complaints should not be relabeled as a unified recent consensus.\n\n## Loading failures can outweigh a simple premise\n\nSeveral 2025 accounts describe crashes at launch. One reviewer initially likes the alternate objects, then updates their review to say the app now crashes every time. The latest included 2026 entry also reports being unable to open it.\n\nThese are unverified technical accounts, not a tested finding on every device. They nevertheless make reliable entry a substantive selection question rather than a minor convenience.\n\n## Payment does not settle the interruption evidence\n\nMultiple purchasers say ads continued or returned after a no-ads purchase. Some also report progress resets. A more tolerant historical reviewer finds ads manageable, which should remain visible rather than becoming an invented unanimous rejection.\n\nThe US download is free. Current prices, removal scope, offline access, and restoration behavior remain unresolved. Consider Bead Sort for its small-object organizing appeal, not an assumed low-risk purchase or a clinically established calming effect.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBead Sort has specific supporters of alternate objects and satisfying organization. Its historical launch and paid-ad reports are significant limits, especially for readers who value a dependable low-demand routine.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does it sort only round beads?\n\nOne reviewer describes food, fruit, presents, and jewels as alternate sorting appearances; the full current collection was not inspected.\n\n### Is it confirmed impossible to lose?\n\nOne historical reviewer describes it that way, but the sparse listing does not establish a universal failure rule.\n\n### Do no-ads purchases reliably work?\n\nSeveral purchasers report continued or returning ads. Current entitlement behavior has not been verified.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bead-sort/id1500802433?uo=4)."
