    "bodyMarkdown": "# Easy Words - Word Puzzle Games: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nEasy Words - Word Puzzle Games offers a turn-based letter-board contest. Build connected words for points, using each letter’s value and the board’s bonus cells to improve the result.\n\n## The board and rack are specifically defined\n\nThe developer specifies a 13×13 board and seven letter tiles per player. Words run horizontally or vertically. The opening word must contain at least two letters and place a tile on the center square.\n\nThe official image shows intersecting words and cells labeled with letter or word multipliers. It supports strategic placement rather than searching a completed letter matrix.\n\nA word’s usefulness therefore depends on more than its length: where its letters land can change the points earned. The supplied description does not establish every crossing-validation or tile-replacement rule, so this is not a complete competitive rulebook.\n\n## Forty-four bonus cells change the target\n\nBonus spaces can multiply an individual letter or a whole word. The text does not specify whether a bonus can be reused, how multiple bonuses combine, or whether using all seven tiles produces a separate reward.\n\nThose familiar word-board questions should not be answered from another game’s conventions. The exact dictionary and challenge system also remain undocumented.\n\nThe opponent’s identity is not established: the listing describes competition but does not independently prove live human matchmaking or a particular computer difficulty setting.\n\n## Help can change the rack, not just reveal a word\n\nA built-in dictionary supplies definitions for words added to the board. This is a more concrete learning-related feature than a general claim to train the brain, although definition quality and coverage were not tested.\n\nHints offer assistance, Swap replaces rack tiles, and Shuffle changes their presentation. The description does not establish their costs, whether they consume a turn, or how frequently they can be used.\n\n## Ending rules deserve attention\n\nThe listed game ends when a player uses the last tile, both skip twice consecutively, or a player has no possible moves. The higher score wins. Those conditions are the developer’s stated rules, not a reason to assume an unlimited back-and-forth session.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, purchases, paid removal, offline access, and timing remain unresolved. No customer review bodies are available in this sample.\n\nConsider Easy Words if rack management and multiplier placement are the task you want. It is less relevant to someone seeking a solitary clue crossword or a confirmed ad-free vocabulary lesson.\n\n## Verdict\n\nEasy Words supplies unusually concrete board dimensions and scoring foundations. Its dictionary is a useful stated tool, while opponent identity and assistance costs remain important unanswered selection points.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many letters does each player receive?\n\nThe developer specifies seven tiles on a 13×13 board.\n\n### Can words run diagonally?\n\nThe supplied rules describe horizontal and vertical words, not diagonal placement.\n\n### Does it provide definitions?\n\nYes. A built-in dictionary is advertised for words added to the board; quality was not independently assessed.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/easy-words-word-puzzle-games/id6612024746?uo=4)."
