    "bodyMarkdown": "# Letter Soup: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nLetter Soup makes a bowl of letters its word-building space. Swipe the supplied characters into answers, finish the required words, and move from one café to another.\n\n## The food metaphor does not change the task\n\nThe written description is very short, but its central action is clear. You form words from letters in soup rather than locate already printed words in a large matrix.\n\nThe official image shows W, R, E, and O inside a bowl, a finger tracing between letters, and several rows of answer spaces above. WORD appears as an example in the promotional layout.\n\nThat image establishes the pool-and-slots presentation, not proof that every pictured example can be formed from the currently visible letters. Promotional layouts should not be treated as a complete playable board.\n\n## Cafés provide a progression frame\n\nMoving between cafés is explicitly described. It gives repeated word finding a destination, but does not establish restaurant management, recipe creation, or decoration choices.\n\nThe screenshot advertises more than 1,000 word puzzles. That is an attributed image claim, not an independently counted catalog or a guarantee of unique vocabulary across every stage.\n\nThe text’s promise to keep readers occupied for months is not a measured duration. Word knowledge, use of help, and play habits can all differ, and no hands-on completion estimate is offered.\n\n## Word rules remain important unknowns\n\nThe supplied material does not define accepted dictionaries, repeated-letter handling, bonus-word credit, shuffle, or hint availability. Those can strongly affect a letter-connecting game and should not be borrowed from another café-themed title.\n\nIt also does not establish a daily-only schedule, unlimited board access, time limits, or penalties for unsuccessful submissions. A small bowl of letters is not proof of a short or penalty-free session.\n\nReaders seeking clue definitions should note that none are documented. The core presented activity is recognition of possible words, not a verified teaching program.\n\nThe US download is free. Ads, purchases, removal offers, offline operation, and progress transfer are unresolved. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support claims about fair word acceptance or dependable saving.\n\nConsider Letter Soup if its compact letter pool and café progression are enough of a reason to investigate. It is less appropriate as a recommendation for a particular dictionary, unlimited hints, or an independently verified no-pressure mode.\n\n## Verdict\n\nLetter Soup communicates its basic word-building metaphor clearly despite a sparse description. The café journey is the thematic hook; assistance, vocabulary rules, and commercial access require separate confirmation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it a word-search grid?\n\nThe description and image instead show swiping from a small bowl of supplied letters.\n\n### Can you run or decorate cafés?\n\nThe text says to move between cafés but does not establish management or decoration mechanics.\n\n### Is a puzzle count advertised?\n\nThe official image says over 1,000 puzzles; that count was not independently audited.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/letter-soup/id1084252650?uo=4)."
