    "bodyMarkdown": "# Jade Mahjong: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-10-13–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.4.4–1.4.6. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nJade Mahjong offers a layered pair-removal board with a daily routine and senior-oriented presentation. Its explicit no-ads listing claim is not consistently supported by the review sample.\n\n## Match pairs and reveal the next layer\n\nThe play guide says to tap two identical tiles and reveal more as the board clears. The official image shows a traditional-looking layered arrangement with shuffle and hint icons beneath.\n\nThe supplied text does not fully define covered-tile or side-access restrictions, so those should not be invented from another mahjong app. The image supports solitaire-style pairing rather than traditional multiplayer hand building.\n\nDaily boards, large tiles, gentle colors, and offline play are advertised. These are product claims rather than independent accessibility or network tests.\n\n## The supporting details are more useful than stars\n\nOne older player specifically enjoys spoken encouragement and a waterfall background. Another likes mixed symbols and bonuses found under pairs. A separate account appreciates the Chinese-year tile designs and finds the game challenging.\n\nThese comments identify reasons to enjoy the presentation. Health and memory testimonials are not evidence that the app treats dementia or produces measured cognitive benefits.\n\nMany positive entries simply say fun or great. They do not independently establish unlimited hints, no technical problems, or an unrestricted free catalog.\n\n## Advertised pockets are not a verified rule\n\nA critical reviewer expected pockets for storing tiles from a promotion and says actual play does not include them. The inspected official board does not demonstrate such a storage system either.\n\nThat mismatch is a reason not to recommend the app for a holding-tray mechanic. Another player wants to finish the entire board themselves, but their brief complaint does not establish precisely what automation or ending behavior occurred.\n\n## No ads remains a conflict, not a filter fact\n\nThe listing expressly promises no ads, and October reviewers report no commercials. Later entries describe ads after games, long chains of close screens, or an ad that prevents returning to the board.\n\nVersion or date differences may be relevant, but the evidence does not establish the cause. It would be misleading either to erase the positive accounts or to repeat the no-ads claim as a verified current guarantee.\n\nThe US download is free. Purchase and removal terms, current offline coverage, and stability remain untested. Consider Jade for its pair matching and encouraging presentation, while treating interruption-free play as unresolved.\n\n## Verdict\n\nJade Mahjong has specific supporters of its tile designs and encouragement. Its strongest caveat is the direct advertising conflict, with promotional tile-storage expectations kept separate from the documented board.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does the listing promise no ads?\n\nYes, but the review sample contains both no-ad accounts and reports of substantial interruptions.\n\n### Are storage pockets a confirmed feature?\n\nNo. One reviewer expected them from a promotion and says ordinary play differs.\n\n### Is senior-friendly design independently verified?\n\nNo. Large tiles and gentle colors are advertised, but accessibility was not tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/jade-mahjong/id6752887466?uo=4)."
