    "bodyMarkdown": "# Parking Order!: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2024-01-19–2026-07-24 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 0.9.4–1.15.11. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nParking Order! asks you to choose when a car follows its route into a designated space. The decision is sequence, with other cars, pedestrians, lights, and barriers complicating the timing.\n\n## Read the routes before starting movement\n\nThe developer says to select cars by tapping and park each in its assigned slot. Button-controlled obstacles and long-trailing trucks are also advertised.\n\nThe official image shows colored cars attached to crossing angular routes. It supports predetermined-path coordination rather than a free-steering driving simulator.\n\nOne reviewer enjoys the logic involved, while another likes being able to retry. A more specific complaint says overlapping routes make it hard to identify which belongs to each vehicle. That directly affects planning before any car moves.\n\n## Input delay changes the puzzle\n\nSeveral historical accounts describe cars that fail to respond or become stuck after an earlier move. Another says repeated taps allow pedestrians or lights to change before the vehicle finally reacts.\n\nThese are unverified input and continuity reports, not proof that every collision is caused by a defect. A March reviewer also says the skip button can cover a car, identifying a separate layout concern.\n\nThe sample spans January 2024 through July 2026. A February 2024 reviewer explicitly thanks the developers for fixing a glitch, while later accounts still describe stuck cars. The older repair cannot establish a universal current fix, but should not be erased.\n\n## Daily calendars have their own failure reports\n\nMany 2024 and 2025 entries describe dates already marked complete, locked challenges, or an ad that plays without reopening the requested board. These concern the daily access system rather than ordinary parking rules.\n\nA reported future date being marked complete is not proof of a deliberate scheme. No current calendar or account was independently inspected.\n\n## Paid removal may require repeated restoration\n\nOne purchaser says restoring the purchase removes bottom ads but must be repeated on every launch. Others describe continued advertising or mid-level interruptions. Those are different entitlement experiences, not a verified universal failure.\n\nThe US download is free. Current prices, offer coverage, offline access, and stability remain unresolved. The listing expressly includes time challenges, so the whole app should not be labeled untimed.\n\nConsider Parking Order if coordinating visible routes appeals. Readers who need precise touch response and a dependable daily calendar have specific historical reasons to check those before spending.\n\n## Verdict\n\nParking Order has a clear sequencing premise and logic fans. Its broad historical sample makes route legibility, responsive cars, and daily-board access more meaningful cautions than a generic statement about difficulty.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you freely steer the cars?\n\nThe listing describes tapping selected cars toward designated slots, and the image shows fixed-looking routes rather than free steering.\n\n### Is it universally untimed?\n\nNo. Time challenge levels are explicitly advertised.\n\n### Did anyone report a fix?\n\nA February 2024 reviewer thanks the developers for a glitch repair, while later accounts describe other continuing problems.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/parking-order/id1667253521?uo=4)."
