    "bodyMarkdown": "# Marble Sort: Ball Color Puzzle: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nMarble Sort: Ball Color Puzzle is not a static arrangement of ball-filled tubes. Its defining constraint is a moving conveyor that carries released marbles toward color-matched trays.\n\n## Release now or preserve belt space\n\nThe description says to tap upper boxes, release marbles, and let them travel on a circular conveyor. The belt keeps moving, so choosing the correct color is only part of the decision.\n\nA release also commits more objects to a shared transport space. The stated danger is clogging that belt before the sorting work is complete, making timing and capacity relevant alongside matching.\n\nThe official image shows colored supply boxes above a loop, balls approaching the belt, and trays below. It supports that top-to-bottom transport concept without demonstrating the precise capacity of any tray.\n\n## This is not ordinary container pouring\n\nNothing in the supplied description establishes moving a complete stack from one tube into another. Advice based on empty-tube conservation would therefore miss the documented mechanism.\n\nThe developer instead emphasizes timed drops and continuous motion. Waiting for an appropriate opening may matter, although no hands-on observation establishes a particular optimal release rhythm.\n\nTray ordering, ball batches, overflow recovery, and mistakes are not fully explained. A reader should not assume that every visible color can be released safely at once or that an occupied belt automatically clears itself.\n\n## Physics and ASMR are presentation claims\n\nBouncing, clinking, and marbles settling into place are advertised attractions. The image cannot verify sound quality, haptic feedback, or physical realism.\n\nLikewise, the one-tap description establishes a simple stated input, not proven one-handed accessibility or absence of accidental releases. Control reach and responsiveness were not tested.\n\nHundreds of levels and advanced mechanics are advertised without an itemized catalog. The material does not identify each later obstacle or prove an endless mode despite its broad strategy language.\n\n## An offline promise with unresolved access\n\nThe listing explicitly promotes play without WiFi. That is useful source evidence for an offline proposition, but no disconnected session or content-download boundary was tested.\n\nThe US download is free. Ad frequency, purchases, removal offers, and retry costs remain unknown. No customer review bodies are available to support claims that belt congestion is forgiving or that the progression is consistently fair.\n\nConsider Marble Sort for a color-sorting problem with active transport and release timing. It is less suitable as a confirmed no-pressure recommendation simply because other sorting games let pieces wait indefinitely.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMarble Sort has a concrete distinguishing idea: managing color flow on a belt that does not stop. The published premise is more dynamic than ordinary tube sorting, while exact congestion and recovery rules still need confirmation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does the conveyor stop while you plan?\n\nThe listing explicitly says the belt never stops moving.\n\n### What causes trouble?\n\nThe stated risk is clogging the belt while trying to send marbles to matching trays.\n\n### Is offline play advertised?\n\nYes. The developer says it works without WiFi; that behavior was not independently tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/marble-sort-ball-color-puzzle/id6754558455?uo=4)."
