    "bodyMarkdown": "# Unblock Me: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nUnblock Me keeps the objective compact: move obstructing wooden pieces until the red block can leave. The attraction is creating space in a crowded board, not placing fresh shapes into an empty grid.\n\n## Clearance is the problem\n\nThe developer explicitly describes sliding the red block out. Moving another piece is useful when it opens a needed lane or creates room for a later rearrangement.\n\nThat makes the stated task different from a falling-block game or line-clearing puzzle. The supplied text does not define every piece’s allowed direction, exact board dimensions, or the exit’s location in all puzzles.\n\nThe first official image shows a winter-themed title menu with Play, Puzzles, Settings, and Store buttons. It establishes a visible entry interface, not the full movement rules of a particular wooden board.\n\n## Choose a mode, not an invented difficulty rule\n\nRelax and Challenge modes are named in the listing. Their scoring differences and fail conditions are not explained in enough detail to promise that one is unlimited or the other imposes a deadline.\n\nMore than 18,000 puzzles are advertised. That is a substantial catalog claim, but it was not independently counted and should not become a completion-time estimate.\n\nThe description also names tutorials and daily rewards that include free hints. It does not establish unlimited help, the timing of every reward, or how much a particular solution costs.\n\n## Theme variety does not change the exit goal\n\nSeasonal and festive themes are advertised, matching the snowy first image. These may change the presentation without proving a separate mechanical mode for every theme.\n\nA reader mainly interested in a visual change should keep it separate from a claim about new board logic. The listing does not itemize all available themes or their unlock conditions.\n\nHistorical download milestones and quoted endorsements are part of the developer’s promotional text. They do not establish the app’s current chart position, a present-day player consensus, or a fresh independent review.\n\n## Offline and progress transfer are attributed promises\n\nThe developer says both standard and Premium editions can be played online and offline, with progress syncing after reconnection. This analysis concerns the supplied standard app, not a verified comparison of both editions.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, current purchases, cloud prerequisites, and restoration reliability were not tested, and there are no customer review bodies in this sample.\n\nConsider Unblock Me for a concentrated spatial-clearance premise and its advertised breadth. Readers needing exact scoring, unrestricted hints, or independently verified syncing should treat those as separate selection questions.\n\n## Verdict\n\nUnblock Me offers a well-defined exit goal with an extensive advertised library. Its listing supports offline use and multiple modes, but does not settle the practical differences between Relax, Challenge, and paid access.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is the goal to clear full lines?\n\nNo. The stated goal is to slide the red block out by moving obstructing pieces.\n\n### How many puzzles are advertised?\n\nThe listing says more than 18,000; this was not independently audited.\n\n### Will progress sync after offline play?\n\nThe developer says it will after reconnection, but account requirements and reliability were not tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/unblock-me/id315019111?uo=4)."
