    "bodyMarkdown": "# Solitaire Classic Puzzle Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nSolitaire Classic Puzzle Game targets the familiar card-table experience rather than a town-building campaign. Draw modes, scoring choices, daily challenges, and presentation settings are its concrete advertised options.\n\n## Familiar cards with specific choices\n\nThe listing explicitly names draw one and draw three, alongside Standard and Vegas scoring. The official image shows a green table, four empty ace foundations, a stock, and columns of face-up and face-down cards.\n\nThat supports a Klondike-style presentation. The text also compares it with spider and pyramid solitaire, but those references do not establish separate playable Spider or Pyramid modes.\n\nThe full rulebook is not supplied. Redeal limits, undo terms, solvable-deal selection, and scoring arithmetic should not be inferred from the names of those options.\n\n## Customization is more than a background claim\n\nThe developer advertises table and card styles, multiple themes, and left-handed support. Those are useful selection details for someone seeking a familiar layout rather than new mechanics.\n\nNo independent accessibility or reach test establishes how thoroughly the layout mirrors, how large the cards become, or whether all themes preserve contrast.\n\nThe inspected image includes moves, time, score, and bonus displays. A visible clock does not establish a countdown failure condition; it may be a performance indicator, but that distinction is not fully documented.\n\n## Daily records and ordinary deals coexist\n\nA hard daily challenge, daily marks, and a trophy display are advertised alongside random deals. That supplies a possible progression routine without turning the whole app into a mandatory daily-only game.\n\nThe listing says 9,000+ solitaire games are included. It does not clarify whether that means prepared deals, challenges, or another catalog measure, so the figure should not be rewritten as thousands of distinct rule variants.\n\nA statistics tracker and resuming after backgrounding are also described. There are no customer review bodies here to verify long-term records or recovery after an interrupted game.\n\n## No annoying ads is not a measured guarantee\n\nBoth the text and inspected image promote an experience without annoying ads. That wording does not establish every placement, reward offer, or removal condition; no ad session was conducted.\n\nOffline random deals are explicitly advertised. Installation, purchase restoration, daily content delivery, and current stability remain untested. The US download is free, with exact purchase terms unresolved.\n\nConsider this app for the documented draw and scoring choices, especially if left-handed layout support matters. It is less appropriate as a confirmed ad-free recommendation or a collection of every solitaire variant named in the promotional introduction.\n\n## Verdict\n\nSolitaire Classic Puzzle Game has a useful traditional feature list. Draw modes and left-handed support are the firm selection points; its broad catalog and advertising language deserve narrower interpretation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are draw one and draw three offered?\n\nYes. Both are explicitly named in the listing.\n\n### Does it include Spider and Pyramid modes?\n\nThe supplied text compares the game with them but does not establish separate playable modes.\n\n### Are random deals available offline?\n\nThe listing says so; disconnected play and access boundaries were not independently tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/solitaire-classic-puzzle-game/id1471592644?uo=4)."
