    "bodyMarkdown": "# Hexout: Follow the Arrows, but Keep the Timing Claims Separate\n\nHexout makes the direction printed on a tile part of the puzzle. Its sample suggests an approachable rhythm, but the listing's step and time restrictions deserve more attention than the broad relaxation pitch. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Clear the route before taking it\n\nThe developer describes tapping hexagons to move them in their arrow directions and clear the board. Color organization, limited steps, and obstacles such as frozen tiles, switches, and suspended areas complicate that process.\n\nThe next available move is not necessarily the most useful one. Opening a route for another tile can matter more than clearing an isolated piece. Hammers, bombs, and extra moves are advertised as recovery tools, although their complete costs are not documented.\n\nDaily challenges and collection rankings supply reasons to return beyond finishing an individual arrangement. They also introduce limited-time rewards, which are separate from a countdown within a board.\n\n## An easy rhythm, with qualifications\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-08-11 through 2026-10-16 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 0.0.55, 0.0.56, 0.0.57, 0.0.58, 0.0.59, 0.0.60, 0.0.61, 0.0.62, 0.0.63, 0.0.64; the listing records version 0.0.66. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSeveral reviewers enjoy the thought required and describe the game as relaxing once they understand its rhythm. Some find it fairly easy; another specifically appreciates obstacles becoming more challenging.\n\nTwo comments mention no timer, while the listing explicitly discusses levels with limited steps or time. Those accounts cannot establish that every mode is untimed. Someone choosing primarily to avoid a clock should inspect the relevant instructions rather than rely on either statement in isolation.\n\nOther players report slow response, menu overlays, or tools working inconsistently. These are individual experiences, not a measured current failure rate.\n\n## The interruption can arrive before the board ends\n\nA recurring complaint concerns ads in the middle of play. Another reviewer finds the between-game ads brief and tolerable. The difference should remain visible rather than become one universal duration.\n\nA purchaser reports paying on an iPhone but continuing to see ads on an iPad. That raises a restoration question without establishing the current purchase entitlement or proving restoration fails for everyone. Current removal prices remain unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nHexout is most relevant to players who like tracing directional dependencies across a compact arrangement. Its approachable reputation in the sample is encouraging, but it is not a verified no-timer or uninterrupted-play recommendation. Check the active constraints and purchase terms before committing to a longer routine.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What controls a tile's movement?\nIts arrow direction, according to the listing.\n\n### Is every level untimed?\nThat is not established; the listing and some player accounts differ.\n\n### What assistance is advertised?\nHammers, bombs, and extra moves.\n\n### Is cross-device removal verified?\nNo. One purchaser reports an unsuccessful transfer of the ad-free experience.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/hexout-color-hexa-puzzle/id6753866113?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
