    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Tour: Connecting Letters With a Destination in Mind\n\nWord Tour makes a travel itinerary out of finding words. The concrete proposition is connecting letters, with destinations and music intended to make the repetition feel like a journey rather than an isolated exercise. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Connect letters, then move forward\n\nThe developer describes swiping to connect letters and form answers across more than 5,000 handcrafted levels. Increasingly demanding arrangements are presented as the main source of challenge.\n\nThat premise involves generating a possible word and finding a valid way to connect its letters. The supplied text does not fully specify the arrangement, whether letters may be reused, or how bonus answers are handled. It should not be converted into the exact rules of another travel-themed word app.\n\nThe listing mentions crosswords and word search as comparisons. Those labels do not independently establish separate complete modes with conventional rules.\n\n## A journey with two different clocks\n\nNo time limits, no pressure, and unlimited attempts are expressly advertised for ordinary play. Daily challenges, seasonal events, and friendly competitions are also described.\n\nThose systems need not conflict, but they are different commitments. Solving at your own pace is not the same as having unlimited time to claim an event reward. The supplied evidence does not document every event's access conditions.\n\nLocations range from beaches to mountains and landmarks, according to the developer. No screenshot was inspected, so the actual imagery, text size, and map design are not independently assessed.\n\n## Optional help has unspecified prices\n\nHints and extra features are available through optional purchases. The developer says spending is not mandatory and offline play is possible. These remain attributed claims, not a complete economy analysis or disconnected test.\n\nA player who dislikes a time limit may find the stated pacing attractive. Someone who requires a particular dictionary, regional spelling policy, or accessibility setting has less information to compare. The listing's vocabulary and spelling benefits are plausible intentions, not measured educational outcomes.\n\nNo usable customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about answer quality, repetition, advertising, or later difficulty. A free download also does not resolve whether or how often advertisements appear.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWord Tour is a plausible option for readers who want letter-connecting puzzles with a relaxed travel theme. It is less conclusive for anyone choosing based on verified dictionary quality or predictable hint costs. The ordinary no-timer and unlimited-attempt claims are specific; the surrounding economy and execution still require confirmation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How are words entered?\nThe listing describes swiping to connect letters.\n\n### Are ordinary puzzles advertised as timed?\nNo. The developer explicitly says there are no time limits.\n\n### Are hints necessarily free?\nNo. Optional hint purchases are disclosed without full prices.\n\n### Is offline operation independently tested?\nNo. It is a developer claim.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/word-tour-word-puzzle/id6599847974?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
