    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong Journey: A Long Route, With a Reported Gate at the End\n\nMahjong Journey ties tile pairs to a trip with a young girl and her cat. The sample suggests that players can remain attached for years, yet still reach a point where the next gate or another interruption becomes the defining experience. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Pair tiles and pursue faster clears\n\nThe listing describes dismantling layouts by matching pairs, collecting achievements, and earning gold stars for faster completion. Shuffle changes a difficult arrangement, while Firecracker removes multiple pairs.\n\nThat creates two potential goals: finish the board and improve the result. A reader who enjoys unhurried matching may value the first more than a speed-based star award.\n\nThe supplied description does not fully document tile-access restrictions or every star threshold. Faster completion is promoted, but that is not automatically the same as a hard countdown ending every attempt.\n\n## Affection survives inside the criticism\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-04-19 through 2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.26.11300, 1.26.11400, 1.27.11500; the listing records version 1.27.11500. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers describe years of enjoyable, relaxing play. Several also report reaching locked gates after level 3,325 and waiting for additional main-route content. Those reports do not establish the exact current endpoint, but they qualify an expectation of limitless new destinations.\n\nOther accounts concern lost event progress, tools not responding, or sound settings turning back on after reopening. One player specifically describes unwanted sound through hearing aids, making persistence of preferences more than a cosmetic concern.\n\nThese are individual reports rather than independently verified faults across all devices.\n\n## Paid bonuses do not automatically remove ads\n\nThe listing names optional auto-renewing Silver and Gold plans at $19.99 and $49.99 per month respectively. Those are description-stated US amounts, not a newly inspected checkout. No ad-removal entitlement should be inferred from the existence of those plans.\n\nMany reviewers object to ads between games or banners making the board smaller. Some ask for a removal option, while others describe interruptions during events. Current frequency and removal availability remain unverified.\n\nOffline play is advertised, but no disconnected session establishes what remains available.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong Journey is most relevant to players who want pair clearing with an extended travel framework and optional score chasing. Its established appeal in the sample is tempered by reports about main-route content, persistent settings, and reduced tile space. A new player should separate the appeal of the matching from assumptions about an endless or ad-free journey.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does faster completion matter?\nThe listing connects speed with gold-star rewards.\n\n### Is the current last level confirmed?\nNo. Reviewers report a gate at 3,325, not a verified present catalog.\n\n### Are both subscriptions monthly?\nYes, according to the supplied description.\n\n### Do those plans prove ad-free access?\nNo. Their existence alone does not establish that entitlement.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-journey-tile-match/id938207958?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
