    "bodyMarkdown": "# Fruit Merge: The Fruit Stack Has Fans, the Breaks Have Fewer\n\nFruit Merge builds a clear visual goal around combining identical fruit into larger fruit. The sample repeatedly distinguishes liking that loop from being willing to tolerate the interruptions around it. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Make space for the next combination\n\nThe developer describes stacking fruit inside a box, combining identical types, and reaching stage targets before the available space fills. Progress is organized around levels as well as larger fruit and higher scores.\n\nThat makes space a resource. A useful merge can consolidate the pile, while an awkward placement can separate pieces that need to meet. The supplied description does not fully document physics, every fruit's order, or the exact failure boundary.\n\nIts challenge to complete a particular level and score is marketing language, not evidence that those numbers are the current maximum.\n\n## Enjoyment and interruption are not opposites\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-12-04 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.6.1, 3.6.2; the listing records version 3.6.2. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers call the activity cute, calming, easy, or enjoyable during a break. Many critical reviewers still say the underlying game is fun.\n\nThe dominant concern is an ad break arriving after only a few actions. Reported intervals differ substantially, so they should not become a measured universal frequency. Several accounts specifically distinguish an unwanted mid-stage break from voluntarily watching for a powerup.\n\nOthers describe freezing, crashes, or being returned to the lock screen. Those are unverified reports, not proof of malware or a measured device-wide defect. One reviewer's suspicion of a virus should not be repeated as a security finding.\n\n## Removal can leave other kinds of offers\n\nA purchaser says paid removal eliminated ads but left frequent microtransaction prompts. Another reports crashes continuing after payment. Removing an ad format is not a guarantee of stability or a quiet interface.\n\nCurrent removal prices and entitlements are not independently established. Some reviewers arrived through outside reward apps; those relationships do not make this a verified cash-earning game or establish the terms of another service.\n\nThe listing does not provide a reliable offline promise. A report about needing connectivity is not a complete disconnected-feature test either.\n\n## Verdict\n\nFruit Merge may suit players who enjoy organizing a growing pile toward a larger combined object. The sample gives stronger reason for caution about access and interruptions than for confidently recommending a long free session. Its basic appeal is recognizable, but liking the fruit loop and accepting the surrounding experience are separate decisions.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What combines?\nIdentical fruit, producing larger fruit according to the listing.\n\n### Is this only an endless high-score game?\nThe description also specifies stage targets and levels.\n\n### Does payment guarantee no internal offers?\nNo. A purchaser reports continued microtransaction prompts.\n\n### Do freeze complaints prove a virus?\nNo. They are not a security analysis.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/fruit-merge-juicy-melon-puzzle/id6470483468?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
