    "bodyMarkdown": "# Clockmaker: The Town Mystery and the Events Compete for Attention\n\nClockmaker gives jewel matching a cursed-town mystery. Its sample suggests that the appeal of the main boards can coexist with fatigue over side activities, offers, and interruptions. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Match gems to advance the rescue\n\nThe developer describes matching crystals and other gems, collecting combinations, using boosters, and working with Uncle to defeat the Clockmaker. A science-fiction and mystery framework supplies continuity between levels.\n\nWeekly events include activities such as growing plants, making cakes, and bingo. Tournaments add competitive goals. The description does not fully document every event's rules or the exact board controls.\n\nOffline puzzle play is advertised, while online tournaments explicitly require a connection. That is a useful scope distinction rather than a guarantee that every event remains available without a network.\n\n## Players disagree about the surrounding adventure\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-03-29 through 2026-11-05 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 101.2.0, 102.0.0, 102.1.0, 103.0.1, 103.1.0, 104.0.0, 104.1.0, 104.1.1, 105.0.0, 105.1.0, 105.2.0, 106.0.0, 106.1.1, 106.2; the listing records version 106.3. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts praise the visuals, entertaining challenge, boss battles, or breadth of avatar choices. One reviewer likes the gameplay while having little interest in the story.\n\nOther long-time players feel side activities interfere with reaching the main game. A detailed complaint describes being redirected away from a timed activity; another objects to numerous screens on returning after a break.\n\nSome also report reward-video options disappearing. That is an account-specific history, not proof that every player has lost the same feature or must now spend to advance.\n\n## Do not count advertising elsewhere as in-game frequency\n\nSeveral unusually strong reviews criticize advertisements for Clockmaker inside other apps. Those accounts cannot establish how often a player encounters ads within Clockmaker itself.\n\nOther reviewers do describe internal interruptions, videos that cannot be closed, or purchase offers. A player says a timed booster keeps counting down during a break, making the interruption more consequential. Another reports no intrusive commercials so far.\n\nCurrent purchase prices, removal entitlements, and a universal schedule were not verified. Nor do allegations that boards require boosters constitute proof of a forced-payment design.\n\n## Verdict\n\nClockmaker is most relevant to readers who want match-3 boards embedded in a busy mystery adventure. It is less clearly suited to someone who wants immediate access to one quiet puzzle with few surrounding systems. The sample supports the board appeal while explaining why event redirection and timed benefits can affect an otherwise enjoyable routine.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this primarily a clock-repair simulator?\nNo. The listing identifies jewel-based match-3 as its central puzzle.\n\n### Are tournaments advertised offline?\nNo. The developer explicitly requires connectivity for them.\n\n### Do all ad complaints describe this app's gameplay?\nNo. Some concern promotions appearing in other games.\n\n### Are lost reward options universal?\nThat is not established by individual player reports.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/clockmaker-match-3-puzzles/id929195587?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
