    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Hike: A Crossword Pitch With a Separate Subscription Layer\n\nWord Hike presents a crossword adventure, but its description provides more detail about membership than about the construction of individual grids. That imbalance matters when deciding what can responsibly be recommended. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## The words are clear; the entry system is less so\n\nThe developer advertises crossword puzzles for beginners and experienced solvers, increasing challenge, and regularly added content. Vocabulary and spelling are the stated learning themes.\n\nA crossword normally invites checking an answer against intersecting entries, but the supplied text does not fully document this app's clue format, grid sizes, keyboard, or any letter-selection system. It should not be rewritten into an exact walkthrough from the title alone.\n\nThe description also does not identify puzzle constructors, explain regional spelling choices, or provide a precise current catalog. Its unlimited-puzzles language is a promise of continuing entertainment rather than a verified infinite inventory.\n\n## Offline is advertised, not independently demonstrated\n\nThe listing explicitly names an offline mode. That may be relevant to a reader seeking a commuting activity, although the evidence does not establish which downloads or membership features remain available without a connection.\n\nA family-friendly pitch and invitation to compare solving speed do not prove every clue is suitable for every age or that every board has a timer. Learning benefits likewise remain intended outcomes rather than measured results.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available here. There is no supported player assessment of clue quality, repetition, difficulty, or spelling accuracy.\n\n## Free access and recurring access are different offers\n\nThe description repeatedly calls the game free to play. It separately advertises monthly and annual Brain Booster Club plans with rewards, booster discounts, daily benefits, ad-free access, and access to the developer's games.\n\nThose benefits should remain attributed. The supplied text does not provide a current price or enough detail to verify the exact games and features covered. It does clearly describe automatic renewal; membership is not a one-time purchase.\n\nThe subscription's ad-free claim also does not establish the ad frequency outside it. A free download, an advertised benefit, and a practical test of the full session are three different kinds of information.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWord Hike is a plausible option for readers interested in crossword-style progression and an advertised offline mode. It is less conclusive for someone choosing based on a documented constructor library or a precisely priced membership. The strongest editorial distinction is between the broad free-play invitation and the recurring club described alongside it.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is an offline mode mentioned?\nYes, explicitly, though its full scope is untested.\n\n### Is the club a one-time purchase?\nNo. Monthly and annual auto-renewing plans are described.\n\n### Are current prices supplied?\nNo. The description refers readers elsewhere for details.\n\n### Is exact clue quality independently assessed?\nNo. No usable review bodies or hands-on solving evidence are available.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/crossword-word-hike/id1633021483?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
