    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block Puzzle - Sudoku Style: Three Ways to Clear, One Easy Reset\n\nBlock Puzzle - Sudoku Style borrows a Sudoku board's geometry, not its number rules. Its sample shows how a straightforward placement game can be affected by something as small as the location of a reset button. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Rows, columns, and boxes all count\n\nThe developer specifies a nine-by-nine grid. Placed blocks disappear when they complete a horizontal line, vertical line, or three-by-three box. Play continues until no further piece can fit.\n\nThat gives a placement several possible purposes. Completing a box may create space differently from completing a row, and preserving room for future shapes matters alongside immediate points.\n\nCombos and streaks reward consecutive clearing. The supplied text does not establish rotation or a guaranteed favorable sequence, so difficulty should not be explained through invented assistance rules.\n\n## Simplicity earns praise; reaching the board can take effort\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2024-06-15 through 2026-08-25 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.7, 2.8, 3.02, 3.04, 3.05, 3.06, 3.07, 3.08; the listing records version 3.11. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers appreciate the no-fuss design and satisfying haptic response. Others describe a rewarding challenge that sits between too easy and too hard.\n\nA detailed account wants the block to move farther than the thumb so upper regions are easier to reach. Several players report accidentally touching New Game or Quit near the piece area and losing their run. One specifically identifies the problem as worse when playing left-handed.\n\nThese are practical interface concerns, not universal accessibility findings. Another account says operation is flawless, while complaints about difficult piece sequences do not prove deliberate manipulation.\n\n## Interruptions can affect the score attempt\n\nAd reports range from no ads in an older account to midgame interruptions in a later one. Some reviewers say leaving an ad or reopening the app resets the score. Those histories should remain tied to their dates and versions.\n\nRequests to pay for removal do not establish that a removal product currently exists. Offline advice from players also is not a verified disconnected-mode policy. No current purchase or stability test was performed.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis app is most relevant to readers who enjoy managing a placement grid with an extra box-clearing option. Its compact rules earn specific praise, while touch reach and accidental resets deserve attention for high-score sessions. The Sudoku label should help explain the board, not mislead a reader into expecting a number-solving game.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you enter Sudoku numbers?\nNo. The listing describes placing blocks.\n\n### What clears a region?\nA completed row, column, or three-by-three box.\n\n### Is rotation documented?\nNo. The supplied description does not establish it.\n\n### Is paid ad removal confirmed?\nNo. Reviewer requests are not proof of availability.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-puzzle-sudoku-style/id1566174072?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
