    "bodyMarkdown": "# Brilliant Sort: Diamond Art With a Clock and a Working Shelf\n\nBrilliant Sort describes making art through gem sorting rather than filling numbered regions. The clock and working shelf are central to that proposition, even though the listing also repeatedly calls the experience relaxing. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Reveal the picture by organizing its pieces\n\nThe developer describes sorting diamonds by color, clearing shelf space, and placing gems in the correct spots. A pixel-art image emerges as progress continues.\n\nThat creates a dual objective: move toward a finished picture while keeping the intermediate shelf usable. An item that does not help immediately may still occupy space needed for the next useful action.\n\nThe description does not fully specify shelf capacity, transfer restrictions, or how many gems an action moves. Those rules should not be borrowed from another sorting app or inferred from unseen screenshots.\n\n## Two kinds of deadline appear in the text\n\nThe listing explicitly says the picture develops as a clock counts down. Time Freeze pauses that clock, Extra Shelf provides working room, and Auto Sort places diamonds.\n\nThese tools address different constraints: time, capacity, and execution. Their existence suggests strategic support, but does not establish free availability or prove that difficult stages require purchases.\n\nThemed galleries group levels, while Big Picture combines segments into a larger artwork. Event locations introduce special energy and a deadline for completing the map. An event deadline is a separate commitment from the countdown within a puzzle.\n\n## Albums add another collection to complete\n\nSeason albums use cards obtained through packs from events, offers, or the event shop. That adds a longer collection goal beyond finishing a single image. The supplied text does not establish pack probabilities, purchase prices, or whether every album can be completed without spending.\n\nNo independent customer-review bodies are available here. Enthusiastic quotations embedded in the description remain developer-selected promotional testimonials, not a separate review sample or an editorial score.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. The phrase play anywhere does not explicitly establish offline support. Advertising frequency, removal products, stability, and the practical strength of the tactile effects also remain unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBrilliant Sort is most relevant to readers who enjoy color organization with a visible picture-completion payoff and do not mind time pressure. It is less suitable as an assured untimed coloring alternative. The shelf and helper distinctions give the design a clear identity; their balance and access economy remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this ordinary paint by numbers?\nThe listing explicitly distinguishes it from that format.\n\n### Is there a countdown?\nYes. A clock and a Time Freeze tool are described.\n\n### Do picture segments form a larger work?\nThat is the advertised Big Picture feature.\n\n### Are listing testimonials independent player evidence?\nNo. They are promotional excerpts selected for the description.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/brilliant-sort-diamond-puzzle/id6749456493?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
