    "bodyMarkdown": "# Jelly Run 2048: Split the Number to Survive the Course\n\nJelly Run 2048 uses powers of two as something to steer through a course. It is not the familiar puzzle where every swipe shifts a board full of numbered tiles. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Connection and separation are both useful\n\nThe listing describes left-right swipes, connecting cubes, splitting them in half, and avoiding obstacles on the way to a finish line. Reaching a larger value matters, but so does choosing when the current form can pass the next obstruction.\n\nThat makes the task partly about movement timing rather than only arithmetic. Splitting is not necessarily a setback if it allows progress through an obstacle before reconnecting.\n\nThe description does not fully explain every collision, gate, or number abbreviation. It should not be converted into a complete scoring formula from the 2048 name.\n\n## The sample likes the sensation of progression\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-09 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.62.0; the listing records version 1.62.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nMany reviewers call the activity satisfying or an enjoyable way to fill an idle moment. Some mention collecting skins, upgrading cubes, or building with accumulated blocks.\n\nA more detailed account wants additional construction projects because unused blocks have accumulated. Others say they can no longer upgrade or access more hard levels. Those are reported progression ceilings, not a verified current content maximum.\n\nAnother player reports trouble collecting a key in Infinity Mode. The listing does not fully document that mode, and the account is not an independently reproduced defect. A separate brief review reports lag.\n\n## A short run can make a break feel frequent\n\nMany reviewers complain about ads after runs. Others are less bothered, and one explicitly reports no ads. Those experiences should not be flattened into a single schedule.\n\nA reviewer mentions a small one-time removal price, but that is an account report rather than a verified current checkout. References to VIP skins likewise do not establish a recurring subscription or its terms.\n\nPlayer enthusiasm about learning addition is not evidence of a measured educational benefit. The supplied text also does not establish complete offline support or a universally suitable audience.\n\n## Verdict\n\nJelly Run 2048 is most relevant to readers who enjoy changing a moving character's form while pursuing larger values. It is less suitable as a substitute for deliberate, untimed grid merging. The sample supports its satisfying progression while raising concrete questions about upgrade endpoints and the time spent between runs.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does a swipe shift an entire number grid?\nThe listing instead describes steering cubes left and right along a course.\n\n### Why split a cube?\nSplitting is described as a way to negotiate obstacles before reaching the finish.\n\n### Are upgrades endless?\nThat is not established; several players report reaching a ceiling.\n\n### Is a current removal price verified?\nNo. Reviewer-mentioned amounts are not a current offer check.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/jelly-run-2048-number-games/id1575544562?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
