    "bodyMarkdown": "# Hidden City: Different Ways to Search the Same Mystery World\n\nHidden City offers more than one way to recognize an object. Its listing is unusually specific about search modes, making those differences more useful for comparison than its broad promises of a captivating mystery. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## The instruction changes what you look for\n\nWords mode uses a text list. Silhouettes asks the player to identify shapes. Night mode restricts inspection through a spotlight in a dim scene. Pairs involves identical or logically related objects.\n\nThose are meaningfully different observation tasks. A written name invites recognizing an object from language; an outline emphasizes shape; a spotlight changes how much of the scene can be inspected at once.\n\nThe supplied text does not fully document timers, mistake allowances, zoom, or the schedule on which each mode becomes available. A detailed list of modes is still not a complete rulebook.\n\n## Collections connect the searches\n\nThe City of Shadows contains character quests, magical events, anomalies, and artifact collections. Objects and clues support the investigation rather than only disappearing from a checklist.\n\nThe developer also describes difference spotting, crystal match-3, memory, and logic activities. Those can provide variety for a player who likes a continuing world, but someone seeking only one straightforward search format may value them less.\n\nThe claim that side puzzles do not take over the main experience is promotional. Without player evidence or hands-on sessions, the actual balance between searching and surrounding activities is not assessed.\n\n## Offline and synchronization are distinct promises\n\nOffline investigation and switching devices through saved progress are both advertised. Neither claim independently establishes every event's availability or a tested restoration procedure.\n\nSomeone planning to alternate between devices should check the account and sync requirements rather than assume an offline save automatically appears everywhere. The supplied description does not explain the full process.\n\nNo usable customer-review bodies are available here, despite the app's recognizable name. There is no supported player consensus about readability, scene repetition, difficulty, or support.\n\nOptional bonus purchases are explicitly disclosed, and the metadata records a free download. Current prices, ad frequency, removal offers, and practical offline scope remain unverified. No screenshot was inspected, so the described locations remain listing examples rather than observed scenes.\n\n## Verdict\n\nHidden City looks most relevant to readers who want a continuing detective setting with varied observation tasks. It is less conclusive for someone requiring verified timers or reliable cross-device recovery. The search-mode distinctions are concrete; the pace, economy, and visual execution remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What does Words mode use?\nA text list of hidden objects.\n\n### What changes in Night mode?\nThe listing describes a spotlight within dim scenes.\n\n### Are there non-search puzzles?\nYes. Difference spotting, match-3, memory, and logic activities are advertised.\n\n### Is progress synchronization tested?\nNo. It is a developer claim with incomplete procedural detail.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/hidden-city-object-seekers/id722217471?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
