    "bodyMarkdown": "# That's My Seat: Enjoy the Deduction, Read the Character Caveats\n\nThat's My Seat turns a seating chart into a rule-based deduction. The sample supports the pleasure of finding the arrangement, while raising separate concerns about the characters and the screens around the puzzle. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Use each rule to narrow the possibilities\n\nThe developer describes placing characters in correct seats by process of elimination. Settings include restaurants, classrooms, buses, and imaginative scenes with animals, planets, or parody figures.\n\nA clue can constrain a location or a relationship between occupants. Combining restrictions can reveal a seat that no single clue identifies alone. The supplied text does not establish a complete mistake allowance or every drag-and-drop rule.\n\nMore than 1,000 puzzles and special levels, optional competition, and no time limits are advertised. The IQ-test wording is promotional, not a validated assessment.\n\n## The concept earns affection without ending debate\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-16 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.6.0, 2.7.1; the listing records version 2.7.1. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy figuring out where everyone belongs and describe a comfortable level of challenge. One notes that reading the instructions is essential, a useful qualification to the broad all-ages pitch.\n\nAnother account reports a clue rejecting an apparently valid placement. That is not an independently reproduced logic error. A different reviewer likes the game while objecting to stereotypes involving disability, body size, or homelessness.\n\nThose content reactions matter without requiring a claim that every character is offensive. A player also reports waiting months for new levels, which does not establish the current catalog's exact endpoint.\n\n## Busy presentation and advertising are separate issues\n\nSome reviewers describe few ads; others report interruptions within a single puzzle. A particularly critical account also feels overwhelmed by invitations to additional activities and flashy presentation.\n\nInternal event prompts should not automatically be counted as external videos. Current removal prices and coverage were not verified, and a reviewer mentioning a dollar amount does not establish the offer shown to everyone.\n\nThe no-time-limit claim also does not prove unlimited lives. A player requests more lives, while the full system is not documented in the description.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThat's My Seat is most relevant to readers who enjoy interpreting written constraints and gradually narrowing a seating arrangement. It is a more cautious choice for someone sensitive to stereotyped character jokes or a busy interface. The sample supports the deduction itself while keeping those practical and content reservations visible.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this only color sorting?\nNo. Written rules and seating relationships drive the stated puzzle.\n\n### Is a timer advertised?\nThe listing explicitly says there are no time limits.\n\n### Does no timer mean unlimited lives?\nNo. Those are separate systems, and the full lives policy is not documented.\n\n### Are character concerns independently audited?\nNo. They are specific player interpretations, not a complete content assessment.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/thats-my-seat-logic-puzzle/id6532601126?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
