    "bodyMarkdown": "# Merge Gardens: Two Puzzle Loops Feed One Inherited Estate\n\nMerge Gardens offers two related puzzle activities rather than one renamed matching rule. Merging evolves objects, while separate match-3 boards earn rewards for Daisy's inherited estate. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Three of a kind becomes something better\n\nThe developer specifies merging three identical items into a superior form. Items and creatures populate the garden, where clearing Evergrowth reveals additional areas and secrets.\n\nThat gives objects both immediate and future value. A group can supply an upgrade now, while the next evolution may require collecting more of the same chain. The supplied text does not document every item chain, placement limit, or larger-group bonus.\n\nIt would therefore be misleading to promise a five-item efficiency rule or a specific board capacity based on other merge games.\n\n## Match-3 provides a second source of rewards\n\nThe listing separately advertises hundreds of match-3 levels, combinations of blocks, and rewards used in the garden. This is distinct from evolving three identical garden items.\n\nSomeone who enjoys switching between organization and discrete boards may find that combination attractive. Someone wanting a pure merge sandbox may be less interested in completing another puzzle format to support progress.\n\nThe text does not fully explain how often one activity gates the other. No player evidence supports calling the balance either generous or restrictive.\n\n## The mystery changes the renovation's purpose\n\nDaisy initially intends to prepare her missing uncle's estate for sale. Peculiar plants, human-shaped topiary, and family history give the garden a more mysterious role.\n\nThose are narrative elements described by the developer, not independently observed scenes. No screenshot was inspected, and the writing quality or effectiveness of the story's reveals is not assessed.\n\nRare creatures and quests beyond the garden are also advertised. Their actual collecting pace and availability remain unverified; a broad catalog claim is not a tested inventory.\n\n## Practical access remains undocumented\n\nNo usable customer-review bodies are available here. There is no supported player consensus about repetition, pacing, reward value, or late progression.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. The supplied description does not establish ads, removal terms, helper prices, energy systems, offline operation, or cross-device recovery. Those systems should not be supplied simply because they are common in garden games.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMerge Gardens looks most relevant to readers who want item evolution, a second board-puzzle format, and a continuing estate mystery. It is less conclusive for someone seeking pure unrestricted merging. The two-loop design is identifiable; the pace connecting those loops and the access economy remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many identical items make a merge?\nThe listing explicitly describes three of a kind.\n\n### Is match-3 a separate activity?\nYes. Its levels earn garden rewards.\n\n### Is a five-item bonus documented?\nNo. That rule should not be borrowed from another game.\n\n### Is an energy system established?\nNo. The supplied text does not document one.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/merge-gardens/id1464675244?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
