    "bodyMarkdown": "# Ball Sort Color Water Puzzle: The Extra Tube Is an Access Question\n\nBall Sort Color Water Puzzle uses balls, despite the water reference in its title. The sample's most practical issue is access to another tube: a small amount of working space can become central to whether a player wants to continue. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Move the top ball toward a single-color bottle\n\nThe listing describes tapping a tube to move its top ball into another, with the goal of grouping matching colors. An additional tube can make a stuck arrangement easier.\n\nThat extra space can allow a temporary move which exposes a needed color below. It changes the room available for planning rather than directly solving every remaining transfer.\n\nThe supplied text does not fully document destination restrictions, capacity, or exact assistance limits. No penalties and no time limits are advertised, but that does not establish free or unlimited access to every helper.\n\n## A comfortable routine, until access changes\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2024-06-30 through 2026-10-01 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 25.0.1, 25.2.0, 25.2.1, 25.2.2, 25.2.3, 25.2.6, 25.2.7, 28.1.0; the listing records version 28.1.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nA long-time reviewer enjoys simple sorting and welcomes optional videos for extra undo or space. Other accounts describe the game as a pleasant daily activity.\n\nSeveral critics say the extra-tube button does nothing or starts an ad that crashes. Another player reports a changed mode with different stacking restrictions after reinstalling. Those are specific histories, not a verified current rule change for every installation.\n\nA reviewer claiming a level is impossible cites an AI conversation. Neither that conversation nor the review proves impossibility; no independent search of the state space was performed.\n\n## Removal does not necessarily cover reward help\n\nMultiple purchasers say paid removal still leaves videos for an extra tube. That is a distinct entitlement question from whether ordinary between-level ads disappear.\n\nAd accounts also differ: some say levels remain uninterrupted, while others describe midgame breaks or banners interfering with music. These reports span years and do not establish a universal current frequency.\n\nCurrent prices, removal coverage, and offline support remain unverified. Privacy and malware accusations in reviews should not become findings without a separate technical investigation.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBall Sort is most relevant to readers who enjoy top-layer planning and organized single-color stacks. It is a more cautious choice for someone whose enjoyment depends on unrestricted extra space or fully silent advertising. The sample supports the relaxing core while making reliable access to optional help a central qualification.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you pour water?\nThe supplied instructions describe transferring balls.\n\n### Which ball moves?\nThe one on top of a tube.\n\n### Does no time limit mean every helper is free?\nNo. Those are separate conditions.\n\n### Does an AI's answer prove a board impossible?\nNo. A quoted conversation is not a verified solvability analysis.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/ball-sort-color-water-puzzle/id1550569446?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
