    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block Blitz: Rotation and Two Different Reasons to Keep Placing\n\nBlock Blitz gives block placement two different goals: finish a prepared stage or extend a personal score attempt. Its advertised rotation button is a concrete distinction from games that require every shape to stay as supplied. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Clear a line and preserve useful space\n\nThe developer describes dragging blocks onto a board, filling lines to clear them, and rotating pieces when space becomes tight. That adds an orientation decision to the usual placement choice.\n\nRotation may make an awkward shape useful in a different region. It does not establish that every sequence is solvable or that space management no longer matters. The supplied description does not specify the board dimensions, piece-tray size, or the number and cost of rotations.\n\nThose omissions matter to comparison. A rotation button is not automatically a promise of unlimited free turns of every piece.\n\n## Journey and High Score have different objectives\n\nJourney Mode offers handcrafted stages with particular tasks. High Score Mode emphasizes clearing lines and pushing a personal record, with no timers explicitly advertised for that mode.\n\nThe scope of that statement is important: it should not become a no-timer certification for every Journey stage. The listing does not give a complete failure rule for each mode.\n\nA worldwide leaderboard supplies competition, while personal records offer a self-contained goal. Someone who prefers solving a finite arrangement may value Journey more than an open-ended score run.\n\n## A percentage is not self-explanatory evidence\n\nEach Journey level is advertised with a Chance to Solve percentage. The description does not explain whether that number comes from player completion rates, an algorithm, a test group, or another method.\n\nIt therefore should not be presented as a verified probability that a particular person will succeed. Nor does a low percentage prove that a stage is unfair or requires purchases. The statistic is a stated interface feature with an undisclosed calculation.\n\nNo independent customer-review bodies are available here. Claims about smooth controls and satisfying flow remain promotional rather than a player consensus. No screenshot was inspected to assess the statistic or rotation control directly.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Ads, removal offers, tool prices, offline operation, and stability remain unverified. Play-anywhere language does not by itself document disconnected access.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlock Blitz is most relevant to readers who want orientation choices and both staged and score-driven play. It is less conclusive for someone seeking a transparent difficulty statistic or guaranteed unrestricted rotation. The two modes are clearly distinguished; the percentage's meaning and access conditions remain open questions.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is rotation advertised?\nYes. A rotation button is explicitly named.\n\n### Which mode has no timers in the text?\nHigh Score Mode.\n\n### Is Chance to Solve a verified success probability?\nNo. Its calculation is not explained.\n\n### Are board dimensions documented?\nNot in the supplied description.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-blitz-puzzle-adventure/id6741576655?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
