    "bodyMarkdown": "# Match Villains: The Heists Have Fans, but Difficulty Divides Them\n\nMatch Villains gives match-3 a family of aristocratic thieves and layered obstacles. Its sample is notable for opposing accounts of difficulty: one player wants a tougher challenge, while another feels trapped without more moves. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Break through more than one layer\n\nThe developer describes hidden sublayers and overlayers that require matches and combinations to clear. Powerups provide another way through the obstruction.\n\nThat means a board's objective can involve uncovering or dismantling something, not simply removing the most immediately visible pieces. The supplied text does not fully document every powerup or layer interaction.\n\nThe Count, his daughter, and a butler carry the heist theme. Posters reveal story progress every 50 levels, according to the listing. That is a specific narrative cadence, not evidence of a full animated story after every stage.\n\n## Different players describe different ceilings\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-06 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.63.1, 1.64.0, 1.64.1, 1.65.0; the listing records version 1.66.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy the villains, side quests, and continuing challenge. One reports reaching more than 5,000 levels without spending and still finds parts too easy.\n\nOther accounts describe limited moves, frustrating mechanics, or a sense that coins become necessary later. Those experiences do not prove either guaranteed free completion or deliberate manipulation.\n\nSide activities also divide opinion. One reviewer likes them; another feels too many are active at once. A separate account reports the main boards crashing while side icons still work, an unverified technical history rather than a current universal fault.\n\n## Optional continuation videos are not a simple ad-free label\n\nSome reviewers report no ads. Others describe videos for extra moves, difficulty leaving them, or the loss of that continuation option after an update.\n\nThose reports concern different experiences and entitlements. Having no forced break is not necessarily the same as never being offered a reward video, while losing an optional video can itself make progression less comfortable.\n\nThe listing does not establish current removal prices or a universal assistance policy. Complaints that the advertising looks different also should not be used to invent an alternative game mode.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMatch Villains is most relevant to readers who enjoy layered match-3 objectives and a playful heist frame. It is a more qualified choice for someone expecting a predictable difficulty curve or identical continuation options across accounts. The sample's enthusiasm is substantial, but its conflicting progression and advertising experiences belong in the recommendation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What distinguishes the obstacles?\nThe listing describes both hidden sublayers and overlayers.\n\n### When are story posters advertised?\nEvery 50 levels.\n\n### Is paid progress proven mandatory?\nNo. Player accounts directly conflict.\n\n### Does every account have reward videos?\nThat is not established; several reviewers report losing the option.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/match-villains/id6479752688?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
