    "bodyMarkdown": "# Crossword Explorer: A Broad Library Pitch With Few Construction Details\n\nCrossword Explorer promises a puzzle for different moods and experience levels. Its strongest comparison points are the advertised range of sizes and difficulties, not a verified assessment of how well the clues are written. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## A collection rather than one daily grid\n\nThe developer describes thousands of crosswords ranging from easy to expert, with different themes and sizes. Daily puzzles add another entry point, while achievements track continued progress.\n\nA crossword's general appeal lies in using known answers to constrain uncertain ones through crossings. The supplied description does not fully document this app's grid conventions, exact clue format, constructor credits, or entry controls.\n\nIt should therefore not be converted into a precise instruction guide or an assurance that every puzzle follows a particular newspaper standard. Calling the collection definitive is marketing, not an editorial comparison with other libraries.\n\n## Choosing the right scale can matter\n\nDifferent sizes can support different session lengths, but the text does not provide exact dimensions or independently measured solving times. A small board is not automatically easy, just as a large board is not necessarily expert-level.\n\nThemes and backgrounds are advertised as customization. No screenshot was inspected to assess font weight, keyboard placement, contrast, or whether background changes affect readability.\n\nSomeone who enjoys choosing between a daily routine and a larger archive may find the concept relevant. Someone selecting primarily for a favorite constructor, cultural perspective, or regional spelling policy has less documented information.\n\n## Hints are promised without a complete economy\n\nThe description says hints and tips help with difficult clues. It does not establish their exact forms, prices, replenishment, or whether using them changes achievements.\n\nThe text also promotes vocabulary and knowledge benefits. Crosswords can provide exposure to unfamiliar words, but the supplied evidence does not measure learning outcomes or validate a brain-training claim.\n\nNo usable customer-review bodies are available. There is no supported player consensus about clue fairness, repeated answers, dictionary quality, or late difficulty.\n\nThe metadata records a free download, and the developer calls the experience free to play. Ad frequency, removal offers, offline support, and stability are not documented here. None can be inferred from the archive size or daily-reward system.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCrossword Explorer looks most relevant to readers who want a broad crossword collection with daily goals and visual customization. It is less conclusive for someone requiring verified constructor quality or transparent hint pricing. The range of options is identifiable; their execution remains untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is there a daily puzzle feature?\nYes. Daily challenges are advertised.\n\n### Are exact board dimensions given?\nNo. The description promises different sizes without specifying them.\n\n### Are hint prices established?\nNo. Only the broad availability of hints and tips is described.\n\n### Is offline access confirmed?\nNo. The supplied text does not establish it.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/crossword-explorer/id1512407890?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
