    "bodyMarkdown": "# Numpuz: The Sliding Puzzle Is Only Part of What Players Describe\n\nNumpuz has a clearly documented sliding-number puzzle, but its sample describes a much broader collection. A review needs to preserve that distinction rather than pretend every reported activity follows the numbered grid's rules. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Use the empty space to restore order\n\nThe listing describes a frame of scrambled numbered tiles with one empty position. Sliding moves through that space place the numbers in order.\n\nSix sizes are advertised, from three-by-three to eight-by-eight. A timer records playtime; the text does not identify that recording function as a countdown that automatically ends the puzzle.\n\nMoving one tile can displace another part of the arrangement, so progress is not simply placing every number correctly once. Offline play and a wooden-style interface are advertised rather than independently tested.\n\n## The collection is part of players' attachment\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-03-11 through 2026-10-29 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 10.7, 10.8, 11.0, 11.1, 11.2, 11.3; the listing records version 11.3. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers enjoy the variety of games and describe continuing enjoyment over years. Some specifically value sharing challenges with family.\n\nOther accounts miss removed favorites, including a card game, a spinning-target activity, or a slices game. Players also report problems switching an older visual style back on in Match Blocks.\n\nThese accounts establish what reviewers describe, not a verified current catalog. A request for competitive sliding-puzzle play is likewise not proof that player-versus-player mode exists.\n\n## Updates and access deserve separate attention\n\nA reviewer reports water-sort progress disappearing after an update. Another describes being unable to restore a removal purchase after reinstalling. Those are individual histories, not a tested recovery policy for the whole collection.\n\nAdvertising experiences range from relatively light to intrusive, with some describing ads before continuing or difficult exits. Current removal prices and full coverage remain unverified.\n\nA favorable offline report does not resolve every mode's network requirements. Similarly, a complaint about sound controls does not prove that every activity shares identical audio settings.\n\n## Verdict\n\nNumpuz is most relevant to readers who enjoy a numbered sliding puzzle and are also interested in the broader collection described by reviewers. It is a more cautious choice for someone installing for one particular mini-game or dependable restoration. The original grid rules are concrete; the changing collection is a separate reason both for affection and disappointment.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How does the numbered puzzle move?\nTiles slide using a single empty position.\n\n### Is the recorded timer necessarily a deadline?\nNo. The listing describes recording playtime.\n\n### Are all reported mini-games confirmed available now?\nNo. Several reviewers specifically discuss removed favorites.\n\n### Is competitive sliding-puzzle play established?\nNo. A reviewer requests it rather than confirms it.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/numpuz-number-puzzle-games/id1340841589?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
