    "bodyMarkdown": "# Spider Solitaire: A Senior-Focused Interface, Not a Complete Rulebook\n\n.Spider Solitaire! emphasizes making a familiar card game more comfortable for older players. Its listing provides more detail about assistance and presentation than about the particular Spider rule options included. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Familiar cards with two input choices\n\nThe developer describes preserved classic Spider gameplay, moved through tapping or dragging. Large cards and enlarged fonts are intended to make the tableau easier to read.\n\nTraditional Spider generally centers on arranging descending card sequences, but the supplied description does not fully specify this app's suit choices, sequence-movement rules, scoring, or dealing restrictions. Those options should not be promised from the familiar name alone.\n\nSomeone returning from a desktop version may recognize the intended format. Whether the exact variant and controls match that person's habits remains an implementation question rather than a guaranteed transfer of every PC feature.\n\n## Assistance changes the cost of experimentation\n\nUnlimited free hints and undo are explicitly advertised. Those are distinct tools: a hint suggests a possible action, while undo lets a player reverse one.\n\nThe text does not establish whether hints explain the reasoning or merely identify a legal move. Nor does unlimited undo prove that every deal is winnable. Assistance can make experimentation more comfortable without resolving the underlying challenge.\n\nAuto-complete is also advertised, though the exact trigger and scope are not documented. It should not be described as automatically solving any difficult deal.\n\n## Comfort depends on more than the senior label\n\nCustom themes, statistics, tablet support, multiple languages, and smooth animation are named features. No screenshot was inspected, so actual card dimensions, contrast, and button spacing are not independently assessed.\n\nA large-font promise is relevant but not a certification for every visual or motor need. The developer's memory and focus language likewise does not establish a clinical benefit or validated cognitive training.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about readability, deal fairness, touch response, or the usefulness of hints.\n\n## Saving is not the same as syncing\n\nAutomatic saving and offline play are advertised. The description does not establish cross-device synchronization or a tested restoration procedure. A saved local game should not automatically be described as portable to another device.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, removal prices, other purchases, and stability remain unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Spider app looks most relevant to readers seeking familiar cards with advertised large text and generous recovery tools. It is less conclusive for someone who requires a particular suit variant or certified accessibility. Its assistance features are specific, while the exact rule configuration and practical comfort remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can cards move by tapping?\nYes. Both tap and drag controls are advertised.\n\n### Are hints and undo described as free?\nYes, and unlimited, according to the developer.\n\n### Are suit choices documented?\nNot in the supplied description.\n\n### Does auto-save prove device syncing?\nNo. Those are different features.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/spider-solitaire/id1508356462?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
