    "bodyMarkdown": "# Tidy Master: Finding Objects Is What Cleans the Room\n\nTidy Master turns a hidden-object checklist into a cleanup project. The listing's practical distinction is that finding the requested objects leads toward a cleaner room, rather than documenting a separate scrubbing or decoration simulator. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## A checklist gives the mess a purpose\n\nThe developer describes searching kitchens, bedrooms, and living rooms for objects on a list. Each scene contains four levels, and completing the challenges unlocks more scenes.\n\nThat can supply a visible sense of progress within a familiar location. A player is not only finding a named object but moving toward a cleared environment. The text does not fully explain whether objects relocate between the four stages or how completion changes every remaining detail.\n\nNo screenshot was inspected. Specific clutter, object placement, or before-and-after transformations are therefore not independently described.\n\n## Zoom supports inspection, with an unknown limit\n\nZooming is explicitly named as a way to locate hidden items. It may be relevant to readers who dislike inspecting small details, but the description does not establish maximum magnification, image sharpness, or tap-target size.\n\nThose are separate accessibility questions. Having zoom is not a certification that every object remains comfortable to identify or select.\n\nThe supplied text also does not specify timers, mistake penalties, hint costs, or the exact search-list format. A broad all-ages claim should not become a guarantee about every stage's visual demands.\n\n## Daisy supplies companionship, not a documented solving rule\n\nA Westie named Daisy accompanies the cleanup journey. The developer presents the dog as a helper and friend, but does not explain a particular hint ability or independent command system.\n\nSomeone who enjoys a gentle character framework may value that company. Someone seeking detailed home renovation choices or realistic cleaning tools has less support in this description. The main documented action remains finding checklist objects.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available. There is no supported player verdict about repeated rooms, increasing difficulty, readability, or the quality of Daisy's involvement.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, paid removal, other purchases, offline support, and stability remain unverified. The invitation to build a tidy life is promotional, not evidence of a real-world organizational benefit.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTidy Master looks most relevant to readers who enjoy room-based searching with a cleanup payoff and a companion character. It is less conclusive for someone wanting a fully specified untimed experience or a renovation sandbox. The checklist and four-level scene structure are concrete; the pacing and practical access remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What performs the cleanup?\nThe listing connects finding checklist items with clearing the room.\n\n### How many levels does each scene advertise?\nFour.\n\n### Is Daisy's exact hint ability documented?\nNo. The dog is described as a companion and helper without full mechanics.\n\n### Does zoom prove accessibility?\nNo. Its practical scale and clarity remain untested.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/tidy-master-hidden-objects/id6477343484?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
