    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong Triple 3D: Three Matches, Seven Spaces, and a Clock\n\nMahjong Triple 3D is not ordinary pair-clearing Mahjong solitaire. Its instructions combine triples, a limited collection box, and time pressure, making the order of selection central to the challenge. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Every unmatched choice occupies space\n\nThe developer says tapping sends a tile into a box and three identical tiles are collected. Clearing all tiles wins; reaching seven tiles in the box fails the attempt.\n\nThe useful question is therefore not only whether a tile has a match somewhere. It is whether selecting it now helps complete a triple before unrelated choices consume the remaining room.\n\nThe listing explicitly describes limited time and bonuses for faster selections. Pause, auto-save, hints, and boosters are advertised, but their complete limits and costs are not supplied.\n\n## Fast play is a benefit for some, not everyone\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-02-27 through 2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.10, 2.11.3, 2.11.5, 2.12.0, 2.12.1, 2.12.2, 2.7.14, 2.7.15, 2.7.17, 2.7.18, 2.9; the listing records version 2.12.2. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy the pace, challenge, or attractive tiles. One specifically likes the added complexity of flipping tiles.\n\nOther players dislike the piles or say the advertised structures do not appear as expected. Another objects to certain themed objects. No screenshot was inspected to resolve those visual comparisons or establish the current layout catalog.\n\nThe same busy search that feels engaging to one reviewer can feel like cleaning a mess to another. Neither reaction proves the game is universally relaxing or stressful.\n\n## A timed round makes interruptions more consequential\n\nMany reviewers describe ads during a stage rather than only between attempts. One says an ad covers part of the board, preventing tile selection while the round is active. Others report audio ads, freezing, or lost progress.\n\nThese are player reports, not independently measured timer behavior or a technical audit. A suspicion that reviews are fabricated also is not evidence sufficient to dismiss other players' experiences.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Current removal prices and scope are unverified; an optional purchase should not be promised to resolve every control, layout, or stability concern. Play-anywhere language is not a documented offline guarantee.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong Triple 3D is most relevant to readers who enjoy fast recognition combined with a limited buffer. It is less suitable as a presumed untimed alternative to classic Mahjong pairs. The explicit seven-tile failure rule and timer define the game more clearly than its broad relaxation pitch, while sampled interruption concerns qualify the pace.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many tiles make a match?\nThree identical tiles, according to the instructions.\n\n### What happens at seven tiles in the box?\nThe listing identifies that as failure.\n\n### Is this advertised as untimed?\nNo. Limited selection time and speed bonuses are explicit.\n\n### Is ad coverage of tiles independently observed?\nNo. It is a specific player report.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-triple-3d-tile-match/id1539562220?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
