    "bodyMarkdown": "# Traffic Frenzy: Fixed Routes, Special Missions, and Required Internet\n\nTraffic Frenzy makes ordering the cars the puzzle. Unlike many casually worded play-anywhere pitches, its listing also gives a direct access condition: a stable internet connection is required. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## You choose the sequence, not an arbitrary route\n\nThe developer describes tapping cars that move in fixed directions, clearing a traffic jam without crashes. Planning means checking how a selected movement interacts with the vehicles still present.\n\nA useful move can create room for another exit. Moving too early can instead produce a collision along the route. The supplied description does not establish whether cars stop automatically, whether a tap can be reversed, or what a mistake costs.\n\nThose gaps should not be filled with the exact controls of another traffic title. Fixed-direction movement is documented; a complete driving simulation is not.\n\n## Special objectives change the priority\n\nAmbulance escorts and one-way streets are advertised as additional challenges. An escort objective can give one vehicle priority rather than treating every exit as equivalent, although the precise mission rules are not supplied.\n\nSpecial cars with unique abilities are also described as rewards. The text does not identify each ability, its cooldown, or whether using a particular car is optional in every relevant stage.\n\nThousands of levels and growing complexity are promotional claims, not an independently counted catalog or measured curve. Someone who wants route dependencies may like the premise without needing to accept every scale claim as verified.\n\n## Online access is an explicit limitation\n\nThe developer says internet is required to play and to access all features smoothly. This should not be reframed as a verified offline traffic puzzle simply because its central actions appear simple.\n\nThe wording does not disclose exactly which network operation is essential or how the app behaves during a brief dropout. Those technical details remain untested, but the stated requirement is clear enough to matter for commuting or travel plans.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available here. There is no supported player consensus about collisions, ad load, difficulty, responsiveness, or connection failures.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Current purchases, removal offers, helper prices, and advertising frequency remain unverified. A network requirement does not by itself prove a particular advertising model.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTraffic Frenzy looks most relevant to readers who enjoy fixed-route sequencing and mission variations while connected. It is not a supported recommendation for someone requiring offline access. The ambulance and special-car claims broaden the premise, but their exact balance and the surrounding economy still need direct confirmation.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can vehicles move in any chosen direction?\nThe listing says each moves in a fixed direction.\n\n### Are ambulance missions mentioned?\nYes, explicitly.\n\n### Is offline play advertised?\nNo. A stable internet connection is expressly required.\n\n### Does required internet prove frequent ads?\nNo. Connection needs and ad frequency are separate questions.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/traffic-frenzy-unblock-jams/id6740790110?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
