    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mergedom: Decorating Is the Draw, Forced Breaks Are the Objection\n\nMergedom connects item merging to home renovation. The sample's clearest divide is not whether decorating is appealing, but whether players can remain engaged with it through the interruptions. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Make the tool that completes the order\n\nThe listing describes combining items into tools, fulfilling customer orders, using boosters, and opening new rooms after completing an area. Tournaments and events add goals beyond the current makeover.\n\nThat gives a merge a purpose within a longer task. An item can be useful because it belongs to a chain needed for an order, not simply because combining it produces something new.\n\nThe supplied description does not fully specify how many items combine, generator cooldowns, or board capacity. Those common merge-game details should not be prescribed without further evidence.\n\n## Some players like the decor more than the genre\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-05-25 through 2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 8.7.0, 8.7.3, 8.7.4, 8.7.5, 8.7.7; the listing records version 8.7.7. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nOne reviewer says decorating makes the app interesting despite not generally liking merge games. Another finds the star-to-item relationship reasonable and likes the presentation.\n\nA different player objects that certain task furniture has an assigned color scheme that clashes with the room. That is a useful qualification to the listing's complete-control language, but not an independently inspected limit on every decoration.\n\nOther accounts request more board space or report disappearing items. These are individual experiences rather than a verified capacity rule or technical fault rate.\n\n## Reward videos and forced breaks are distinct\n\nSeveral reviewers explicitly accept videos for extra items or shorter waits while objecting to ads appearing during ordinary merging. One says interruptions consume much of a timed benefit.\n\nThose reports describe perceived impact, not measured frequencies or timer behavior. A critical account can still call the core game well made while rejecting that access pattern.\n\nCurrent removal prices vary in reviewer recollections and were not independently checked. The listing advertises offline play, but no disconnected test establishes its scope or whether every event remains available.\n\nReferences to outside reward apps also do not verify a cash-payout feature within Mergedom. Similarly, allegations of copied design are not an independent intellectual-property finding.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMergedom is most relevant to readers who want item-chain goals to fund a continuing room makeover. It is a more cautious choice for someone expecting completely unrestricted design control or long uninterrupted sessions. The sample's decoration appeal is specific, while access to working space and the frequency of forced breaks are its strongest qualifications.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What do merged tools support?\nCustomer orders and renovation progress, according to the listing.\n\n### Is every furniture choice independently confirmed editable?\nNo. A reviewer reports assigned colors for some task items.\n\n### Are all videos voluntary rewards?\nThat is not established; many reviewers describe forced interruptions.\n\n### Is offline operation tested?\nNo. It remains an attributed developer claim.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mergedom-home-design-merge/id1549205071?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
