    "bodyMarkdown": "# Fluffy Drop: The No Timer Title Meets a Changing Player Record\n\nFluffy Drop puts No Timer in its title. Its sample makes that promise unusually difficult to repeat without qualification: players describe timers being introduced, removed, and presented as optional within the same short window. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Color placement is the common thread\n\nThe developer describes dragging fluffy balls into matching color zones, clearing the board, and planning chain reactions. Hundreds of levels and full offline play are advertised.\n\nThe supplied description does not fully specify destination capacities, legal transfers, or the spike mechanics mentioned by reviewers. It should not be rewritten into a complete ruleset from the title or an unseen image.\n\nThe calm presentation is an intended mood, not proof that every stage lacks pressure.\n\n## Preserve the timer conflict rather than choosing a convenient answer\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-24 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.20.0, 1.21.1, 1.21.2, 1.22.0; the listing records version 1.22.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts enjoy the challenge and initially unhurried play. Several critics say timed challenge levels replaced that experience; another specifically explains how rapid actions are uncomfortable after shoulder surgery.\n\nA reviewer updates their complaint to thank the developer for restoring untimed play. Another says a deadline offers rewards without penalizing failure. A later account still dislikes encountering timers.\n\nThese histories cannot establish one current rule for every account or mode. They do show why the title alone is insufficient for a no-timer recommendation. A separate criticism of redesigns concerns both cuteness and color discrimination, not just resistance to change.\n\n## Spikes and removal have separate conditions\n\nSome purchasers report video gates for resetting spikes despite buying ad removal. Others describe restoration failing on a second device or paid benefits disappearing. These are reports, not verified entitlements or a tested synchronization policy.\n\nCurrent removal prices were not independently checked. A removal purchase also does not establish that timer rules stay unchanged after updates.\n\nOffline use remains a developer claim, and no screenshot was inspected to compare old and new artwork. The sample supports affection for the original routine without proving every disputed change persists.\n\n## Verdict\n\nFluffy Drop is most relevant to readers who enjoy color-placement puzzles and are willing to confirm the current mode's constraints. It is a cautious choice for someone selecting solely because the title promises no timer. The evidence's strongest conclusion is the conflict itself, including reports of reversals rather than only the complaints.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does the title guarantee every stage is untimed?\nThe sample does not support that guarantee.\n\n### Do any reviewers report untimed play returning?\nYes. An updated account explicitly thanks the developer for it.\n\n### Are spike resets necessarily covered by removal?\nThat is not established; a purchaser reports required videos.\n\n### Is cross-device restoration independently tested?\nNo. Reported failures are not a complete restoration audit.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/fluffy-drop-no-timer/id6762342518?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
