    "bodyMarkdown": "# Cake Sort: Six Matching Slices, With Breaks That Disrupt the Plan\n\nCake Sort makes a completed cake the reward for organizing matching slices. Its sample repeatedly separates an appealing planning task from an advertising experience that players feel interrupts that planning. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Assemble the cake without filling the workspace\n\nThe listing describes moving plates in useful directions and combining six similar slices. New cakes, pies, coins, bonuses, and a reward wheel provide progression.\n\nThe important unit is the complete six-slice result, not merely an adjacent pair. A partly assembled cake can be a step toward completion while still leaving an awkward arrangement elsewhere.\n\nThe supplied text does not fully explain the transfer priority when several plates could exchange slices. That matters because a reviewer specifically objects to merges leaving an unwanted fragment. The account is not proof that the game intentionally causes losses.\n\n## Accessible does not have to mean trivial\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-04 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 6.5.0, 6.5.1, 6.5.2; the listing records version 6.5.2. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nA positive reviewer calls the game easy to pick up but capable of providing challenge. Many critics also explicitly say they enjoy the underlying play.\n\nOne account reports losing collected cakes after changing phones. That is a device-transfer history, not an independently tested saving policy. The description does not document a complete synchronization procedure.\n\nNo time limits, no penalties, and offline play are advertised. None automatically establishes that every helper is free or that a picture collection follows a user to another device.\n\n## Podcast interruptions create a specific loop\n\nMany reviewers describe ads before, during, or after play, with different intervals. A detailed account says ads stop a podcast; reopening the audio app and returning to the game starts another ad, stopping the podcast again.\n\nThat is more informative than a generic complaint about too many videos. It identifies how switching between two otherwise compatible activities can become frustrating, although no independent audio test was performed.\n\nOther reports mention browser redirects, difficult exits, or reward videos not supplying tools. Those accounts do not establish malware or a universal current failure rate.\n\nCurrent removal prices, ticket offers, and coverage were not verified. The free download alone does not describe the full access experience.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCake Sort is most relevant to readers who enjoy completing sets while managing partial progress around them. It is a more qualified recommendation for podcast listeners or anyone expecting a verified uninterrupted routine. The sample supports the six-slice concept's appeal while placing continuity and ad behavior ahead of the dessert catalog's size.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many matching slices complete the advertised merge?\nSix.\n\n### Are exact multi-plate transfer priorities documented?\nNo. The supplied rules are brief.\n\n### Is phone-transfer recovery verified?\nNo. A reviewer reports losing collected content.\n\n### Do browser-redirect reports prove malware?\nNo. They are not a technical security investigation.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cake-sort-color-puzzle-game/id6448392144?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
