    "bodyMarkdown": "# Blockscapes: A Wooden High-Score Pitch With a Sparse Rulebook\n\nBlockscapes emphasizes a rustic atmosphere and personal score chasing. The supplied listing is much clearer about its intended mood than about the exact placement and clearing rules. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## The documented goal is improving a personal record\n\nThe developer describes a wooden block-tile board game that challenges players to beat their own score. No pressure and no time limit are expressly advertised.\n\nThe text does not provide board dimensions, piece-tray size, rotation rules, or a complete explanation of what clears. Those details should not be filled in merely because many wood-block apps share familiar mechanics.\n\nThe description also uses jigsaw language, but does not document assembling a fixed picture. The safest account identifies a block-board scoring game without turning that loose comparison into a separate confirmed mode.\n\n## Scenery is part of the proposition\n\nDifferent scenic themes and a natural wooden look are promoted as reasons to relax. A player who likes returning to the same compact task may appreciate that framing more than someone seeking narrative stages or complex collection goals.\n\nThose preferences are design considerations, not player praise or complaints. No screenshot was visually inspected, so actual material texture, contrast, font size, and theme controls are not independently evaluated.\n\nA no-timer statement also does not establish unlimited undo, unrestricted helpers, or a guarantee that every piece sequence remains solvable. Those are different claims which the supplied description does not make clear.\n\n## The quoted praise belongs to the marketing\n\nThe listing includes enthusiastic testimonial excerpts, some describing relaxation or continuing play at an older age. No separate customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence.\n\nThose selected excerpts should not become an independent player-review digest, a demographic conclusion, or a public editorial rating. The developer's statements about brain health and well-being similarly do not establish medical or measured cognitive benefits.\n\nThe metadata records a free download, and the text promotes free play. It does not establish ad frequency, removal purchases, offline access, or stability. The quiet presentation should not be mistaken for verified quiet operation.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlockscapes looks most relevant to readers who want a wood-themed board game organized around personal scores and an advertised lack of time pressure. It is less conclusive for someone comparing precise rotation, clearing, or assistance rules. The intended mood is identifiable; the detailed implementation remains untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is a time limit advertised?\nThe listing explicitly says there is none.\n\n### Is the board size documented?\nNo. The supplied description does not specify it.\n\n### Are the quoted testimonials an independent sample?\nNo. They are part of the promotional listing.\n\n### Are brain-health outcomes established?\nNo. The evidence does not validate those claims.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/blockscapes-block-puzzle/id1473836342?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
