    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bubble Word Jam: Category Connections With a Progress-Continuity Question\n\nBubble Word Jam asks what words have in common rather than how to spell them from a letter set. The sample supports that associative challenge while raising questions about unclear links and preserving progress between sessions. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Find the theme, then organize the bubbles\n\nThe developer describes tapping word bubbles into correct categories and clearing the board. That combines recognizing meaning with sorting, rather than conventional crossword entry or tile-value scoring.\n\nThree tools are distinguished: Magnifying Glass reveals a category connection, Magnet pulls matching bubbles together, and Add Bubble supplies more room or opportunities.\n\nThe supplied description does not specify exact group sizes, capacity limits, or a complete timing rule. References to Scrabble and crossword themes should not be rewritten as those games' literal mechanics.\n\n## Associations are the attraction and the sticking point\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-10-22 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.5.0, 1.6.1, 1.7.0, 1.7.1; the listing records version 1.7.2. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy connecting meanings, a manageable challenge, and an engaging daily activity. One explicitly values associations beyond basic vocabulary.\n\nOthers find some links too remote or say required words do not appear without a powerup. Those are player interpretations, not independently inspected proof of an unsolvable arrangement or a paywall.\n\nA detailed reviewer reports a completed difficult stage repeating. Another describes losing an in-progress board when the phone sleeps or the app closes. That matters for an activity presented as patient and calm, though the current saving policy was not tested.\n\n## Ad experiences do not form one simple consensus\n\nSome reviewers find ads tolerable; others report long videos, no exit, or failed continuation. One purchaser says ads remain after buying a removal pack.\n\nThe evidence does not establish current removal coverage or a universal schedule. A complaint about timing out during an ad also does not define the rules of every ordinary puzzle.\n\nOffline-friendly play is advertised but not independently confirmed. Claims of improved memory or IQ remain promotional or personal impressions, not validated outcomes. Current helper prices are likewise unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBubble Word Jam is most relevant to readers who enjoy identifying shared meanings and organizing a category. It is a more qualified choice for someone who wants only literal associations or dependable stop-and-resume play. The sample's enthusiasm for thinking through relationships is specific, while ambiguous links and progress continuity remain meaningful reservations.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Do you build words from letters?\nThe listing describes sorting already-written words into categories.\n\n### What does Magnifying Glass reveal?\nA hidden category connection.\n\n### Are exact group sizes documented?\nNo. The supplied text does not establish them.\n\n### Is progress preserved after sleep verified?\nNo. A reviewer reports losing it, and no save test was performed.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bubble-word-jam/id6754839783?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
