    "bodyMarkdown": "# Arrow Out Puzzle: A No-Timer Claim With Directly Opposing Reports\n\nArrow Out Puzzle promises patient route planning without a timer. Its sample includes players describing exactly the deadline that the listing says should not be there. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Clear the path that opens another\n\nThe developer describes tapping and sliding arrows along designated routes, avoiding interference with other pieces, and clearing every arrow from the grid. Narrow corridors make action order important.\n\nThat is a dependency puzzle, not an archery simulation despite comparisons with bow-and-arrow games. A useful move removes an obstruction or makes another escape possible.\n\nThe description explicitly advertises no timer, while leaving error allowances and exact gesture rules less fully documented. Hundreds of levels and increasing difficulty are stated claims, not an inspected catalog.\n\n## A good challenge can become a visibility problem\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-01-30 through 2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.5.0, 1.5.1, 1.5.2, 1.5.3, 1.7.1; the listing records version 1.7.1. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy having to work for a win or appreciate an uncluttered arrow-focused presentation. Others find early stages easy.\n\nSeveral accounts report repeated boards after extended play. More detailed complaints concern losing the ability to reduce the whole puzzle to fit an iPad screen, making offscreen obstructions harder to anticipate. Another asks for larger arrows for accurate tapping.\n\nThose are specific and different visibility concerns: making an item larger can help selection, while seeing the full arrangement can help planning. No screenshot was inspected to resolve the current controls.\n\n## Timing and removal need qualification\n\nSome reviewers say timers appear later and want a way to disable them. That directly conflicts with the listing's no-timer claim. The evidence does not resolve whether versions, modes, or account differences explain it, so an untimed recommendation would be too confident.\n\nOther reports concern freezing, crashes, or continued ads after paid removal. Current prices and entitlements were not independently checked. A player describing an upgrade prompt also does not establish that every prompt is a required update rather than another offer.\n\nNeither alleged AI-written reviews nor allegations of developer greed are independently verified findings.\n\n## Verdict\n\nArrow Out is most relevant to readers who enjoy tracing interlocking routes and can check the current timer and scaling controls. It is a more cautious choice for someone relying on a verified no-clock experience or a complete-board view. The sample's appreciation for the puzzle survives alongside substantial practical reservations.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this archery?\nNo. The documented task is clearing directional pieces from a grid.\n\n### Does the listing say no timer?\nYes, but several players report timers.\n\n### Is full-board scaling verified?\nNo. Some iPad reviewers report difficulty with it.\n\n### Are paid removal terms independently checked?\nNo. Purchaser accounts do not establish every current entitlement.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/arrow-out-puzzle/id6753965803?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
