    "bodyMarkdown": "# RGB Cube Solver & Timer: A Solving Companion With Disputed Access\n\nRGB Cube Solver & Timer is a companion for solving a cube, not simply another self-contained puzzle game. Its sample suggests that getting the configuration accepted and reaching the solution can be more consequential than the advertised algorithm range. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Enter the cube before following the moves\n\nThe developer describes manual color entry or camera scanning of every face, followed by a step-by-step solution. Adjustable solving speed and methods are advertised, alongside cube sizes extending through eleven-by-eleven in the supported list.\n\nThe crucial dependency is accurate input. Instructions for a different configuration cannot establish a correct solution for the cube in hand. The supplied evidence does not independently test recognition or solution validity at any size.\n\nA solving timer and one-versus-one timing feature are also advertised. Those measure performance rather than necessarily ending an attempt at a deadline.\n\n## Helpful guidance and rejected configurations coexist\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-03-12 through 2026-10-18 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.0.6, 1.0.7, 1.0.8, 1.0.9, 1.1.0, 1.1.1, 1.1.2, 1.1.4, 1.1.6, 1.1.8, 1.2.1, 1.2.2, 1.2.5; the listing records version 1.2.7. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers say the app is handy, produces a working solution, or offers helpful videos. Another finds a limited free path useful without knowing exactly which restrictions apply.\n\nOther accounts report color errors, inaccurate scans, or difficulty entering a larger cube manually. Those reports do not prove every rejected state is a software error; input mistakes or a nonstandard configuration are not independently ruled out.\n\nThe conflict is nevertheless practical: the advertised convenience depends on a usable route from the physical object to accepted data.\n\n## A free download is not a free solution guarantee\n\nMany reviewers object to subscription prompts, a trial gate, or recurring charges. One says the limited version is not usable, while another reports successfully finding it.\n\nThe supplied description does not provide a complete current price or entitlement. Reviewer-mentioned weekly amounts and annual extrapolations should not become verified present offers. A trial is also not equivalent to permanently free access.\n\nThere are advertising complaints, but the evidence does not establish a universal frequency or removal product. No privacy, camera-permission, or offline audit was performed.\n\n## Verdict\n\nRGB Cube Solver is most relevant to readers seeking guided assistance with a physical cube and a way to track solves. It is a more cautious choice for someone expecting guaranteed free solutions or verified support for every advertised size. The sample preserves genuine success reports while emphasizing input reliability and clear access terms.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is camera input the only method?\nNo. Manual entry is also advertised.\n\n### Is eleven-by-eleven support independently tested?\nNo. It appears in the developer's supported list.\n\n### Is the timer necessarily a countdown?\nThe description presents it as measuring solving speed.\n\n### Is limited free access clearly established?\nIts exact scope is not; player accounts conflict.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/rgb-cube-solver-timer/id6499205938?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
