    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong - Brain Puzzle Games: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-01-25–2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering version 2.1. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMahjong - Brain Puzzle Games uses open pairs rather than a multiplayer mahjong hand. Match identical available tiles and remove the full layout, with undo and hints advertised as ways out of a blocked sequence.\n\n## Think about what the pair reveals\n\nThe listing calls this Mahjong Solitaire or Shanghai Solitaire. Its key rule is matching open identical pairs; it does not fully define every side-access or flower-category exception.\n\nThe official image shows an A-shaped layered layout on green, a running time display, three star outlines, and controls for hints and undo. It supports traditional tile removal without proving a universal board size or countdown.\n\nOne detailed reviewer enjoys thinking through which tiles to eliminate before matching. That is the clearest mechanical praise: choosing a legal pair can affect access to other pieces, not merely remove two matching pictures.\n\n## Routine appeal is personal, not medical\n\nOther reviewers describe nightly or daily play and enjoying an occupied mind. An account titled for seniors says they play daily, but does not independently assess tile size, contrast, or every accessibility need.\n\nNumerous entries praise brain exercise in brief terms. Those are personal impressions rather than evidence that the app prevents cognitive decline or guarantees improved memory.\n\nThe sample is heavily positive in its selected ratings, yet many bodies are only a few words. The strongest editorial conclusion comes from specific planning and routine comments, not converting short endorsements into claims about every feature.\n\n## Assistance is unusually explicit in the listing\n\nMore than 1,000 free boards, intelligent free hints, and unlimited free undo are advertised. These are useful stated boundaries, although their exact operation and the catalog count were not audited.\n\nDaily challenges, auto-fit, backgrounds, statistics, and optional sound are also named. Auto-fit is not an independently verified magnification or large-text feature.\n\nThe listing explicitly claims offline play. Initial downloads, connected content, and restoration are separate questions that were not tested.\n\n## Ads are present in attributed accounts\n\nA reviewer who otherwise loves the game asks how to remove annoying ads. Another wants prompts to download other mahjong apps stopped. Those identify interruption and cross-promotion concerns without establishing a present removal price.\n\nOne critical entry discusses playing Arrows Go instead; it is not reliable evidence about this app’s board behavior. The US download is free, while current purchases and ad schedules remain unresolved.\n\nConsider this Mahjong for its documented open-pair format and assistance promises, especially if planning pair order is more appealing than racing a clock.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Mahjong has genuine support for thoughtful pair selection and a recurring evening routine. Its free assistance claims are specific, but positive ratings do not erase ad complaints or establish clinical brain benefits.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it traditional multiplayer mahjong?\n\nNo. The developer explicitly describes Mahjong Solitaire and removing open identical pairs.\n\n### Is unlimited undo advertised?\n\nYes, as unlimited and free; this was not independently tested.\n\n### Does the visible clock prove a deadline?\n\nNo. A time display alone does not establish countdown failure conditions.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-brain-puzzle-games/id6450403706?uo=4)."
