    "bodyMarkdown": "# Merge Fashion: Romance Story: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-09-26–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.2.2–3.3.1. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nMerge Fashion: Romance Story uses cosmetics, clothes, and jewelry as the items to combine for customer orders. Alice’s betrayal and new romance supply the narrative reason for the makeover choices.\n\n## Merging is the repeated work\n\nThe listing describes serving fashion items to customers and unlocking more items through merging. Dress-up, accessories, and makeup surround that order loop rather than replacing it with a pure wardrobe simulator.\n\nThe first official image shows a formally dressed couple beneath a true-love caption. It supports romance marketing but does not demonstrate puzzle rules or the breadth of outfit customization.\n\nA supporter specifically likes both romance and combining things. Another enjoys dressing up with a childhood-play comparison. These identify a genuine audience for the combined format.\n\n## Story values split the sample\n\nSome reviewers find the plot interesting, while others dislike dialogue pacing and relationship narratives. One objects to makeover advertising that frames a woman as needing to change herself after a partner cheats.\n\nThat criticism concerns the message and promotional framing, not a verified clinical effect on every reader. Parents or readers sensitive to that theme have a concrete reason to examine the premise.\n\nSeveral accounts accuse the artwork of being AI-generated. Another likes the visuals even if that were true. The production process is not independently established here, so neither allegation nor conditional praise becomes a sourcing fact.\n\n## Energy changes affect access\n\nSeveral October reviewers say bubbles stopped supplying energy and now yield coins instead. One describes more reliance on a balloon or spin for a possible reward.\n\nThese are reports across versions, not a reproduced current economy. They explain frustration more precisely than a broad claim that the game is impossible without payment.\n\nA separate reviewer has accumulated currency while waiting for new chapters. Another describes lost progress after loading trouble and reinstallation, without a confirmed recovery. Waiting for content and losing an account are different limitations.\n\n## No-ad accounts do not settle the picture\n\nSome reviewers explicitly report no ads. Others describe lengthy videos or too many ads inside the game. Criticism of ads promoting the app is a separate category from ads encountered while playing it.\n\nThe US download is free. Current purchase terms, offline access, restoration, and technical stability remain untested. A five-star entry conditional on receiving diamonds is not independent praise of fair rewards.\n\nConsider Merge Fashion if order merging and a dramatic makeover story both appeal, while weighing narrative tone and energy availability separately.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMerge Fashion has supporters of its romance-and-combining combination. Energy-reward changes and story framing are its most distinctive disagreements, with ad conflicts and artwork allegations kept appropriately qualified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it only a dress-up game?\n\nNo. The listing and reviewers identify customer orders and merging as central activities.\n\n### Are energy bubbles definitely unchanged?\n\nNo. Several October reviewers report a change; current rewards were not independently inspected.\n\n### Is the artwork’s production method verified?\n\nNo. Reviewers disagree about and speculate on AI use, without independent production evidence.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/merge-fashion-romance-story/id6737874398?uo=4)."
