    "bodyMarkdown": "# Woodle Screw® Pin Nuts & Bolts: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nWoodle Screw® Pin Nuts & Bolts pairs unscrewing order with color triples. Clear the board, earn keys, and use those successes to support a city-rebuilding progression.\n\n## Removal has a second sorting condition\n\nThe developer says to match three screws of one color and plan the order of removing nuts and bolts. That is more specific than merely tapping every visible fastener.\n\nThe official image shows a flower-like wooden construction covered in colored, marked screws beneath collection boxes. It supports layered removal and color destinations without defining the capacity of every holding space.\n\nThe description does not fully explain how boards fall, which screws are blocked, or what happens when a destination cannot accept the next piece. Those rules should not be supplied from another screw title.\n\n## Keys give completion another destination\n\nConquering boards earns keys that unlock treasure chests. The listing connects that progression to restoring landmarks and rebuilding a city.\n\nThat is a stated reward structure, not a measured assessment of chest value or renovation speed. Exact milestone costs, theme choices, and the ability to revisit a completed board are not documented.\n\nTools and drill boosters are advertised for difficult situations. Their existence does not establish unlimited free recovery, precise costs, or a guarantee that any unscrewing order can be repaired.\n\n## Commercial claims are unusually explicit\n\nThe developer promises zero forced interruptions, with optional rewarded videos for extra progression items. It also says core boards and rebuild milestones are reachable through logic, while purchases concern cosmetics or convenience.\n\nThose are stronger attributed promises than a generic free-to-play label. They remain product claims rather than results of an ad session or a complete payment-path audit.\n\nThe US download is free. Current offer prices, video availability, and entitlement restoration remain untested. No customer review bodies are available in this sample to corroborate the claimed access boundaries.\n\n## Visibility and continuity are separate promises\n\nA color-blind-friendly option is explicitly described as switchable in Settings. The pictured symbols offer a visible cue, but no accessibility test establishes their usefulness for every viewer or board.\n\nOffline play and automatic cross-device saving are also advertised. Neither is independently verified; automatic saving does not document account requirements or confirm recovery after device loss.\n\nASMR language concerns the advertised clicking and metallic soundscape, not a measured stress treatment. Consider Woodle Screw for its triple-color removal and city goals, while keeping its unusually broad convenience promises attributed rather than turning them into filter facts.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWoodle Screw explains both its triple-clearing rule and its advertised free-access boundaries clearly. Those specifics make a useful shortlist case, but forced-ad, offline, and accessibility claims still require actual verification.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many screws make a match?\n\nThe listing specifies three screws of the same color.\n\n### Are forced ads advertised?\n\nThe developer explicitly promises none, while offering optional rewarded videos. This was not independently tested.\n\n### Is there a color-vision option?\n\nYes. A setting is advertised, without a completed accessibility assessment.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/woodle-screw-pin-nuts-bolts/id6642698438?uo=4)."
