    "bodyMarkdown": "# Block Puzzle Wood: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2021-11-14–2026-09-28 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.9.0–2.1.6. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nBlock Puzzle Wood gives each placed shape a double role: complete a line now and preserve somewhere for later pieces. A run ends when the supplied blocks no longer fit.\n\n## Lines, not digits or falling pieces\n\nThe description says horizontal and vertical lines disappear when filled. It does not describe sudoku-number entry or an automatic falling-piece clock.\n\nThe official image shows a wood-toned partially filled grid, score displays, and a pause button. The supplied text leaves rotation and every board variant unspecified.\n\nA historical reviewer likes chasing a score higher than the last run. Another describes the game as relaxing, while a contrasting short account finds it too simple. Those are differences in preferred demand, not a universal cognitive benefit.\n\n## A batch changes planning\n\nOne detailed 2024 reviewer dislikes having to use all three available pieces before a new batch arrives. An extensive 2022 account describes the same structure and frustration with shapes that cannot fit.\n\nThat means the chosen order within a batch can matter. It does not independently establish how pieces are generated, whether an algorithm guarantees solvability, or deliberate manipulation of a good run.\n\nThe older account also distinguishes a voluntary retry ad from commercials after losing. A retry that produces another awkward batch is a different complaint from an ad that blocks input before a shape can be placed.\n\n## Commercial pressure is not only an upsell\n\nA January 2026 supporter appreciates not being pressured to buy upgrades. That does not mean the game contains no ads: other reviewers describe pop-ups before placing a block or interrupting a board.\n\nHistorical removal accounts conflict. Some request a paid version or say none exists, while another describes ads returning on a new iPad. The sample cannot settle the current offer or restoration terms.\n\nReported ad intervals are personal observations, not measurements. Several older entries also report freezing and losing a promising score, without an independently verified repair.\n\n## Read the dates before assuming a current rule\n\nThis sample extends from November 2021 through September 2026. Its long span is useful for identifying repeated concerns, but an old rotation request or retry description is not a complete present-day rulebook.\n\nThe US download is free. Offline support, current purchase terms, and save continuity were not tested.\n\nConsider Block Puzzle Wood if the compact survival loop and wood presentation are the attraction. Readers seeking guaranteed solvable draws or a verified interruption-free score chase should not assume those from the simple interface.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBlock Puzzle Wood has committed score chasers and a clear line-clearing task. Batch planning is its useful mechanical detail; historical draw and ad complaints should remain reports rather than proof of rigging.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### When does a run end?\n\nThe listing says it ends when there is no space for the supplied blocks.\n\n### Are pieces replaced individually?\n\nTwo detailed historical reviewers describe using all three before getting another batch; current behavior was not tested.\n\n### Does no upgrade pressure mean no ads?\n\nNo. A supportive account about purchases coexists with explicit advertising complaints.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/block-puzzle-wood/id1281878296?uo=4)."
