    "bodyMarkdown": "# Solitaire - 2026: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-06-13–2026-10-27 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.004 and 2.005. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nSolitaire - 2026 combines a conventional seven-column card layout with a garden earned through play. Its title’s year does not by itself establish a required annual migration or an independently verified improvement over another app.\n\n## The listed rules are narrower than the introduction\n\nThe developer asks you to sort 52 cards into four suit foundations from ace to king. Empty tableau columns accept kings or king-led sequences, and the draw pile supplies more cards when needed.\n\nThat is a Klondike-style foundation. The opening references Spider and TriPeaks fans, but does not establish separate playable variants of each.\n\nThe official image shows a green table, four foundation slots, seven columns, and hint, undo, and solution controls. Exact draw counts and redeal rules are not completely documented.\n\n## A routine has genuine supporters\n\nOne reviewer finds the hint useful when they cannot see another move. Another looks forward to evening play and also starts the morning with it.\n\nSeveral call it challenging, while an October reviewer asks why they never lose. Those comments indicate different expectations for difficulty, not proof that every deal is intentionally automatic or guaranteed solvable.\n\nThe garden system advertises sunshine earnings, themed cultivation, and sharing. Short comments about enjoying the game do not independently establish the depth of that system or the cost of every plant.\n\n## Update prompts can be cross-promotion\n\nSeveral reviewers dislike frequent requests to update. One detailed October account says an ad led from another solitaire app to this one, then promoted a third separate app before the menu was accessible.\n\nThat is a report about advertising and navigation, not evidence that each promoted download is an official required patch. A reader should not be told their current game has expired simply because a promotion says so.\n\nSome reviewers perceive faster play than a previous version. No benchmark or verified comparison supports a universal performance claim.\n\n## Premium terms are only partly specified\n\nThe listing names monthly, quarterly, and yearly subscriptions, with a seven-day trial attached only to monthly. It does not quote a current dollar price.\n\nFeatures under the Premium heading include customization, daily challenges, tools, handedness modes, offline play, and statistics. The exact free-versus-paid boundary is unclear; their presence in a list is not proof all are unrestricted.\n\nThe US download is free. Ads before any card play are reported, while removal coverage and offline behavior were not tested. Consider this app for familiar solitaire with a garden wrapper, not an assumption that the dated title solves access or interruption concerns.\n\n## Verdict\n\nSolitaire 2026 has support for familiar card play and useful hints. Repeated update-style promotion is the distinctive caution, with subscription entitlements and mode claims kept narrower than the marketing introduction.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are Spider and TriPeaks confirmed modes?\n\nNo. They are mentioned in the introduction, while the supplied rules describe a seven-column Klondike-style game.\n\n### Which plan advertises a trial?\n\nOnly the monthly plan explicitly includes a seven-day trial in the supplied text.\n\n### Does an ad prove the app needs replacing?\n\nNo. Reviewers describe separate-app promotions presented as updates, not verified required migrations.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/solitaire-2026/id6462871973?uo=4)."
