    "bodyMarkdown": "# Endless Balls 3D: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nEndless Balls 3D makes hitting bricks with balls the entire written pitch. The clearest selection detail comes from the official image, which shows a trajectory through a numbered target formation.\n\n## Read the pictured shot\n\nThe inspected screen shows Level 2, numbered blocks arranged into a fish-like silhouette, a dotted route bouncing around a framed playfield, and a ball source below.\n\nThat supports a brick-breaker presentation involving shot direction. It does not independently establish how many hits each number requires, how new balls are collected, or whether every block follows the same rule.\n\nAn aiming line can make a prospective path visible without guaranteeing that the shot executes exactly as pictured. No collision or input test was performed.\n\n## The 3D label is not a physics verdict\n\nThe developer calls this a 3D brick breaker and advertises animation and effects. The image gives the pieces depth, but it does not establish free camera movement or a fully navigable three-dimensional arena.\n\nLikewise, the assertion that this is the only game of its kind is a promotional exclusivity claim, not an independently researched fact.\n\nA reader interested in a particular bounce simulation, object destruction style, or camera control should not assume it from the title. The supplied description does not explain those systems.\n\n## Thousands of levels, few documented boundaries\n\nA large level library is advertised without an audited inventory. The word Endless in the title does not prove an endless survival mode, unlimited retries, or a catalog without a final stage.\n\nThe image includes a horizontal slider-like control and icons beneath the board. Their exact purposes are not documented clearly enough to label them speed adjustment, extra balls, or paid tools as facts.\n\nTime limits, shot budgets, loss conditions, and advancement rules remain unresolved. The presence of Level 2 does not settle whether blocks descend after every volley or whether missing a shot ends the run.\n\n## Select for the visible target task\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising frequency, purchase offers, paid removal, offline access, and save behavior are unknown. No customer review bodies are available in this sample to support claims about fair difficulty or satisfying impact.\n\nConsider Endless Balls 3D if the numbered target shapes and predicted shot path are enough to warrant investigation. It is less suitable as a verified recommendation for a particular physics model or an uninterrupted score chase.\n\nThe sparse source supports a concrete visual premise, but not a detailed experience report. Keeping that boundary leaves the useful information intact without turning a brief developer pitch into an invented playtest.\n\n## Verdict\n\nEndless Balls 3D shows a recognizable aiming-and-bricks problem. Its image is more informative than its short description, while progression, failure rules, and commercial access remain substantially unspecified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is a predicted path pictured?\n\nYes. The official image shows a dotted multi-bounce trajectory through the playfield.\n\n### Does Endless prove an endless mode?\n\nNo. The description advertises thousands of levels without defining an endless mode.\n\n### Are the numbered-brick hit rules documented?\n\nNot in enough detail to confirm their exact meaning or exceptions.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/endless-balls-3d/id1463802616?uo=4)."
