    "bodyMarkdown": "# Logic Puzzles - Clue Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nLogic Puzzles - Clue Game turns written facts into a grid of possible relationships. Tick what must be true, cross out what cannot be true, and use those decisions to complete the remaining matches.\n\n## Every category constrains another\n\nThe developer specifies equal numbers of parameters in each category and a one-to-one match across categories. A confirmed association therefore rules out competing associations in that category.\n\nThat is the mechanical reason to keep recording exclusions, not simply wait for a clue to name every answer. A negative fact can become useful after other cells have been eliminated.\n\nThe official image shows categories for people and places, ticks and crosses, a written clue list, and hearts above. It supports a relationship grid rather than a number-filling Sudoku board.\n\n## The story is a wrapper for deductions\n\nThe listing groups puzzles into theft investigations, vacation planning, a space expedition, and other themed stories. These contexts can make abstract associations easier to remember.\n\nThey do not establish branching dialogue, free exploration, or a complete detective simulation. The documented work remains reading limited clues and assigning category relationships.\n\nThe supplied material does not define every clue form or whether every puzzle can be solved without trial assumptions. Quality assurances from a familiar developer are promotional claims, not an independent uniqueness audit.\n\n## Untimed does not mean no mistake system\n\nNo time limit is explicitly advertised, making deliberate reading a stronger documented fit than reflex play. That remains an attributed product claim rather than a completed mode test.\n\nThe hearts in the inspected image leave a separate question: how mistakes affect a puzzle. The description does not establish the allowance, penalties, or whether checking can be disabled.\n\nHints are offered to reach the solution faster, but their costs, refill conditions, and explanatory depth are unspecified. A hint button should not be called a full deduction tutor without evidence that it explains the logical step.\n\n## Consider the reading demands\n\nThis format is a reasonable match for readers who like converting sentences into formal exclusions. It is less relevant to someone seeking purely visual shape placement or automatic matching.\n\nThe US download is free. Ads, purchases, paid removal, offline support, and save reliability remain unresolved. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to assess clue wording, localization, or late-story access.\n\nConsider Logic Puzzles for its clearly described one-to-one deduction system. Keep the no-clock promise separate from unspecified mistakes and commercial boundaries, rather than assuming every constraint disappears when a game advertises calm play.\n\n## Verdict\n\nLogic Puzzles gives a comparatively clear account of how category deduction works. Story variety and no-time-limit language are useful stated features, while checking rules and hint quality remain open.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can one item match two people in the same category?\n\nThe supplied rule says each parameter has only one match in each other category.\n\n### Is a time limit advertised?\n\nNo. The listing explicitly says there is no time limit.\n\n### Do hints necessarily explain the reasoning?\n\nThat is not established. Hints are advertised without a complete description of their teaching depth.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/logic-puzzles-clue-game/id1641732564?uo=4)."
