    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong: Matching Games: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nMahjong: Matching Games presents solitaire tile removal in portrait orientation. Its distinctive advertised additions include timed records, automatic completion, and a boss cat every tenth level.\n\n## Tiles, not playing cards\n\nThe description explicitly calls this Mahjong Solitaire and references Taipei or Shanghai. Its comparison to Klondike does not mean it uses a card stock or suit foundations.\n\nThe official image shows a layered arrangement of traditional symbols on a wood-toned background. It supports pair matching rather than a multiplayer mahjong hand-building system.\n\nThe exact uncovered-tile, side-access, flower, and season rules are not fully written out. A broad claim of authentic solitaire should not substitute for every detail of the legal-move rulebook.\n\n## Portrait readability is an attributed design goal\n\nLarge tiles and a phone-optimized portrait interface are advertised. The actual legibility, contrast, and hit areas were not tested across different boards or tile sets.\n\nFour matching tile sets are named without a full catalog or unlock list. Theme variety does not establish an alternative noncolor cue for every accessibility need.\n\nThe developer also calls the download small. No installation-size comparison was conducted, and future content may have separate requirements not described here.\n\n## Progression has several layers\n\nMore than 1,400 levels and fresh daily boards are advertised. A boss cat every ten levels supplies a specific recurring milestone, but the text does not explain its rules or prizes.\n\nHints and undo are described as learning support. Their allowance and costs remain unspecified, so neither should be called unlimited or entirely free simply because the app download is free.\n\nAutomatic completion is another stated feature. The supplied material does not explain when it becomes available, whether it spends a resource, or how it affects personal records.\n\n## Timing can be an objective without being a deadline\n\nThe description encourages beating your best time and sharing it with friends. That supports a record-chasing element, not necessarily a countdown that forces a loss.\n\nOffline play is explicitly advertised, while connectivity for records or shared features is not fully documented. No disconnected session was performed.\n\nThe US download is free. Ads, purchase offers, removal terms, and current stability remain unresolved. There are no customer review bodies here to verify the developer’s user-count claims or reception.\n\nConsider this Mahjong if portrait matching, assistance, and occasional character milestones appeal. It is less appropriate as a confirmed no-pressure or unrestricted-hint recommendation based solely on its simple-matching introduction.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong: Matching Games has specific portrait, record, and boss-cat features on paper. Its practical assistance and timing conditions are less clear, so the basic solitaire task is more certain than the access promises.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is the Klondike comparison a card mode?\n\nNo such mode is established. The listing explicitly describes tile-based Mahjong Solitaire.\n\n### Are boss cats mentioned?\n\nYes, every ten levels, without full encounter rules.\n\n### Does chasing a best time prove a countdown?\n\nNo. Record timing and a mandatory deadline are different features.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-matching-games/id1329803869?uo=4)."
