    "bodyMarkdown": "# Ball Sort : Color Sort Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nBall Sort : Color Sort Game adds concealed colors and unusually tall containers to a familiar sorting proposition. The stated destination is one color grouped into a tube, not popping the balls by shooting at them.\n\n## Sorting exposes the next problem\n\nThe listing explicitly says to put all balls of the same color into one tube. More colors arrive as progression advances, increasing the number of groups a board must accommodate.\n\nThe official image shows mixed stacks across multiple tubes and a ball suspended above a destination. It supports individual transfers, although the supplied text does not fully specify legal destination colors or empty-tube exceptions.\n\nThose missing details matter. A matching-looking picture is not enough to import another app’s exact capacity, pouring, or multi-ball movement rules.\n\n## Hidden colors change planning information\n\nConcealed balls are explicitly advertised, as are extra-tall tubes. A hidden color can limit what the player knows about a buried part of a stack before it is exposed.\n\nThat is a meaningful variation from fully visible sorting, but the description does not establish when concealment begins, how much is hidden, or whether help can reveal it.\n\nLikewise, a taller tube changes the visible space available for stacking without proving a particular difficulty increase. More capacity may create different rearrangements, and no hands-on progression test is offered here.\n\n## No clock is an explicit claim\n\nThe developer says there are no time limits. That supports a deliberate sorting proposition rather than a speed challenge, while leaving mistakes, restarts, and other constraints unspecified.\n\nHints are advertised, but their price, allowance, and explanatory depth are not documented. An available hint is not automatically an unlimited supply of correct moves.\n\nThe claimed library exceeds 20,000 levels. It was not independently audited, and the text does not explain how many are initially accessible or whether special board types use separate progression.\n\n## Cosmetics are another selection point\n\nBubble textures, alternative ball shapes, and elastic animal or mushroom tube caps are named. They offer specific appearance choices, not verified improved visibility or new transfer mechanics.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising frequency, purchase offers, removal benefits, offline play, and save reliability remain unresolved. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to confirm enjoyable late-game variety or fair assistance.\n\nConsider this Ball Sort if concealed information and taller stacks sound more interesting than another standard tube layout. Readers seeking fully visible deterministic planning should take the hidden-color feature seriously rather than overlook it as decoration.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis Ball Sort has a concrete variation in hidden balls and tall tubes, alongside an explicit no-clock promise. Transfer exceptions and help costs remain less clear than the destination goal.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are all ball colors always visible?\n\nThe listing explicitly advertises hidden-color balls as a special challenge.\n\n### Is a time limit advertised?\n\nNo. The developer says there are no time limits.\n\n### Do cosmetic tube caps establish new rules?\n\nNo. Their mechanical effects, if any, are not documented.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/ball-sort-color-sort-game/id6730118370?uo=4)."
