    "bodyMarkdown": "# Match Jong - Zen Tile Puzzle: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nMatch Jong - Zen Tile Puzzle connects tile clearing to an art collection. Match three identical pictures and use completed puzzles to reveal scenery and advance across an adventure map.\n\n## Three is the defining number\n\nThe developer explicitly calls this triple matching. Mahjong inspiration therefore should not become an assertion that ordinary two-tile solitaire pairing is the core rule.\n\nThe official image shows layered picture tiles, a lower holding tray, and strawberries being grouped. It supports selecting visible pieces into temporary storage rather than swapping neighboring board gems.\n\nThe caption says 3D tiles, which describes the pictured raised appearance more securely than a free-camera three-dimensional puzzle. Rotation and depth controls are not documented.\n\n## The tray raises a practical question\n\nTemporary storage can make selection order matter because unmatched pieces occupy room while waiting for partners. The supplied material does not fully specify capacity, a full-tray failure condition, or whether every pictured tool is free.\n\nThese are relevant questions rather than proven hidden limitations. No hands-on test establishes forgiving recovery or a guarantee that any selection can be undone.\n\nBoosters and helpful tools are advertised for difficult stages. Their price, earning rate, and ability to preserve a run remain unspecified.\n\n## Art and garden are distinct progression claims\n\nCompleted puzzles are said to bring artwork to life and build a collection. That is a completion reward, not evidence of a freehand painting editor or original composition tools.\n\nThe listing separately mentions upgrading a garden, leagues, and worldwide events. Exact garden choices, competitive matching, event schedules, and rewards are not explained in full.\n\nAn adventure map and thousands of levels are advertised without an audited catalog. There is no evidence here that every board or piece of artwork is available immediately without unlocking.\n\n## Offline does not cover every connected feature\n\nThe developer says the mobile and tablet game can be played offline. Leaderboards and worldwide challenges have separate connectivity questions that are not settled by that broad statement.\n\nThe US download is free. Ads, purchases, paid removal, saving, and present stability remain unresolved. No customer review bodies are available to corroborate a smooth curve, fair tools, or dependable art unlocks.\n\nThe zen language concerns the intended presentation, not a measured stress benefit or a confirmed absence of clocks. Consider Match Jong when triple selection and art rewards appeal together, while keeping its commercial and competitive boundaries open.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMatch Jong has a clear triple-tile identity with an art-collection destination. The tray and progression layers give useful selection detail, but no actual player sample establishes their balance or reliability.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does it use pairs or triples?\n\nThe listing explicitly says three matching tiles.\n\n### Can you draw the collected art yourself?\n\nNo freehand drawing feature is established; art is described as revealed through puzzle completion.\n\n### Does offline mean worldwide events work disconnected?\n\nNo. The listing does not establish complete offline coverage of connected competition.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/match-jong-zen-tile-puzzle/id6446582118?uo=4)."
