    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bubble Pop Origin! Puzzle Game: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nBubble Pop Origin! Puzzle Game makes an unusually absolute promise: no ads, ever. That is a direct developer claim, not a verified finding of this listing-based analysis.\n\n## The core task is clearing a bubble field\n\nAim into a group and match at least three bubbles of one color to burst them. The listing says to clear every bubble, earn stars, and advance through a campaign.\n\nThe official image shows colored spheres around gaps, a predicted aiming route, and a launcher with a number-21 display. It supports aiming at existing clusters rather than matching balls in tubes.\n\nThe full shot allowance, color-swap rules, scoring formula, and loss conditions are not documented. A number in one promotional board is not a universal budget.\n\n## Help includes named destructive tools\n\nBombs, rockets, and fireballs are advertised for difficult situations. Those give the reader specific assistance categories rather than only a generic hint promise.\n\nThe material does not explain their exact area effects, supply rates, or prices. A statement that they can overcome obstacles is not a guarantee of unlimited free rescues.\n\nThe campaign is advertised as more than 9,000 handcrafted levels, with additions every two weeks. Neither count nor cadence was independently audited, and handcrafted is a production claim rather than an inspected development process.\n\n## Daily goals and racing are separate attractions\n\nDaily tasks offer coins and prizes, while Explorer Race supplies global leaderboards. The description does not fully define the race duration, qualification, or whether players share a board.\n\nA reader seeking a quiet campaign may not value the same extras as someone motivated by rankings. Their presence does not establish that competition is required to access every ordinary level.\n\nClaims of millions of players and uniquely satisfying feedback remain marketing language. There are no customer review bodies here to support a current consensus about either.\n\n## Keep the access promises attributed\n\nThe developer explicitly promotes offline play and an entirely ad-free experience. These are useful statements to know, but neither was tested during an actual session.\n\nThe US download is free. Current purchase offers, booster spending, and removal terms remain unknown; there is no reason to infer no purchases merely because no ads are promised.\n\nOffline wording also does not establish disconnected leaderboard updates or future content downloads. Consider Bubble Pop Origin for its documented shooting and campaign proposition, while treating the strong convenience promises as claims still needing verification rather than qualifying needs-filter facts.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBubble Pop Origin explains its shooting goals and named tools clearly. Its absolute ad-free claim is noteworthy but remains unverified, and should not be converted into either a tested guarantee or an invented contrary complaint.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Does the developer explicitly promise no ads?\n\nYes, repeatedly and absolutely. No actual advertising session was tested here.\n\n### How large is the advertised catalog?\n\nMore than 9,000 levels, with additions every two weeks; neither figure was audited.\n\n### Does ad-free imply no booster purchases?\n\nNo. Advertising and purchase access are separate questions.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bubble-pop-origin-puzzle-game/id1487907763?uo=4)."
