    "bodyMarkdown": "# Find Hidden Objects: Sherlock: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nFind Hidden Objects: Sherlock sends Holmes and Watson into familiar books whose plots have gone wrong. Its useful mechanical proposition is a choice between object searching and gem matching for a scene.\n\n## The investigation is literary fantasy\n\nThe developer names The Hound of the Baskervilles, Alice in Wonderland, and The Wizard of Oz as altered story worlds. Villains are winning and familiar characters need their original plots restored.\n\nThat is a specific fictional premise rather than a realistic simulation of police procedure. A reader who enjoys recognizable settings may value the crossover more than another anonymous mansion search.\n\nThe official image shows Sherlock, an ornate room, a highlighted rose, and an object strip. It supports a hidden-scene presentation without demonstrating a complete case solution.\n\n## Two routes into a scene\n\nThe listing explicitly invites finding hidden objects or matching gems in a row for any scene. This is stronger evidence of mode choice than merely mentioning match-3 in an introduction.\n\nIt does not fully establish whether both modes have identical rewards, budgets, or difficulty. Nor does it explain whether every event permits the same substitution.\n\nThe object route emphasizes recognizing targets in detailed artwork. The matching route changes the repeated task while keeping the story destination, which may appeal to someone who likes the narrative but not continuous visual search.\n\n## Quests connect locations\n\nCharacters, cases, regular new books, and quest completion are advertised. Those give progression a clear story purpose, but the update schedule and amount of content were not independently audited.\n\nHints, energy, timers, and penalties are not fully described in the supplied text. A choice of puzzle families does not mean every story step is unrestricted or every object scene is untimed.\n\nThe available language list is broad, including separate Spanish and Latin American Spanish labels. The presence of localization does not establish equal clue quality or a perfect translation in each language.\n\n## Offline and free have separate limits\n\nThe developer explicitly says the game works offline and acknowledges optional purchases for bonuses. This analysis does not test initial downloads, new-book access, account recovery, or connected events.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising frequency, removal offers, exact bonus prices, and present stability remain unresolved. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to assess story reception or spending pressure.\n\nConsider Sherlock if a literary mystery and a documented search-or-match choice are the attraction. Readers wanting a deduction grid, a pure detective interview game, or guaranteed unlimited scene access should not infer those from its famous protagonist.\n\n## Verdict\n\nSherlock offers a concrete story setting and an explicitly documented alternative to hidden-object searching. Its mode choice is the strongest selection point, while campaign access and practical constraints remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can you choose matching instead of searching?\n\nThe developer explicitly offers hidden-object or gem-matching play for a scene, without fully documenting event exceptions.\n\n### Is the story realistic policing?\n\nNo. The premise involves altered fictional books and literary magic.\n\n### Are purchases acknowledged?\n\nYes. Optional bonuses can be purchased; current prices and access boundaries were not tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/find-hidden-objects-sherlock/id1489732936?uo=4)."
