    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjöng: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nMahjöng offers a concise proposition: a relaxing matching game with 500 layouts. Its supplied description is a single sentence, so the official image provides most of the practical detail.\n\n## A traditional-looking board is visible\n\nThe inspected screenshot shows layered tiles bearing characters, bamboo-like marks, and circle patterns on a green background. Three stars sit above, with assistance controls below.\n\nThat supports a solitaire-style matching presentation rather than a card game or a four-player table. The text does not independently define every legal pair or when a tile is considered available.\n\nCovered tiles, free sides, flower exceptions, and season exceptions should not be imported from a different mahjong app simply because the art looks familiar. Their exact implementation remains unresolved.\n\n## Layout variety is the concrete catalog claim\n\nThe developer names 500 layouts without giving a list, difficulty tiers, or an update schedule. That is a stated breadth figure, not an independently counted catalog or a promise of unlimited new boards.\n\nDifferent layouts can change which pieces become exposed next. The source does not establish a solvable-deal guarantee, the randomness of tile placement, or whether every legal sequence can still win.\n\nA reader wanting a particular traditional arrangement should not assume it is included merely because the total is substantial.\n\n## The pictured tools have finite-looking counters\n\nShuffle, Restart, Undo, and Hint labels appear beneath the board. Some display numbers, which shows a counter-based interface without proving what replenishes the allowance.\n\nThe image therefore supports the existence of assistance concepts, not unlimited free access. Tool prices, ad requirements, and whether a restart preserves a score are not explained.\n\nThe star display similarly does not identify the scoring formula. Speed, move efficiency, tool use, or another factor could matter, but none is established by the supplied text.\n\n## Relaxing is a design claim\n\nRealistic tiles and intuitive controls are developer descriptions. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to support legibility, responsiveness, or the comfort of a long session.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, purchases, removal offers, offline access, timer behavior, and save continuity remain unresolved. A quiet green board is not evidence of an uninterrupted live interface.\n\nConsider Mahjöng if a traditional-looking tile layout and the advertised 500-board collection are the desired starting point. It is less appropriate as a recommendation for a particular rule variant, unlimited assistance, or independently verified calm access.\n\nThe sparse listing justifies a focused description of what is visible, not an invented account of how the game feels after several levels.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjöng presents a familiar board and a specific layout count. Its very short description leaves legality, scoring, and help allowances too unclear for stronger practical claims.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many layouts are advertised?\n\nThe description says 500; the catalog was not independently audited.\n\n### Are hint and undo controls visible?\n\nYes, in the official image, with counters that do not establish their access terms.\n\n### Is the app confirmed untimed?\n\nNo timer policy is established by the supplied description or inspected image.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahj%C3%B6ng/id1070255846?uo=4)."
