    "bodyMarkdown": "# Knit Out: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nKnit Out is a color-flow sorting puzzle, not a freeform knitting lesson. Release ropes from the upper knot lines and choose matching bobbins from a grid to collect them below.\n\n## Select a destination for the released thread\n\nThe description makes the bobbin choice part of the task. A color needs a suitable collector, so choosing from the grid is more than selecting a decorative spool.\n\nThe official image shows colored rows above, threads running to bobbins, and several lower containers holding colored pieces. It supports top-to-bottom sorting rather than stitching a chosen garment pattern.\n\nThe exact temporary-storage capacity, rules for blocked bobbins, and consequences of an unmatched release are not explained fully. Those restrictions should not be borrowed from another yarn game.\n\n## Untangling is the theme, matching is the stated rule\n\nThe developer uses knot and rope language, but does not describe freely dragging every endpoint to unweave a physical knot. Matching ropes to bobbins is the more concrete action.\n\nHundreds of puzzles and no timers are explicitly advertised. That supports deliberate planning as an intended format, not proof of unlimited lives, retries, or unrestricted assistance.\n\nAnimation and sound are described as satisfying. No hands-on feedback, audio, or accessibility test supports a universal sensory verdict.\n\n## The internet requirement is explicit\n\nUnlike listings that merely say play anywhere, this one expressly requires an internet connection and warns that network fees may apply.\n\nIt should therefore not be recommended as a verified offline option, nor should its simple-looking board be used to override that stated requirement. Connection behavior was not independently tested.\n\nThe legal text also names a minimum age of thirteen or a higher locally required age. Colorful art is not a reason to relabel it as suitable for every age.\n\n## Paid random items deserve distinct attention\n\nThe US download is free, with optional purchases including paid random-item drops. The developer says drop-rate information is available inside the game; those rates were not accessed or audited here.\n\nA random item is not a guaranteed specific purchase outcome. Present prices, the contents of the pool, duplicate handling, and any effect on puzzle progress remain unresolved.\n\nAdvertising frequency, removal offers, save reliability, and current stability are also unknown. No customer review bodies are available to corroborate pacing or commercial pressure.\n\nConsider Knit Out for color-matched thread collection if a connected puzzle fits your needs. Readers prioritizing fully offline play or avoiding paid random rewards have explicit source-backed reasons to examine its access terms carefully.\n\n## Verdict\n\nKnit Out communicates a concrete sorting relationship between threads and bobbins. Its internet, age, and paid-random-item disclosures are unusually important boundaries alongside the advertised no-timer format.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is offline play promised?\n\nNo. The listing explicitly requires an internet connection.\n\n### Are purchases always a specified item?\n\nNot necessarily. Paid random-item drops are disclosed, with rates said to be available in-game.\n\n### Is there an age requirement?\n\nThe supplied legal text says at least thirteen or a higher age required locally.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/knit-out/id6740406762?uo=4)."
