    "bodyMarkdown": "# Water Sort!: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-08-16–2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.2.8–1.3.2. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nWater Sort! is primarily a liquid-sorting puzzle, even though its promotion also emphasizes helping distressed characters. Pour layers between bottles until each holds a single color.\n\n## Sorting is better documented than the rescue\n\nThe developer names pouring, cosmetic bottle choices, daily puzzles, mini-stories, jigsaw events, and a season pass. It does not fully define every legal destination or how much liquid moves at once.\n\nThe official image instead shows a woman holding a baby in a snowy room with a they-need-help caption. That is story-focused marketing rather than a demonstration of the sorting board.\n\nOne reviewer expected helping scenes but found only pouring; another says they spend much more time sorting than helping. Those are progression-emphasis complaints, not proof that every listed story feature is absent.\n\n## The ordinary puzzle has supporters\n\nA reviewer calls it a favorite chill activity despite ads. Another finds the actual water game challenging and fun, while a contrasting account thinks difficulty rises too fast.\n\nThese differences matter because a familiar rule does not produce one universal difficulty preference. No independent solvability test establishes a deliberately impossible board.\n\nA more specific complaint says bottle designs changed and color choice disappeared. That conflicts with broad customization marketing without settling every current option or account.\n\n## Reward clarity is a separate problem\n\nTwo reviewers ask what accumulated coins can do. That is a discoverability concern, not evidence the currency has no function.\n\nAnother believes tight bottle space forces recovery ads. That remains an attributed interpretation rather than a verified rule that every puzzle requires a video.\n\n## Advertising and subscription terms need separation\n\nMany reviewers report between-stage or on-screen ads, lengthy closing sequences, or promotions for another app framed as an upgrade. A review titled No ads actually says ads now appear after every level; its title is not an ad-free testimonial.\n\nOne September supporter says an update makes ads go away, while later accounts still report them. No universal current frequency follows.\n\nThe listing quotes auto-renewing plans at $2.99 weekly, $5.99 monthly, and $23.99 yearly. It does not fully define every entitlement, and those supplied amounts were not checked at checkout.\n\nThe US download is free. Offline access, purchase restoration, and present stability remain unresolved. Claims of data theft in a brief review are not independently established security findings.\n\nConsider Water Sort for layered color planning, while evaluating story frequency, customization continuity, and commercial interruptions as separate selection issues.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWater Sort has support for its central pouring activity, with the rescue imagery creating a different expectation. Unclear coin uses and changed cosmetic controls add specific cautions beyond the sample’s substantial advertising frustration.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is the rescue image the main puzzle?\n\nThe stronger written rule describes pouring colors; some reviewers expected more rescue-story interaction.\n\n### Does the No ads review prove absence?\n\nNo. Its body explicitly reports ads after each level.\n\n### Are subscription benefits fully established?\n\nThe listing quotes three recurring prices but does not fully specify entitlement coverage; checkout was not tested.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/water-sort/id6633436144?uo=4)."
