    "bodyMarkdown": "# The Wizard of Oz Magic Match 3: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nThe Wizard of Oz Magic Match 3 uses the film’s characters as more than background wallpaper. Medallions, storybook pages, and movie Moments give matching several different collection goals.\n\n## Characters can affect a current board\n\nThe listing describes matching at least three gems and dropping character Medallions to the bottom so the corresponding character appears to assist. Ruby Slippers, lollipops, and the Tin Man’s Axe are named tools.\n\nThat is a specific objective relationship: a match can help bring a Medallion closer to its destination rather than only increase score.\n\nThe first official image features Dorothy with Toto and a match-3 caption. It establishes licensed presentation, not the exact effect or free availability of every advertised power-up.\n\n## Story points and campaign progress differ\n\nOrdinary matching takes the reader along the Yellow Brick Road. A separately described infinite level supplies story points used to complete storybook pages.\n\nThat infinite-level claim should not be expanded into a guarantee that the entire campaign is endless or unrestricted. The material does not fully define entry conditions, energy, or the story-point earning formula.\n\nMovie Moments offer stills, facts, and trivia, including rare entries. Those are advertised collection features rather than an independently verified film archive or a guarantee of a particular rare reward.\n\n## Winning order affects reward opportunities\n\nThe Munchkin Prize Parade rewards consecutive victories. Magic Keys are linked to first-try wins, while the Gem Bazaar describes rewards for success on the first, second, or third attempt.\n\nThese are different incentives. A reader who enjoys perfecting first attempts may value them differently from someone who prefers repeated low-pressure experimentation.\n\nExtra items can also become Emerald Points for a leaderboard. The supplied text does not establish exchange rates, prize value, or how many systems are mandatory to follow the main story.\n\n## Paid random items need their own boundary\n\nThe US download is free with optional purchases, including random items. Drop rates are said to be available in-game, but were not inspected here.\n\nNo specific reward, profit, or improvement in success is guaranteed by buying a random item. Current prices, free-versus-paid collection access, and restoration terms remain unresolved.\n\nAdvertising, offline support, and current stability are also unknown. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to corroborate story pacing or fair difficulty.\n\nConsider Magic Match when the Oz characters and collection systems add meaning to match-3. Readers wanting only a clean isolated board may find its many stated reward destinations less compelling, without this analysis claiming they are intrusive in actual play.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMagic Match supplies unusually concrete character and reward mechanics for a licensed puzzle. Its appeal is matching plus Oz collections, while access costs and the value of those many currencies remain unverified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How do character Medallions help?\n\nThe listing says dropping them to the bottom brings a character to assist on the current level.\n\n### Does infinite level mean an endless campaign?\n\nNo. It is separately described as a way to earn story points.\n\n### Are random purchases disclosed?\n\nYes. Rates are said to be available in-game but were not independently inspected.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/the-wizard-of-oz-magic-match-3/id1073286211?uo=4)."
