    "bodyMarkdown": "# Color by Number : Calm Art: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nColor by Number : Calm Art turns a blank numbered image into a finished picture through assigned colors. The reader chooses an illustration rather than inventing its outlines or color relationships from scratch.\n\n## A numbered region is the target\n\nThe developer says to tap blanks by number and build a collection of completed art. Animals, architecture, national customs, flowers, fantasy, and other categories are advertised.\n\nThe first official image shows a bird among flowers, a numbered palette, and comparison-style picture panels. It supports guided coloring without establishing unrestricted recoloring or freehand brush tools.\n\nThe exact wrong-color behavior, zoom range, and size of tiny regions are not documented. Attractive promotional art is not an independent legibility or accessibility test.\n\n## The catalog claim has an access qualification\n\nThe listing advertises up to three thousand pictures and daily artwork updates, with wording that says all are free. Later, VIP is described as unlocking all video pictures.\n\nThose statements leave a distinction between ordinary free access and video-gated content. They do not independently establish whether each picture can be opened without payment by watching a video.\n\nNo catalog was audited, and no update schedule was observed. The total should remain an attributed figure rather than a promise that every category receives new pages daily.\n\n## VIP specifies one type of ad removal\n\nThe subscription says it removes non-rewarded ads. That wording is narrower than eliminating every video or promotional opportunity.\n\nVIP also advertises unlimited hints and access to video pictures. These are entitlement claims, not proof that free users have no hints or that every available tool is unrestricted under membership.\n\nThe description quotes $4.99 weekly, $9.99 monthly, and $69.99 yearly after a three-day trial, with automatic renewal. Current checkout prices, trial eligibility, and restoration were not independently tested.\n\n## Select for guided completion\n\nMusic and one-tap sharing are advertised, but the material does not describe offline access, automatic color switching, or recovery of unfinished pages. A no-pencil requirement is not the same as a no-internet promise.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample to support claims that progress saves reliably or the ad load is light. The calming pitch is a design intention rather than evidence of clinical stress relief.\n\nThe US download is free. Consider Calm Art if predetermined artwork and number-guided completion appeal, while treating page gates and the precise distinction between rewarded and non-rewarded ads as important access questions rather than technicalities.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCalm Art gives a clear numbered-coloring premise and a broad category list. Its VIP description is more precise than its all-free introduction, especially about removing only non-rewarded advertising.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is it freehand drawing?\n\nNo freehand tool is established. The documented action fills supplied numbered regions.\n\n### Does VIP promise to remove every video?\n\nIt explicitly removes non-rewarded ads, which is narrower than every possible video.\n\n### Are the listed subscription prices verified at checkout?\n\nNo. They are amounts quoted in the supplied description, not a completed purchase check.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/color-by-number-calm-art/id1577292561?uo=4)."
