    "bodyMarkdown": "# Neon Lines: Tap & Clear: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nNeon Lines: Tap & Clear gives arrows a state as well as a direction. A gray arrow first needs energy; once blue, it is ready to be sent off the board.\n\n## Charging and leaving are different actions\n\nThe developer explains the sequence clearly: tap a golden arrow to release an energy pulse, transfer power to inactive gray arrows, then tap blue arrows to clear them.\n\nThat is more specific than a generic tap-away puzzle where every visible arrow is already eligible to exit. The order of activation can change which choices remain available.\n\nThe official image shows bent arrow paths, several golden segments, and pale blue routes on a dark background. It supports the neon circuit theme without demonstrating every possible energy interaction.\n\n## Direction creates a second constraint\n\nThe description says arrows can block one another and a wrong move may lock the grid. A charged arrow therefore is not automatically a safe choice simply because it has become blue.\n\nThe exact transfer range, pulse behavior at intersections, and whether an arrow can recharge another more than once are not documented fully. A correct explanation of the basic sequence should not expand into an invented complete walkthrough.\n\nThe text also does not define a restart allowance, undo, hints, or whether a locked state consumes a life. The pictured indicators alone cannot settle those consequences.\n\n## No time limit is an explicit promise\n\nThe listing says there are no time limits and promotes simple one-tap controls. That supports a deliberate sequence-planning proposition, not evidence of unrestricted mistakes or pressure-free scoring.\n\nHundreds of stages and increasingly intricate layouts are advertised without an audited count or hands-on difficulty assessment. Early relaxation and later challenge remain developer descriptions.\n\nSci-fi micro-sounds, energy charges, and smooth glowing animation are also claimed. No audio, haptic, frame-rate, or visual-comfort test was conducted, and luminous effects may not suit every viewer equally.\n\n## A focused mechanic with open access questions\n\nThe US download is free. Ad frequency, purchases, removal offers, offline play, saving, and present stability are unresolved. The phrase play anywhere does not establish disconnected operation.\n\nThere are no customer review bodies in this sample to corroborate intuitive state feedback or fair recovery after an incorrect activation.\n\nConsider Neon Lines if planning power flow before releasing arrows sounds more engaging than clearing already active paths. It is less suitable as a confirmed unlimited-retry recommendation or a game for someone who wants no additional state to track beyond arrow direction.\n\n## Verdict\n\nNeon Lines has a concrete two-step distinction: energize arrows before clearing them. Its documented state changes give the puzzle identity, while recovery and commercial conditions remain unspecified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can gray arrows leave immediately?\n\nThe listing says they must be charged and turn blue first.\n\n### What do golden arrows do?\n\nThey release energy pulses when tapped, according to the developer.\n\n### Does no timer mean no failure state?\n\nNo. The description separately warns that a wrong move can lock the grid.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/neon-lines-tap-clear/id6760103862?uo=4)."
