    "bodyMarkdown": "# Homicide Squad: Hidden Objects: Player Review Analysis\n\n*Player-review analysis, not a playtest. Sources: the US App Store listing and 50 US reviews dated 2026-02-05–2026-10-31 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.35.6800 and 2.36.6900. This limited, self-selected sample is not representative; player reports are not independently verified.*\n\nHomicide Squad: Hidden Objects places object searches and match-3 inside New York murder investigations. Detectives Turino and Lamonte provide the story frame, with clues, suspects, and laboratory work extending the case.\n\n## A scene has a stated mode choice\n\nThe developer invites readers to choose hidden-object searching or gem matching for a scene. Interrogation and analysis are advertised around that puzzle choice.\n\nThe official image shows a detailed interior above a pictured object strip. It supports visual searching without independently verifying every promised case interaction or the depth of suspect conversations.\n\nA reviewer likes the variety of play, and another specifically enjoys searching against a time limit and the story. Those are concrete reasons to prefer the investigation wrapper over an anonymous object list.\n\n## Time pressure has both fans and critics\n\nA September reviewer finds later limits too short for casual enjoyment. Another wants more time to inspect the objects. These do not erase the supporter who likes racing to find everything.\n\nThe exact allowance and mode progression were not tested. The app should not be labeled universally untimed, and difficulty complaints do not prove malicious level design.\n\n## Repeated searches can slow the case\n\nThe latest detailed reviewer quits after the first case because it requires too many returns to the same map. Another reaches the end of main cases and sees no fresh continuation.\n\nThese are two different content problems: repetition before a resolution and a lack of new destinations after completion. The developer’s regular-update promise does not independently resolve either.\n\nA July account also describes slow or paused hints, while an October reviewer reports a delayed city-map response. These are unverified performance reports rather than a reproduced timing benchmark.\n\n## Subscriptions target distinct resources\n\nThe listing quotes monthly Bonus Tokens at $9.99, a daily thirty-minute Energy Freeze at $19.99, and faster energy restoration at $14.99. A trial is explicitly described for the first two, not all three.\n\nThose are supplied prices rather than verified checkout terms. Energy Freeze is not a claim that every scene timer freezes too; the benefit concerns energy spending.\n\n## Ad removal may be temporary\n\nAn October reviewer reports a paid removal offer lasting sixty days. Earlier titles say no ads, while later comments describe fullscreen interruptions. Current coverage and duration were not independently inspected.\n\nThe US download is free, and offline play is advertised. Present disconnected coverage, restoration, and stability remain unverified. Consider Homicide Squad for crime-themed scene variety if repeated searches and time pressure suit the experience you want.\n\n## Verdict\n\nHomicide Squad has supporters of its story and timed object searching. Case repetition and different resource subscriptions are its most useful selection details, with temporary ad-removal reports kept distinct from a permanent unlock.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can matching substitute for object search?\n\nThe developer advertises a choice for scenes, without a complete audit of event exceptions.\n\n### Does Energy Freeze stop the scene clock?\n\nThat is not established. The listed benefit is thirty minutes without spending energy.\n\n### Is ad removal proven permanent?\n\nNo. One reviewer reports a sixty-day offer; current terms were not checked.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/homicide-squad-hidden-objects/id1179007417?uo=4)."
