    "bodyMarkdown": "# Monkey Wrench - Word Search: iOS Listing Analysis\n\n*Store-listing analysis, not a playtest. This article examines the US App Store description and attributed product claims. Customer review sample: N=0; no review date or version window is available.*\n\nMonkey Wrench - Word Search asks you to identify an answer before tracing it. Category knowledge and a hexagonal letter path work together, with difficulty changing how much answer information is shown.\n\n## A category is not a complete word list\n\nThe developer names movies, science, food, sports, music, history, and other subjects. Categories at the top can be swiped sideways to reveal more.\n\nSome hex tiles are shaded as starting-letter cues. Trace or tap connected tiles to spell a category answer; accepted answers remove their tiles and bring the grid closer to empty.\n\nThe official image shows a phone with a hexagonal matrix, category text, and a cartoon monkey. It supports connected paths, not an ordinary rectangular word search where each answer is always a straight line.\n\n## Difficulty changes the supplied information\n\nEasy shows the full answers to find. Medium gives only first letters of the words, while Hard supplies blanks.\n\nThat is a concrete difference in knowledge demand rather than merely increasing the number of tiles. On Easy, visual location is more central; on Hard, identifying a plausible category answer becomes additional work.\n\nThe listing says a puzzle can be reset to move between difficulties. It does not establish that doing so preserves all partial progress, awards, or timing records.\n\n## Hints reveal different kinds of help\n\nThe advertised options can show vowels, the last letter, or a location in the grid. Those are distinct nudges: one may help identify the answer, while another helps locate a word already suspected.\n\nHint costs, supplies, and refill rules are not fully described. The existence of several types should not be rewritten as unlimited free assistance.\n\nThe supplied text also does not establish every path restriction, answer dictionary, wrong-guess penalty, or compulsory clock. Hex adjacency is documented; a full mathematical path rulebook is not.\n\n## The free boundary is comparatively explicit\n\nThe app advertises an initial pack of twenty-five puzzles and the three most recent daily puzzles. More than a thousand additional puzzles are offered for purchase.\n\nThat is a useful limit for a reader who wants to understand what free means. It does not establish current pack prices or that the complete daily archive is freely available.\n\nThe US download is free. Advertising, removal offers, offline support, saving, and present stability remain unresolved. There are no customer review bodies in this sample to corroborate clue fairness or dependable daily delivery.\n\nConsider Monkey Wrench if recognizing category answers is as appealing as tracing them. Readers wanting only a supplied word list can use the advertised Easy format rather than assume all difficulties ask the same task.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMonkey Wrench has a clearly explained difficulty system and useful targeted hints. Its free-pack and recent-daily boundaries are more concrete than a generic free-word-game promise, while clue quality remains unreviewed by a player sample.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What changes across difficulties?\n\nEasy shows full answers, Medium shows first letters, and Hard shows blanks.\n\n### Is the board rectangular?\n\nThe listing explicitly describes connected hexagonal tiles.\n\n### Is the whole catalog free?\n\nNo such claim is supported. A free twenty-five-puzzle pack and three recent dailies are described, with additional packs for purchase.\n\n[US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/monkey-wrench-word-search/id999022137?uo=4)."
