    "bodyMarkdown": "# Tile Dynasty: Triple Matching With a Divided Sense of Calm\n\nTile Dynasty borrows Mahjong imagery but asks for triples, not the pairs associated with solitaire Mahjong. Reviewers can find that change relaxing while still objecting to what surrounds the board. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Match three, not a traditional Mahjong hand\n\nThe developer describes clearing three tiles of the same color, advancing through arrangements, and unlocking assistance. Landscapes and an ancient-dynasty theme frame the progression.\n\nThe description does not specify the exact collection capacity, tile-access restrictions, or every powerup. A seven-slot tray and standard solitaire edge rules should not be assumed. The meaningful documented decision is choosing tiles toward a triple instead of treating every familiar Mahjong symbol as a pair.\n\n## Enjoyment survives alongside interruptions\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-07-15 through 2026-10-31 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.44.29, 2.44.30, 2.44.31, 2.44.32, 2.75.00, 2.75.01, 2.75.02; the listing records version 2.75.02. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers describe a pleasant after-work routine or a satisfying combination of relaxation and challenge. Another calls the triple format a welcome change of pace. These are preferences, not proof that the game improves memory or sleep.\n\nLonger accounts report stuck advertisements, black screens, crashes, or losing progress when switching phones. One veteran describes waiting for daily challenges while finding later levels harder and assistance less generous. That is a historical account, not a verified current challenge schedule.\n\n## Free play does not settle the ad question\n\nSeveral reviewers object to ads between games; others describe interruptions during a level or difficulty closing them. Their exact durations are individual reports, not a measured average.\n\nA reviewer unable to purchase removal does not establish that no removal offer exists for anyone. Current pricing, restoration terms, and offline availability are unverified. Reports that later stages are impossible without many videos likewise do not independently prove an unsolvable board design.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTile Dynasty is relevant to readers who want familiar tile recognition with three-at-a-time clearing. It is a cautious recommendation for someone whose main requirement is uninterrupted quiet or dependable cross-device continuity. The sample supports genuine enthusiasm without erasing the importance of transitions and recovery.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this pair matching?\nNo. The listing specifies matching three tiles.\n\n### Is the collection limit documented?\nNot in the supplied description.\n\n### Are memory benefits demonstrated?\nNo. Player impressions and marketing are not a measured outcome.\n\n### Is progress transfer verified?\nNo. One reviewer reports starting over after changing phones.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/tile-dynasty-triple-mahjong/id6499129174?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
