    "bodyMarkdown": "# Cook & Merge: A Beachside Story With Competing Demands\n\nCook & Merge gives its food combinations a narrative purpose: help Kate protect Bakers Valley and its restaurants. The sample's most interesting disagreement is whether the surrounding events make that routine richer or harder to enjoy. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Ingredients serve the town's story\n\nThe listing describes combining ingredients into dishes such as cakes, pies, and burgers, discovering recipes, and restoring buildings. Kate's conflict with Rex Hunter and Granny's secret-agent background supply the story hook.\n\nWeekly and seasonal events extend the food-merging loop. The description does not establish a precise merge count, every generator rule, or freeform renovation choices. One reviewer specifically wants more control over café appearance, which cautions against equating renovation with unrestricted design.\n\n## Routine pleasure versus competing obligations\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-08-10 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 0.76.10, 0.77.10, 0.78.5, 0.79.7, 0.80.10, 0.80.6, 0.80.8; the listing records version 0.80.10. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nA longtime reviewer describes a morning-coffee habit and appreciates ongoing development. Others enjoy the merge variety and extra activities. One says energy remains obtainable, while another says it runs out quickly with limited recovery unless paying.\n\nThose conflicting accounts should remain separate. Session length, goals, and event participation may affect the experience, but this evidence does not isolate a cause.\n\nDetailed complaints concern pointing hands, waving customers, unclear icons, and event requirements interfering with a desire to follow the main story. These are not all the same issue as external advertising: an internal tutorial cue or event offer can distract without being a video ad.\n\n## Ads and outside rewards are different questions\n\nOne reviewer recalls only optional extra-reward videos before noticing ads when opening the game. Others report long ads or a particular in-stream format freezing. Current frequency is not independently measured.\n\nComplaints naming third-party reward platforms do not establish an official cash payout program operated by Cook & Merge. No earnings are promised here. Current purchase prices, offline access, and restoration terms are also unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCook & Merge is most relevant to readers who enjoy accumulating food discoveries while following a town-saving story. It is less appealing on this evidence for someone wanting undistracted merging with no event pressure. The sample's warmth is real, but so is its disagreement about energy and how much activity is welcome.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What supports the renovation story?\nCombining ingredients into dishes and progressing through Bakers Valley.\n\n### Is every decoration freely selectable?\nThat is not established.\n\n### Do all reviewers agree about energy?\nNo. Accounts conflict about its availability.\n\n### Are outside reward-app payouts verified?\nNo. Those complaints do not establish this game's own earnings program.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cook-merge-merge-games/id1620904768?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
