    "bodyMarkdown": "# Ball Match Lab: Sorting Top Balls With Room to Reconsider\n\nBall Match Lab explains its sorting rules more clearly than many short puzzle listings. The central constraint is not just color: the destination also needs enough room. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## One exposed ball at a time\n\nThe developer says to tap a bottle to select its top ball, then tap another bottle to transfer it. A destination must have the same color on top and sufficient capacity. Sorting matching colors into their own bottles is the stated goal.\n\nThat makes access order important. A desired ball below another cannot simply be chosen directly under the documented top-ball rule. Moving something now can change which color becomes available next.\n\nThe description does not separately explain every empty-bottle exception or give a fixed capacity. Those omissions should not be filled with assumptions from another ball-sort app.\n\n## Recovery is part of the pitch\n\nUndo is explicitly advertised, along with hints. The listing also claims no timer and no penalties, positioning the game around reconsidering moves rather than racing a countdown.\n\nThose statements are useful for understanding intended play, but they do not specify whether assistance has limits, costs, or an advertising condition. No penalties does not necessarily mean every helper is unlimited.\n\nMultiple modes are mentioned without a detailed breakdown. A reader should not expect a particular challenge format merely because the general feature list uses the plural.\n\n## An offline claim, not an access audit\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises play without a network connection. That is stronger wording than a vague play-anywhere slogan, yet it remains a listing claim here rather than a tested finding.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. It does not resolve ad frequency, removal products, purchase pricing, or whether every auxiliary feature works offline. Nor does the description demonstrate the cognitive and stress-reduction benefits it promotes.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about difficulty, visual clarity, repetition, or the usefulness of undo. Screenshots were not visually inspected, so specific ball colors and tube layouts are not described as observations.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBall Match Lab looks relevant to readers who enjoy planning around exposed pieces, same-color destinations, and limited space. Its advertised undo and untimed approach may appeal to patient solvers. It is less conclusive for someone needing verified unrestricted hints or a particular empty-bottle rule. The core is documented; the assistance economy is not.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can a buried ball be selected directly?\nThe instructions specify the top ball.\n\n### Does capacity matter?\nYes. The destination must have enough space.\n\n### Is undo advertised?\nYes, without complete entitlement details.\n\n### Is offline play independently confirmed?\nNo. It is explicitly claimed by the developer, not tested here.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/ball-match-lab/id6479984627?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
