    "bodyMarkdown": "# Goods Sort: Clear Triples Quickly, but Read the Timer Claim\n\nGoods Sort presents shopping as a rapid matching problem: locate three identical products across shelves. Its listing explicitly describes strict time limits, even though one sampled reviewer believes the game is untimed. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Three products under time pressure\n\nShinrays describes selecting three identical items from different shelves to eliminate them, increasing score and earning rewards. Fruit, vegetables, candy, and toys provide the published product categories.\n\nThe description does not fully explain item movement, shelf capacity, or every obstruction. Strategies built around an assumed fixed number of spare shelves would go beyond the evidence.\n\nThe important distinction is that this is not merely arranging attractive shelves indefinitely. A deadline is part of the documented challenge.\n\n## Visual changes divide established players\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-22 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 5.80, 5.90, 6.00; the listing records version 6.00. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers find the game easy to see and enjoy its increasing challenge. Others dislike redesigned items or movement, despite having previously enjoyed the game.\n\nClaims that the art is AI-generated are not independently verified. The editorial point supported by the sample is that a presentation change affected recognition and attachment for those players.\n\nSeveral accounts say a round ends with an out-of-moves or no-space message despite an apparent move remaining. Those reports warrant caution without proving deliberate cheating. One reviewer updates an earlier difficulty complaint to say a developer change appears to have helped them progress.\n\n## Advertising and the clock interact\n\nA player praising no timer conflicts with both the listing and multiple accounts of tight deadlines. Different versions or activities may contribute, but the evidence does not resolve the discrepancy.\n\nReviewers also distinguish general ads, rewarded extensions, and videos needed to open shelves. Some paid-removal purchasers report continuing video requirements; another perceives fewer ads overall. Current entitlement terms and frequency were not checked.\n\n## Verdict\n\nGoods Sort is most relevant to readers who enjoy quick object recognition and triple matching. It is less convincing for someone specifically avoiding clocks or depending on a fixed visual style. The sample preserves enjoyable readability and reported improvements alongside premature-failure concerns and frustration with assistance.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many identical products clear?\nThree, according to the listing.\n\n### Is untimed play established?\nNo. The listing explicitly describes time limits.\n\n### Are failure-message complaints proof of rigging?\nNo. They are unverified player reports.\n\n### Does paid removal guarantee no reward videos?\nThat entitlement is not established here.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/goods-sort-triple-master-3d/id1658958534?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
