    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Garden: Letter Connections With a Collection Ceiling\n\nWord Garden gives letter searching a garden-themed frame and a second reward loop through collectible pictures. For some established reviewers, finishing those collections changes the appeal even when the word puzzles remain enjoyable. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Trace a word, then use the crossings\n\nThe developer describes sliding across letters to form words. A crossword arrangement provides information about missing answers, while extra accepted words earn bonus coins.\n\nHints reveal a letter. More than 9,000 puzzles, eight languages, daily grids, and offline play are advertised. None of those claims establishes a particular dictionary's treatment of every regional word or spelling.\n\nThis is not a conventional clue-written crossword. The documented process begins with supplied letters and possible words, with the board providing constraints.\n\n## Gentle beginnings do not settle long-term difficulty\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-03-01 through 2026-11-07 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.12.1, 3.12.2, 3.14, 3.15.1, 3.15.2, 3.16.2, 3.17.8; the listing records version 3.17.8. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts describe daily entertainment, thinking through words, or a relaxed bedtime routine. Some newcomers specifically want the challenge to become harder. That supports different difficulty preferences, not a universal claim that the game is easy.\n\nOne reviewer likes the large readable tiles. No screenshot was inspected to assess contrast or confirm the current layout independently. Claims about improved memory, language skills, or sleep remain personal impressions rather than demonstrated benefits.\n\n## Collections can outlast their rewards\n\nA longtime purchaser says completing all the stamps reduced the reward appeal. Another reports completed collections and a missing seasonal event. These are distinct from running out of ordinary word grids: the sample does not establish that every kind of content has ended.\n\nAn interruption complaint also appears. A purchase described as no ads is not enough to verify current removal pricing or every surviving promotion. Offline support is explicitly claimed by the listing but was not tested, and stability is not independently assessed.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWord Garden is relevant to readers who enjoy building words from a small letter set and using crossings to narrow the answer. Its collection layer may provide another reason to return, but the sample suggests that layer can lose momentum for established players. Readers choosing for a specific dictionary or permanent new rewards need firmer evidence.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How are words entered?\nBy sliding a finger across letters.\n\n### What do hints reveal?\nA letter on the board, according to the listing.\n\n### Do completed collections prove no word puzzles remain?\nNo. Those are different content systems.\n\n### Is offline operation tested here?\nNo. It remains an explicit developer claim.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/word-garden-crosswords/id1291854522?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
