    "bodyMarkdown": "# Picture Cross: Nonogram Scenes Behind a Disputed Token System\n\nPicture Cross organizes nonograms into larger themed scenes. Its sample repeatedly separates affection for the puzzles from frustration about obtaining enough tokens to open them. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Reveal a picture through grid deductions\n\nThe listing identifies the game as nonograms, also called Hanjie or Griddlers, and advertises classic and color variants across different sizes and difficulties. In this puzzle family, row and column clues constrain which cells are filled; that is genre context, not an inspection of every variant here.\n\nA tutorial and guide are advertised. More than 12,000 puzzles and eighty themed packs are product claims rather than an independently counted catalog.\n\n## A scene can give individual grids a purpose\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-06-28 through 2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 7.0, 7.1.0, 7.2.1, 7.3.0; the listing records version 7.3.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nOne longtime reviewer likes the larger themed-picture structure and finds replay valuable. Another wants a reset option after completing available material. These accounts show sustained attachment, not merely introductory approval.\n\nOthers report confusion with color or arcade-style material despite experience with ordinary nonograms. An advertised guide does not prove that every player finds a particular variant adequately explained.\n\n## Coins and tokens are not interchangeable\n\nSeveral reviewers describe plentiful coins but insufficient tokens. Their suggestion that coins should buy tokens is a request, not confirmation that conversion is currently available.\n\nReports also conflict about earning tokens through replay. One says it works, while others say rewards changed or stopped. The evidence should not be turned into a reliable present-day token-farming guide.\n\nThe listing acknowledges optional payments to unlock content more quickly. Reviewers discuss surveys, disqualifications, and personal questions as another access route; these are accounts, not a privacy audit.\n\n## Ads also have a change history\n\nSome reviewers describe tolerable ads, while others report disruptive mid-puzzle formats. One reports improvement after contacting the developer about audio ads. No universal frequency, current removal entitlement, or offline guarantee follows from those mixed experiences.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPicture Cross is relevant to readers who enjoy connecting small logic grids into a larger themed discovery. It is a cautious fit for someone expecting unrestricted sessions or one simple currency. The puzzles inspire long-term loyalty, but access rules and variant guidance materially shape the experience described here.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What puzzle family is this?\nNonograms, with classic and color variants advertised.\n\n### Are coins the same as tokens?\nReviewers describe them as different resources.\n\n### Is replay guaranteed to earn tokens?\nNo. Reports conflict and describe changes.\n\n### Are all variants independently explained here?\nNo. Some players report difficulty finding suitable guidance.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/picture-cross/id977150768?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
