    "bodyMarkdown": "# Puzzle Games: Jigsaw Puzzles: Personal Pictures, Practical Friction\n\nPuzzle Games: Jigsaw Puzzles offers both a picture library and the option to turn personal photos into puzzles. Reviewers appreciate the images while raising concrete questions about organizing pieces and returning to a session. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Piece count and rotation change the task\n\nThe listing describes four difficulty levels, optional piece rotation, border-only filtering, adjustable backgrounds, and multiple puzzles in progress. A mystery format withholds the complete picture, while coins unlock additional puzzles.\n\nThose features address different needs. More pieces increase sorting work; rotation adds orientation; an edge filter narrows the visible selection. The description does not give a complete current piece-count table or every custom-photo entitlement.\n\n## Enjoyable variety is not effortless handling\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-10-04 through 2026-11-03 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 10.0.0, 10.0.1, 10.1.0, 10.2.0, 10.3.0, 10.5.0, 10.7.0, 10.7.1, 10.8.0, 9.2.1, 9.3.0, 9.4.0, 9.4.2, 9.6.0, 9.8.0; the listing records version 10.8.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers praise original pictures, a balance of challenge and relaxation, and the ability to personalize puzzles. Another likes choosing among different piece counts rather than treating the largest puzzle as automatically best.\n\nMore specific complaints concern a transparent piece area overlapping the picture, sorted pieces becoming mixed after returning, and unclear helper descriptions. A player discovering edge mode only after years illustrates a discoverability issue rather than proving the feature was absent.\n\nNo screenshot was inspected, so these remain reports about visibility and controls, not an independent accessibility assessment.\n\n## Returning to the app can interrupt more than a puzzle\n\nSeveral accounts say background music, videos, or audiobooks stop when opening the puzzle. Others describe ad interruptions or a failed video gate when using personal images.\n\nThe latter matters because an advertised feature may still have an access condition. It does not establish that all custom puzzles are permanently unavailable or paid.\n\nCurrent removal pricing, offline support, and cross-device restoration are unverified. One request for a collection search bar is likewise not evidence that a search feature already exists.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis app is relevant to readers who want a conventional jigsaw framework with rotation, edge filtering, and personal images. It is more cautious for someone depending on uninterrupted background audio or persistent manual piece sorting. The sample's appreciation of picture variety remains meaningful alongside those very practical reservations.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can personal photos become puzzles?\nThat feature is advertised and discussed by reviewers.\n\n### Is rotation required?\nThe listing describes it as an optional mode.\n\n### Is an edge-only filter advertised?\nYes, although one reviewer struggled to discover it.\n\n### Is background-audio compatibility verified?\nNo. Several reviewers report interruptions.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/puzzle-games-jigsaw-puzzles/id1510189987?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
