    "bodyMarkdown": "# Take Away: Directional Cubes and a Disputed Change in Forgiveness\n\nTake Away asks players to find cubes whose arrow directions lead to an exit. Its sample repeatedly describes a change in how mistakes are counted, making the same spatial task feel less forgiving to established players. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Read the arrow, then inspect another side\n\nThe developer says cubes leave only along their arrow direction. Rotating and zooming the arrangement helps identify movable pieces, while bombs provide assistance.\n\nThe listing describes clearing a board within limited moves. Story scenes add item collection and rebuilding projects such as a farm or living room. That is an extra progression frame, not evidence of a fully open construction game.\n\nThe description does not fully document every current error, life, and move allowance.\n\n## A change in counting changes the mood\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-10-16 through 2026-09-04 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.4.90, 1.5.50, 1.5.60, 1.5.70; the listing records version 1.5.70. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers enjoy a manageable challenge or like how the story leads into more puzzles. Others describe a move budget being replaced by a small allowance for errors. Those are reported changes, not independently verified universal rules.\n\nThe distinction matters: estimating total efficient moves is different from avoiding accidental taps on a dense arrangement. Reviewers specifically connect the latter to finger size and visibility.\n\nSeveral dislike bombs clearing pieces they wanted to remove themselves. Others object to altered themes or extra visual activity. Assistance can reduce challenge while still making a favorite routine less appealing.\n\n## Story availability and ads are separate limits\n\nSome players report finishing available stories and waiting for additions. Their waits do not establish that development is abandoned or identify a current total.\n\nAdvertising complaints concern midgame breaks, reward choices that still lead to an ad, and difficult exits. A reviewer who cannot find paid removal does not prove that every account lacks an offer. Current prices, offline access, and stability remain unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTake Away is relevant to readers who enjoy viewing an object from several angles and choosing a removal order. It is less conclusive for someone expecting the older error tolerance or minimal presentation described by longtime players. The mechanics, visual changes, and story pace all affect the calm experience the listing promotes.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Can cubes leave in any direction?\nNo. The listing restricts them to their arrows.\n\n### Are rotate and zoom advertised?\nYes, to inspect the arrangement.\n\n### Is the present error allowance independently verified?\nNo. Reviewers report changes, while the listing describes limited moves.\n\n### Does a bomb necessarily improve everyone's experience?\nNo. Some reviewers prefer clearing the pieces themselves.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/take-away-tap-3d-blocks-out/id1664365966?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
