    "bodyMarkdown": "# Maze Madness: Directional Exits, Not a Conventional Maze Walk\n\nMaze Madness calls its boards mazes, but the published instructions focus on removing pieces along fixed directions. That makes escape order more important than steering a character through corridors. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## An exit can depend on another removal\n\nThe developer says tapping releases a piece along its path, with each piece moving in only one direction. Removing an outer layer opens the way for pieces deeper in the arrangement.\n\nThis describes an unblock-style dependency problem. A sensible reading is to inspect routes before committing, rather than assuming the title means tracing one continuous line from entrance to exit.\n\nThe description does not give exact collision consequences, mistake allowances, or whether a blocked attempt consumes a resource. Those rules remain open rather than being borrowed from other arrow games.\n\n## Grid lines are a planning aid\n\nZoom is advertised for tight spots, and grid lines can be toggled to align the view. They serve different purposes: magnification makes a detail larger, while alignment helps compare positions along a route.\n\nThat pairing may interest readers who enjoy inspecting a dense arrangement deliberately. It does not independently establish ideal tap targets, comfortable contrast, or complete accessibility for any particular visual need.\n\nThe source describes glowing lines against a dark background, but no screenshot was inspected. The appearance is therefore attributed to the listing, not described as a firsthand observation.\n\n## Artwork gives clearing a destination\n\nThe developer advertises more than 3,000 levels involving familiar artwork, sculptures, animals, and abstract shapes. Revealing an image supplies a reward beyond an empty board.\n\nThese references do not prove museum-grade reproductions, art-history lessons, or verified licensing. The advertised catalog was not independently counted. Nor are claims about sharpening IQ established by the supplied evidence.\n\n## No timer is explicit; other access terms are not\n\nThe listing clearly promotes no timer. It does not provide enough detail to certify unlimited assistance, offline operation, or a particular advertising model.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about challenge, repetition, comfort, or reliability. The metadata records a free download, not proof that every surrounding feature is unrestricted.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMaze Madness looks relevant to readers who enjoy patiently releasing interdependent pieces and watching an image emerge. It is less conclusive for someone expecting traditional maze navigation or verified penalty-free tapping. Zoom and grid tools are concrete advertised aids, while their practical usability remains untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is a walking character described?\nNo. The instructions concern tapping pieces out.\n\n### Can pieces move in any direction?\nThe listing says each moves in one direction.\n\n### Are grid lines advertised?\nYes, as an alignment aid.\n\n### Is this verified offline?\nNo. Offline operation is not established here.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/maze-madness-brain-puzzle/id6756872090?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
