    "bodyMarkdown": "# Zen Tiles: Three-Tile Sets and Two Different Meanings of Easy\n\nZen Tiles puts three-tile matching inside a meditation-island progression. Its sample shows that gentle play can be a selling point for one reader and the reason another loses interest. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Triples change the Mahjong expectation\n\nThe developer describes clearing three identical Mahjong tiles, using boosters, and adding elements to an island as levels are completed. This is not a full traditional Mahjong ruleset or ordinary pair solitaire.\n\nMore than 10,000 boards, haptics, monthly additions, and offline play are advertised. The source does not fully explain the selection capacity, accessible-layer rules, or booster effects.\n\nAn island that evolves with progress also does not establish unrestricted landscaping. The documented reward is a visual representation of completed puzzles.\n\n## Calm and challenge are preferences, not contradictions to erase\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-22 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.9.0, 3.0.0, 3.1.0; the listing records version 3.1.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers enjoy playing at their own pace and describe calming music. Others find the triples too easy or too tame, while another says the boards are challenging.\n\nThose opinions can coexist without concluding that an age group automatically needs an easier puzzle. One reviewer identifying as a senior explicitly wants more difficulty.\n\nClaims about memory, focus, or general wellbeing remain personal impressions and promotional language, not independently demonstrated benefits.\n\n## Money expectations are not verified game rules\n\nSeveral reviews refer to earning money, cash-out goals, or changing requirements. The supplied store description does not document a cash program or identify the terms behind those expectations.\n\nIt would be misleading to promise earnings, certify a payout scheme, or conclude from these comments alone that the developer operates the described reward arrangement. Accounts mentioning success and failure remain unverified.\n\nAd reports also conflict: one says they are tolerable, while others describe repeated midgame interruptions. Current removal products and prices are unknown. The explicit offline claim was not tested.\n\n## Verdict\n\nZen Tiles is relevant to readers who like triples and a gentle progression setting. It is less persuasive for someone seeking consistently demanding logic or a verified money-making activity. The best-supported appeal is the matching routine; the sample leaves difficulty preferences divided and financial expectations unresolved.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many tiles make a set?\nThree identical tiles.\n\n### Is the island a verified freeform builder?\nNo. The listing describes growth through puzzle progress.\n\n### Are earnings established by the listing?\nNo. The sampled money-related comments do not establish a documented cash program.\n\n### Do all reviewers find it challenging?\nNo. Several specifically find it too easy.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/zen-tiles-mahjong-match/id6744312872?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
