    "bodyMarkdown": "# Merge Mayor: Building a Town Around Space and Recharge Limits\n\nMerge Mayor makes item combinations serve a growing town. Its sample suggests that satisfaction comes from watching that larger project develop, while space limits and surrounding events can make a familiar routine feel crowded. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Pair items to move the town forward\n\nThe developer explicitly identifies merge-two puzzles, crafting, repairs, new areas, and relationships with townspeople. Assistant Olivia frames the player's mayoral role.\n\nSeasonal events and additional modes extend the main progression. The description does not establish that every building can be freely positioned or that every upgrade is purely decorative.\n\nA city-building theme should therefore be understood alongside the item-merging requirements, not as a promise of an unrestricted construction sandbox.\n\n## Long attachment does not imply frictionless sessions\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-02-09 through 2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 5.1.635, 5.10.677, 5.10.678, 5.11.688, 5.11.689, 5.11.690, 5.12.697, 5.13.705, 5.13.708, 5.14.714, 5.2.640, 5.2.641, 5.2.642, 5.3.644, 5.4.649, 5.5.653, 5.5.654, 5.5.656, 5.6.660, 5.7.662, 5.8.667, 5.9.672; the listing records version 5.14.714. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers describe years of consistent enjoyment. One reports completing available zones without spending and appreciates the absence of forced ads.\n\nOthers describe long generator recharges or board-space pressure. A recent account says a holding queue was reduced after an update. This is a specific reported inventory problem, not independent verification of every current queue limit.\n\nEvent notifications and unclear task organization also draw complaints. A player can enjoy the core merging while wanting fewer screens between opening the app and reaching it.\n\n## Ads, artwork, and support require attribution\n\nThe listing discloses paid items, possible randomized purchases, and advertising. One reviewer praises optional reward videos while another objects to long videos for bonuses. Optional placement does not guarantee a short or reliable ad.\n\nRepeated complaints characterize sticker art as AI-generated. No provenance audit was performed; the supported finding is that the perceived art direction alienates those reviewers.\n\nSupport experiences differ too: one account describes quick recovery help, while others report loading or lost-progress problems. Offline access and current purchase prices are unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMerge Mayor is relevant to readers who like a long-lived merge-two project with a town-level payoff. It is less suited on this evidence to someone expecting uninterrupted generator access or minimal event screens. The sample retains strong loyalty while showing how storage, pacing, and presentation can change that relationship.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is merge-two explicitly advertised?\nYes.\n\n### Is freeform building placement established?\nNo. Town growth does not by itself confirm a sandbox.\n\n### Does optional advertising mean short videos?\nNo. Reviewers report long bonus videos.\n\n### Is artwork provenance independently checked?\nNo. The AI-art characterization comes from reviewers.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/merge-mayor/id1487461340?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
