    "bodyMarkdown": "# Screw Sort 3D: Hidden Colors and the Price of Extra Space\n\nScrew Sort 3D turns removing screws into a color-matching task. The sample's central tension is whether extra space feels like optional help or something needed to complete the puzzle. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Unscrewing is only part of the decision\n\nThe developer describes objects covered in colorful screws, with matching colors used to clear them. Hundreds of progressively harder levels are advertised, but exact temporary-hole and box rules are not fully documented.\n\nReviewers add accounts of needing colors concealed under other pieces while limited holding spaces fill. That is useful context, not a verified complete rulebook.\n\nSeveral request the ability to make a piece temporarily transparent. That request should not become an advertised feature or a recommendation to use a control that may not exist.\n\n## Challenge can be welcome until information runs out\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-07-15 through 2026-11-06 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 5.0, 5.1, 5.2, 5.3, 5.4, 5.5; the listing records version 5.6. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive reviewers enjoy the puzzle or describe a balance between challenge and relaxation. Others report repeating arrangements or reaching a point where accessing hidden colors feels dependent on assistance.\n\nOne account says the game ends when the last temporary hole fills even if the removal exposes another useful screw. That is a reported order-of-operations concern, not proof of deliberate manipulation.\n\nAnother reports a lucky-wheel reward differing from the expected prize. Current probabilities, award rules, and technical reliability were not independently checked.\n\n## Optional videos can still dominate a session\n\nSome reviewers say ads occur only when requesting another box. Others say those boxes become practically necessary or describe ads after rounds. Both accounts matter: no forced video during a successful run is not equivalent to never needing a video to make progress.\n\nA purchaser reports effective removal and little pressure; another reports continuing ads after payment. The listing discloses weekly and monthly auto-renewing VIP plans but does not provide complete prices or benefits here.\n\nComments involving outside reward platforms do not establish this game's own verified cash program. Offline access also remains unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nScrew Sort 3D is relevant to readers who enjoy color planning around layered objects and constrained holding space. It is less convincing for someone requiring full visibility before a move or a verified ad-free progression path. The sample preserves genuine enjoyment without turning disputed assistance into a universally optional convenience.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are hidden-color complaints independently reproduced?\nNo. They are player reports.\n\n### Is see-through inspection confirmed?\nNo. Reviewers request it.\n\n### Does the listing disclose subscriptions?\nYes, weekly and monthly VIP auto-renewal.\n\n### Does paid removal establish no videos at all?\nNo. Player accounts conflict and entitlements are unverified.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/screw-sort-3d-pin-puzzle-game/id6633425539?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
