    "bodyMarkdown": "# Wood Block Jam: Sliding to Exits, Not Filling Rows\n\nWood Block Jam's title may suggest a familiar line-clearing board, but its instructions describe sliding blocks out through doors. Its timer is a documented rule, not merely a complaint appearing in reviews. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Make an exit possible before time expires\n\nThe developer describes moving varied block shapes into position and removing them through grinding doors. Obstacles and a deadline create the published challenge.\n\nThat differs from placing incoming pieces until a complete row disappears. Comparisons with Tetris or block-blast games should not erase the actual exit-based instructions.\n\nTeams, leaderboards, tournaments, and offline play are also advertised. The description does not fully specify every color-door restriction or explain which social functions require connection.\n\n## A clock can feel generous or intrusive\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-07-14 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.6, 2.7, 2.8, 2.9; the listing records version 3.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers like the spatial planning, satisfying movement, or challenge. One explicitly enjoys having a timer. Another thinks its duration is more accommodating than comparable games.\n\nOthers want the clock removed or describe extensions as too short to regain their bearings. A reviewer calling the pace unhurried does not establish that a countdown is absent.\n\nThe evidence therefore supports divided timing preferences rather than an untimed recommendation. No single reviewer-mentioned duration should be promoted as the limit for every board.\n\n## Recovery has its own cost\n\nReviewers describe changes to coin rewards, unavailable reward videos, and ads after levels. These reports do not independently prove a level requires payment or establish every current extension price.\n\nSome recent accounts also describe sluggish response or freezing after completion, with missed rewards. Offline play faces a direct contradiction from a reviewer who says it does not work without connection, despite the listing's explicit promise.\n\nOutside reward-app comments do not establish an official cash-earning feature. Current removal terms and stability are unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWood Block Jam is relevant to readers who like clearing routes and can enjoy a time challenge. It is a poor match on the documented rules for someone specifically avoiding countdowns. Its spatial premise is clear, but the sample leaves offline access and the practical value of recovery tools unresolved.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is the main task filling rows?\nThe listing instead describes sliding blocks through exit doors.\n\n### Is the timer documented?\nYes, explicitly.\n\n### Do all reviewers dislike the clock?\nNo. Some appreciate it.\n\n### Is offline play independently verified?\nNo. A player report conflicts with the listing.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wood-block-jam/id6742648678?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
