    "bodyMarkdown": "# Tile Match 3D: A Timed Object Hunt With Disputed Updates\n\nTile Match 3D asks for target objects among a tangled collection. Its sample shows how changes in recognition, targets, and support can matter as much as the difficulty of finding a triple. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Targets give searching a direction\n\nThe listing describes identifying identical three-dimensional objects, collecting them, and matching against time. Boosters and collectible chests extend the basic hunt.\n\nThis is not automatically classic Mahjong simply because the description mentions Mahjong fans. The documented activity is timed object recognition, not clearing accessible pairs from a traditional tile layout.\n\nThe exact holding capacity and each booster's behavior are not fully described. Offline access is explicitly advertised but remains a separate practical claim.\n\n## Updated visuals do not please everyone\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-03-05 through 2026-09-29 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 4.9.6, 4.9.7, 4.9.8, 4.9.9, 5.0.0, 5.0.1, 5.0.3, 5.0.5, 5.0.6, 5.0.7, 5.0.8, 5.0.9; the listing records version 5.1.1. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers describe daily attachment and engaging play. Another likes new graphics and a reported fix. Others say enlarged or more cartoon-like objects are harder to identify, especially when targets and board pieces seem visually inconsistent.\n\nNo screenshot was inspected to resolve those recognition complaints independently. They should not be reduced to a claim that every user dislikes the new appearance.\n\nOther accounts report missing target objects or blank target icons. Those are specific possible defects, not proof that every difficult level is intentionally unwinnable without payment.\n\n## Team systems make recovery consequential\n\nCard requests, event progress, and team communication appear in the reviews. Some report unanswered support requests or lost purchases and rewards after an event problem. Another reports a quick fix, showing that support outcomes are not uniform.\n\nSeveral accounts also describe long between-level ads. Current removal prices, purchase entitlements, and typical frequency are unverified.\n\nThe offline claim is directly challenged by reviewers who say they cannot open or use the game without connection. The evidence does not identify the scope of that discrepancy across versions and activities.\n\n## Verdict\n\nTile Match 3D is relevant to readers who like searching quickly for target triples and participating in surrounding events. It is more cautious for someone relying on verified offline play or stable visual recognition across updates. The sample combines strong attachment with practical concerns about targets and recovery.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is the hunt timed?\nYes, according to the listing.\n\n### Are Mahjong pair rules established?\nNo. The documented task concerns matching target objects.\n\n### Are missing-object reports proven deliberate?\nNo. They remain player reports.\n\n### Do all support accounts describe failure?\nNo. At least one reports issues being fixed quickly.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/tile-match-3d-triple-match/id1578204014?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
