    "bodyMarkdown": "# Puzzle Page: A Daily Puzzle Collection With Access Tradeoffs\n\nPuzzle Page aims to replace a newspaper puzzle section with a daily collection. Its appeal is breadth, but the sample shows that a large archive is only useful when access and saved progress cooperate. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Choose a different kind of thinking\n\nAppyNation lists word, number, picture, and logic puzzles, including crosswords, Sudoku, nonograms, Kakuro, and Bridges. A daily five-letter word challenge and themed special issues add other entry points.\n\nA calendar opens past pages, and instructions, hints, and difficulty options are advertised. This is a collection rather than one mechanic; no single rulebook applies to every puzzle.\n\nThe description itself gives different archive counts in different sections. It is safer to call it a large advertised archive than independently certify one exact total.\n\n## A favorite routine can survive a reported fix\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-07-26 through 2026-11-08 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 7.7.2, 7.8.0, 7.8.1, 7.8.2, 7.8.3, 7.8.4, 7.8.5, 7.9.0, 7.9.2; the listing records version 7.9.2. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nReviewers appreciate the variety and newspaper-style challenge. One finds the subscription worthwhile while requesting dark mode. Others report problems launching after an update, and one explicitly updates the account to say a later version fixed it.\n\nA separate progress-loss complaint contrasts with another account where restarting restored the game. These are distinct reported recoveries, not a guaranteed troubleshooting procedure for everyone.\n\nVisibility concerns include requests for a darker screen and a complaint that enlarged text becomes harder to process. Larger is not automatically more comfortable for every reader.\n\n## Subscription and free access are different offers\n\nThe listing describes auto-renewing VIP access, unlocked daily pages, subscriber issues, and ad removal. It does not provide a complete current price here.\n\nNon-subscribers describe token shortages, failed survey rewards, and ad interruptions. Another reports an ad issue improving. None establishes every present entitlement, typical frequency, or reliable survey return.\n\nAn alarming browser warning is a reported experience, not independent proof of infection. No security or data-usage audit was performed, and offline behavior remains unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPuzzle Page is relevant to readers who want several puzzle families in a recurring daily format. It is more cautious for someone expecting unrestricted free sessions or flawless archive continuity. The sample's appreciation of variety remains strong, but access, visibility, and recovery deserve equal attention.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this only crosswords?\nNo. The listing includes multiple word, number, picture, and logic families.\n\n### Is a past-page calendar advertised?\nYes.\n\n### Does VIP renewal happen automatically?\nThe listing says subscriptions renew automatically.\n\n### Are all launch complaints unresolved?\nNo. One reviewer reports a later update fixing theirs.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/puzzle-page-daily-games/id1308181322?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
