    "bodyMarkdown": "# Jelly Splash: Connecting Longer Lines With Explicit Access Caveats\n\nJelly Splash describes connecting a longer line of Jellys rather than simply swapping a pair of neighboring tiles. Its colorful pitch also includes concrete purchase, advertising, and age disclosures worth reading alongside the relaxed-play language. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Look beyond the first three\n\nWooga says to match three or more Jellys in a row or diagonally and form the longest line possible. The journey involves freeing friends, defeating Slimes, and overcoming obstacles.\n\nThat gives the documented decision a connection-based character: a longer available line may be more relevant than an isolated minimum match. The description does not fully explain every gesture, special effect, or level objective, so a precise scoring formula cannot be supplied.\n\nNor does the source document a standard swap-based match-three board. Similar characters and bright colors are not enough to make different matching rules interchangeable.\n\n## A campaign with additional goals\n\nDifferent worlds, tournaments, events, and a weekly Tower are advertised. Those features can provide reasons to return beyond clearing the next board, but their detailed current schedules and rewards are not independently checked.\n\nThe phrase unlimited game time and encouragement to play at one's pace do not fully explain lives, energy, move limits, or event deadlines. It would be too confident to turn them into a promise of unlimited attempts and no waiting throughout the app.\n\nA reader wanting one simple, repeatable matching format may therefore have different priorities from someone who enjoys a campaign surrounded by changing activities.\n\n## Free play includes disclosed conditions\n\nThe developer says downloading and playing do not require payment, while virtual items can be purchased, including random items. The description does not supply complete prices or random-item odds.\n\nIt also says advertising may appear and that internet may be required for play and social features. This is not an established offline recommendation. The published text says the game is intended for adults eighteen and older; the bright presentation should not override that disclosure.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about difficulty, generosity, repetition, or ad frequency. Screenshots were not inspected, and cognitive benefits were not measured.\n\n## Verdict\n\nJelly Splash looks relevant to adults who enjoy looking for longer matching connections within a continuing campaign. It is less conclusive for someone seeking guaranteed offline play or unconditional, unlimited sessions. Its useful distinction is diagonal line building, not a verified promise of friction-free access.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the minimum advertised match?\nThree Jellys.\n\n### Are diagonal connections described?\nYes.\n\n### Are random purchases disclosed?\nYes, without complete odds in this description.\n\n### Is this verified offline?\nNo. The listing explicitly warns that a connection may be required.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/jelly-splash-fun-puzzle-game/id645949180?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
