    "bodyMarkdown": "# Unsolved: A Hidden-Object Library Built Around Investigations\n\nUnsolved collects hidden-object adventures inside one app rather than presenting a single endless search board. Its attraction is the connection between finding an object and advancing a mystery. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Searches support a story\n\nArtifex Mundi describes investigations involving clues, useful objects, puzzles, and encounters with friendly or hostile characters. Crime scenes, hotels, forests, and other settings frame the searches.\n\nThat suggests alternating observation with narrative and practical problem-solving. The description does not supply a complete inventory interface, hint schedule, or rule for using every discovered object.\n\nIt would therefore be misleading to portray the app as nothing more than tapping a list of concealed items, or to promise a particular inventory-combination system without further documentation.\n\n## Several mysteries share one home\n\nThe developer presents the Eventide trilogy, including a bonus chapter, as a starting point. Mary Gilbert's journey through Slavic folklore provides a more specific example of the collection's tone.\n\nOther advertised premises involve disappearances, family secrets, criminal cases, and supernatural possibilities. These are story hooks, not evidence that every tale uses the same difficulty, length, or puzzle structure.\n\nA reader interested in moving between settings may welcome an anthology. Someone expecting one seamless continuous storyline should distinguish that from several adventures available in one application.\n\n## Promotional praise is not the review sample\n\nThe listing quotes praise for other Artifex Mundi titles. Those selected testimonials are part of the developer's promotional text, not independent customer-review evidence for this app.\n\nNo usable review bodies for Unsolved are supplied here. There is no supported player consensus about story quality, localization, difficulty, energy, or release cadence.\n\nThe artwork is described as hand-painted by the developer, but screenshots and provenance were not inspected. Specific scenes should not be presented as visually observed.\n\n## Free does not explain every gate\n\nThe listing repeatedly calls the collection free. It does not provide a complete access model, purchase schedule, advertising policy, or chapter-unlock terms in this text.\n\nOffline operation is also unverified. Travel-friendly language does not prove network-free access or that every adventure is already downloaded. Current storage demands, stability, and helper conditions remain open.\n\n## Verdict\n\nUnsolved looks relevant to readers who want searches embedded in narrative investigations and several mysteries under one app. It is less conclusive for someone requiring verified unrestricted chapter access or offline travel use. The anthology structure is clear; its practical session economy is not.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this one continuous story?\nThe listing describes a collection of adventures.\n\n### What is the named starting collection?\nThe Eventide trilogy.\n\n### Are quoted developer testimonials independent evidence here?\nNo. They promote other titles within the listing.\n\n### Is offline play confirmed?\nNo. Travel-friendly wording does not establish it.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/unsolved-hidden-mystery-games/id1522149479?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
