    "bodyMarkdown": "# Wordington: Word Solving and Renovation Do Not Always Keep Pace\n\nWordington connects word puzzles to Emma's mansion renovation. Its sample's strongest tension is not simply whether the puzzles are enjoyable, but whether their rewards keep pace with the story and decorating. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Solve words to open another project\n\nThe developer describes forming words from supplied letters, completing crossword levels, and unlocking rooms and chapters. Decoration choices extend from interiors to the garden and garage.\n\nCharacters such as David, Bob, and Max the dog add a social story around the repairs. This is a word-first renovation loop, not a conventional match-three campaign or an unrestricted architecture editor.\n\nThe description promises choices, but does not establish that every component is freely movable or that all design tasks require the same number of puzzle wins.\n\n## One half can appeal more than the other\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-05-29 through 2026-11-01 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 2.9.2, 2.9.3, 2.9.5, 2.9.8, 2.9.9, 3.0.0; the listing records version 3.0.0. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers enjoy combining word solving with restoration and describe the challenge as manageable. Another likes the crosswords but finds the storyline tedious. These preferences need not produce the same recommendation.\n\nOther accounts report waiting for new projects despite accumulating stars. That is a progression mismatch, not necessarily a lack of word puzzles. A reviewer describing plentiful levels provides a useful counterpoint to content-end complaints.\n\nWord acceptance also draws criticism: one account says an entry counts in one context but not another. The supplied evidence does not independently audit the dictionary or establish why an answer is rejected.\n\n## The reward loop includes access friction\n\nReviewers describe ads around rounds and actions, sometimes long ones. Another considers removal inexpensive. Those are subjective and dated impressions, not verified current offers.\n\nThe listing discloses an auto-renewing VIP membership covering advertised benefits such as ad-free play, spins, and wordpass access. Exact present prices and every exclusion are not checked.\n\nSaved progress is another uncertainty: one account describes successful restoration at one time and loss after a later reinstall. Offline support and stability are unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWordington is relevant to readers who want word puzzles to produce a visible home-improvement payoff. It is less conclusive for someone wanting uninterrupted decorating or a guaranteed fresh story after a long run. The sample preserves enthusiasm for the combination while exposing its two different progression speeds.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What drives renovation progress?\nCompleting letter-based crossword puzzles, according to the listing.\n\n### Are decoration choices advertised?\nYes, without unrestricted editing being established.\n\n### Do content-end reports mean no puzzles remain?\nNot necessarily. Some concern story projects rather than word levels.\n\n### Is VIP a one-time payment?\nThe listing describes an auto-renewing subscription.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wordington-word-find-design/id1437273344?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
