    "bodyMarkdown": "# Stack Smash: Choose a Weak Point Before Spending a Cannonball\n\nStack Smash uses a farm festival as the setting for a limited-shot demolition puzzle. The published challenge is choosing where a cannonball can do useful work, not firing indefinitely until everything eventually falls. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Look for leverage, not just a target\n\nTripledot describes aiming a cannon at stacked barrels, crates, jars, hay, fruit, and other objects. One impact can trigger further movement or a chain reaction.\n\nSome levels are advertised as solvable with one carefully placed shot, while others require several. The feature list explicitly mentions limited shots, making angle selection and weak points central to the intended task.\n\nThe exact allowance, success threshold, and targeting gesture are not fully documented. The source does not establish that every visible object must be destroyed or that every collapse earns the same score.\n\n## Physics is a promise to inspect carefully\n\nThe description highlights tumbling wood, rolling barrels, and shattering jars. Those examples explain the intended spectacle, but are not an independent test of repeatable trajectories or collision behavior.\n\nNo screenshot was inspected and no shot was simulated. Specific structure geometry, cannon position, aiming guides, or camera controls are therefore not presented as observed facts.\n\nA player interested in spatial cause and effect may find the premise more relevant than someone seeking pure symbol deduction. The outcome depends on the advertised physical response, whose consistency is not established here.\n\n## The farm frame is not a management game\n\nBarns, stalls, balloons, and farmhand characters provide a festival setting. They do not establish crop production, resource trading, animal care, or an editable farm economy.\n\nLikewise, quick levels and a relaxing pitch do not prove the absence of timers, lives, or other session limits. Limited ammunition is documented; every surrounding condition is not.\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about satisfaction, difficulty spikes, precision, or repeated layouts.\n\n## Payment and replay remain open\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, removal offers, retry pricing, offline support, and stability are unverified. No claim about fair monetization or unlimited practice follows from the cheerful setting.\n\n## Verdict\n\nStack Smash looks relevant to readers who enjoy finding a structure's weak point and seeing a carefully aimed shot cascade through it. It is less conclusive for someone requiring proven physics consistency or unrestricted retries. The limited-shot premise is clear, while the actual feedback and recovery conditions remain untested.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is ammunition unlimited?\nThe listing explicitly advertises limited shots.\n\n### Can one shot clear a level?\nThat possibility is advertised for some levels.\n\n### Is this a farming simulator?\nThe documented activity is cannon-based demolition in a farm setting.\n\n### Are collision physics independently verified?\nNo. They remain developer claims.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/stack-smash-3d-puzzle-game/id6784602967?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
