    "bodyMarkdown": "# Mahjong Solitaire: Classic: A Short Ruleset With Clear Ad Disclosure\n\nMahjong Solitaire: Classic makes a more concrete advertising disclosure than many free puzzle listings. Its actual play instructions, however, are brief enough that some familiar Mahjong assumptions still need qualification. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Clear tiles from an arrangement\n\nBitMango says to match and eliminate all tiles, advertising more than 180 levels and varied layouts. The genre suggests solitaire tile matching rather than a multiplayer Mahjong hand.\n\nIn conventional Mahjong solitaire, players match available pairs and plan removals to expose others. That is genre context, not a complete verified ruleset for this specific app. The supplied description does not explicitly spell out side access, covered tiles, exceptions, or every symbol group.\n\nIt would therefore be too confident to publish a detailed tile-access guide or promise that every deal is solvable without a shuffle.\n\n## Auto-fit needs a definition\n\nAn auto-fit option appears in the feature list without further explanation. It may interest someone concerned about fitting a layout to a display, but the exact behavior is not documented here.\n\nIt should not be turned into an automatic solver, automatic matching, zoom guarantee, or accessibility certification. A short feature name cannot establish all those capabilities.\n\nNo screenshot was visually inspected. Tile markings, contrast, layout density, and display orientation are not independently assessed. The source's attractive-graphics language remains promotional.\n\n## Ads are explicitly part of the free offer\n\nThe developer lists banner, interstitial, video, and house ads. That supports saying advertising is disclosed, not guessing how often any particular format interrupts play.\n\nAn in-app AD FREE item is also advertised. Its current price, duration, restore behavior, and treatment of optional reward videos are not supplied. A removal item should not automatically be called a permanent purchase covering every promotion.\n\nOffline play is explicitly claimed. No network test was performed, so that statement remains attributed to the listing rather than certified as a practical travel recommendation.\n\n## The missing player perspective\n\nNo usable customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported consensus about challenge, hints, repetition, or the value of removal.\n\nThe permission notes in the description are also not an independent platform-permission audit. They should not be expanded into a finding about privacy or technical reliability.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMahjong Solitaire: Classic looks relevant to readers who want a layout-based tile-clearing game and appreciate explicit ad disclosure. It is less conclusive for someone requiring a detailed selection rulebook or verified removal terms. The basic format is identifiable, but several practical controls remain underspecified.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is multiplayer Mahjong documented?\nNo. This is presented as solitaire tile clearing.\n\n### Are ads disclosed?\nYes, including several named formats.\n\n### Is an AD FREE item advertised?\nYes, without complete current terms.\n\n### Does auto-fit mean automatic solving?\nThat is not established by the description.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mahjong-solitaire-classic/id1288458862?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
