    "bodyMarkdown": "# Screw Pin Puzzle: Clear Boards, but Keep the Receivers in View\n\nScrew Pin Puzzle combines removing a board with filling color-specific screw boxes. The sample shows why the receivers themselves matter: hiding information there can change how confidently someone chooses the next pin. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Remove in an order that clears the structure\n\nPIXON describes unscrewing in sequence so boards drop away, then filling boxes with matching-color screws. Boosters and no time limit are explicitly advertised.\n\nThe source does not fully specify temporary-hole capacity, every special screw, or all receiver shapes. The unlimited-levels claim is also not an independently counted catalog.\n\nThis is a constrained sorting task, not simply tapping every exposed screw without regard to where it goes.\n\n## Receiver changes affect planning and comfort\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2025-01-22 through 2026-09-16 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 3.7, 3.8, 3.9; the listing records version 3.9. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nPositive accounts enjoy the puzzle even while noticing occasional duplicates. Others describe boxes changing into covered, animated vehicles, making the next requirement harder to anticipate.\n\nA reviewer with sensory-processing concerns specifically objects to bouncing receivers and unclear new powerups. That is a reported comfort issue, not a universal medical conclusion or an independently observed animation.\n\nSeveral accounts say available holding holes or matching receivers do not behave as expected. Such reports identify possible rule-clarity or technical problems without proving deliberate cheating.\n\n## Rewards and interruptions require separate questions\n\nOne reviewer questions whether a wheel prize is attainable. Repeated misses alone do not establish its probability or prove it impossible.\n\nAd complaints include difficult exits, long waits, and internal purchase prompts. One multi-part account contains both an improved return visit and renewed frustration; it should not be flattened into a permanent fix or permanent failure.\n\nCurrent removal prices, reward odds, offline access, and stability were not independently checked. Reports of security-themed ads do not establish that the app was hacked.\n\n## Verdict\n\nScrew Pin Puzzle is relevant to readers who like combining removal order with matching destinations. It is more cautious for someone relying on fully visible receivers or minimal animation. The sample's continued enjoyment belongs beside concerns about information access, repeated boards, and predictable assistance.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What fills each screw box?\nSame-color screws, according to the listing.\n\n### Is a time limit advertised?\nThe developer explicitly says there is no time limit.\n\n### Are wheel probabilities verified?\nNo. A player's unsuccessful spins cannot establish exact odds.\n\n### Are animated receivers independently inspected?\nNo. Those details come from player reports.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/screw-pin-puzzle/id6480591925?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
