    "bodyMarkdown": "# I Love Hue: Put Color Relationships Ahead of Matching Symbols\n\nI Love Hue makes the color relationship the puzzle rather than using color merely to label matching objects. The developer explicitly frames it as perception-based play, not a conventional logic exercise. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Find a tile's place in a spectrum\n\nZut describes moving colored tiles into ordered mosaics. Success means restoring a coherent spectrum, not clearing three equal pieces or completing a word.\n\nAs genre-level context, comparing how a shade relates to nearby shades is a different activity from identifying one named color in isolation. The supplied description does not fully explain fixed anchors, the move gesture, or which placements are counted as correct at each stage.\n\nThose omissions should not be filled with a precise control guide or claims about unrestricted movement. The core documented action is arranging color, not a particular interface implementation.\n\n## Subtle differences are the intended challenge\n\nThe feature text emphasizes noticing small differences between similar colors. That may appeal to readers who enjoy visual calibration and gradual order rather than a narrative campaign or action sequence.\n\nIt also makes color discrimination central to the decision. The source does not document alternate labels, symbols, contrast settings, or accommodations for different color-vision needs. Their absence from the description is not proof they do not exist, but accessibility should not be certified from the serene pitch.\n\nNo screenshot was visually inspected. Specific palettes, tile counts, mosaic shapes, and screen density are not independently described.\n\n## A comparison system sits inside the calm framing\n\nThe developer advertises more than 900 levels, a synth soundtrack, achievements, and comparison against a world average. That comparison offers an additional performance goal beyond simply completing the arrangement.\n\nThe description does not define the underlying statistic or how the comparison population is selected. It should not be treated as an independently verified ability test, intelligence measure, or live ranking.\n\nLikewise, tranquility does not establish that there are no timers, paid access conditions, or interruptions.\n\n## What this evidence cannot rate\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about the learning curve, visual comfort, repetition, or value.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, removal offers, offline operation, and stability are unverified. Promoted calm is not a demonstrated health outcome.\n\n## Verdict\n\nI Love Hue looks relevant to readers who enjoy ordering visual relationships for their own sake. It is less conclusive for someone needing non-color cues or a fully documented comparison metric. Its distinctive idea is spectrum placement, not a verified promise that every surrounding condition is equally gentle.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this matching identical colors to clear them?\nNo. The listing describes arranging tiles into a spectrum.\n\n### Is color discrimination central?\nYes, explicitly.\n\n### Is the world-average metric independently explained?\nNo. Its exact calculation is not supplied.\n\n### Is a no-timer rule confirmed?\nNo. Calm language alone does not establish it.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/i-love-hue/id1081075274?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
