    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bubble Witch 3 Saga: Rescue Targets and a Home-Rebuilding Loop\n\nBubble Witch 3 Saga gives bubble shooting several narrative jobs: help Stella confront Wilbur, rescue characters, and rebuild her home. The main game and its described iMessage Mini experience also have different connection statements. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Match three with a stated aiming aid\n\nKing explicitly describes matching three bubbles to pop them and using an aiming line. Rescue objectives include owls, ghosts, and the Fairy Queen.\n\nThat gives a board more purpose than simply making the largest possible clear. The exact shot allowance, objective count, and behavior of each special bubble are not fully documented in the supplied description.\n\nThe aiming line is a product feature, not independently verified evidence of perfect accuracy. It should not become a guarantee that every displayed path predicts the same result.\n\n## Shooting supports a second kind of progress\n\nStar dust contributes toward rebuilding Stella's house, while visiting friends' homes is advertised as a reward source. Boosters and leaderboards add assistance and comparison.\n\nThese features may appeal to readers who like a recurring journey around individual shooting puzzles. They do not establish unrestricted house design, a complete furniture economy, or an exact reward for every visit.\n\nNo screenshot was visually inspected. Specific bubble colors, home decorations, and interface arrangements are not described as observed facts.\n\n## Keep Mini separate from the main app\n\nThe listing describes Bubble Witch 3 Mini as a smaller iMessage experience and explicitly requires internet for it. That requirement should not automatically be assigned to every activity in the main game.\n\nFor the main app, connected device syncing is advertised. Offline operation remains unverified. Older compatibility wording in the Mini description is not a current independent device-support check.\n\n## Free play includes paid items\n\nKing says the game is free to play with optional purchases. The source does not give every price, boost entitlement, life rule, or removal offer.\n\nThe privacy text also discloses sharing information with advertising partners. That is a developer disclosure, not a measured ad-frequency or privacy audit. No usable customer-review bodies are supplied, so player praise, complaints, and fairness judgments cannot be invented.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBubble Witch 3 looks relevant to readers who want bubble shooting with rescue goals and a home-restoration frame. It is less conclusive for someone choosing by guaranteed offline access or independently tested aim precision. Its documented objective variety is clearer than its complete session economy.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many bubbles make the advertised match?\nThree.\n\n### Is an aiming line advertised?\nYes, without independently tested accuracy.\n\n### Does Mini require internet?\nThe listing explicitly says so.\n\n### Does that settle all main-game offline behavior?\nNo. The two experiences should not be conflated.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bubble-witch-3-saga/id1095254858?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
