    "bodyMarkdown": "# Wordlook: Variable Word Lengths, Familiar Daily Appeal\n\nWordlook combines hidden-word guesses with colored letter-block clues. A detailed reviewer likes not being able to reuse the same opening guess every round because the available letters and word lengths change. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Choose an order, then read the feedback\n\nThe developer says to select letters in the correct order, use block colors as clues, and spend coins on hints when needed. Daily challenges, weekend events, and chapter rewards extend the ordinary levels.\n\nThe supplied description does not define every feedback color or a universal guess limit. Familiarity with Wordle should not substitute for those missing rules.\n\nNor does a daily challenge necessarily mean only one word can be played each day; the listing separately advertises a larger level collection.\n\n## A routine can stay enjoyable or become repetitive\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2023-04-24 through 2026-10-23 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.112, 1.128, 1.132; the listing records version 1.132. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSeveral reviewers describe years of daily use and enjoy working out a difficult word. One praises variable blanks and letter choices, while another likes a short daily sequence.\n\nOther accounts report repeated words or rejected ordinary entries. A reviewer specifically describes TRUE being rejected. That is a reported dictionary problem, not a complete audit of accepted vocabulary.\n\nThe sample spans several years, so older complaints should not automatically be called current. No measured cognitive benefit follows from players saying the routine helps them think.\n\n## Access claims meet contrary reports\n\nThe listing explicitly advertises offline play. An older reviewer says it does not work without connection, leaving that practical requirement unresolved.\n\nAd accounts also differ. One longtime reviewer reports consistent challenges and enduring removal, while others describe ads after short rounds or difficult exits. More recent accounts report freezing and restarting.\n\nGolden Ticket is an auto-renewing subscription whose benefits can vary by price tier, according to the listing. An ad-free benefit should not be assigned to every tier without checking. Current pricing and restoration terms are unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWordlook is relevant to readers who like word deduction without a fixed-length routine. It is more cautious for someone needing a dependable dictionary or verified offline travel use. The sample supports lasting daily appeal while retaining meaningful disagreements about repetition, interruptions, and access.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Are exact color meanings documented here?\nNo. The listing only identifies colors as clues.\n\n### Do all words have a fixed length?\nA reviewer specifically describes varying lengths.\n\n### Is every Golden Ticket tier necessarily ad-free?\nThe listing says benefits can depend on the tier.\n\n### Is offline use independently confirmed?\nNo. A player account conflicts with the listing.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/wordlook-word-puzzle-games/id1373043021?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
