    "bodyMarkdown": "# Minesweeper Puzzle Bomb: Count the Neighbors Before Opening a Tile\n\nMinesweeper Puzzle Bomb gives familiar minefield deduction a mobile interface with zoom, flags, and custom boards. Its rules are more explicit than a nostalgic title alone would establish. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## A number describes neighboring danger\n\nMaple Media says each covered square reveals a number, blank space, or mine when tapped. Numbers indicate how many mines touch that tile; tapping a mine causes an explosion.\n\nThat means a clue is a constraint on nearby squares, not a suggested next move. Comparing overlapping clues is genre-level reasoning that can narrow which covered squares are dangerous.\n\nThe listing does not guarantee a safe opening, a guess-free board, or protection against an accidental tap. Those can be decisive preferences for experienced players, but are not documented features here.\n\n## Flags mark a belief rather than solve the field\n\nFlag mode is advertised for marking tiles thought to contain bombs. It can help preserve a deduction without opening the suspected square.\n\nA hint is described more narrowly: it reveals the location of one bomb. That should not be expanded into a complete explanation of the logic or a guaranteed safe-cell reveal.\n\nThe source does not fully explain switching between flag and reveal input, undo, or the cost and supply of hints. No control responsiveness or error-prevention system was tested.\n\n## Customization changes the context of a score\n\nFive named difficulty levels extend from Easy to Chaos, and a custom board builder is advertised. The precise dimensions and mine densities are not provided in this description.\n\nStatistics and leaderboards offer performance goals, including beating previous times and scores. Tracking time is not automatically a countdown deadline. Nor does a leaderboard by itself prove that all compared boards have identical configurations.\n\nPinch-to-zoom is a concrete advertised aid for inspecting a board. It is not an independently assessed accessibility solution; screenshots were not inspected and actual tap targets remain unverified.\n\n## Access evidence is thinner than the rules\n\nNo usable customer-review bodies are supplied. There is no supported player consensus about fairness, classic feel, hint quality, or reliability.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Advertising frequency, removal offers, offline operation, and assistance pricing are unknown. A familiar offline PC ancestor does not establish this app's network behavior.\n\n## Verdict\n\nMinesweeper Puzzle Bomb looks relevant to readers who want explicit neighbor-count logic with flags and configurable boards. It is less conclusive for someone requiring guaranteed deduction-only deals or safe first taps. The core minefield is documented; its convenience and recovery policies are not.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What does a number tell the player?\nHow many mines touch that tile.\n\n### What does a hint reveal?\nOne bomb location, according to the listing.\n\n### Are safe first taps guaranteed?\nNot in the supplied description.\n\n### Is a recorded solve time necessarily a deadline?\nNo. Time tracking and countdown limits are different.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/minesweeper-puzzle-bomb/id306937222?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
