    "bodyMarkdown": "# Polygon: Build a Low-Poly Picture Without Drawing Its Outline\n\nPolygon offers picture completion through numbered color regions. Its polygonal style is the distinguishing frame, while the source makes a relatively specific statement about which ad formats premium removes. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Finish the facets rather than sketch the subject\n\nSeverex describes a color-by-number activity with low-poly illustrations. Animals, birds, marine subjects, flowers, places, food, and people are advertised categories.\n\nAs genre context, following assigned numbers emphasizes locating and completing regions rather than drawing an original outline. The description does not give a complete tool guide, zoom behavior, or rule for selecting tiny unfinished areas.\n\nRecoloring is explicitly advertised, but that does not independently establish freehand editing, photo import, or unlimited arbitrary palettes.\n\n## The collection is part of the appeal\n\nDaily additions and holiday content are promised. That can appeal to a reader who wants a recurring choice of pictures rather than one long campaign.\n\nNeither schedule nor catalog breadth was independently verified. No screenshot was inspected, so specific facets, line thickness, image complexity, and actual colors are not described as observed details.\n\nThe style may interest someone who enjoys seeing a picture resolve from geometric regions. Someone seeking open-ended painting or a demonstrable drawing curriculum would be expecting more than the listed guided activity.\n\n## Premium names its scope\n\nPolygon Premium is described as unlocking all pictures and removing interstitial and banner ads. That wording is more precise than a broad assumption that every possible promotion or optional reward video disappears.\n\nThe listing describes monthly and annual plans, including a trial attached to the monthly offer, and automatic renewal. Exact current prices and offer eligibility are not supplied here. A trial is not a permanent free entitlement.\n\nThe free download therefore should not be treated as access to every illustration. At the same time, the description does not establish that a subscription is necessary to complete every free picture.\n\n## Offline is claimed, not tested\n\nThe developer explicitly advertises network-free coloring. Its scope, including obtaining new images or sharing completed work, was not independently tested.\n\nNo usable customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about region selection, picture freshness, ad burden, or premium value. Promoted stress relief remains a claim rather than a measured outcome.\n\n## Verdict\n\nPolygon looks relevant to readers who enjoy guided, geometric picture completion and recoloring. It is less conclusive for someone seeking unrestricted painting or verified access to every image for free. The main format and named premium ad categories are clear; practical controls and current offers are not.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is this freehand sketching?\nThe listed activity is coloring numbered regions.\n\n### Is recoloring advertised?\nYes.\n\n### Which ads does premium explicitly remove?\nInterstitial and banner ads, according to the description.\n\n### Is offline operation independently tested?\nNo. It remains a developer claim.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/polygon-paint-color-by-number/id1411400127?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
