    "bodyMarkdown": "# Bubble Cloud: A Spinning Target With an Explicit No-Ad Promise\n\nBubble Cloud advertises a rotating twist on bubble shooting and repeatedly states that it has no in-game ads. That is a clear developer promise, but it remains different from an independently verified access finding. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## Shooting at a changing arrangement\n\nValas describes a match-three bubble shooter built around spinning mechanics, boosters, and progression through worlds. The rotating element is the stated distinction from a static bubble arrangement.\n\nAs a general design consequence, a changing orientation can make the relationship between a shot and the cluster important. The source does not fully document what triggers each rotation, how much the board turns, or every aiming control.\n\nThose details should not be reconstructed from the word spin alone. A precise bank-shot guide or claimed physics model would exceed the supplied evidence.\n\n## Unlimited lives is a specific promise\n\nThe listing explicitly advertises unlimited lives and play at one's own pace. That addresses an attempt-based waiting system, but does not establish unlimited ammunition within a puzzle, free boosters, or the absence of every event deadline.\n\nDaily puzzles, seasonal events, and more than 1,500 levels are also advertised, with weekly additions. Those counts and schedules were not independently inspected.\n\nA reader who likes revisiting a familiar aiming task may value those additional goals. Someone seeking a single self-contained puzzle should distinguish a continuing progression system from one fixed challenge.\n\n## No ads does not mean no purchases\n\nThe description states no in-game advertising and separately acknowledges optional paid items. Those statements can coexist. A purchase offer is not automatically a third-party video ad, and ad-free language does not establish free access to every helper.\n\nCurrent prices, boost value, and the practical need for assistance are unknown. No purchase-fairness judgment is supported here.\n\nThe listing also says players must be at least thirteen. Its bright theme should not replace that explicit age disclosure when considering suitability.\n\n## Evidence limits\n\nNo customer-review bodies are available in the supplied evidence. There is no supported player consensus about difficulty, interruption, or repeated boards.\n\nScreenshots were not visually inspected, and offline operation and stability were not tested. The privacy disclosure is not an independent privacy audit. The no-ad claim is attributed, not converted into a verified needs tag.\n\n## Verdict\n\nBubble Cloud looks relevant to readers seeking a spinning bubble shooter with an explicit no-ad and unlimited-life pitch. It is less conclusive for someone requiring verified offline support or unrestricted free tools. The promises are clear; their practical implementation remains untested here.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is spinning part of the advertised mechanic?\nYes.\n\n### Does the developer explicitly say no in-game ads?\nYes, without independent verification here.\n\n### Does unlimited lives mean unlimited shots?\nThat is not established.\n\n### Are purchases still disclosed?\nYes. Optional paid items are acknowledged.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bubble-cloud-spin-pop/id806904019?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
