    "bodyMarkdown": "# Cube Tap Sort: Active Orders Make Buffer Space Matter\n\nCube Tap Sort is not just a matter of tapping a colorful structure until it disappears. The active order determines whether a block goes directly toward a goal or occupies limited buffer space. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Read the destination before the block\n\nTripledot says to match block colors to active order boxes. A wrong selection enters the buffer, and filling that buffer ends the level.\n\nThat gives an exposed block two different values: it may satisfy the current order, or it may need temporary storage while clearing access. The description does not document exact buffer capacity or every exposure rule.\n\nHundreds of hand-designed levels and increasing difficulty are advertised, not independently counted or tested.\n\n## Visibility can be helpful until the needed piece is hidden\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-28 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 1.6.3, 1.7.0, 1.7.5; the listing records version 1.7.7. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nOne reviewer likes the bright, distinguishable colors and response. Others enjoy the routine or appreciate slowing down to inspect it. A dissenting reviewer finds the selection task insufficiently challenging.\n\nMore specific complaints concern hidden blocks, apparently missing colors, or boxes still requiring pieces after no matching ones remain. These are reported problems, not independently verified proof of deliberately impossible levels.\n\nNo screenshot was inspected to resolve their exact layouts. One player's claimed evidence cannot substitute for direct observation here.\n\n## Coffee breaks are described as interruptions\n\nSeveral reviewers distinguish optional ads for another basket from unwanted coffee-break videos during a round. That difference matters in a task where remembering colors and locations is part of planning.\n\nA paid-removal purchaser says further videos or costly helpers remain necessary. The report does not establish every current entitlement, but it does prevent treating paid removal as a verified unrestricted path.\n\nOther reviews concern tracking challenges in another app. Those are separate from the basic color-order rules and do not establish this game's own cash payouts. Current removal prices, offline support, and stability remain unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nCube Tap Sort is relevant to readers who enjoy choosing selections around active orders and limited overflow. It is more cautious for someone needing uninterrupted concentration or verified visibility of every required color. The buffer rule is clear; the sample raises practical concerns about interruptions and what remains searchable.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Where does a wrong-color block go?\nInto the buffer, according to the listing.\n\n### What happens when the buffer fills?\nThe listing says the level ends.\n\n### Are missing-color reports proven deliberate?\nNo. They are player accounts, not an audited design finding.\n\n### Are coffee breaks necessarily voluntary?\nSeveral reviewers explicitly describe them as midgame interruptions.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cube-tap-sort/id6756307486?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
