    "bodyMarkdown": "# Scavenger Hunt: Search a Growing Town Without Assuming an Editor\n\nScavenger Hunt describes searching an animated fictional town for objects on a list. A separate customization claim appears in the listing, but it is much less fully explained than the ordinary search loop. This source-based analysis uses the US App Store listing, not hands-on play.\n\n## The list gives a large map a purpose\n\nENJOY GAMES says to inspect the map, tap found objects, and unlock further areas through level completion. A target panel identifies what to locate, while zoom supports inspecting a smaller region.\n\nMoving characters and unexpected object placements form part of the developer's description. They are not firsthand observations here; no screenshot was visually inspected.\n\nThe instructions do not fully explain inaccurate taps, target counts, or whether every listed object is visible from the start. Those details matter in a dense scene and should not be guessed.\n\n## Untimed does not mean aimless\n\nThe feature list explicitly says there is no time limit. Finding items more quickly is presented as a way to reveal maps sooner, not proof of a countdown or competitive speed requirement.\n\nThat combination may appeal to readers who enjoy a lingering inspection task with a visible completion goal. Someone seeking short, isolated scenes should distinguish that from the advertised enormous, expanding maps.\n\nA general search method is dividing a large scene into regions and checking them methodically. That is genre-level advice, not a tested route through any particular map in this app.\n\n## Custom hunts need more explanation\n\nThe listing says players can choose locations, objects, clues, and hints to create personalized scavenger hunts. It does not provide editor controls, sharing rules, unlock conditions, or a walkthrough of that feature.\n\nThat claim should remain attributed rather than being turned into a verified level-creation tool. It also does not establish real-world location tracking or an outdoor treasure hunt; the detailed gameplay description concerns virtual maps.\n\n## Hints and access remain separate issues\n\nHints are advertised for locating an item, without complete prices or usage limits. No customer-review bodies are supplied, so there is no supported player consensus about hidden-item fairness, hint usefulness, repetition, or visual comfort.\n\nThe metadata records a free download. Ad frequency, removal offers, offline support, and stability are unverified. Claims of improved attention or meditative benefits are not independently measured outcomes.\n\n## Verdict\n\nScavenger Hunt looks relevant to readers who enjoy a large, evolving object-search scene and an explicitly untimed pitch. It is less conclusive for someone choosing primarily for a custom-level editor or guaranteed offline access. The standard map search is clearer than the broader customization promise.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What identifies the search targets?\nA panel listing objects, according to the description.\n\n### Is zoom advertised?\nYes.\n\n### Is custom-hunt creation verified here?\nNo. It is a listing claim without detailed controls.\n\n### Is this necessarily an outdoor GPS hunt?\nNo. The documented loop concerns virtual maps.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/scavenger-hunt-hidden-objects/id1661342715?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
