    "bodyMarkdown": "# Word Master: Adjacent-Letter Searches With Art as the Reward\n\nWord Master pairs adjacent-letter searches with an art collection. The sample's strongest praise concerns that combination, while practical criticism asks for clearer rewards and more freedom to keep searching after a round ends. This source-based review combines the US App Store listing with player reports; it is not a hands-on playtest.\n\n## Follow connected letters, not arbitrary combinations\n\nFugo describes swiping across adjacent letter tiles to form words and unlock famous artworks. That adjacency requirement distinguishes the game from an unrestricted letter-wheel anagram puzzle.\n\nThe listing explicitly says no timers. It does not fully document tile reuse, every scoring rule, or the exact point target for each round.\n\nNames such as Da Vinci and Van Gogh describe the advertised art collection, not an independent check of reproduction quality or historical accuracy.\n\n## The collection gives repeated searches another purpose\n\nReviewers in this sample contributed 50 US App Store reviews dated 2026-09-19 through 2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai), covering versions 0.6.6, 0.6.7, 0.6.8, 0.6.9; the listing records version 0.6.9. Apple's most-recent RSS sample is capped at 50 and self-selected, not representative of all players or a live consensus. These are player reports, not independently verified findings.\n\nSome reviewers enjoy the untimed, Boggle-like activity and appreciate short facts accompanying the artwork. Others want broader representation among artists or an option to continue finding words after meeting the required points.\n\nThose requests should not become existing features. They show that a player can value a short round while another wants to exhaust the same board more thoroughly.\n\nDictionary accounts also conflict. One says English words are broadly accepted, while another names missing entries. Neither establishes a complete dictionary audit, and universal acceptance would be too strong a conclusion.\n\n## Coins do not explain themselves\n\nSeveral reviewers say they accumulate coins without understanding their purpose. Another reports changed hint rewards affecting a side challenge. These are clarity and progression concerns, not proof that every currency is unusable.\n\nAd experiences range from praise for few interruptions to long videos, flashing placements, and a purchaser reporting continued ads. Current removal price and entitlements are unverified.\n\nA reported security-themed warning is not proof of malware, and accusations about AI-generated assets were not independently investigated. Offline support and stability also remain unverified.\n\n## Verdict\n\nWord Master is relevant to readers who enjoy finding connected words and collecting art alongside the puzzle. It is more cautious for someone requiring a complete dictionary, fully explained currencies, or unrestricted post-goal searching. The sample's affection for the art context survives without converting promotional learning claims into measured benefits.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Must letters be adjacent?\nThe listing explicitly says so.\n\n### Is the main activity advertised as untimed?\nYes.\n\n### Is continuing after the goal confirmed?\nNo. Reviewers request that option.\n\n### Are all words guaranteed acceptance?\nNo. The sampled accounts conflict.\n\n[View the official US App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/word-master/id6739423612?uo=4) · [How evidence differs from playtesting](https://puzzlereviewlab.com/how-we-test/)"
